window.DOMINANCE_TOPICS = [{"slug":"cv-introduction","title":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"1-6","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system","title":"1. Why do we need a circulatory system?"},{"id":"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways","title":"2. The two major circulatory pathways"},{"id":"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers","title":"3. The heart and its four chambers"},{"id":"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right","title":"4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?"},{"id":"5-systole-and-diastole","title":"5. Systole and diastole"},{"id":"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system","title":"6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system"},{"id":"7-understanding-check","title":"7. Understanding Check"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Introduction to the Heart and Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system\">1. Why do we need a circulatory system?</h2>\n<p>Every cell in the body needs a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients. At the same time it keeps making waste, including carbon dioxide, which must be carried away.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can't cells simply rely on diffusion?</strong> Over very short distances, diffusion is fast: oxygen crosses the few micrometres from a capillary to a nearby cell in a fraction of a second. But the time diffusion takes grows with the <strong>square</strong> of the distance. Moving oxygen 1 mm by diffusion alone takes minutes, and 1 cm takes hours.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Distance</th>\n  <th>Rough time for oxygen to diffuse</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>10 µm (capillary to a nearby cell)</td>\n  <td>about 0.02 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>1 mm</td>\n  <td>a few minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>1 cm</td>\n  <td>several hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A body many centimetres thick could never be supplied that way. So the body uses <strong><span data-term=\"bulk-flow\">bulk flow</span></strong> instead. The heart pumps blood quickly through vessels to within a few micrometres of almost every cell, and diffusion only has to cover that last tiny step. The lecture sums up the heart's job simply: to move the blood about, sending it to all the tissues.</p>\n<p>The <strong>circulatory system</strong> has three parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> the pump.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood vessels:</strong> the pipes that carry blood to every tissue and back.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood:</strong> the carrier. It transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a tissue's needs rise, the circulation responds. Working muscle during exercise receives more blood, which brings extra oxygen and removes extra carbon dioxide.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Diffusion times are approximate (time ≈ distance² ÷ 2D, with D for oxygen in water). They are foundation material added to explain why a pump is needed. They are not on the lecture slides.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">2. The two major circulatory pathways</h2>\n<p>Blood travels through two circuits, one after the other:</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> The right side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs. There, carbon dioxide leaves the blood and oxygen enters it. The oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> The left side of the heart pumps oxygen-rich blood to the tissues of the whole body. The tissues take up oxygen and add carbon dioxide, and the blood returns to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-01\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size pulmonary and systemic circulation diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of systemic and pulmonary circulation, showing blood travelling between the right heart and lungs, then the left heart and body organs.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>The two circuits are <span data-term=\"in-series\">in series</span>.</strong> Blood must pass through one circuit before it can enter the other: body → right heart → lungs → left heart → body. There is no other route.</p>\n<p><strong>Why must both ventricles pump the same amount?</strong> Because every drop the right ventricle sends through the lungs arrives at the left ventricle. Over any period longer than a few beats, the right and left ventricles must pump the same volume per minute: about 5 L/min each at rest. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more than the left for a long time, blood would pile up in the lungs. If the left pumped more than the right, the lungs would slowly drain. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">→ Cardiac Output, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Within the systemic circuit, the organs sit side by side (<span data-term=\"in-parallel\">in parallel</span>).</strong> The aorta divides into branches that supply the brain, the heart muscle, the gut and liver, the kidneys, the skeletal muscles and the skin. Because each organ has its own branch:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>every organ receives fresh, oxygen-rich blood from the arteries, not blood another organ has already used;</li>\n<li>the flow to each organ can be turned up or down on its own, by narrowing or widening that organ's small arteries, without starving the others. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">→ Haemodynamics, §8</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lungs are the exception: all of the blood passes through them, in series.</p>\n<p><strong>The heart feeds itself the same way.</strong> The heart does not take its oxygen from the blood inside its chambers. Its muscular wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers to most of its cells. So the heart muscle has its own parallel branch from the aorta: the <strong><span data-term=\"coronary-arteries\">coronary arteries</span></strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\">→ Coronary Circulation, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"circuit-map\"><figcaption>Series and parallel</figcaption><p>One loop runs right heart → lungs → left heart → aorta, labelled in series. The aorta then splits into side-by-side branches for the brain, the heart muscle (coronary arteries), the gut and liver, the kidneys, skeletal muscle and the skin, labelled in parallel. The branches rejoin in the veins and return to the right heart. Gut and liver are drawn as one branch because blood from the gut passes through the liver.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture gives about 5.5 L/min for each side of the heart; 5 L/min is also widely quoted. The parallel arrangement of organs is foundation material for the regional-circulation chapters.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\">3. The heart and its four chambers</h2>\n<p>The heart is a muscular organ that works as <strong>two pumps side by side</strong>. The right heart pumps blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps blood to the body. Each pump has two chambers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right atrium:</strong> receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae.</li>\n<li><strong>Right ventricle:</strong> pumps that blood into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs.</li>\n<li><strong>Left atrium:</strong> receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Left ventricle:</strong> pumps that blood into the aorta, which carries it to the whole body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>atria</strong> mainly receive blood and top up the ventricles. The <strong>ventricles</strong> do the main pumping: they generate the pressure that drives blood into the arteries.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-02\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size labelled heart anatomy diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" alt=\"Sectional anatomy of the human heart showing four chambers, cardiac valves, great vessels, interventricular septum, and the thicker left ventricular wall.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>How does blood flow through the heart?</strong> Follow the path:</p>\n<p><strong>Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What keeps blood moving one way?</strong> Four valves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>inlet valves</strong> sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the <strong>tricuspid</strong> valve on the right and the <strong>mitral</strong> valve on the left. Together they are the <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV (atrioventricular) valves</span>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>outlet valves</strong> sit between each ventricle and its artery: the <strong>pulmonary</strong> valve on the right and the <strong>aortic</strong> valve on the left. Together they are the <span data-term=\"semilunar-valves\">semilunar valves</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Valves contain no muscle. They open and shut <strong>passively</strong>, pushed by blood pressure. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts the moment that reverses. So blood can only move forward. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Arteries and veins are named by direction, not by oxygen.</strong> The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. This is because <strong>arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood towards it</strong>, whatever its oxygen content.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why can't diffusion alone supply the cells of the body?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Diffusion stops working once blood starts to flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Oxygen cannot cross cell membranes without a pump</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Cells use oxygen faster than any blood flow can bring it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Diffusion time rises steeply as the distance grows</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance. Micrometres take a fraction of a second, but centimetres take hours. The circulation carries blood close to every cell, so diffusion only covers the last few micrometres.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> For a week, a person's right ventricle pumps slightly more blood per minute than the left. Where does the extra blood collect?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the systemic veins, which hold most of the blood volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the pulmonary vessels, between the two ventricles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the right atrium, because the tricuspid valve shuts</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the aorta, which stretches to store the extra volume</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The circuits are in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps goes through the lungs to the left heart. If the left ventricle sends on less than arrives, the difference builds up in the lung vessels.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does the brain still receive oxygen-rich blood when the leg muscles are working hard?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood reaches the brain before it reaches the leg muscles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The brain extracts oxygen faster than the leg muscles can</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Each organ has its own branch from the aorta, side by side</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart pumps a separate circuit just for the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Systemic organs are supplied in parallel. The brain's branch carries fresh arterial blood, not blood the muscles have already used. Flow to each organ can also be adjusted separately.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A vessel carries oxygen-rich blood towards the heart. What is it?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A pulmonary vein, because it carries blood towards the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A pulmonary artery, because its blood is rich in oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A systemic artery, because oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A coronary artery, because it supplies the heart muscle</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Vessels are named by direction. Veins carry blood towards the heart, and the pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\">4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?</h2>\n<p>Both ventricles pump the same volume each minute (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">§2</a>). Yet the left ventricle's wall is two to three times thicker than the right's. The difference is <strong>pressure, not volume</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What sets the pressure a pump must make?</strong> Blood flows only when there is a pressure difference to push it. The more the vessels oppose flow (their <strong><span data-term=\"resistance\">resistance</span></strong>), the bigger the pressure difference needed to keep the same flow. The lecture puts it as: flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=2-what-actually-determines-flow\">→ Haemodynamics, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> a vast network of small, muscular arteries and arterioles, so total resistance is high. The left ventricle must reach about <strong>120 mmHg</strong> with each beat.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> short, wide, thin-walled vessels, so resistance is low. The right ventricle needs only about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Generating more pressure takes more muscle, so the left ventricular wall is thicker. <strong>Same volume per minute, different pressure.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why is it useful for the lungs to have low pressure?</strong> Lung capillaries are extremely thin, so that gases can cross them quickly. High pressure inside them would push fluid out into the lung tissue and air spaces. This is <strong><span data-term=\"pulmonary-oedema\">pulmonary oedema</span></strong>, and it gets in the way of gas exchange. Low pulmonary pressure keeps the lungs dry. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">→ Microcirculation, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-systole-and-diastole\">5. Systole and diastole</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">Systole</span></strong> means contraction and <strong>diastole</strong> means relaxation. Always say which chambers you mean, because the atria and ventricles contract at different times.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> the atria contract and push the final portion of blood into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial diastole:</strong> the atria relax and fill with blood returning from the body and lungs.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole:</strong> the ventricles contract and eject blood into the pulmonary trunk (right) and the aorta (left).</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular diastole:</strong> the ventricles relax and fill for the next beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The order matters.</strong> The atria contract <strong>at the end of ventricular diastole</strong>, just before the ventricles contract. By then, most ventricular filling (about 70% in the lecture) has already happened passively. Atrial contraction only tops it up.</p>\n<p>At a resting heart rate, one beat lasts about 0.8 s. The ventricles spend about 0.3 s in systole and 0.5 s in diastole. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system\">6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system</h2>\n<p>To pump well, the heart needs two things: <strong>force</strong> and <strong>timing</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Force: cardiac muscle (myocardium).</strong> Cardiac muscle cells are electrically linked to one another. An impulse therefore spreads from cell to cell, and the muscle of each chamber contracts as one unit. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">→ Cardiac Muscle, §2</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Timing: the conducting system.</strong> The impulse starts in the <strong><span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span></strong>, at the junction of the superior vena cava and the right atrium. It spreads across the atria to the <strong><span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span></strong>, which holds it back briefly. It then races down the <strong>bundle of His</strong> and the <strong><span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">Purkinje fibres</span></strong> through both ventricles. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §2</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture notes that every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. The SA node normally sets the pace because it fires <strong>fastest</strong>, so it triggers the rest before they can fire themselves. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Electrical activation always comes first; contraction follows.</strong> The order in which the impulse spreads sets the order of contraction: the atria first, then, after the AV-node delay, the ventricles.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Lecture connection: Circulation 1, opening slides (the heart's structure and function, conducting tissues, systole and diastole). The diffusion explanation and the parallel arrangement of organs are added foundations.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Both ventricles eject about 70 mL per beat. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It must eject a larger volume of blood each minute</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It must generate higher pressure against higher resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It contracts for longer because the aortic valve is smaller</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It must also pump blood through the coronary arteries first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The two ventricles move the same volume, but the systemic circulation offers far more resistance. The left ventricle needs about 120 mmHg against the right's 25, and more pressure needs more muscle.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is low pressure in the pulmonary circulation an advantage?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It lets the right ventricle pump less blood than the left</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It slows the blood so that more carbon dioxide can be made</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It stops the pulmonary valve from opening during systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It keeps fluid from being pushed out of the lung capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Thin lung capillaries leak fluid if pressure inside them is high, which causes pulmonary oedema. Low pressure keeps the lungs dry. Both ventricles still pump the same volume.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> During atrial systole, what are the ventricles normally doing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Contracting, so the atria and ventricles squeeze together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Ejecting blood into the aorta and pulmonary trunk at once</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Relaxed, finishing filling just before their own contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Relaxing after ejection, with all four valves still shut</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. They top up the relaxed ventricles just before the ventricles contract; the AV-node delay keeps that order.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why does the SA node normally set the heart rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It fires fastest, so it triggers the others first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is the only part connected to the nervous system</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It lies in the left ventricle, the strongest chamber</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It holds the impulse back so the atria contract first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The fastest pacemaker wins: the SA node fires before the others reach their own firing point. D describes the AV node. The SA node lies in the right atrium.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-understanding-check\">7. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> For several minutes, a patient's left ventricle pumps less than the right ventricle. Where does blood build up first?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the lungs, raising the pressure in the pulmonary vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the leg veins, causing swelling around the ankles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the right ventricle, whose wall thickens to cope</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the pulmonary veins, which then carry oxygen-poor blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The lungs sit between the right and left ventricles. If the left side sends on less than the right side delivers, blood accumulates in the lung vessels first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which statement about the heart valves is correct?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Valves open when their muscle fibres contract</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Valves open and shut because of pressure differences</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Valves are opened by impulses from the AV node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Valves shut as soon as the atria begin to relax</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Valves have no muscle and no nerve control. Pressure behind a valve opens it, and reversed pressure shuts it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood into the pulmonary trunk. Why is the pulmonary trunk still called an artery?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It has a thick muscular wall like the aorta</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It carries blood at the highest pressure in the body</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It carries blood away from the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It carries blood back to the left atrium</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry it towards the heart, whatever the oxygen content.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Diffusion only works over micrometres.</strong> The circulation brings blood within a few micrometres of almost every cell.</li>\n<li><strong>The pulmonary and systemic circuits are in series.</strong> Both ventricles pump the same volume per minute (about 5 L/min at rest). A lasting mismatch makes blood pile up in one circuit.</li>\n<li><strong>The systemic organs are in parallel.</strong> Each gets fresh arterial blood, and flow to each can be adjusted separately. The heart muscle has its own branch: the coronary arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Four chambers:</strong> the atria receive blood and top up the ventricles; the ventricles generate the pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Valves are passive.</strong> They open and shut with pressure differences, keeping flow one-way.</li>\n<li><strong>Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry it towards the heart</strong>, whatever its oxygen content.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle is thicker because it needs more pressure</strong> (about 120 vs 25 mmHg), not because it pumps more volume. Low lung pressure keeps the lungs dry.</li>\n<li><strong>Systole is contraction; diastole is relaxation.</strong> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation (SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje) comes first and sets the order of contraction.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation 1. Why do we need a circulatory system? Every cell in the body needs a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients. At the same time it keeps making waste, including carbon dioxide, which must be carried away. Why can't cells simply rely on diffusion? Over very short distances, diffusion is fast: oxygen crosses the few micrometres from a capillary to a nearby cell in a fraction of a second. But the time diffusion takes grows with the square of the distance. Moving oxygen 1 mm by diffusion alone takes minutes, and 1 cm takes hours. Distance Rough time for oxygen to diffuse 10 µm (capillary to a nearby cell) about 0.02 s 1 mm a few minutes 1 cm several hours A body many centimetres thick could never be supplied that way. So the body uses bulk flow instead. The heart pumps blood quickly through vessels to within a few micrometres of almost every cell, and diffusion only has to cover that last tiny step. The lecture sums up the heart's job simply: to move the blood about, sending it to all the tissues. The circulatory system has three parts: Heart: the pump. Blood vessels: the pipes that carry blood to every tissue and back. Blood: the carrier. It transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat. When a tissue's needs rise, the circulation responds. Working muscle during exercise receives more blood, which brings extra oxygen and removes extra carbon dioxide. Source note: Diffusion times are approximate (time ≈ distance² ÷ 2D, with D for oxygen in water). They are foundation material added to explain why a pump is needed. They are not on the lecture slides. 2. The two major circulatory pathways Blood travels through two circuits, one after the other: Pulmonary circulation: The right side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs. There, carbon dioxide leaves the blood and oxygen enters it. The oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart. Systemic circulation: The left side of the heart pumps oxygen-rich blood to the tissues of the whole body. The tissues take up oxygen and add carbon dioxide, and the blood returns to the right side of the heart. Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation. Image credit OpenStax College, original illustration, CC BY 3.0; unmodified. The two circuits are in series. Blood must pass through one circuit before it can enter the other: body → right heart → lungs → left heart → body. There is no other route. Why must both ventricles pump the same amount? Because every drop the right ventricle sends through the lungs arrives at the left ventricle. Over any period longer than a few beats, the right and left ventricles must pump the same volume per minute: about 5 L/min each at rest. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more than the left for a long time, blood would pile up in the lungs. If the left pumped more than the right, the lungs would slowly drain. ( → Cardiac Output, §1) Within the systemic circuit, the organs sit side by side ( in parallel). The aorta divides into branches that supply the brain, the heart muscle, the gut and liver, the kidneys, the skeletal muscles and the skin. Because each organ has its own branch: every organ receives fresh, oxygen-rich blood from the arteries, not blood another organ has already used; the flow to each organ can be turned up or down on its own, by narrowing or widening that organ's small arteries, without starving the others. ( → Haemodynamics, §8) The lungs are the exception: all of the blood passes through them, in series. The heart feeds itself the same way. The heart does not take its oxygen from the blood inside its chambers. Its muscular wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers to most of its cells. So the heart muscle has its own parallel branch from the aorta: the coronary arteries. ( → Coronary Circulation, §1) Series and parallel One loop runs right heart → lungs → left heart → aorta, labelled in series. The aorta then splits into side-by-side branches for the brain, the heart muscle (coronary arteries), the gut and liver, the kidneys, skeletal muscle and the skin, labelled in parallel. The branches rejoin in the veins and return to the right heart. Gut and liver are drawn as one branch because blood from the gut passes through the liver. Source note: The lecture gives about 5.5 L/min for each side of the heart; 5 L/min is also widely quoted. The parallel arrangement of organs is foundation material for the regional-circulation chapters. 3. The heart and its four chambers The heart is a muscular organ that works as two pumps side by side. The right heart pumps blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps blood to the body. Each pump has two chambers: Right atrium: receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae. Right ventricle: pumps that blood into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs. Left atrium: receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Left ventricle: pumps that blood into the aorta, which carries it to the whole body. The atria mainly receive blood and top up the ventricles. The ventricles do the main pumping: they generate the pressure that drives blood into the arteries. Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart. Image credit Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., original illustration, CC BY 3.0; unmodified. How does blood flow through the heart? Follow the path: Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues. What keeps blood moving one way? Four valves: The inlet valves sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the tricuspid valve on the right and the mitral valve on the left. Together they are the AV (atrioventricular) valves. The outlet valves sit between each ventricle and its artery: the pulmonary valve on the right and the aortic valve on the left. Together they are the semilunar valves. Valves contain no muscle. They open and shut passively, pushed by blood pressure. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts the moment that reverses. So blood can only move forward. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §2) Arteries and veins are named by direction, not by oxygen. The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. This is because arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood towards it, whatever its oxygen content. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why can't diffusion alone supply the cells of the body? A. Diffusion stops working once blood starts to flow B. Oxygen cannot cross cell membranes without a pump C. Cells use oxygen faster than any blood flow can bring it D. Diffusion time rises steeply as the distance grows Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance. Micrometres take a fraction of a second, but centimetres take hours. The circulation carries blood close to every cell, so diffusion only covers the last few micrometres. 2. For a week, a person's right ventricle pumps slightly more blood per minute than the left. Where does the extra blood collect? A. In the systemic veins, which hold most of the blood volume B. In the pulmonary vessels, between the two ventricles C. In the right atrium, because the tricuspid valve shuts D. In the aorta, which stretches to store the extra volume Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The circuits are in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps goes through the lungs to the left heart. If the left ventricle sends on less than arrives, the difference builds up in the lung vessels. 3. Why does the brain still receive oxygen-rich blood when the leg muscles are working hard? A. Blood reaches the brain before it reaches the leg muscles B. The brain extracts oxygen faster than the leg muscles can C. Each organ has its own branch from the aorta, side by side D. The heart pumps a separate circuit just for the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Systemic organs are supplied in parallel. The brain's branch carries fresh arterial blood, not blood the muscles have already used. Flow to each organ can also be adjusted separately. 4. A vessel carries oxygen-rich blood towards the heart. What is it? A. A pulmonary vein, because it carries blood towards the heart B. A pulmonary artery, because its blood is rich in oxygen C. A systemic artery, because oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs D. A coronary artery, because it supplies the heart muscle Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Vessels are named by direction. Veins carry blood towards the heart, and the pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. 4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right? Both ventricles pump the same volume each minute ( §2). Yet the left ventricle's wall is two to three times thicker than the right's. The difference is pressure, not volume. What sets the pressure a pump must make? Blood flows only when there is a pressure difference to push it. The more the vessels oppose flow (their resistance), the bigger the pressure difference needed to keep the same flow. The lecture puts it as: flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. ( → Haemodynamics, §2) Systemic circulation: a vast network of small, muscular arteries and arterioles, so total resistance is high. The left ventricle must reach about 120 mmHg with each beat. Pulmonary circulation: short, wide, thin-walled vessels, so resistance is low. The right ventricle needs only about 25 mmHg. Generating more pressure takes more muscle, so the left ventricular wall is thicker. Same volume per minute, different pressure. Why is it useful for the lungs to have low pressure? Lung capillaries are extremely thin, so that gases can cross them quickly. High pressure inside them would push fluid out into the lung tissue and air spaces. This is pulmonary oedema, and it gets in the way of gas exchange. Low pulmonary pressure keeps the lungs dry. ( → Microcirculation, §5) 5. Systole and diastole Systole means contraction and diastole means relaxation. Always say which chambers you mean, because the atria and ventricles contract at different times. Atrial systole: the atria contract and push the final portion of blood into the ventricles. Atrial diastole: the atria relax and fill with blood returning from the body and lungs. Ventricular systole: the ventricles contract and eject blood into the pulmonary trunk (right) and the aorta (left). Ventricular diastole: the ventricles relax and fill for the next beat. The order matters. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole, just before the ventricles contract. By then, most ventricular filling (about 70% in the lecture) has already happened passively. Atrial contraction only tops it up. At a resting heart rate, one beat lasts about 0.8 s. The ventricles spend about 0.3 s in systole and 0.5 s in diastole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §1) 6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system To pump well, the heart needs two things: force and timing. Force: cardiac muscle (myocardium). Cardiac muscle cells are electrically linked to one another. An impulse therefore spreads from cell to cell, and the muscle of each chamber contracts as one unit. ( → Cardiac Muscle, §2) Timing: the conducting system. The impulse starts in the SA node, at the junction of the superior vena cava and the right atrium. It spreads across the atria to the AV node, which holds it back briefly. It then races down the bundle of His and the Purkinje fibres through both ventricles. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §2) The lecture notes that every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. The SA node normally sets the pace because it fires fastest, so it triggers the rest before they can fire themselves. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) Electrical activation always comes first; contraction follows. The order in which the impulse spreads sets the order of contraction: the atria first, then, after the AV-node delay, the ventricles. Source note: Lecture connection: Circulation 1, opening slides (the heart's structure and function, conducting tissues, systole and diastole). The diffusion explanation and the parallel arrangement of organs are added foundations. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Both ventricles eject about 70 mL per beat. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker? A. It must eject a larger volume of blood each minute B. It must generate higher pressure against higher resistance C. It contracts for longer because the aortic valve is smaller D. It must also pump blood through the coronary arteries first Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The two ventricles move the same volume, but the systemic circulation offers far more resistance. The left ventricle needs about 120 mmHg against the right's 25, and more pressure needs more muscle. 2. Why is low pressure in the pulmonary circulation an advantage? A. It lets the right ventricle pump less blood than the left B. It slows the blood so that more carbon dioxide can be made C. It stops the pulmonary valve from opening during systole D. It keeps fluid from being pushed out of the lung capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Thin lung capillaries leak fluid if pressure inside them is high, which causes pulmonary oedema. Low pressure keeps the lungs dry. Both ventricles still pump the same volume. 3. During atrial systole, what are the ventricles normally doing? A. Contracting, so the atria and ventricles squeeze together B. Ejecting blood into the aorta and pulmonary trunk at once C. Relaxed, finishing filling just before their own contraction D. Relaxing after ejection, with all four valves still shut Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. They top up the relaxed ventricles just before the ventricles contract; the AV-node delay keeps that order. 4. Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why does the SA node normally set the heart rate? A. It fires fastest, so it triggers the others first B. It is the only part connected to the nervous system C. It lies in the left ventricle, the strongest chamber D. It holds the impulse back so the atria contract first Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The fastest pacemaker wins: the SA node fires before the others reach their own firing point. D describes the AV node. The SA node lies in the right atrium. 7. Understanding Check 1. For several minutes, a patient's left ventricle pumps less than the right ventricle. Where does blood build up first? A. In the lungs, raising the pressure in the pulmonary vessels B. In the leg veins, causing swelling around the ankles C. In the right ventricle, whose wall thickens to cope D. In the pulmonary veins, which then carry oxygen-poor blood Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The lungs sit between the right and left ventricles. If the left side sends on less than the right side delivers, blood accumulates in the lung vessels first. 2. Which statement about the heart valves is correct? A. Valves open when their muscle fibres contract B. Valves open and shut because of pressure differences C. Valves are opened by impulses from the AV node D. Valves shut as soon as the atria begin to relax Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Valves have no muscle and no nerve control. Pressure behind a valve opens it, and reversed pressure shuts it. 3. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood into the pulmonary trunk. Why is the pulmonary trunk still called an artery? A. It has a thick muscular wall like the aorta B. It carries blood at the highest pressure in the body C. It carries blood away from the heart D. It carries blood back to the left atrium Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry it towards the heart, whatever the oxygen content. 8. Chapter Essentials Diffusion only works over micrometres. The circulation brings blood within a few micrometres of almost every cell. The pulmonary and systemic circuits are in series. Both ventricles pump the same volume per minute (about 5 L/min at rest). A lasting mismatch makes blood pile up in one circuit. The systemic organs are in parallel. Each gets fresh arterial blood, and flow to each can be adjusted separately. The heart muscle has its own branch: the coronary arteries. Four chambers: the atria receive blood and top up the ventricles; the ventricles generate the pressure. Valves are passive. They open and shut with pressure differences, keeping flow one-way. Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry it towards the heart, whatever its oxygen content. The left ventricle is thicker because it needs more pressure (about 120 vs 25 mmHg), not because it pumps more volume. Low lung pressure keeps the lungs dry. Systole is contraction; diastole is relaxation. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation (SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje) comes first and sets the order of contraction."},{"slug":"cardiac-muscle","title":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"4 and 18-20; added foundations","coverage":"supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different","title":"1. What makes cardiac muscle different?"},{"id":"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together","title":"2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together"},{"id":"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential","title":"3. The cardiac muscle action potential"},{"id":"4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force","title":"4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force"},{"id":"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax","title":"5. How does cardiac muscle relax?"},{"id":"6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus","title":"6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?"},{"id":"7-how-do-the-sympathetic-and-vagal-nerves-change-cardiac-muscle","title":"7. How do the sympathetic and vagal nerves change cardiac muscle?"},{"id":"8-why-these-properties-matter-for-pumping","title":"8. Why these properties matter for pumping"},{"id":"9-understanding-check","title":"9. Understanding Check"},{"id":"10-chapter-essentials","title":"10. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Muscle Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different\">1. What makes cardiac muscle different?</h2>\n<p>Heart muscle has to do something no other muscle does: contract about once a second for a whole lifetime, without tiring, and relax completely between beats so the chambers can refill. Its special features follow from those demands.</p>\n<p>Like skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle is <strong>striated</strong>. Its actin and myosin filaments are arranged in repeating units called <strong><span data-term=\"sarcomere\">sarcomeres</span></strong>, which shorten when the muscle contracts. Unlike skeletal muscle, it is <strong>involuntary</strong>, and its cells are short and branched, usually with one central nucleus.</p>\n<p>Every heartbeat follows the same chain of events, and this chapter follows it link by link:</p>\n<p><strong>electrical signal → calcium enters the cell → the cell's calcium store releases much more → actin and myosin pull → calcium is removed → the muscle relaxes.</strong></p>\n<p>Four features make this chain work in the heart:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intercalated discs</strong> join the cells, so the signal spreads and the cells pull together (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">§2</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>A long action potential with a plateau</strong> lets calcium in and stops the muscle from locking in contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential\">§3</a>, <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">§6</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Calcium-induced calcium release</strong> links the electrical signal to contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">§4</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Many mitochondria</strong> supply a constant stream of ATP. Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism, so it needs oxygen all the time. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\">→ Coronary Circulation, §1</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A few specialised heart cells can fire on their own; this is <strong><span data-term=\"automaticity\">automaticity</span></strong>. Ordinary working muscle cells normally don't: they wait to be excited by their neighbours. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together</h2>\n<p>One heart muscle cell on its own cannot pump blood. Thousands must contract together, and each cell's pull must pass to the next. Both jobs happen at the <strong>intercalated discs</strong>, where neighbouring cells meet end to end.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Structure</th>\n  <th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fascia adherens</strong></td>\n  <td>Anchors the actin filaments of neighbouring cells, so the pull passes from one cell to the next</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Desmosomes</strong></td>\n  <td>Hold neighbouring cells firmly together, so they don't tear apart under repeated force</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Gap junctions</strong></td>\n  <td>Tiny channels that let ions (electrical current) flow straight from one cell into the next</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>When one cell is excited, current flows through its gap junctions into its neighbours and brings them to threshold. The signal spreads from cell to cell, and the whole chamber contracts almost as one. The other two junctions hold the cells together while they pull.</p>\n<p>So heart muscle behaves as a <strong><span data-term=\"functional-syncytium\">functional syncytium</span></strong>: <em>syn</em> (together) + <em>cyt</em> (cell), meaning separate cells working as one unit. The atria form one syncytium and the ventricles another. Between them lies a ring of fibrous tissue that does not conduct. The only normal electrical route across it is the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span>, which is why the AV node controls when the ventricles contract after the atria. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" alt=\"Branched cardiac muscle fibres and an enlarged intercalated disc showing desmosomes and gap junctions.\" width=\"1018\" height=\"476\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential\">3. The cardiac muscle action potential</h2>\n<p>An <strong>action potential</strong> is a brief electrical change across the cell membrane: the inside of the cell swings from negative to positive and back again. It is the signal that tells the cell to contract.</p>\n<p>In ventricular muscle, the cell rests at about <strong>−90 mV</strong>. Its action potential lasts about <strong>200–300 ms</strong>, roughly 100 times longer than a skeletal muscle action potential. That extra length comes from a long, flat <strong>plateau</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Phase</th>\n  <th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>0: Rapid upstroke</strong></td>\n  <td>Fast Na⁺ channels open. Na⁺ rushes in, and the inside of the cell becomes positive within about a millisecond.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>1: Small dip</strong></td>\n  <td>The Na⁺ channels close, and a brief outflow of K⁺ pulls the voltage down slightly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>2: Plateau</strong></td>\n  <td>L-type Ca²⁺ channels (L = long-lasting) let Ca²⁺ in, while K⁺ leaks out. The two roughly cancel, so the voltage stays nearly flat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>3: Repolarisation</strong></td>\n  <td>The Ca²⁺ channels close, the K⁺ outflow wins, and the inside returns to negative.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>4: Rest</strong></td>\n  <td>The cell stays steady at about −90 mV, held there by K⁺ channels that are open at rest.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does the plateau matter?</strong> It has two jobs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>It lets calcium in.</strong> The Ca²⁺ entering during the plateau is the trigger for contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">§4</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>It keeps the cell <span data-term=\"refractory-period\">refractory</span></strong>, meaning it cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over. This prevents tetanus (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">§6</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>You can see the action potential on the ECG.</strong> The ECG lecture links the flat <strong>ST segment</strong> to the plateau, when all the ventricular muscle is depolarised. The <strong>QT interval</strong> roughly matches how long a ventricular action potential lasts. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/ecg?section=4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">→ Electrocardiography, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" alt=\"Ventricular action potential and refractory period with a comparison of skeletal and cardiac muscle electrical and mechanical activity.\" width=\"1837\" height=\"2019\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> this is the action potential of a working ventricular muscle cell. Pacemaker cells in the <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> have no steady resting voltage, and their upstroke is driven mainly by Ca²⁺, not by fast Na⁺. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Neighbouring cardiac cells stay firmly attached but no longer contract together. Which junction has most likely failed?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Desmosomes, which hold neighbouring cells together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Gap junctions, which let current pass between cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Fascia adherens, which anchor the actin filaments</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. T-tubules, which carry the signal into each cell</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Gap junctions carry the electrical current from cell to cell. Without them the signal can't spread, so the cells stop contracting together, even though desmosomes and fascia adherens still hold them firmly attached.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What keeps the ventricular action potential flat during the plateau?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Fast Na⁺ channels staying open for about 200 ms</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The Na⁺–K⁺ pump stopping until contraction ends</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Ca²⁺ flowing in while K⁺ flows out at a similar rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Gap junctions holding the voltage of neighbouring cells</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Inward Ca²⁺ through L-type channels balances outward K⁺, so the voltage barely changes. The fast Na⁺ channels close within a few milliseconds of the upstroke.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> On the ECG, which part matches the plateau of the ventricular action potential?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ST segment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The P wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The QRS complex</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The T wave</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> During the plateau, all the ventricular muscle is depolarised at once, so no current flows between regions and the ECG trace stays flat. The QRS complex is the upstroke spreading through the ventricles, and the T wave is repolarisation.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"ecc\">Excitation–contraction coupling</span></strong> is the chain that turns the electrical signal into force. The link between the two is <strong>calcium (Ca²⁺)</strong>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The signal travels into the cell.</strong> The action potential spreads along the cell surface and down the <strong><span data-term=\"t-tubules\">T-tubules</span></strong>, tubes of surface membrane that dive deep into the cell.</li>\n<li><strong>A little Ca²⁺ enters.</strong> The change in voltage opens <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> in the T-tubules, and a small amount of Ca²⁺ flows in from outside the cell.</li>\n<li><strong>That Ca²⁺ releases much more.</strong> The incoming Ca²⁺ opens the release channels (<strong>RyR2</strong>, ryanodine receptor type 2) of the <strong><span data-term=\"sarcoplasmic-reticulum\">sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR)</span></strong>, the cell's internal calcium store. The SR pours out far more Ca²⁺. This is <strong><span data-term=\"cicr\">calcium-induced calcium release (CICR)</span></strong>: the entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger, and the SR supplies most of the Ca²⁺ that drives contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>Ca²⁺ unlocks the filaments.</strong> Ca²⁺ binds to <strong>troponin C</strong> on the actin filament. This shifts <strong>tropomyosin</strong>, uncovering the spots where myosin can grab actin.</li>\n<li><strong>Myosin pulls.</strong> Myosin heads attach to actin and pull, using energy from ATP. The sarcomeres shorten and the cell contracts.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"calcium-cycle\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>The calcium cycle, step by step</figcaption><p>One simple drawing of a heart muscle cell shows the cell membrane with a T-tubule, an L-type channel, the SR with its release channel (RyR2) and its pump (SERCA2a), the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger (NCX), and one actin–myosin unit. Next/Back steps through seven stages. At each stage the part involved lights up, with one plain sentence underneath: (1) the signal arrives down the T-tubule; (2) a little Ca²⁺ enters through the L-type channel; (3) the SR releases much more Ca²⁺; (4) Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, uncovering actin; (5) myosin pulls, and the cell contracts; (6) SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR; (7) NCX pushes Ca²⁺ out of the cell, and the cell relaxes. Ca²⁺ is shown as dots that move between compartments. The text explains everything without the widget.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-03\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" alt=\"Diagram showing cardiac T-tubule L-type calcium channels, ryanodine receptor, sodium–calcium exchanger, sarcoplasmic reticulum, SERCA and phospholamban.\" width=\"756\" height=\"567\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PeaBrainC</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why does heart muscle depend on calcium from outside the cell?</strong> In skeletal muscle, the voltage sensor in the T-tubule is mechanically linked to the SR's release channel, so the signal opens the store directly; no outside Ca²⁺ is needed. Heart muscle has no such direct link. Ca²⁺ must first enter from outside to open the store. <strong>Skeletal muscle uses a direct mechanical link; heart muscle uses CICR.</strong> So anything that reduces Ca²⁺ entry, such as calcium-channel-blocking drugs, weakens the heart's contraction, even if the SR is still full.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax\">5. How does cardiac muscle relax?</h2>\n<p>To relax, the cell must lower its Ca²⁺ again. Ca²⁺ leaves troponin C, tropomyosin covers the binding spots on actin again, and myosin lets go.</p>\n<p>Two main pumps remove the Ca²⁺:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"serca\">SERCA2a</span></strong> (the sarco-endoplasmic reticulum Ca²⁺ pump) is a pump in the SR membrane, powered by ATP. It pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, ready for the next beat.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"ncx\">NCX</span></strong> (the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger) sits in the cell membrane. It uses the Na⁺ gradient to swap Na⁺ in for Ca²⁺ out, removing roughly the Ca²⁺ that entered during the beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other pathways, including a Ca²⁺ pump in the cell membrane, play smaller parts. <strong>Relaxation therefore uses energy, just as contraction does.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What if SERCA2a works too slowly?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relaxation is delayed.</strong> Ca²⁺ stays high in the cell for longer, so the muscle stays partly contracted and the ventricle fills less well.</li>\n<li><strong>Later beats can be weaker.</strong> Less Ca²⁺ goes back into the SR, so less is available to release on the next beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Good pumping needs <strong>both a strong contraction and a fast, complete relaxation</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?</h2>\n<p>In skeletal muscle, the action potential is over in a few milliseconds, long before the twitch ends. A second stimulus can arrive while the muscle is still contracting, and rapid stimuli add up into one sustained contraction: <strong>tetanus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Heart muscle can't do this. As the lecture puts it, it is impossible to tetanise cardiac muscle because of the <strong>long refractory period</strong> of its action potential. The refractory period lasts almost as long as the contraction itself. By the time the cell can fire again, it has already begun to relax.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> a heart locked in sustained contraction could not refill, so it would pump nothing. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Figure 02 shows how the refractory period and the contraction line up.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Ca²⁺ still enters through L-type channels, but the SR's release channels (RyR2) fail to open. What happens to contraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It is much weaker, because the SR's large Ca²⁺ store is not released</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is normal, because the entering Ca²⁺ is enough on its own</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It is stronger, because more Ca²⁺ stays in the extracellular fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It becomes sustained, because Ca²⁺ can no longer be pumped away</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The entering Ca²⁺ is only the trigger. Most of the Ca²⁺ that activates the filaments comes from the SR, so without RyR2 release the rise in Ca²⁺ is far too small.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> SERCA2a pumps work more slowly than normal. What is the most likely immediate effect?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The action potential upstroke becomes slower</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Gap junctions close, so cells contract one at a time</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart can now be tetanised by rapid stimuli</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Relaxation is delayed because Ca²⁺ in the cell stays high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> SERCA2a is the main route for removing Ca²⁺ after a beat. If it slows, Ca²⁺ stays bound to troponin for longer and relaxation is delayed. Over later beats, less Ca²⁺ is stored for release.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can't a rapid series of stimuli make cardiac muscle contract continuously?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Cardiac muscle stores no calcium in its SR</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Gap junctions block stimuli that arrive too quickly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its long refractory period lasts most of the contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The vagus nerve relaxes the muscle between beats</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The cell cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over, so contractions cannot add up into tetanus. This guarantees relaxation and filling between beats.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-how-do-the-sympathetic-and-vagal-nerves-change-cardiac-muscle\">7. How do the sympathetic and vagal nerves change cardiac muscle?</h2>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation</strong> prepares the heart for exercise or stress: stronger beats, faster relaxation and a faster rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Noradrenaline</strong> (released by sympathetic nerve endings) and <strong>adrenaline</strong> (released by the adrenal medulla) both act on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> on heart cells:</p>\n<p><strong>β₁ receptor → G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP rises → PKA is switched on</strong></p>\n<p>Here <strong>cAMP</strong> (cyclic AMP) is a messenger molecule inside the cell. <strong>PKA</strong> (protein kinase A) is an enzyme that switches other proteins on or off by adding phosphate to them. PKA acts on the Ca²⁺ machinery in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stronger contraction.</strong> L-type channels let in more Ca²⁺, so the SR releases more, and more myosin heads pull on actin.</li>\n<li><strong>Faster relaxation.</strong> PKA disables <strong><span data-term=\"phospholamban\">phospholamban</span></strong>, a protein that normally acts as a brake on SERCA2a. With the brake off, Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the SR faster.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"xanthines\">Caffeine and theophylline (xanthines)</span></strong> work further along the same pathway. The enzyme <strong>phosphodiesterase</strong> normally breaks cAMP down. Xanthines block that enzyme, so cAMP builds up and contraction gets stronger.</p>\n<p><strong>Vagal stimulation</strong> works the other way. Acetylcholine released by the vagus nerve slows the SA node (a slower heart rate) and weakens <strong>atrial</strong> contraction. Its direct effect on the strength of the ventricles is small, because few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Three words for three effects:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Term</th>\n  <th>Word parts</th>\n  <th>What it describes</th>\n  <th>Positive effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">Inotropy</span></strong></td>\n  <td><em>ino</em> = fibre</td>\n  <td>Strength of contraction</td>\n  <td>Stronger contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"lusitropy\">Lusitropy</span></strong></td>\n  <td><em>lusi</em> = loosening</td>\n  <td>Speed of relaxation</td>\n  <td>Faster relaxation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span></strong></td>\n  <td><em>chrono</em> = time</td>\n  <td>Heart rate</td>\n  <td>Faster heart rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> positive inotropy means the muscle contracts more strongly <strong>at the same filling</strong>. A ventricle that contracts harder simply because it holds more blood is showing the Frank–Starling effect, not increased inotropy. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">→ Cardiac Output, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture slide says adrenaline comes from sympathetic nerve endings and noradrenaline from the adrenals. It is the other way round: sympathetic nerve endings release mainly noradrenaline, and the adrenal medulla releases mainly adrenaline. Both act on β₁ receptors. The lecture also lists a negative inotropic effect of vagal stimulation; this effect is mainly on the atria.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-why-these-properties-matter-for-pumping\">8. Why these properties matter for pumping</h2>\n<p>Each property of heart muscle supports one part of the pumping cycle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gap junctions</strong> make each chamber contract as one, giving a strong, coordinated squeeze.</li>\n<li><strong>The long refractory period</strong> guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats.</li>\n<li><strong>Calcium handling sets the strength of each beat.</strong> More Ca²⁺ means more myosin heads pulling, a stronger contraction and a bigger stroke volume. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=7-what-changes-contractility\">→ Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Fast relaxation matters most at high heart rates.</strong> Diastole shortens most as the rate rises, so the ventricles must relax quickly to have time to fill. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §10</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Contraction is not the same as ejection.</strong> The ventricles start contracting a moment before blood leaves: pressure must first rise above the pressure in the aorta or pulmonary artery. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §4</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Lecture connection: Circulation 1 (cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised; contractility through β₁ receptors and cAMP; xanthines; vagal effect) and the ECG lecture (ST segment and QT interval). The cellular detail (intercalated discs, phases of the action potential, CICR, SERCA2a and NCX) is standard physiology added as foundation.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-understanding-check\">9. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> How does β₁ stimulation make heart muscle both contract more strongly and relax faster?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The plateau is shortened, so less Ca²⁺ is needed for each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. More Ca²⁺ is released, and SERCA2a is freed to pump it back faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Gap junctions open wider, so the cells contract and relax together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Vagal fibres are switched off, which removes their braking effect</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> PKA increases Ca²⁺ entry and release, giving a stronger beat. It also disables phospholamban, the brake on SERCA2a, so Ca²⁺ is pumped back faster and the muscle relaxes sooner.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> According to the lecture, why can caffeine and theophylline make heart muscle contract more strongly?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They block the breakdown of cAMP, so cAMP builds up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They open fast Na⁺ channels, so the upstroke is larger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They block β₁ receptors, so noradrenaline acts longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They slow SERCA2a, so more Ca²⁺ stays in the cell</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Xanthines block phosphodiesterase, the enzyme that breaks down cAMP. More cAMP means more PKA activity and stronger contraction.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A ventricle contracts more strongly because it was filled with more blood. Is this a positive inotropic effect?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Yes, because any stronger contraction counts as inotropy</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Yes, because extra filling releases more Ca²⁺ from the SR</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. No, because inotropy only describes the heart rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. No, because inotropy means stronger at the same filling</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> A stronger beat caused by more filling is the Frank–Starling effect. Inotropy is a change in the muscle's own strength at the same filling.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Strong vagal stimulation mainly affects the heart by:<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Weakening ventricular contraction more than heart rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Blocking the gap junctions between ventricular cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Slowing the SA node and weakening atrial contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Shortening the refractory period of the ventricles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Acetylcholine slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle, so the direct effect on ventricular strength is small.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Why does fast relaxation matter more at high heart rates?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Diastole shortens most, so filling must happen faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Systole lengthens, so contraction must end sooner</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The refractory period disappears at high heart rates</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Ca²⁺ stops entering the cells when the rate is high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> As the rate rises, diastole is cut far more than systole. A ventricle that relaxes slowly has too little time left to fill.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-chapter-essentials\">10. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The chain of events:</strong> electrical signal → Ca²⁺ enters → the SR releases more Ca²⁺ → myosin pulls on actin → Ca²⁺ is removed → the muscle relaxes.</li>\n<li><strong>Intercalated discs:</strong> gap junctions pass current from cell to cell; desmosomes and fascia adherens hold the cells together and pass on their pull. The cells act as one unit (a functional syncytium).</li>\n<li><strong>The atria and ventricles are separate units.</strong> Fibrous tissue insulates them, and the AV node is the only normal electrical route between them.</li>\n<li><strong>The ventricular action potential:</strong> phase 0 is Na⁺ rushing in; phase 2, the plateau, is Ca²⁺ in balanced by K⁺ out; phase 3 is K⁺ out. On the ECG, the plateau is the ST segment.</li>\n<li><strong>CICR:</strong> a little Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2, and the SR releases much more. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, and myosin pulls on actin.</li>\n<li><strong>Relaxation needs energy:</strong> SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, and NCX pushes it out of the cell.</li>\n<li><strong>No tetanus:</strong> the long refractory period lasts most of the contraction, so the ventricles always relax and refill.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic (β₁ → cAMP → PKA):</strong> stronger contraction and faster relaxation (phospholamban released). Xanthines raise cAMP by blocking its breakdown. Vagal stimulation slows the SA node and weakens the atria.</li>\n<li><strong>Inotropy</strong> is strength at the same filling; <strong>lusitropy</strong> is speed of relaxation; <strong>chronotropy</strong> is heart rate.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology 1. What makes cardiac muscle different? Heart muscle has to do something no other muscle does: contract about once a second for a whole lifetime, without tiring, and relax completely between beats so the chambers can refill. Its special features follow from those demands. Like skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle is striated. Its actin and myosin filaments are arranged in repeating units called sarcomeres, which shorten when the muscle contracts. Unlike skeletal muscle, it is involuntary, and its cells are short and branched, usually with one central nucleus. Every heartbeat follows the same chain of events, and this chapter follows it link by link: electrical signal → calcium enters the cell → the cell's calcium store releases much more → actin and myosin pull → calcium is removed → the muscle relaxes. Four features make this chain work in the heart: Intercalated discs join the cells, so the signal spreads and the cells pull together ( §2). A long action potential with a plateau lets calcium in and stops the muscle from locking in contraction ( §3, §6). Calcium-induced calcium release links the electrical signal to contraction ( §4). Many mitochondria supply a constant stream of ATP. Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism, so it needs oxygen all the time. ( → Coronary Circulation, §1) A few specialised heart cells can fire on their own; this is automaticity. Ordinary working muscle cells normally don't: they wait to be excited by their neighbours. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) 2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together One heart muscle cell on its own cannot pump blood. Thousands must contract together, and each cell's pull must pass to the next. Both jobs happen at the intercalated discs, where neighbouring cells meet end to end. Structure What it does Fascia adherens Anchors the actin filaments of neighbouring cells, so the pull passes from one cell to the next Desmosomes Hold neighbouring cells firmly together, so they don't tear apart under repeated force Gap junctions Tiny channels that let ions (electrical current) flow straight from one cell into the next When one cell is excited, current flows through its gap junctions into its neighbours and brings them to threshold. The signal spreads from cell to cell, and the whole chamber contracts almost as one. The other two junctions hold the cells together while they pull. So heart muscle behaves as a functional syncytium: syn (together) + cyt (cell), meaning separate cells working as one unit. The atria form one syncytium and the ventricles another. Between them lies a ring of fibrous tissue that does not conduct. The only normal electrical route across it is the AV node, which is why the AV node controls when the ventricles contract after the atria. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §5) Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs. Image credit Image: OpenStax · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. 3. The cardiac muscle action potential An action potential is a brief electrical change across the cell membrane: the inside of the cell swings from negative to positive and back again. It is the signal that tells the cell to contract. In ventricular muscle, the cell rests at about −90 mV. Its action potential lasts about 200–300 ms, roughly 100 times longer than a skeletal muscle action potential. That extra length comes from a long, flat plateau. Phase What happens 0: Rapid upstroke Fast Na⁺ channels open. Na⁺ rushes in, and the inside of the cell becomes positive within about a millisecond. 1: Small dip The Na⁺ channels close, and a brief outflow of K⁺ pulls the voltage down slightly. 2: Plateau L-type Ca²⁺ channels (L = long-lasting) let Ca²⁺ in, while K⁺ leaks out. The two roughly cancel, so the voltage stays nearly flat. 3: Repolarisation The Ca²⁺ channels close, the K⁺ outflow wins, and the inside returns to negative. 4: Rest The cell stays steady at about −90 mV, held there by K⁺ channels that are open at rest. Why does the plateau matter? It has two jobs: It lets calcium in. The Ca²⁺ entering during the plateau is the trigger for contraction ( §4). It keeps the cell refractory, meaning it cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over. This prevents tetanus ( §6). You can see the action potential on the ECG. The ECG lecture links the flat ST segment to the plateau, when all the ventricular muscle is depolarised. The QT interval roughly matches how long a ventricular action potential lasts. ( → Electrocardiography, §4) Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction. Image credit Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Important distinction: this is the action potential of a working ventricular muscle cell. Pacemaker cells in the SA node have no steady resting voltage, and their upstroke is driven mainly by Ca²⁺, not by fast Na⁺. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Neighbouring cardiac cells stay firmly attached but no longer contract together. Which junction has most likely failed? A. Desmosomes, which hold neighbouring cells together B. Gap junctions, which let current pass between cells C. Fascia adherens, which anchor the actin filaments D. T-tubules, which carry the signal into each cell Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Gap junctions carry the electrical current from cell to cell. Without them the signal can't spread, so the cells stop contracting together, even though desmosomes and fascia adherens still hold them firmly attached. 2. What keeps the ventricular action potential flat during the plateau? A. Fast Na⁺ channels staying open for about 200 ms B. The Na⁺–K⁺ pump stopping until contraction ends C. Ca²⁺ flowing in while K⁺ flows out at a similar rate D. Gap junctions holding the voltage of neighbouring cells Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inward Ca²⁺ through L-type channels balances outward K⁺, so the voltage barely changes. The fast Na⁺ channels close within a few milliseconds of the upstroke. 3. On the ECG, which part matches the plateau of the ventricular action potential? A. The ST segment B. The P wave C. The QRS complex D. The T wave Show answer and explanation Answer: A. During the plateau, all the ventricular muscle is depolarised at once, so no current flows between regions and the ECG trace stays flat. The QRS complex is the upstroke spreading through the ventricles, and the T wave is repolarisation. 4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force Excitation–contraction coupling is the chain that turns the electrical signal into force. The link between the two is calcium (Ca²⁺). The signal travels into the cell. The action potential spreads along the cell surface and down the T-tubules, tubes of surface membrane that dive deep into the cell. A little Ca²⁺ enters. The change in voltage opens L-type Ca²⁺ channels in the T-tubules, and a small amount of Ca²⁺ flows in from outside the cell. That Ca²⁺ releases much more. The incoming Ca²⁺ opens the release channels ( RyR2, ryanodine receptor type 2) of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), the cell's internal calcium store. The SR pours out far more Ca²⁺. This is calcium-induced calcium release (CICR): the entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger, and the SR supplies most of the Ca²⁺ that drives contraction. Ca²⁺ unlocks the filaments. Ca²⁺ binds to troponin C on the actin filament. This shifts tropomyosin, uncovering the spots where myosin can grab actin. Myosin pulls. Myosin heads attach to actin and pull, using energy from ATP. The sarcomeres shorten and the cell contracts. The calcium cycle, step by step One simple drawing of a heart muscle cell shows the cell membrane with a T-tubule, an L-type channel, the SR with its release channel (RyR2) and its pump (SERCA2a), the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger (NCX), and one actin–myosin unit. Next/Back steps through seven stages. At each stage the part involved lights up, with one plain sentence underneath: (1) the signal arrives down the T-tubule; (2) a little Ca²⁺ enters through the L-type channel; (3) the SR releases much more Ca²⁺; (4) Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, uncovering actin; (5) myosin pulls, and the cell contracts; (6) SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR; (7) NCX pushes Ca²⁺ out of the cell, and the cell relaxes. Ca²⁺ is shown as dots that move between compartments. The text explains everything without the widget. Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling. Image credit Image: PeaBrainC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Why does heart muscle depend on calcium from outside the cell? In skeletal muscle, the voltage sensor in the T-tubule is mechanically linked to the SR's release channel, so the signal opens the store directly; no outside Ca²⁺ is needed. Heart muscle has no such direct link. Ca²⁺ must first enter from outside to open the store. Skeletal muscle uses a direct mechanical link; heart muscle uses CICR. So anything that reduces Ca²⁺ entry, such as calcium-channel-blocking drugs, weakens the heart's contraction, even if the SR is still full. 5. How does cardiac muscle relax? To relax, the cell must lower its Ca²⁺ again. Ca²⁺ leaves troponin C, tropomyosin covers the binding spots on actin again, and myosin lets go. Two main pumps remove the Ca²⁺: SERCA2a (the sarco-endoplasmic reticulum Ca²⁺ pump) is a pump in the SR membrane, powered by ATP. It pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, ready for the next beat. NCX (the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger) sits in the cell membrane. It uses the Na⁺ gradient to swap Na⁺ in for Ca²⁺ out, removing roughly the Ca²⁺ that entered during the beat. Other pathways, including a Ca²⁺ pump in the cell membrane, play smaller parts. Relaxation therefore uses energy, just as contraction does. What if SERCA2a works too slowly? Relaxation is delayed. Ca²⁺ stays high in the cell for longer, so the muscle stays partly contracted and the ventricle fills less well. Later beats can be weaker. Less Ca²⁺ goes back into the SR, so less is available to release on the next beat. Good pumping needs both a strong contraction and a fast, complete relaxation. 6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus? In skeletal muscle, the action potential is over in a few milliseconds, long before the twitch ends. A second stimulus can arrive while the muscle is still contracting, and rapid stimuli add up into one sustained contraction: tetanus. Heart muscle can't do this. As the lecture puts it, it is impossible to tetanise cardiac muscle because of the long refractory period of its action potential. The refractory period lasts almost as long as the contraction itself. By the time the cell can fire again, it has already begun to relax. Why this matters: a heart locked in sustained contraction could not refill, so it would pump nothing. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Figure 02 shows how the refractory period and the contraction line up. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Ca²⁺ still enters through L-type channels, but the SR's release channels (RyR2) fail to open. What happens to contraction? A. It is much weaker, because the SR's large Ca²⁺ store is not released B. It is normal, because the entering Ca²⁺ is enough on its own C. It is stronger, because more Ca²⁺ stays in the extracellular fluid D. It becomes sustained, because Ca²⁺ can no longer be pumped away Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The entering Ca²⁺ is only the trigger. Most of the Ca²⁺ that activates the filaments comes from the SR, so without RyR2 release the rise in Ca²⁺ is far too small. 2. SERCA2a pumps work more slowly than normal. What is the most likely immediate effect? A. The action potential upstroke becomes slower B. Gap junctions close, so cells contract one at a time C. The heart can now be tetanised by rapid stimuli D. Relaxation is delayed because Ca²⁺ in the cell stays high Show answer and explanation Answer: D. SERCA2a is the main route for removing Ca²⁺ after a beat. If it slows, Ca²⁺ stays bound to troponin for longer and relaxation is delayed. Over later beats, less Ca²⁺ is stored for release. 3. Why can't a rapid series of stimuli make cardiac muscle contract continuously? A. Cardiac muscle stores no calcium in its SR B. Gap junctions block stimuli that arrive too quickly C. Its long refractory period lasts most of the contraction D. The vagus nerve relaxes the muscle between beats Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The cell cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over, so contractions cannot add up into tetanus. This guarantees relaxation and filling between beats. 7. How do the sympathetic and vagal nerves change cardiac muscle? Sympathetic stimulation prepares the heart for exercise or stress: stronger beats, faster relaxation and a faster rate. Noradrenaline (released by sympathetic nerve endings) and adrenaline (released by the adrenal medulla) both act on β₁ receptors on heart cells: β₁ receptor → G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP rises → PKA is switched on Here cAMP (cyclic AMP) is a messenger molecule inside the cell. PKA (protein kinase A) is an enzyme that switches other proteins on or off by adding phosphate to them. PKA acts on the Ca²⁺ machinery in two ways: Stronger contraction. L-type channels let in more Ca²⁺, so the SR releases more, and more myosin heads pull on actin. Faster relaxation. PKA disables phospholamban, a protein that normally acts as a brake on SERCA2a. With the brake off, Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the SR faster. Caffeine and theophylline (xanthines) work further along the same pathway. The enzyme phosphodiesterase normally breaks cAMP down. Xanthines block that enzyme, so cAMP builds up and contraction gets stronger. Vagal stimulation works the other way. Acetylcholine released by the vagus nerve slows the SA node (a slower heart rate) and weakens atrial contraction. Its direct effect on the strength of the ventricles is small, because few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle. Three words for three effects: Term Word parts What it describes Positive effect Inotropy ino = fibre Strength of contraction Stronger contraction Lusitropy lusi = loosening Speed of relaxation Faster relaxation Chronotropy chrono = time Heart rate Faster heart rate Important distinction: positive inotropy means the muscle contracts more strongly at the same filling. A ventricle that contracts harder simply because it holds more blood is showing the Frank–Starling effect, not increased inotropy. ( → Cardiac Output, §4) Source note: The lecture slide says adrenaline comes from sympathetic nerve endings and noradrenaline from the adrenals. It is the other way round: sympathetic nerve endings release mainly noradrenaline, and the adrenal medulla releases mainly adrenaline. Both act on β₁ receptors. The lecture also lists a negative inotropic effect of vagal stimulation; this effect is mainly on the atria. 8. Why these properties matter for pumping Each property of heart muscle supports one part of the pumping cycle: Gap junctions make each chamber contract as one, giving a strong, coordinated squeeze. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Calcium handling sets the strength of each beat. More Ca²⁺ means more myosin heads pulling, a stronger contraction and a bigger stroke volume. ( → Cardiac Output, §7) Fast relaxation matters most at high heart rates. Diastole shortens most as the rate rises, so the ventricles must relax quickly to have time to fill. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §10) Contraction is not the same as ejection. The ventricles start contracting a moment before blood leaves: pressure must first rise above the pressure in the aorta or pulmonary artery. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §4) Source note: Lecture connection: Circulation 1 (cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised; contractility through β₁ receptors and cAMP; xanthines; vagal effect) and the ECG lecture (ST segment and QT interval). The cellular detail (intercalated discs, phases of the action potential, CICR, SERCA2a and NCX) is standard physiology added as foundation. 9. Understanding Check 1. How does β₁ stimulation make heart muscle both contract more strongly and relax faster? A. The plateau is shortened, so less Ca²⁺ is needed for each beat B. More Ca²⁺ is released, and SERCA2a is freed to pump it back faster C. Gap junctions open wider, so the cells contract and relax together D. Vagal fibres are switched off, which removes their braking effect Show answer and explanation Answer: B. PKA increases Ca²⁺ entry and release, giving a stronger beat. It also disables phospholamban, the brake on SERCA2a, so Ca²⁺ is pumped back faster and the muscle relaxes sooner. 2. According to the lecture, why can caffeine and theophylline make heart muscle contract more strongly? A. They block the breakdown of cAMP, so cAMP builds up B. They open fast Na⁺ channels, so the upstroke is larger C. They block β₁ receptors, so noradrenaline acts longer D. They slow SERCA2a, so more Ca²⁺ stays in the cell Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Xanthines block phosphodiesterase, the enzyme that breaks down cAMP. More cAMP means more PKA activity and stronger contraction. 3. A ventricle contracts more strongly because it was filled with more blood. Is this a positive inotropic effect? A. Yes, because any stronger contraction counts as inotropy B. Yes, because extra filling releases more Ca²⁺ from the SR C. No, because inotropy only describes the heart rate D. No, because inotropy means stronger at the same filling Show answer and explanation Answer: D. A stronger beat caused by more filling is the Frank–Starling effect. Inotropy is a change in the muscle's own strength at the same filling. 4. Strong vagal stimulation mainly affects the heart by: A. Weakening ventricular contraction more than heart rate B. Blocking the gap junctions between ventricular cells C. Slowing the SA node and weakening atrial contraction D. Shortening the refractory period of the ventricles Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Acetylcholine slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle, so the direct effect on ventricular strength is small. 5. Why does fast relaxation matter more at high heart rates? A. Diastole shortens most, so filling must happen faster B. Systole lengthens, so contraction must end sooner C. The refractory period disappears at high heart rates D. Ca²⁺ stops entering the cells when the rate is high Show answer and explanation Answer: A. As the rate rises, diastole is cut far more than systole. A ventricle that relaxes slowly has too little time left to fill. 10. Chapter Essentials The chain of events: electrical signal → Ca²⁺ enters → the SR releases more Ca²⁺ → myosin pulls on actin → Ca²⁺ is removed → the muscle relaxes. Intercalated discs: gap junctions pass current from cell to cell; desmosomes and fascia adherens hold the cells together and pass on their pull. The cells act as one unit (a functional syncytium). The atria and ventricles are separate units. Fibrous tissue insulates them, and the AV node is the only normal electrical route between them. The ventricular action potential: phase 0 is Na⁺ rushing in; phase 2, the plateau, is Ca²⁺ in balanced by K⁺ out; phase 3 is K⁺ out. On the ECG, the plateau is the ST segment. CICR: a little Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2, and the SR releases much more. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, and myosin pulls on actin. Relaxation needs energy: SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, and NCX pushes it out of the cell. No tetanus: the long refractory period lasts most of the contraction, so the ventricles always relax and refill. Sympathetic (β₁ → cAMP → PKA): stronger contraction and faster relaxation (phospholamban released). Xanthines raise cAMP by blocking its breakdown. Vagal stimulation slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Inotropy is strength at the same filling; lusitropy is speed of relaxation; chronotropy is heart rate."},{"slug":"cardiac-conduction","title":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"7-10","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system","title":"1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?"},{"id":"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction","title":"2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction"},{"id":"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker","title":"3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?"},{"id":"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm","title":"4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?"},{"id":"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction","title":"5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?"},{"id":"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid","title":"6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?"},{"id":"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity","title":"7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?"},{"id":"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances","title":"8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances"},{"id":"9-chapter-essentials","title":"9. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system\">1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?</h2>\n<p>Pumping works only if the chambers contract in the right order and at the right moment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>the atria before the ventricles</strong>, so the atria can top up the ventricles first;</li>\n<li><strong>all parts of each ventricle together</strong>, so pressure builds fast enough to eject blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That gives the heart two electrical jobs: <strong>start each beat</strong>, and <strong>deliver the signal in the right order</strong>. The specialised conducting system does both.</p>\n<p>Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a nerve impulse for every contraction. Specialised heart cells fire on their own; this is <strong><span data-term=\"automaticity\">automaticity</span></strong>. The nerves only speed this built-in rhythm up or slow it down (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\">§7</a>). As the lecture summarises, depolarisation spreads from the SA node to the AV node, then down the bundle of His to the rest of the heart, and contraction starts after depolarisation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\">2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction</h2>\n<p><strong>SA node → atrial muscle → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Structure</th>\n  <th>Where it is and what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node (sinoatrial node)</span></strong></td>\n  <td>In the wall of the right atrium, where the superior vena cava joins it. Starts each beat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Internodal pathways</strong></td>\n  <td>Carry the signal across the right atrium to the AV node. Bachmann's bundle also carries it to the left atrium.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node (atrioventricular node)</span></strong></td>\n  <td>Low in the right atrium, in the wall between the two atria, just behind the tricuspid valve. Holds the signal back briefly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Bundle of His</strong></td>\n  <td>Carries the signal from the AV node through the fibrous barrier into the wall between the ventricles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Right and left bundle branches</strong></td>\n  <td>Run down each side of the wall between the ventricles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong></td>\n  <td>Spread through the inner walls of both ventricles and hand the signal to the muscle.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Sectioned heart showing the SA node, atrial pathways including Bachmann's bundle, AV node, His bundle, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres.\" width=\"2021\" height=\"1354\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>What are the internodal pathways?</strong> The lecture names three: <strong>anterior</strong> (including Bachmann's bundle), <strong>middle</strong> (Wenckebach) and <strong>posterior</strong> (Thorel). They are routes the signal prefers, not insulated wires; the signal also spreads through ordinary atrial muscle. <strong>Bachmann's bundle</strong> matters most because it carries the signal across to the left atrium, so both atria contract almost together.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The three internodal tracts are named as in the lecture. Most anatomists now describe them as preferred routes through atrial muscle rather than separate insulated bundles.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?</h2>\n<p>Because it reaches threshold first.</p>\n<p>A working ventricular muscle cell sits at a steady −90 mV until a neighbour excites it. An SA node cell never sits still. After each beat, its voltage immediately starts <strong>creeping upwards by itself</strong> until it reaches threshold and fires. This slow climb is the <strong><span data-term=\"pacemaker-potential\">pacemaker potential</span></strong>, also called spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: <em>spontaneous</em> (by itself), <em>diastolic</em> (between beats), <em>depolarisation</em> (becoming less negative).</p>\n<p><strong>The three phases of the SA node action potential:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase 4: the slow climb.</strong> At its most negative, about −60 mV, the cell opens special channels that let positive ions, mainly Na⁺, leak in. This leak is the <strong><span data-term=\"funny-current\">funny current (I_f)</span></strong>, and its channels are called HCN channels. It is called &quot;funny&quot; because most channels open when a cell becomes <em>less</em> negative, but these open when it becomes <em>more</em> negative. Later in the climb, Ca²⁺ channels start to open too, and the outward K⁺ leak fades. The voltage rises steadily towards threshold, about −40 mV.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 0: the upstroke.</strong> At threshold, <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> open and Ca²⁺ rushes in. This upstroke is slower than a ventricular cell's, which is driven by fast Na⁺ channels. The SA node never gets negative enough to use its fast Na⁺ channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 3: repolarisation.</strong> The Ca²⁺ channels close, K⁺ flows out, and the voltage falls back to about −60 mV. The climb then starts again.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is no phase 1 or 2: an SA node cell has <strong>no plateau</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" alt=\"Side-by-side SA nodal pacemaker and ventricular cardiomyocyte action potentials: spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation and calcium upstroke in nodal cells contrasted with sodium upstroke and plateau in ventricular cells, above an ECG.\" width=\"1500\" height=\"1125\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells13010004\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jonker, Barnett, Boink &amp; Christoffels, <em>Cells</em> (2024), Figure 1</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Voltages are typical approximate values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture covers the SA node's location and its role as pacemaker; the ionic detail is foundation.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does an SA node cell fire again without any nerve signal?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The vagus nerve releases acetylcholine that triggers each new beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its voltage creeps up by itself after each beat until it reaches threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Neighbouring atrial muscle cells excite it at the end of each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Its resting voltage stays at −90 mV until stretch opens Na⁺ channels</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The SA node has no steady resting voltage. The funny current and later Ca²⁺ currents push the voltage up after every beat until it reaches threshold, so it fires again on its own.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is the funny current called \"funny\"?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It flows only when the heart rate is unusually high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is carried by Ca²⁺ instead of Na⁺ like other currents</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its channels open when the cell becomes more negative</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It flows outwards even though it makes the cell less negative</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Most channels open as a cell becomes less negative. HCN channels open as it becomes more negative, just after a beat, and let positive ions in, which starts the next climb.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why is the SA node's upstroke slower than a ventricular cell's?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It must wait for the AV node to release the signal first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its gap junctions slow the entry of Na⁺ from its neighbours</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It starts from −90 mV, so it has further to climb</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is driven by Ca²⁺ through L-type channels, not fast Na⁺</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The SA node never gets negative enough to use fast Na⁺ channels, so its upstroke relies on slower L-type Ca²⁺ channels.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm\">4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?</h2>\n<p>The lecture puts it simply: all parts of the conducting system can fire on their own, but they are controlled by the SA node, the pacemaker.</p>\n<p>The reason is speed. The SA node climbs to threshold fastest. Each beat it starts spreads through the conducting system and fires the slower cells <strong>before</strong> they reach threshold on their own. That resets them, so their own rhythm never gets a chance. <strong>The fastest pacemaker wins</strong>; the others still have automaticity, but they are always beaten to it.</p>\n<p>Being driven repeatedly at a faster rate also temporarily suppresses the slower cells' own firing. This is <strong><span data-term=\"overdrive-suppression\">overdrive suppression</span></strong>, and it is why there can be a short pause before a backup pacemaker takes over.</p>\n<p><strong>What if the SA node fails?</strong> The next fastest pacemaker takes over and produces an <strong><span data-term=\"escape-rhythm\">escape rhythm</span></strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Pacemaker</th>\n  <th>Typical rate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>SA node</td>\n  <td>60–100 beats/min (normal resting rhythm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>AV junction</td>\n  <td>about 40–60 beats/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricles (Purkinje fibres)</td>\n  <td>about 20–40 beats/min</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An escape rhythm keeps the ventricles beating, but more slowly, so it may not pump enough blood.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?</h2>\n<p>The AV node conducts <strong>slowly</strong>. Its cells are small, they have few gap junctions, and like the SA node their upstroke is driven by Ca²⁺. The signal takes about a tenth of a second to get through.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is that delay useful?</strong> It gives the atria time to <strong>finish contracting</strong> before the ventricles start. The atrial kick pushes the last portion of blood into the ventricles while the mitral and tricuspid valves are still open. Most filling, about 70% in the lecture, has already happened passively; the kick only tops it up.</p>\n<p><strong>You can see the delay on the ECG.</strong> The lecture describes the <strong><span data-term=\"pr-interval\">PR interval</span></strong>, normally 0.12–0.20 s, as the time from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crawling through the AV node. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/ecg?section=4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">→ Electrocardiography, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why can't the signal simply spread from the atria to the ventricles?</strong> A ring of fibrous tissue between them does not conduct. The AV node and bundle of His are the only normal route across. Because the AV node can only pass impulses so fast, it also protects the ventricles from being driven by a very fast atrial rhythm.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?</h2>\n<p>After the AV node, the signal enters the <strong><span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">His–Purkinje system</span></strong> and speeds up dramatically:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Tissue</th>\n  <th>Approximate conduction speed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>AV node</td>\n  <td>about 0.05 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ordinary ventricular muscle</td>\n  <td>about 0.3–0.5 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Purkinje fibres</td>\n  <td>about 2–4 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Purkinje fibres are fast because they are <strong>wide</strong>, packed with <strong>gap junctions</strong>, and use <strong>fast Na⁺ channels</strong> for their upstroke. They spread the signal over the inner walls of both ventricles in a few hundredths of a second, so the whole ventricular muscle is activated within about 0.1 s, the width of the QRS complex.</p>\n<p>The order is roughly: the wall between the ventricles first, then the inner walls near the apex, then outwards through the muscle to the outer surface, and the base last. The details belong in the ECG chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Slow in one place, fast in another.</strong> The two speeds do two different jobs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slow AV conduction gets the timing between chambers right:</strong> the atria finish before the ventricles start.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast His–Purkinje conduction gets the coordination within the ventricles right:</strong> all the muscle contracts together, so pressure builds quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"conduction-timeline\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Follow one heartbeat's signal</figcaption><p>What it shows: a simple heart outline with the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres labelled. Below it, an ECG strip. Controls: Play sends a glowing signal along the pathway, and the ECG strip draws in step with it: the atria light up with the P wave; the signal pauses at the AV node, labelled \"AV delay\", along the flat PR segment; it then races down the His bundle and the Purkinje fibres, and both ventricles light up together with the QRS complex. Step buttons let students stop at each stage. Schematic timing.</p></figure>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"ScZVGHDisa0\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ScZVGHDisa0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Conduction speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture gives the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s).</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why doesn't the AV junction set its own rhythm?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Its own firing is blocked by the fibrous skeleton</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Each SA impulse fires it before it reaches threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It only fires when the vagus nerve stimulates it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It fires faster, but its beats are too weak to count</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The SA node's impulse arrives and fires the AV junction before it can reach threshold by itself, resetting it every beat. The fastest pacemaker wins.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The SA node stops firing, and the ventricles keep beating at about 45 beats/min. What is most likely setting this rhythm?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The SA node, firing more slowly because of vagal tone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The Purkinje fibres in the ventricle walls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A backup pacemaker in the AV junction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Bachmann's bundle in the wall of the atria</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The AV junction's own rate is about 40–60 beats/min, so it is the next pacemaker in line. A ventricular escape rhythm is usually slower, about 20–40 beats/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> On the ECG, which interval mostly reflects the AV-node delay?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The QT interval</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The QRS duration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ST segment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The PR interval</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The PR interval runs from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crossing the AV node.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why must conduction be slow in the AV node but fast in the Purkinje fibres?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Slow AV conduction lets the atria finish first; fast Purkinje conduction makes the ventricles act together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Slow AV conduction saves energy; fast Purkinje conduction lets each ventricle contract twice in every beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Slow AV conduction prevents tetanus; fast Purkinje conduction shortens the ventricular refractory period</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Slow AV conduction lets the SA node recover; fast Purkinje conduction raises the overall heart rate</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The AV delay gets the timing between atria and ventricles right. The fast Purkinje network makes all the ventricular muscle contract together.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\">7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?</h2>\n<p>The key rule: <strong>the faster the phase 4 climb reaches threshold, the sooner the next beat.</strong> The nerves change heart rate by changing that climb.</p>\n<p><strong>At rest, the vagus is already braking the heart.</strong> An SA node with no nerve input fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest, the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine all the time (<strong><span data-term=\"vagal-tone\">vagal tone</span></strong>), holding the rate down to about 60–80. The lecture's evidence: heart rate is controlled mainly by this constant vagal discharge, so cutting the vagus (vagotomy) or blocking it with <strong>atropine</strong> makes the heart rate rise.</p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation speeds the climb.</strong> Noradrenaline acts on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> and raises cAMP inside the SA node cells. More funny current and more Ca²⁺ current flow, the phase 4 climb gets <strong>steeper</strong>, threshold comes sooner, and the heart rate rises. Conduction through the AV node also speeds up, so the PR interval shortens.</p>\n<p><strong>Vagal stimulation slows the climb.</strong> Acetylcholine acts on <strong>M₂ receptors</strong>. It lowers cAMP, so there is less funny current. It also opens extra K⁺ channels, so the cell starts each climb <strong>lower</strong> (more negative) and climbs <strong>more slowly</strong>. Threshold comes later, and the heart rate falls. AV conduction slows, so the PR interval lengthens.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"pacemaker-slope\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>How nerves set the heart rate</figcaption><p>What it shows: the SA node voltage over 3 seconds, with a dashed threshold line. Controls: four buttons: Strong vagal (starts lower, shallow climb, about 41 beats/min); Rest (vagal tone, about 75 beats/min); No nerve input (about 100 beats/min); Sympathetic (steep climb, about 150 beats/min). The trace redraws for each, the beats-per-minute readout updates, and a one-line explanation names what changed (the slope, the starting voltage, or both). Schematic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Two words for two effects:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span></strong> (<em>chrono</em> = time): an effect on <strong>heart rate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"dromotropy\">Dromotropy</span></strong> (<em>dromo</em> = running): an effect on <strong>conduction speed</strong>, especially through the AV node.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation is positively chronotropic and dromotropic; vagal stimulation is negatively chronotropic and dromotropic.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The intrinsic SA node rate of about 100 beats/min is a standard textbook value. The lecture (Circulation 2) states that heart rate is controlled mainly by tonic vagal discharge, and that vagotomy and atropine raise it.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances</h2>\n<p>Electrical problems come in two kinds: the pacemaker fails, or the signal is delayed or blocked on its way.</p>\n<p><strong>The SA node fails.</strong> It fires too slowly or pauses. A backup pacemaker may take over with a slower escape rhythm (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm\">§4</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>The signal is blocked at the AV node (<span data-term=\"heart-block\">heart block</span>).</strong> Think of it as three levels of trouble:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First degree: every impulse gets through, but late.</strong> The PR interval is longer than 0.20 s.</li>\n<li><strong>Second degree: some impulses don't get through.</strong> Some atrial beats are not followed by a ventricular beat; those beats are &quot;dropped&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Third degree (complete): no impulses get through.</strong> The atria keep beating at the SA node's rate, and the ventricles beat independently at a slow escape rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In complete block, the atria sometimes contract while the tricuspid valve is shut. Blood is pushed back into the neck veins, giving the <strong>giant &quot;a&quot; waves</strong> the lecture describes in the JVP. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses\">→ Pulses and JVP</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Both kinds of problem reduce pumping: the heart rate may be too slow, and the atria and ventricles no longer work in step.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> According to the lecture, atropine (which blocks vagal acetylcholine) raises the resting heart rate. What does this show?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Atropine directly speeds up the funny current in the SA node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Sympathetic nerves cannot act on the heart at rest</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. At rest, the vagus nerve continuously slows the SA node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The SA node needs acetylcholine before it can fire</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Removing the vagal brake lets the SA node rise towards its own rate of about 100 beats/min. So at rest, constant vagal tone is holding the rate down.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does noradrenaline make the SA node fire faster?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It makes the cell rest at −90 mV, closer to the Na⁺ threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It steepens the slow climb of phase 4, so threshold comes sooner</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It adds a plateau, which shortens the time between beats</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It slows the AV node, so the SA node has less to wait for</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases the funny current and Ca²⁺ current. The climb gets steeper, so each beat starts sooner.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Vagal acetylcholine opens extra K⁺ channels in the SA node. Why does this slow the heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. K⁺ entering the cell makes phase 0 much larger and slower</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. K⁺ blocks the gap junctions between the SA node and the atria</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The plateau lengthens, so the refractory period lasts longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The cell starts lower and climbs more slowly, so threshold comes later</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> K⁺ leaving the cell pulls the voltage down, so each climb starts more negative. With less funny current the climb is also shallower, so it takes longer to reach threshold.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> In complete heart block, the atria beat at 75 per minute and the ventricles at 35 per minute. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles follow their own slower escape pacemaker</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The SA node fires twice for every ventricular beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The AV node lets only every other impulse through</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The vagus nerve slows the ventricles but not the atria</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> In complete block no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. A ventricular escape pacemaker takes over at its own slow rate, independent of the atria. Letting every other impulse through would be second-degree block.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\">9. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pathway:</strong> SA node → atria → AV node → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Automaticity:</strong> specialised cells fire on their own; the nerves only change the rate.</li>\n<li><strong>The SA node</strong> sits where the superior vena cava joins the right atrium. It is the pacemaker because it reaches threshold first.</li>\n<li><strong>The SA node action potential:</strong> phase 4 is a slow climb, mainly the funny current (Na⁺ in, through channels that open when the cell is most negative); phase 0 is Ca²⁺ in; phase 3 is K⁺ out. There is no plateau.</li>\n<li><strong>The fastest pacemaker wins.</strong> If the SA node fails, the AV junction (about 40–60/min) or the ventricles (about 20–40/min) take over with an escape rhythm.</li>\n<li><strong>The AV node</strong> sits just behind the tricuspid valve. It conducts slowly (about 0.05 m/s), so the atria finish contracting first. On the ECG this is the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s).</li>\n<li><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong> conduct fast (2–4 m/s), so both ventricles contract together, within about 0.1 s (the QRS).</li>\n<li><strong>Vagal tone:</strong> the SA node's own rate is about 100/min. At rest the vagus holds it at 60–80, which is why atropine or vagotomy raises the heart rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Nerves change the phase 4 climb.</strong> Sympathetic (β₁, cAMP) makes it steeper and the rate faster. Vagal (M₂, more K⁺, less funny current) starts it lower and makes it shallower, so the rate is slower.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart block:</strong> first degree, late; second degree, some dropped; third degree, none get through, with the atria and ventricles beating independently.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity 1. Why does the heart need a conducting system? Pumping works only if the chambers contract in the right order and at the right moment: the atria before the ventricles, so the atria can top up the ventricles first; all parts of each ventricle together, so pressure builds fast enough to eject blood. That gives the heart two electrical jobs: start each beat, and deliver the signal in the right order. The specialised conducting system does both. Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a nerve impulse for every contraction. Specialised heart cells fire on their own; this is automaticity. The nerves only speed this built-in rhythm up or slow it down ( §7). As the lecture summarises, depolarisation spreads from the SA node to the AV node, then down the bundle of His to the rest of the heart, and contraction starts after depolarisation. 2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction SA node → atrial muscle → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle Structure Where it is and what it does SA node (sinoatrial node) In the wall of the right atrium, where the superior vena cava joins it. Starts each beat. Internodal pathways Carry the signal across the right atrium to the AV node. Bachmann's bundle also carries it to the left atrium. AV node (atrioventricular node) Low in the right atrium, in the wall between the two atria, just behind the tricuspid valve. Holds the signal back briefly. Bundle of His Carries the signal from the AV node through the fibrous barrier into the wall between the ventricles. Right and left bundle branches Run down each side of the wall between the ventricles. Purkinje fibres Spread through the inner walls of both ventricles and hand the signal to the muscle. Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system. Image credit Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. What are the internodal pathways? The lecture names three: anterior (including Bachmann's bundle), middle (Wenckebach) and posterior (Thorel). They are routes the signal prefers, not insulated wires; the signal also spreads through ordinary atrial muscle. Bachmann's bundle matters most because it carries the signal across to the left atrium, so both atria contract almost together. Source note: The three internodal tracts are named as in the lecture. Most anatomists now describe them as preferred routes through atrial muscle rather than separate insulated bundles. 3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker? Because it reaches threshold first. A working ventricular muscle cell sits at a steady −90 mV until a neighbour excites it. An SA node cell never sits still. After each beat, its voltage immediately starts creeping upwards by itself until it reaches threshold and fires. This slow climb is the pacemaker potential, also called spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: spontaneous (by itself), diastolic (between beats), depolarisation (becoming less negative). The three phases of the SA node action potential: Phase 4: the slow climb. At its most negative, about −60 mV, the cell opens special channels that let positive ions, mainly Na⁺, leak in. This leak is the funny current (I_f), and its channels are called HCN channels. It is called \"funny\" because most channels open when a cell becomes less negative, but these open when it becomes more negative. Later in the climb, Ca²⁺ channels start to open too, and the outward K⁺ leak fades. The voltage rises steadily towards threshold, about −40 mV. Phase 0: the upstroke. At threshold, L-type Ca²⁺ channels open and Ca²⁺ rushes in. This upstroke is slower than a ventricular cell's, which is driven by fast Na⁺ channels. The SA node never gets negative enough to use its fast Na⁺ channels. Phase 3: repolarisation. The Ca²⁺ channels close, K⁺ flows out, and the voltage falls back to about −60 mV. The climb then starts again. There is no phase 1 or 2: an SA node cell has no plateau. Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials. Image credit Image: Jonker, Barnett, Boink & Christoffels, Cells (2024), Figure 1 · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. Source note: Voltages are typical approximate values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture covers the SA node's location and its role as pacemaker; the ionic detail is foundation. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why does an SA node cell fire again without any nerve signal? A. The vagus nerve releases acetylcholine that triggers each new beat B. Its voltage creeps up by itself after each beat until it reaches threshold C. Neighbouring atrial muscle cells excite it at the end of each beat D. Its resting voltage stays at −90 mV until stretch opens Na⁺ channels Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The SA node has no steady resting voltage. The funny current and later Ca²⁺ currents push the voltage up after every beat until it reaches threshold, so it fires again on its own. 2. Why is the funny current called \"funny\"? A. It flows only when the heart rate is unusually high B. It is carried by Ca²⁺ instead of Na⁺ like other currents C. Its channels open when the cell becomes more negative D. It flows outwards even though it makes the cell less negative Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Most channels open as a cell becomes less negative. HCN channels open as it becomes more negative, just after a beat, and let positive ions in, which starts the next climb. 3. Why is the SA node's upstroke slower than a ventricular cell's? A. It must wait for the AV node to release the signal first B. Its gap junctions slow the entry of Na⁺ from its neighbours C. It starts from −90 mV, so it has further to climb D. It is driven by Ca²⁺ through L-type channels, not fast Na⁺ Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The SA node never gets negative enough to use fast Na⁺ channels, so its upstroke relies on slower L-type Ca²⁺ channels. 4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm? The lecture puts it simply: all parts of the conducting system can fire on their own, but they are controlled by the SA node, the pacemaker. The reason is speed. The SA node climbs to threshold fastest. Each beat it starts spreads through the conducting system and fires the slower cells before they reach threshold on their own. That resets them, so their own rhythm never gets a chance. The fastest pacemaker wins; the others still have automaticity, but they are always beaten to it. Being driven repeatedly at a faster rate also temporarily suppresses the slower cells' own firing. This is overdrive suppression, and it is why there can be a short pause before a backup pacemaker takes over. What if the SA node fails? The next fastest pacemaker takes over and produces an escape rhythm: Pacemaker Typical rate SA node 60–100 beats/min (normal resting rhythm) AV junction about 40–60 beats/min Ventricles (Purkinje fibres) about 20–40 beats/min An escape rhythm keeps the ventricles beating, but more slowly, so it may not pump enough blood. 5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction? The AV node conducts slowly. Its cells are small, they have few gap junctions, and like the SA node their upstroke is driven by Ca²⁺. The signal takes about a tenth of a second to get through. Why is that delay useful? It gives the atria time to finish contracting before the ventricles start. The atrial kick pushes the last portion of blood into the ventricles while the mitral and tricuspid valves are still open. Most filling, about 70% in the lecture, has already happened passively; the kick only tops it up. You can see the delay on the ECG. The lecture describes the PR interval, normally 0.12–0.20 s, as the time from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crawling through the AV node. ( → Electrocardiography, §4) Why can't the signal simply spread from the atria to the ventricles? A ring of fibrous tissue between them does not conduct. The AV node and bundle of His are the only normal route across. Because the AV node can only pass impulses so fast, it also protects the ventricles from being driven by a very fast atrial rhythm. 6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid? After the AV node, the signal enters the His–Purkinje system and speeds up dramatically: Tissue Approximate conduction speed AV node about 0.05 m/s Ordinary ventricular muscle about 0.3–0.5 m/s Purkinje fibres about 2–4 m/s Purkinje fibres are fast because they are wide, packed with gap junctions, and use fast Na⁺ channels for their upstroke. They spread the signal over the inner walls of both ventricles in a few hundredths of a second, so the whole ventricular muscle is activated within about 0.1 s, the width of the QRS complex. The order is roughly: the wall between the ventricles first, then the inner walls near the apex, then outwards through the muscle to the outer surface, and the base last. The details belong in the ECG chapter. Slow in one place, fast in another. The two speeds do two different jobs: Slow AV conduction gets the timing between chambers right: the atria finish before the ventricles start. Fast His–Purkinje conduction gets the coordination within the ventricles right: all the muscle contracts together, so pressure builds quickly. Follow one heartbeat's signal What it shows: a simple heart outline with the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres labelled. Below it, an ECG strip. Controls: Play sends a glowing signal along the pathway, and the ECG strip draws in step with it: the atria light up with the P wave; the signal pauses at the AV node, labelled \"AV delay\", along the flat PR segment; it then races down the His bundle and the Purkinje fibres, and both ventricles light up together with the QRS complex. Step buttons let students stop at each stage. Schematic timing. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. Source note: Conduction speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture gives the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s). Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why doesn't the AV junction set its own rhythm? A. Its own firing is blocked by the fibrous skeleton B. Each SA impulse fires it before it reaches threshold C. It only fires when the vagus nerve stimulates it D. It fires faster, but its beats are too weak to count Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The SA node's impulse arrives and fires the AV junction before it can reach threshold by itself, resetting it every beat. The fastest pacemaker wins. 2. The SA node stops firing, and the ventricles keep beating at about 45 beats/min. What is most likely setting this rhythm? A. The SA node, firing more slowly because of vagal tone B. The Purkinje fibres in the ventricle walls C. A backup pacemaker in the AV junction D. Bachmann's bundle in the wall of the atria Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The AV junction's own rate is about 40–60 beats/min, so it is the next pacemaker in line. A ventricular escape rhythm is usually slower, about 20–40 beats/min. 3. On the ECG, which interval mostly reflects the AV-node delay? A. The QT interval B. The QRS duration C. The ST segment D. The PR interval Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The PR interval runs from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crossing the AV node. 4. Why must conduction be slow in the AV node but fast in the Purkinje fibres? A. Slow AV conduction lets the atria finish first; fast Purkinje conduction makes the ventricles act together B. Slow AV conduction saves energy; fast Purkinje conduction lets each ventricle contract twice in every beat C. Slow AV conduction prevents tetanus; fast Purkinje conduction shortens the ventricular refractory period D. Slow AV conduction lets the SA node recover; fast Purkinje conduction raises the overall heart rate Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The AV delay gets the timing between atria and ventricles right. The fast Purkinje network makes all the ventricular muscle contract together. 7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity? The key rule: the faster the phase 4 climb reaches threshold, the sooner the next beat. The nerves change heart rate by changing that climb. At rest, the vagus is already braking the heart. An SA node with no nerve input fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest, the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine all the time ( vagal tone), holding the rate down to about 60–80. The lecture's evidence: heart rate is controlled mainly by this constant vagal discharge, so cutting the vagus (vagotomy) or blocking it with atropine makes the heart rate rise. Sympathetic stimulation speeds the climb. Noradrenaline acts on β₁ receptors and raises cAMP inside the SA node cells. More funny current and more Ca²⁺ current flow, the phase 4 climb gets steeper, threshold comes sooner, and the heart rate rises. Conduction through the AV node also speeds up, so the PR interval shortens. Vagal stimulation slows the climb. Acetylcholine acts on M₂ receptors. It lowers cAMP, so there is less funny current. It also opens extra K⁺ channels, so the cell starts each climb lower (more negative) and climbs more slowly. Threshold comes later, and the heart rate falls. AV conduction slows, so the PR interval lengthens. How nerves set the heart rate What it shows: the SA node voltage over 3 seconds, with a dashed threshold line. Controls: four buttons: Strong vagal (starts lower, shallow climb, about 41 beats/min); Rest (vagal tone, about 75 beats/min); No nerve input (about 100 beats/min); Sympathetic (steep climb, about 150 beats/min). The trace redraws for each, the beats-per-minute readout updates, and a one-line explanation names what changed (the slope, the starting voltage, or both). Schematic. Two words for two effects: Chronotropy ( chrono = time): an effect on heart rate. Dromotropy ( dromo = running): an effect on conduction speed, especially through the AV node. Sympathetic stimulation is positively chronotropic and dromotropic; vagal stimulation is negatively chronotropic and dromotropic. Source note: The intrinsic SA node rate of about 100 beats/min is a standard textbook value. The lecture (Circulation 2) states that heart rate is controlled mainly by tonic vagal discharge, and that vagotomy and atropine raise it. 8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances Electrical problems come in two kinds: the pacemaker fails, or the signal is delayed or blocked on its way. The SA node fails. It fires too slowly or pauses. A backup pacemaker may take over with a slower escape rhythm ( §4). The signal is blocked at the AV node ( heart block). Think of it as three levels of trouble: First degree: every impulse gets through, but late. The PR interval is longer than 0.20 s. Second degree: some impulses don't get through. Some atrial beats are not followed by a ventricular beat; those beats are \"dropped\". Third degree (complete): no impulses get through. The atria keep beating at the SA node's rate, and the ventricles beat independently at a slow escape rate. In complete block, the atria sometimes contract while the tricuspid valve is shut. Blood is pushed back into the neck veins, giving the giant \"a\" waves the lecture describes in the JVP. ( → Pulses and JVP) Both kinds of problem reduce pumping: the heart rate may be too slow, and the atria and ventricles no longer work in step. Understanding Check 1. According to the lecture, atropine (which blocks vagal acetylcholine) raises the resting heart rate. What does this show? A. Atropine directly speeds up the funny current in the SA node B. Sympathetic nerves cannot act on the heart at rest C. At rest, the vagus nerve continuously slows the SA node D. The SA node needs acetylcholine before it can fire Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Removing the vagal brake lets the SA node rise towards its own rate of about 100 beats/min. So at rest, constant vagal tone is holding the rate down. 2. How does noradrenaline make the SA node fire faster? A. It makes the cell rest at −90 mV, closer to the Na⁺ threshold B. It steepens the slow climb of phase 4, so threshold comes sooner C. It adds a plateau, which shortens the time between beats D. It slows the AV node, so the SA node has less to wait for Show answer and explanation Answer: B. β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases the funny current and Ca²⁺ current. The climb gets steeper, so each beat starts sooner. 3. Vagal acetylcholine opens extra K⁺ channels in the SA node. Why does this slow the heart? A. K⁺ entering the cell makes phase 0 much larger and slower B. K⁺ blocks the gap junctions between the SA node and the atria C. The plateau lengthens, so the refractory period lasts longer D. The cell starts lower and climbs more slowly, so threshold comes later Show answer and explanation Answer: D. K⁺ leaving the cell pulls the voltage down, so each climb starts more negative. With less funny current the climb is also shallower, so it takes longer to reach threshold. 4. In complete heart block, the atria beat at 75 per minute and the ventricles at 35 per minute. Why? A. The ventricles follow their own slower escape pacemaker B. The SA node fires twice for every ventricular beat C. The AV node lets only every other impulse through D. The vagus nerve slows the ventricles but not the atria Show answer and explanation Answer: A. In complete block no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. A ventricular escape pacemaker takes over at its own slow rate, independent of the atria. Letting every other impulse through would be second-degree block. 9. Chapter Essentials Pathway: SA node → atria → AV node → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle. Automaticity: specialised cells fire on their own; the nerves only change the rate. The SA node sits where the superior vena cava joins the right atrium. It is the pacemaker because it reaches threshold first. The SA node action potential: phase 4 is a slow climb, mainly the funny current (Na⁺ in, through channels that open when the cell is most negative); phase 0 is Ca²⁺ in; phase 3 is K⁺ out. There is no plateau. The fastest pacemaker wins. If the SA node fails, the AV junction (about 40–60/min) or the ventricles (about 20–40/min) take over with an escape rhythm. The AV node sits just behind the tricuspid valve. It conducts slowly (about 0.05 m/s), so the atria finish contracting first. On the ECG this is the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s). Purkinje fibres conduct fast (2–4 m/s), so both ventricles contract together, within about 0.1 s (the QRS). Vagal tone: the SA node's own rate is about 100/min. At rest the vagus holds it at 60–80, which is why atropine or vagotomy raises the heart rate. Nerves change the phase 4 climb. Sympathetic (β₁, cAMP) makes it steeper and the rate faster. Vagal (M₂, more K⁺, less funny current) starts it lower and makes it shallower, so the rate is slower. Heart block: first degree, late; second degree, some dropped; third degree, none get through, with the atria and ventricles beating independently."},{"slug":"cardiac-cycle","title":"The Cardiac Cycle","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"11-21","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing","title":"1. One heartbeat: what the atria and ventricles are doing"},{"id":"2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows","title":"2. The one rule every valve follows"},{"id":"3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle","title":"3. Filling: how blood enters the relaxed ventricle"},{"id":"4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves","title":"4. Isovolumetric contraction: contracting, but no blood leaves"},{"id":"5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves","title":"5. Ejection: why blood finally leaves"},{"id":"6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation","title":"6. Closing the outflow valves and isovolumetric relaxation"},{"id":"7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump","title":"7. How much blood does the ventricle pump?"},{"id":"8-the-whole-cycle-on-one-map","title":"8. The whole cycle on one map"},{"id":"9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time","title":"9. Reading the Wiggers diagram one trace at a time"},{"id":"10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate","title":"10. Right versus left heart, S2 splitting and heart rate"},{"id":"11-understanding-check","title":"11. Understanding Check"},{"id":"12-chapter-essentials","title":"12. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>The Cardiac Cycle</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">1. One heartbeat: what the atria and ventricles are doing</h2>\n<p>The <strong>cardiac cycle</strong> is everything that happens in the heart during one heartbeat: the atria and ventricles each contract and relax once, in a fixed order. <strong>Systole</strong> means contraction and <strong>diastole</strong> means relaxation. On their own, the words are incomplete. Always ask <em>systole of which chambers?</em> The atria and ventricles are often doing different things, though for a large part of each beat they are both relaxed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why do the atria go first?</strong> Each beat starts with an electrical impulse from the <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> in the right atrium. The impulse spreads across the atria first, then pauses briefly at the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span> before reaching the ventricles. So the atria are activated, and contract, just before the ventricles. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Follow one beat:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole.</strong> At the end of ventricular diastole, the atria contract and push a final portion of blood into ventricles that are already nearly full.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole.</strong> The ventricles contract and eject blood. Meanwhile the atria relax and begin refilling with blood returning from the veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Both chambers in diastole.</strong> The ventricles now relax too, so both are relaxed together. For a brief moment the ventricles still cannot fill, because their pressure is higher than atrial pressure. As they relax further, their pressure falls below atrial pressure and blood flows in passively.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole again</strong>, and the next beat begins.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How long does each part take?</strong> At about 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 60 ÷ 75 = <strong>0.8 s</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Part of the cycle</th>\n  <th>Duration</th>\n  <th>Where it comes from</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular systole</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.3 s</td>\n  <td>Typical resting value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular diastole</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.5 s</td>\n  <td>0.8 − 0.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Atrial systole</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.1 s</td>\n  <td>Last part of ventricular diastole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Both chambers relaxed</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.4 s</td>\n  <td>0.5 − 0.1, half of every beat</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are rounded teaching values; the lecture gives no phase durations.</p>\n<p>That shared 0.4 s matters. After a short pause while ventricular pressure is still falling, it is when most ventricular filling happens. This is why atrial contraction is only a top-up. It is also why very fast heart rates, which shorten this period most, can limit filling (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Section 10</a>).</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"chamber-timeline\"><figcaption>One heartbeat at 75 beats/min: what the atria and ventricles are doing</figcaption><p>Two aligned bars across one 0.8 s cycle. Atria: systole for the first 0.1 s, then diastole. Ventricles: systole from 0.1 s to 0.4 s, then diastole. From 0.4 s to 0.8 s both are relaxed: first a short pause while the ventricle is still relaxing with no filling, then passive filling. Proportions approximate, at 75 beats/min.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>What drives each step?</strong> Every phase follows one chain:</p>\n<p><strong>Electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes</strong></p>\n<p>The electrical signal starts the chain but never moves blood or valves directly. Each link takes a moment, so the steps happen in sequence, not all at once. <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Section 2</a> explains the key link: how pressure moves the valves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">2. The one rule every valve follows</h2>\n<p>The heart has four valves, in two pairs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AV (atrioventricular) valves</strong> sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the <strong>mitral</strong> valve on the left and the <strong>tricuspid</strong> valve on the right.</li>\n<li><strong>Semilunar valves</strong> sit between each ventricle and its artery: the <strong>aortic</strong> valve on the left and the <strong>pulmonary</strong> valve on the right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All four obey one rule. A valve <strong>opens</strong> when pressure upstream (in the chamber blood is coming from) is higher than pressure downstream (where blood is going). It <strong>closes</strong> when that difference reverses.</p>\n<p><strong>How can pressure alone do this?</strong> Each valve is made of thin flaps called cusps, shaped to open in one direction only. Higher pressure upstream pushes the cusps apart, and blood flows through. When downstream pressure becomes higher, blood starts to move backwards and pushes the cusps together until they seal. No nerve or electrical signal is involved. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\">→ Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §3</a>: valves keep flow one-way)</em></p>\n<p><strong>What about the papillary muscles?</strong> The AV valve cusps are tethered by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles in the ventricular wall. These muscles contract with the ventricle, but they do not open or close the valve. They hold the cusps in place so that rising ventricular pressure cannot push them inside out into the atrium.</p>\n<p>Now apply the rule to the left heart: <strong>left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"pressure-rules\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Left-heart pressure rules\"><div><strong>Mitral valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LA</sub> &gt; P<sub>LV</sub></span><small>Blood enters the ventricle</small></div><div><strong>Aortic valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LV</sub> &gt; P<sub>aorta</sub></span><small>Blood leaves the ventricle</small></div></div>\n\n<p>Here P means pressure, LA is left atrium and LV is left ventricle. The two valves respond to <strong>different</strong> pressure differences, so there are moments when <strong>both are closed at once</strong>. Those moments explain two phases you will meet in Sections <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">4</a> and <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">6</a>, when the ventricle's pressure changes but its volume does not.</p>\n<p>One refinement comes later: moving blood carries momentum, so flow and valve closure can lag slightly behind a change in pressure (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">Section 6</a>).</p>\n<p>The right heart follows the same rule at lower pressures, through the tricuspid and pulmonary valves. We follow the left heart first and return to the right in <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Section 10</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">3. Filling: how blood enters the relaxed ventricle</h2>\n<p>Blood keeps returning to the heart all the time, even while the ventricles are contracting and their <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span> are shut. During that time the relaxed atria act as <strong>reservoirs</strong>, collecting the returning blood, so atrial pressure slowly rises.</p>\n<p>When the ventricle relaxes, its pressure falls. As soon as ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the <strong>mitral valve opens</strong> and the stored blood rushes in. No contraction is needed for this: blood simply moves down the pressure difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is filling fast at first, then slow?</strong> At first the difference is large: the atrium is full and pressurised, and the ventricle has only just relaxed. So filling is <strong>rapid</strong>. As blood enters, ventricular pressure rises only a little, because the relaxed ventricle stretches easily. It stays low, as the lecture notes, but it creeps closer to atrial pressure. With a smaller difference, inflow slows. This slower period is <strong>diastasis</strong>. Throughout passive filling, blood flows from the veins <em>through</em> the open atrium into the ventricle, so the atrium is acting as a <strong>conduit</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why do the atria contract at all?</strong> Near the end of diastole, the atria contract and push in a final volume, the <strong>atrial kick</strong>, with the atrium acting as a <strong>booster pump</strong>. The lecture describes filling as about <strong>70% passive and 30% atrial</strong>. These are teaching proportions: at rest the atrial share is often 10–30%. The kick matters more in two situations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A stiff ventricle</strong> resists passive stretching, so less blood enters passively and the active push makes up more of the total.</li>\n<li><strong>A fast heart rate</strong> shortens diastole, leaving less time for passive filling, so the final push contributes a larger share.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When filling ends, the ventricle holds its <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong>, about <strong>130 mL</strong> in the lecture's example. This is the volume it starts its next contraction with (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">Section 7</a>).</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"diastolic-plot\"><figcaption>Ventricular filling: why it is fast, then slow, then topped up</figcaption><p>Left atrial and left ventricular pressure across ventricular diastole, with ventricular volume beneath. When the mitral valve opens the pressure gap is large and volume rises steeply (rapid filling). The gap then narrows and volume rises slowly (diastasis). Atrial contraction makes the a wave and adds a final step of volume up to EDV. Ventricular pressure stays low throughout. Schematic; pressures not to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>What is happening electrically during filling?</strong> The ventricles repolarised near the end of their contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a>), before filling began. During rapid filling and diastasis, no excitation wave is spreading through the working heart muscle, and none is needed: the pressure difference alone moves the blood. The <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> is not idle, though. Its pacemaker cells are slowly depolarising towards threshold. When they fire, the impulse spreads across the atria (the <span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span> on an ECG) and atrial contraction follows. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 3</strong></p>\n<p>A patient's atria stop contracting effectively, but ventricular relaxation is normal. Why does most ventricular filling continue?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Venous blood is pushed through the closed AV valves by contracting veins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Ventricular contraction draws blood back from the atria during systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The SA node keeps triggering small atrial contractions during diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Atrial pressure exceeds relaxed ventricular pressure, so blood flows passively</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Filling needs only a pressure difference across an open AV valve. Atrial systole adds a final top-up. Losing it reduces filling but does not stop it.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">4. Isovolumetric contraction: contracting, but no blood leaves</h2>\n<p>We left the ventricle full, holding its <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span>, with the atria just finished contracting (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">Section 3</a>). After the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV nodal</span> pause (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">Section 1</a>), the impulse races through the <strong><span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">His–Purkinje system</span></strong>, activating the ventricular muscle almost all at once. On an ECG, this ventricular activation is the <strong><span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span></strong> (more in <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">Section 9</a>). <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why does force lag slightly behind activation?</strong> Electrical activation first has to release calcium inside each muscle cell; only then can the cell develop force. So ventricular pressure starts rising a moment after the QRS. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">→ Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why does the mitral valve close so quickly?</strong> Atrial pressure is only a few mmHg, so ventricular pressure overtakes it almost as soon as contraction begins. The pressure difference across the mitral and tricuspid valves reverses and they <strong>close</strong>. This closure contributes to the <strong>first heart sound (<span data-term=\"s1\">S1</span>)</strong>.</p>\n<p>But ventricular pressure is still far below aortic pressure, so the <strong>aortic valve stays closed</strong>. Now <strong>both valves are closed</strong>, and the ventricle is a sealed chamber.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does pressure climb so steeply?</strong> Blood cannot be compressed. With no way in or out, the volume cannot change, so all the force the muscle develops goes into raising pressure. The muscle fibres build tension but barely shorten, as the lecture notes. Pressure therefore climbs steeply while volume stays fixed at EDV. This is <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong> (<em>iso</em> = same, <em>volumetric</em> = volume). It is the start of <span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">ventricular systole</span>, not yet ejection.</p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↑</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = EDV</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Meanwhile, the atria</strong> have relaxed and are again collecting venous blood behind closed <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span>. As ventricular pressure rises, the closed AV valves are the only part of the sealed chamber that can give a little. As the lecture puts it, they bulge back into the atrium. This causes a small bump in atrial pressure, the <strong>c wave</strong>. The atrium is not contracting; it is being pushed on.</p>\n<p><strong>What ends this phase?</strong> Ventricular pressure must exceed pressure in its outflow artery. The lecture uses about <strong>80 mmHg</strong> for aortic diastolic pressure and about <strong>10 mmHg</strong> for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure. These are typical reference values, not fixed thresholds in every person. <strong>Contraction is not the same as ejection:</strong> the ventricle must first overcome the pressure already in the artery.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says closure of the AV valves \"initiates systole\". More precisely, ventricular contraction starts first, and the rising pressure then closes the AV valves. Valve closure marks the start of isovolumetric contraction rather than causing it.</aside>\n\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"valve-stepper\"><figcaption>Isovolumetric contraction in three steps</figcaption><p>Step 1: ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, so the mitral and tricuspid valves close. Step 2: all four valves are closed; ventricular pressure rises while volume stays the same. Step 3: ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, so the aortic and pulmonary valves open and ejection begins. The atria stay relaxed and keep filling throughout.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Just after the mitral valve closes, LV pressure climbs steeply but LV volume does not change. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed, so blood has no exit</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The ventricular muscle has not yet been depolarised by the impulse</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood leaves the ventricle as fast as it enters from the atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The aortic valve is open, but aortic pressure prevents any outflow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Contraction is already under way and raising pressure, but with both valves shut there is nowhere for blood to go.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A person's aortic diastolic pressure is higher than usual. Ventricular activation and contractility are unchanged. What happens to isovolumetric contraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It shortens, because the aortic valve opens at a lower LV pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It stays the same, because valve opening depends on electrical timing</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It lengthens, because LV pressure must climb higher to open the valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It disappears, because the mitral valve stays open until ejection</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The aortic valve opens only when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure. A higher starting aortic pressure means the ventricle must build more pressure first, so, all else equal, this phase lasts longer.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">5. Ejection: why blood finally leaves</h2>\n<p>As soon as LV pressure rises above aortic pressure, the <strong>aortic valve opens</strong> (on the right, the pulmonary valve opens the same way). Blood flows into the artery and <strong>ventricular volume begins to fall</strong>. <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">Isovolumetric contraction</span> is over; ejection has begun.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is ejection rapid at first?</strong> At the moment the valve opens, the muscle is contracting strongly and the pressure difference pushing blood out is at its largest. Blood accelerates out quickly: <strong>rapid ejection</strong>. Ventricular pressure keeps rising for a while, peaking at about <strong>120 mmHg</strong> in the lecture's example, even though volume is already falling. Pressure and volume measure different things and need not move together. On the volume curve, this is the steep part of the fall.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does it then slow down?</strong> Two things shrink the pressure difference at once:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The muscle weakens.</strong> As contraction passes its peak, ventricular force and pressure fall.</li>\n<li><strong>The aorta fills.</strong> Blood entering the aorta stretches its elastic wall and raises aortic pressure. With the valve open, LV and aortic pressures now rise and fall almost together. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/blood-pressure?section=2-why-doesn-t-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole\">→ Arterial Blood Pressure, §2</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>With a smaller difference, outflow slows: <strong>reduced ejection</strong>, the gentler part of the volume fall. Late in this phase, ventricular pressure can dip slightly below aortic pressure while blood still flows forward. Moving blood carries momentum and takes time to stop (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">Section 6</a>).</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture describes ejection as \"initially slow, then rapid\". Standard physiology, including the volume curve on every <span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span>, shows the reverse: rapid ejection first, then reduced ejection. We teach the standard order.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Meanwhile, the atria</strong> stay relaxed behind closed <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span>. Early in ejection, atrial pressure briefly dips, the <strong>x descent</strong>. The atrium is finishing its relaxation, and the contracting ventricle pulls the AV valve ring downward, enlarging the atrium. After that, returning venous blood fills the atrium steadily and its pressure rises again. So while the ventricles empty, the atria store the blood that will rush in once the ventricles relax.</p>\n<p><strong>Electrically,</strong> the ventricles were activated before ejection began, and no new impulse is needed to keep it going. During the later part of ejection the ventricular muscle <strong>repolarises</strong> (the <span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span> on an ECG), which sets up the relaxation that follows. Repolarisation does not switch contraction off instantly; force declines over the following moments.</p>\n<p>When ejection ends, some blood remains: the <strong>end-systolic volume (ESV)</strong>. <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">Section 7</a> shows how EDV and ESV give the stroke volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 5</strong></p>\n<p>During early ejection, ventricular volume is falling, yet ventricular pressure is still rising. How can both happen at once?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Volume falls because the aortic valve is open and blood is leaving. Pressure rises because the muscle is still increasing its force faster than blood escapes. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">6. Closing the outflow valves and isovolumetric relaxation</h2>\n<p><strong>Why does the aortic valve close?</strong> <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a> left blood still coasting forward on momentum, even though ventricular pressure had dipped below aortic pressure. That forward flow now slows to a stop and briefly reverses. The tiny backflow pushes the aortic cusps together and they seal (the rule from <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Section 2</a>). The lecture calls this short interval, from the end of forward ejection to <span data-term=\"semilunar-valves\">semilunar valve</span> closure, <strong>protodiastole</strong>.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture times protodiastole from the moment \"ventricular pressure starts falling\". In our account, pressure begins to fall during reduced ejection, while blood is still leaving. We therefore date protodiastole from the end of forward flow, as most physiology texts do. Either way, it ends when the semilunar valves close.</aside>\n\n<p>Aortic valve closure contributes the <strong>aortic component of the second heart sound (<span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span>)</strong>. The pulmonary valve closes the same way, slightly later (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Section 10</a>). On the aortic pressure trace, closure leaves a small dip and rebound, the <strong>dicrotic notch</strong>. The dip is the brief backflow; the rebound is the stretched aortic wall recoiling against the now-closed valve. It is not a second contraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Why doesn't filling restart straight away?</strong> The aortic valve is now shut, but ventricular pressure is still above atrial pressure, so the <strong>mitral valve is also shut</strong>. Both valves are closed again, and the ventricle is once more a sealed chamber.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does pressure fall so steeply?</strong> This is <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">Section 4</a> in reverse. The muscle relaxes as calcium is pumped out of the cytoplasm, so its tension drops. Volume cannot change, so the falling tension shows up entirely as falling pressure. Pressure drops steeply while volume stays at <span data-term=\"esv\">ESV</span>. This is <strong>isovolumetric relaxation</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax\">→ Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↓</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = ESV</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Meanwhile, the atria</strong> have kept filling behind closed <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span> since the <span data-term=\"x-descent\">x descent</span> (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a>). Atrial pressure reaches its peak now: the <strong>v wave</strong>. No atrial contraction is involved; it is simply blood accumulating.</p>\n<p>When ventricular pressure finally falls below atrial pressure, the <strong>mitral valve opens</strong>. The stored atrial blood rushes in, atrial pressure drops (the <strong>y descent</strong>), and rapid filling begins. We are back at <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">Section 3</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Electrically,</strong> ventricular repolarisation (the <span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span>) happened during late ejection, before most of this relaxation. The T wave, aortic valve closure and mitral valve opening are three separate moments, in that order, not one event.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> The aortic valve has just closed. Ventricular pressure is falling quickly, yet the mitral valve is still shut. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Atrial pressure is falling faster than ventricular pressure at this moment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Ventricular pressure is still higher than left atrial pressure at this moment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The mitral valve opens only when the next P wave triggers atrial systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Ventricular repolarisation must finish before either valve is able to move</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The mitral valve opens only when LV pressure falls <em>below</em> LA pressure. Until then, the ventricle relaxes with both valves closed.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises steadily during ventricular systole. Which wave is this, and why does it happen?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The <strong>v wave</strong>. Venous blood keeps entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed, so blood accumulates and pressure rises. When the AV valve opens, that stored blood flows into the ventricle.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">7. How much blood does the ventricle pump?</h2>\n<p>A ventricle never empties completely. <strong><span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span></strong> is the volume just before contraction; <strong><span data-term=\"esv\">ESV</span></strong> is what remains after ejection. The difference is the <strong>stroke volume (SV)</strong>, the volume ejected in one beat.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Stroke volume</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"SV = EDV − ESV\">SV = EDV − ESV</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = <strong>80 mL</strong></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why is some blood always left behind?</strong> Ejection stops when the pressure difference and the blood's momentum run out (Sections <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">5</a> and <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">6</a>), not when the chamber is empty. The remaining ESV is a reserve: a stronger contraction can eject some of it.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ejection fraction (EF)</strong> is the share of EDV that is ejected:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ejection fraction</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"EF = (SV) / (EDV) × 100%\">EF = (SV) / (EDV) × 100%</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 ≈ <strong>61.5%</strong></span></div>\n\n<p>The lecture uses EDV ≈ 130 mL, SV ≈ 70–90 mL and EF ≈ 65% as typical values; small differences between rounded examples are not contradictions. EF is the fraction of <em>that ventricle's</em> end-diastolic blood ejected, not a fraction of total blood volume. Because it compares what is ejected with what was available, EF is widely used clinically as a measure of how well the ventricle contracts. A low EF suggests weakened contraction. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure?section=systolic-and-diastolic-problems\">→ Heart Failure Physiology</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Two ways to raise stroke volume.</strong> Since SV = EDV − ESV, the ventricle can eject more by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>filling more</strong> (a larger EDV), or</li>\n<li><strong>emptying more</strong> (a smaller ESV), for example through stronger contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chapter 7 explains what controls each. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=3-what-determines-stroke-volume\">→ Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Now trace the volume through one beat. It <strong>rises</strong> during filling and reaches EDV after atrial systole. It stays <strong>flat</strong> during isovolumetric contraction, then <strong>falls</strong> during ejection, steeply then gradually. It stays <strong>flat</strong> at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation, then <strong>rises</strong> again when the mitral valve opens.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"volume-bar\"><figcaption>Stroke volume and ejection fraction in one picture</figcaption><p>A ventricle filled to its end-diastolic volume of 130 mL. After ejection, 50 mL remains (end-systolic volume). The 80 mL that left is the stroke volume, which is 61.5% of 130 mL: the ejection fraction.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 7</strong></p>\n<p>EDV stays at 140 mL, but a drug lowers ESV from 70 mL to 50 mL. What happens to SV and EF?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Before: SV = 140 − 70 = 70 mL; EF = 70 ÷ 140 = <strong>50%</strong>. After: SV = 140 − 50 = 90 mL; EF = 90 ÷ 140 ≈ <strong>64.3%</strong>. Emptying more completely raises both SV and EF, even with the same filling.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-the-whole-cycle-on-one-map\">8. The whole cycle on one map</h2>\n<p>Use this after reading the explanation, to rebuild each step from its cause. Start with atrial systole, where the heartbeat begins.</p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget map-quiz\" data-widget=\"map-quiz\"></div>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>What changes?</th>\n  <th>Immediate consequence</th>\n  <th>Next phase</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>SA node fires; atria depolarise</td>\n  <td>Atria contract; final filling to EDV</td>\n  <td>Atrial systole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricles depolarise; ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure</td>\n  <td>Mitral valve closes (S1)</td>\n  <td>Isovolumetric contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure</td>\n  <td>Aortic valve opens</td>\n  <td>Rapid, then reduced ejection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Forward flow stops and briefly reverses (<span data-term=\"protodiastole\">protodiastole</span>)</td>\n  <td>Aortic valve closes (S2)</td>\n  <td>Isovolumetric relaxation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</td>\n  <td>Mitral valve opens</td>\n  <td>Rapid filling, then diastasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>SA node fires again</td>\n  <td>Next atrial systole</td>\n  <td>Next cycle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Compare the two isovolumetric phases</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th>Isovolumetric contraction</th>\n  <th>Isovolumetric relaxation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Muscle</td>\n  <td>Developing force</td>\n  <td>Relaxing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Mitral and aortic valves</td>\n  <td>Both closed</td>\n  <td>Both closed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular pressure</td>\n  <td>Rising</td>\n  <td>Falling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular volume</td>\n  <td>Constant at EDV</td>\n  <td>Constant at ESV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Atria</td>\n  <td>Relaxed, filling; c wave</td>\n  <td>Relaxed, full; v wave peaks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ends when</td>\n  <td>Aortic valve opens</td>\n  <td>Mitral valve opens</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Volume is constant in both because neither valve is open. Pressure moves in opposite directions because the muscle is building force in one and relaxing in the other.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">9. Reading the Wiggers diagram one trace at a time</h2>\n<p>A <strong>Wiggers diagram</strong> lines up everything you have just learned on one time axis: the ECG, atrial, ventricular and aortic pressures, ventricular volume and heart sounds. It looks crowded, but every curve follows the events above. We will build it one trace at a time for the left heart.</p>\n<p><strong>How to read it.</strong> Time runs from left to right, and every trace shares the same clock. A vertical line drawn anywhere cuts all the traces at the same instant: it tells you what the ECG, pressures, volume and sounds are doing at that moment.</p>\n<p><strong>The key insight: valves move where pressure curves cross.</strong> By the rule in <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Section 2</a>, a valve opens or closes exactly when the pressures on its two sides swap order. So on the diagram:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>mitral valve</strong> closes and opens where the <strong>ventricular and atrial</strong> pressure curves cross.</li>\n<li>The <strong>aortic valve</strong> opens and closes where the <strong>ventricular and aortic</strong> pressure curves cross.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Find those four crossings and you have found the four valve events. Between them, the volume curve tells you whether blood is entering, leaving or neither.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget wiggers-figure\" data-widget=\"wiggers\"><figcaption>The Wiggers diagram, built one step at a time (left heart)</figcaption><p>One heartbeat plus the start of the next, with the ECG, left atrial, left ventricular and aortic pressures, left ventricular volume and heart sounds on one time axis. The four valve events are where pressure curves cross: mitral closure and opening where the ventricular and atrial curves cross, aortic opening and closure where the ventricular and aortic curves cross. Labels show the P, QRS and T waves; the a, c and v waves and x and y descents; the dicrotic notch; EDV and ESV; and S1 and S2. Schematic; values approximate.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1: Atrial systole.</strong> The SA node fires and the atria depolarise: the <strong><span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span></strong>. Shortly afterwards the atria contract, producing the <strong>a wave</strong> on the atrial pressure trace and topping up the ventricle to <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span>. <em>P wave → atrial contraction → a wave → EDV</em>: related, but in sequence, not simultaneous. (In a stiff ventricle, this final push can produce an extra sound, <strong>S4</strong>.)</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Ventricular contraction begins.</strong> After the AV nodal delay, ventricular activation gives the <strong><span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span></strong>. Ventricular pressure then rises and crosses atrial pressure: the mitral valve closes (<strong><span data-term=\"s1\">S1</span></strong>), and the bulging valve makes the <strong><span data-term=\"c-wave\">c wave</span></strong>. With both valves shut, the volume trace is flat: <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Ejection.</strong> Ventricular pressure crosses aortic pressure and the aortic valve opens. From here the LV and aortic pressure traces run almost together, because they are connected through the open valve. The volume trace falls steeply (<span data-term=\"rapid-reduced-ejection\">rapid ejection</span>), then more gently (reduced ejection). Meanwhile atrial pressure dips in the <strong><span data-term=\"x-descent\">x descent</span></strong> (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a>), then begins to climb.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Relaxation.</strong> The <strong><span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span></strong> appears during reduced ejection as the ventricles repolarise. Forward flow stops and briefly reverses, the aortic valve closes (<strong><span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span></strong>), and the <strong><span data-term=\"dicrotic-notch\">dicrotic notch</span></strong> marks the aortic trace. Ventricular pressure drops steeply while volume stays flat at <span data-term=\"esv\">ESV</span>; atrial pressure reaches the <strong><span data-term=\"v-wave\">v wave</span></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Filling resumes.</strong> Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, atrial pressure falls (<strong><span data-term=\"y-descent\">y descent</span></strong>) and the volume trace rises again. (Rapid inflow can sometimes produce an extra sound, <strong>S3</strong>.)</p>\n<h3>Read it in four passes</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Find QRS and mitral closure.</strong> QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Find aortic opening.</strong> Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now <strong>falls</strong> as ejection begins.</li>\n<li><strong>Find aortic closure.</strong> Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and <strong>S2</strong>. A small <strong>dicrotic notch</strong> appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation.</li>\n<li><strong>Find mitral reopening.</strong> Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve <strong>rises</strong> as filling resumes.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Wave</th>\n  <th>Cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>a wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>c wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>x descent</strong></td>\n  <td>Atrial relaxation, then the AV valve ring pulled down during ejection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>v wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>y descent</strong></td>\n  <td>AV valve opens and stored atrial blood empties into the ventricle.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> full JVP waves and descents → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents\">Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse, §4</a> · ECG timing → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/ecg?section=5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events\">Electrocardiography, §5</a> · S1 and S2 → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §2</a> · S3 and S4 → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=4-what-causes-s3-and-s4\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §4</a></p>\n\n<h3>Watch the mechanism</h3>\n<div class=\"media-resource animation-resource\" data-animation-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" data-media-alt=\"Animated heart with valve motion and cardiac cycle pressure curves.\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL</span><h3>Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces</h3><p>Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-animation\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play animation</button><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Loaded only when selected.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"IS9TD9fHFv0\" data-media-title=\"The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IS9TD9fHFv0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 9</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> On a Wiggers diagram, ventricular pressure rises sharply while ventricular volume stays flat. Which event ends this phase?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The mitral valve opens as ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The T wave appears and the ventricle begins its electrical recovery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The aortic valve closes, leaving the dicrotic notch on aortic pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The aortic valve opens as ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The flat volume with rising pressure is isovolumetric contraction. It ends when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure and blood can leave.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The T wave has begun, yet the aortic valve is still open and blood is still leaving. Is this possible?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Yes.</strong> Repolarisation happens during reduced ejection. Electrical recovery starts the decline in force, but force and forward flow do not stop instantly. The aortic valve closes only when flow reverses at the end of ejection.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">10. Right versus left heart, S2 splitting and heart rate</h2>\n<p><strong>Same rules, lower pressures.</strong> Both ventricles are activated in the same beat and follow the same valve rule. The right ventricle pumps into the low-resistance pulmonary circulation, reaching about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>; the left reaches about <strong>120 mmHg</strong>. Even so, over time both pump almost exactly the same volume per minute. The two circuits are connected in series, so whatever the right ventricle sends through the lungs must come back to the left. Higher pressure does not mean more blood. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">→ Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why the right ventricle ejects first.</strong> The lecture notes that the left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet right ventricular ejection begins first. Both points are correct. The right ventricle only has to exceed about <strong>10 mmHg</strong> in the pulmonary artery, versus about <strong>80 mmHg</strong> in the aorta, so its <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric phase</span> (contracting with all valves shut, before ejection) is much shorter and the pulmonary valve opens first. The <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> does not send separate beats to each side.</p>\n<p><strong>Why S2 has two parts.</strong> <span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span> is made of <strong>A2</strong> (aortic closure) and <strong>P2</strong> (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During <strong>expiration</strong> they are close together and often sound single. During <strong>inspiration</strong>, P2 moves later and the gap widens: <strong>physiological splitting of S2</strong>. Two effects delay P2:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>More right-heart filling.</strong> Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower pulmonary impedance.</strong> The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"s2-timing\"><figcaption>Why S2 splits on inspiration</figcaption><p>Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says the valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the inspiratory widening. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it.</aside>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> listening for splitting → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §3</a> · breathing and venous return → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return\">Veins and Venous Return, §5</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>What happens when heart rate rises?</strong> At <strong>75 beats/min</strong>, one cycle lasts <strong>0.8 s</strong>; at <strong>150 beats/min</strong>, only <strong>0.4 s</strong>. Both phases shorten, but not equally.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does diastole lose most of the time?</strong> How long systole lasts is tied to how long the ventricular action potential lasts. Faster contraction and repolarisation shorten it a little, giving quicker ejection, as the lecture notes. But diastole is simply whatever time is left before the next beat, so it absorbs most of the cut. That matters, because diastole is when the ventricles fill.</p>\n<p>At very high rates, or when the ventricle relaxes poorly, there may not be enough time to fill. <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span> then falls, and <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span> falls with it. A faster heart rate does <strong>not automatically</strong> mean less stroke volume, though. During exercise, greater venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can maintain or even raise it. The heart also cannot be driven into a sustained, tetanic contraction, because its long refractory period forces relaxation between beats.</p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget hr-slider\" data-widget=\"hr-slider\"><p>Cycle length = 60 ÷ heart rate. At 75 beats/min it is 0.80 s (800 ms). This tool calculates total cycle length only; it does not model the exact split between systole and diastole.</p></div>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> cardiac output and rate → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §2</a> · refractory period → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §6</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Coronary blood flow.</strong> The left ventricle's own muscle receives most of its blood flow during <strong>diastole</strong>. During systole the contracting muscle squeezes the vessels running through it, especially the deeper ones. So marked tachycardia hits twice: it shortens the time for coronary perfusion just as the faster-working heart needs more oxygen. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole\">→ Coronary Circulation, §3</a> and <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia\">§5</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 10</strong></p>\n<p>During inspiration, P2 occurs later than during expiration. Which explanation is best?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. More right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance prolong RV ejection</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Inspiration slows conduction in the right bundle, delaying RV activation</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pulmonary arterial pressure rises on inspiration, so RV ejection starts late</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Less blood returns to the right atrium, so the tricuspid valve closes later</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Both inspiratory effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so the pulmonary valve closes later and the A2–P2 gap widens.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"11-understanding-check\">11. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>Predict first, then reveal.</strong> Each scenario changes one thing. Work out what happens in the rest of the heart before opening the answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario 1: Contracting, but no blood leaving.</strong> The left ventricle has just started contracting. Its pressure is rising, but its volume is not changing. What are the valves and the left atrium doing?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>This is <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>. The mitral valve has just shut, and the aortic valve has not opened yet, because ventricular pressure is still below aortic pressure. With both shut, blood can't get in or out. Meanwhile the left atrium is relaxed and filling with blood from the lungs. Contracting and ejecting are not the same thing.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Scenario 2: Relaxing, but no blood entering.</strong> Ejection has ended and the aortic valve has shut. Ventricular pressure is falling, but volume is not changing. Why is no blood coming in?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>This is isovolumetric relaxation. Ventricular pressure is still higher than atrial pressure, so the mitral valve stays shut. The atrium is full, at its <span data-term=\"v-wave\">v wave</span>. Once ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and blood rushes in.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Scenario 3: The atria stop contracting properly.</strong> Does ventricular filling stop?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>No. Most filling is passive: blood flows in whenever atrial pressure is higher than ventricular pressure. Only the final top-up from atrial contraction is lost. That loss matters most when the ventricle is stiff or the heart rate is fast.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Scenario 4: Heart rate doubles.</strong> The rate rises from 75 to 150 beats/min. What may happen to filling, stroke volume and blood flow to the heart muscle?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Each beat now lasts 0.4 s instead of 0.8 s, and most of the lost time comes out of diastole. So there is less time to fill the ventricle and less time for blood to flow into the left ventricle's own muscle. Stroke volume may fall, but not always: during exercise, more venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can protect it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Final questions</strong> (answer and explanation shown straight after each response)</p>\n<p><strong>Q1.</strong> A patient's ventricle becomes stiff and relaxes poorly. Atrial contraction is normal. What happens to ventricular filling?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Passive filling rises, so the atrial kick becomes unnecessary for filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Filling stops completely, because the mitral valve can no longer open</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Passive filling falls, so the atrial kick supplies a larger share of filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Filling is unchanged, because filling depends only on the SA node firing</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A stiff ventricle doesn't stretch easily. As blood enters, its pressure rises quickly and soon matches atrial pressure, so passive inflow stops early. The atria's final push then makes up a bigger part of the filling. This is why losing atrial contraction hurts these patients most.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q2.</strong> During <span data-term=\"diastasis\">diastasis</span>, before the next P wave, what is happening electrically?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. SA nodal cells are slowly depolarising towards their next threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A slow excitation wave is spreading through the ventricular muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The AV node is firing repeatedly to sustain passive ventricular filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Purkinje fibres are repolarising the atria in preparation for systole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Passive filling needs no electrical impulse; the pressure difference moves the blood. Meanwhile the SA node's pacemaker cells slowly drift towards threshold. When they fire, the P wave and atrial contraction follow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q3.</strong> The left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet the pulmonary valve opens before the aortic valve. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The right ventricle contracts more strongly than the left ventricle does</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The impulse reaches the right ventricle first by a faster route</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The pulmonary valve has thinner cusps that open at any pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The right ventricle only has to exceed a much lower arterial pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The right ventricle only needs to beat about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery; the left must beat about 80 mmHg in the aorta. The right ventricle reaches its target sooner, so its valve opens first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q4.</strong> Ventricles X and Y eject the same stroke volume, but X starts with a larger <span data-term=\"edv\">end-diastolic volume</span>. Which conclusion follows?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both have the same ejection fraction, because their stroke volumes match</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. X has the lower ejection fraction, because SV is a smaller share of its EDV</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. X has the lower end-systolic volume, because it started with more blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Both have the same end-systolic volume, because they ejected equal volumes</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> EF = SV ÷ EDV. If SV is the same but EDV is bigger, the fraction is smaller. X also has the <em>larger</em> ESV, since ESV = EDV − SV.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q5.</strong> Late in ejection, left ventricular pressure falls slightly below aortic pressure, yet blood keeps leaving for a moment. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The left atrium contracts and pushes blood on through the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The aortic cusps actively propel blood forward as they begin closing</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood already moving forward carries momentum that takes time to stop</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The mitral valve opens and adds atrial pressure behind the outflow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Moving blood can't stop instantly. It slows down, then briefly flows backwards, and that backflow is what shuts the aortic valve.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q6.</strong> An arrhythmia raises a resting patient's heart rate from 75 to 150 beats/min. Which change is most likely?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Systole and diastole both halve, so filling time is proportionally kept</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Systole shortens most, so ejection is incomplete but filling is protected</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Coronary perfusion time rises, because the heart relaxes more often</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Diastole shortens most, so filling and coronary perfusion time both fall</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Systole can only shorten a little, so diastole loses most of the time. At rest, without the boosts that exercise brings, less filling time can lower EDV and stroke volume. The left ventricle's muscle also gets less time to receive blood.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q7.</strong> Which pairing of an atrial pressure wave with its cause is correct?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. c wave: the closed AV valve bulging back towards the atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. v wave: the atrium contracting against a closed AV valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. a wave: venous blood filling the atrium while the AV valve is shut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. y descent: the atrium relaxing while the AV valve stays closed</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The c wave comes from the contracting ventricle pushing the shut valve back. The a wave is the atrium contracting, the v wave is the atrium filling up, and the y descent happens when the AV valve opens and the atrium empties.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q8.</strong> End-diastolic volume is 150 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. What are the stroke volume and ejection fraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 90 mL and 40%</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 90 mL and 60%</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 60 mL and 60%</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 60 mL and 40%</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> SV = 150 − 60 = 90 mL. EF = 90 ÷ 150 × 100 = 60%.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"12-chapter-essentials\">12. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The atria and ventricles take turns.</strong> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. While the ventricles contract, the atria relax and refill. For about 0.4 s of a 0.8 s beat, both are relaxed together.</li>\n<li><strong>One chain drives every phase:</strong> electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Valves are passive.</strong> Each opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front, and shuts when that reverses. Papillary muscles stop the AV valves turning inside out; they do not open them.</li>\n<li><strong>Filling is mostly passive:</strong> rapid filling, then slower diastasis, then the atrial kick (lecture: 70% passive, 30% atrial; at rest often 10–30% atrial). The kick matters more with a stiff ventricle or a fast heart rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> all four valves are shut, so the contracting ventricle's pressure shoots up while its volume stays at EDV. Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves contributes to S1.</li>\n<li><strong>Ejection</strong> starts only when ventricular pressure beats arterial pressure: about 80 mmHg in the aorta, 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery. It is rapid first, then reduced, as the muscle weakens and the aorta fills.</li>\n<li><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> forward flow briefly reverses and shuts the aortic and pulmonary valves (S2, dicrotic notch). With all valves shut, pressure falls while volume stays at ESV. It ends when the mitral and tricuspid valves open.</li>\n<li><strong>Volumes:</strong> SV = EDV − ESV and EF = SV ÷ EDV × 100%. Stroke volume rises by filling more (bigger EDV) or emptying more (smaller ESV).</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial pressure waves:</strong> a = atria contract; c = shut AV valves bulge back; x = atria relax and the valve ring is pulled down; v = atria fill behind shut valves; y = AV valves open and the atria empty.</li>\n<li><strong>Electrical comes before mechanical:</strong> the P wave before atrial contraction, the QRS before ventricular contraction, and the T wave during reduced ejection, before the outflow valves shut.</li>\n<li><strong>On a Wiggers diagram, every valve event is a point where two pressure curves cross.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right heart and heart rate:</strong> the right heart follows the same rules at lower pressures, so the right ventricle ejects first; on inspiration P2 is delayed and S2 splitting widens. At faster heart rates diastole loses most of the time, so very high rates cut filling and coronary perfusion time.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"The Cardiac Cycle 1. One heartbeat: what the atria and ventricles are doing The cardiac cycle is everything that happens in the heart during one heartbeat: the atria and ventricles each contract and relax once, in a fixed order. Systole means contraction and diastole means relaxation. On their own, the words are incomplete. Always ask systole of which chambers? The atria and ventricles are often doing different things, though for a large part of each beat they are both relaxed. Why do the atria go first? Each beat starts with an electrical impulse from the SA node in the right atrium. The impulse spreads across the atria first, then pauses briefly at the AV node before reaching the ventricles. So the atria are activated, and contract, just before the ventricles. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §5) Follow one beat: Atrial systole. At the end of ventricular diastole, the atria contract and push a final portion of blood into ventricles that are already nearly full. Ventricular systole. The ventricles contract and eject blood. Meanwhile the atria relax and begin refilling with blood returning from the veins. Both chambers in diastole. The ventricles now relax too, so both are relaxed together. For a brief moment the ventricles still cannot fill, because their pressure is higher than atrial pressure. As they relax further, their pressure falls below atrial pressure and blood flows in passively. Atrial systole again, and the next beat begins. How long does each part take? At about 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 60 ÷ 75 = 0.8 s. Part of the cycle Duration Where it comes from Ventricular systole ≈ 0.3 s Typical resting value Ventricular diastole ≈ 0.5 s 0.8 − 0.3 Atrial systole ≈ 0.1 s Last part of ventricular diastole Both chambers relaxed ≈ 0.4 s 0.5 − 0.1, half of every beat These are rounded teaching values; the lecture gives no phase durations. That shared 0.4 s matters. After a short pause while ventricular pressure is still falling, it is when most ventricular filling happens. This is why atrial contraction is only a top-up. It is also why very fast heart rates, which shorten this period most, can limit filling ( Section 10). One heartbeat at 75 beats/min: what the atria and ventricles are doing Two aligned bars across one 0.8 s cycle. Atria: systole for the first 0.1 s, then diastole. Ventricles: systole from 0.1 s to 0.4 s, then diastole. From 0.4 s to 0.8 s both are relaxed: first a short pause while the ventricle is still relaxing with no filling, then passive filling. Proportions approximate, at 75 beats/min. What drives each step? Every phase follows one chain: Electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes The electrical signal starts the chain but never moves blood or valves directly. Each link takes a moment, so the steps happen in sequence, not all at once. Section 2 explains the key link: how pressure moves the valves. 2. The one rule every valve follows The heart has four valves, in two pairs: AV (atrioventricular) valves sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the mitral valve on the left and the tricuspid valve on the right. Semilunar valves sit between each ventricle and its artery: the aortic valve on the left and the pulmonary valve on the right. All four obey one rule. A valve opens when pressure upstream (in the chamber blood is coming from) is higher than pressure downstream (where blood is going). It closes when that difference reverses. How can pressure alone do this? Each valve is made of thin flaps called cusps, shaped to open in one direction only. Higher pressure upstream pushes the cusps apart, and blood flows through. When downstream pressure becomes higher, blood starts to move backwards and pushes the cusps together until they seal. No nerve or electrical signal is involved. ( → Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §3: valves keep flow one-way) What about the papillary muscles? The AV valve cusps are tethered by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles in the ventricular wall. These muscles contract with the ventricle, but they do not open or close the valve. They hold the cusps in place so that rising ventricular pressure cannot push them inside out into the atrium. Now apply the rule to the left heart: left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta. Mitral valve opens P LA > P LV Blood enters the ventricle Aortic valve opens P LV > P aorta Blood leaves the ventricle Here P means pressure, LA is left atrium and LV is left ventricle. The two valves respond to different pressure differences, so there are moments when both are closed at once. Those moments explain two phases you will meet in Sections 4 and 6, when the ventricle's pressure changes but its volume does not. One refinement comes later: moving blood carries momentum, so flow and valve closure can lag slightly behind a change in pressure ( Section 6). The right heart follows the same rule at lower pressures, through the tricuspid and pulmonary valves. We follow the left heart first and return to the right in Section 10. 3. Filling: how blood enters the relaxed ventricle Blood keeps returning to the heart all the time, even while the ventricles are contracting and their AV valves are shut. During that time the relaxed atria act as reservoirs, collecting the returning blood, so atrial pressure slowly rises. When the ventricle relaxes, its pressure falls. As soon as ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and the stored blood rushes in. No contraction is needed for this: blood simply moves down the pressure difference. Why is filling fast at first, then slow? At first the difference is large: the atrium is full and pressurised, and the ventricle has only just relaxed. So filling is rapid. As blood enters, ventricular pressure rises only a little, because the relaxed ventricle stretches easily. It stays low, as the lecture notes, but it creeps closer to atrial pressure. With a smaller difference, inflow slows. This slower period is diastasis. Throughout passive filling, blood flows from the veins through the open atrium into the ventricle, so the atrium is acting as a conduit. Why do the atria contract at all? Near the end of diastole, the atria contract and push in a final volume, the atrial kick, with the atrium acting as a booster pump. The lecture describes filling as about 70% passive and 30% atrial. These are teaching proportions: at rest the atrial share is often 10–30%. The kick matters more in two situations: A stiff ventricle resists passive stretching, so less blood enters passively and the active push makes up more of the total. A fast heart rate shortens diastole, leaving less time for passive filling, so the final push contributes a larger share. When filling ends, the ventricle holds its end-diastolic volume (EDV), about 130 mL in the lecture's example. This is the volume it starts its next contraction with ( Section 7). Ventricular filling: why it is fast, then slow, then topped up Left atrial and left ventricular pressure across ventricular diastole, with ventricular volume beneath. When the mitral valve opens the pressure gap is large and volume rises steeply (rapid filling). The gap then narrows and volume rises slowly (diastasis). Atrial contraction makes the a wave and adds a final step of volume up to EDV. Ventricular pressure stays low throughout. Schematic; pressures not to scale. What is happening electrically during filling? The ventricles repolarised near the end of their contraction ( Section 5), before filling began. During rapid filling and diastasis, no excitation wave is spreading through the working heart muscle, and none is needed: the pressure difference alone moves the blood. The SA node is not idle, though. Its pacemaker cells are slowly depolarising towards threshold. When they fire, the impulse spreads across the atria (the P wave on an ECG) and atrial contraction follows. ( → Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity, §3) Understanding Check — Section 3 A patient's atria stop contracting effectively, but ventricular relaxation is normal. Why does most ventricular filling continue? A. Venous blood is pushed through the closed AV valves by contracting veins B. Ventricular contraction draws blood back from the atria during systole C. The SA node keeps triggering small atrial contractions during diastole D. Atrial pressure exceeds relaxed ventricular pressure, so blood flows passively Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Filling needs only a pressure difference across an open AV valve. Atrial systole adds a final top-up. Losing it reduces filling but does not stop it. 4. Isovolumetric contraction: contracting, but no blood leaves We left the ventricle full, holding its EDV, with the atria just finished contracting ( Section 3). After the AV nodal pause ( Section 1), the impulse races through the His–Purkinje system, activating the ventricular muscle almost all at once. On an ECG, this ventricular activation is the QRS complex (more in Section 9). ( → Cardiac Conduction, §6) Why does force lag slightly behind activation? Electrical activation first has to release calcium inside each muscle cell; only then can the cell develop force. So ventricular pressure starts rising a moment after the QRS. ( → Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §4) Why does the mitral valve close so quickly? Atrial pressure is only a few mmHg, so ventricular pressure overtakes it almost as soon as contraction begins. The pressure difference across the mitral and tricuspid valves reverses and they close. This closure contributes to the first heart sound ( S1). But ventricular pressure is still far below aortic pressure, so the aortic valve stays closed. Now both valves are closed, and the ventricle is a sealed chamber. Why does pressure climb so steeply? Blood cannot be compressed. With no way in or out, the volume cannot change, so all the force the muscle develops goes into raising pressure. The muscle fibres build tension but barely shorten, as the lecture notes. Pressure therefore climbs steeply while volume stays fixed at EDV. This is isovolumetric contraction ( iso = same, volumetric = volume). It is the start of ventricular systole, not yet ejection. Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↑ Volume = EDV Meanwhile, the atria have relaxed and are again collecting venous blood behind closed AV valves. As ventricular pressure rises, the closed AV valves are the only part of the sealed chamber that can give a little. As the lecture puts it, they bulge back into the atrium. This causes a small bump in atrial pressure, the c wave. The atrium is not contracting; it is being pushed on. What ends this phase? Ventricular pressure must exceed pressure in its outflow artery. The lecture uses about 80 mmHg for aortic diastolic pressure and about 10 mmHg for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure. These are typical reference values, not fixed thresholds in every person. Contraction is not the same as ejection: the ventricle must first overcome the pressure already in the artery. Source note: The lecture says closure of the AV valves \"initiates systole\". More precisely, ventricular contraction starts first, and the rising pressure then closes the AV valves. Valve closure marks the start of isovolumetric contraction rather than causing it. Isovolumetric contraction in three steps Step 1: ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, so the mitral and tricuspid valves close. Step 2: all four valves are closed; ventricular pressure rises while volume stays the same. Step 3: ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, so the aortic and pulmonary valves open and ejection begins. The atria stay relaxed and keep filling throughout. Understanding Check — Section 4 1. Just after the mitral valve closes, LV pressure climbs steeply but LV volume does not change. Why? A. Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed, so blood has no exit B. The ventricular muscle has not yet been depolarised by the impulse C. Blood leaves the ventricle as fast as it enters from the atrium D. The aortic valve is open, but aortic pressure prevents any outflow Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Contraction is already under way and raising pressure, but with both valves shut there is nowhere for blood to go. 2. A person's aortic diastolic pressure is higher than usual. Ventricular activation and contractility are unchanged. What happens to isovolumetric contraction? A. It shortens, because the aortic valve opens at a lower LV pressure B. It stays the same, because valve opening depends on electrical timing C. It lengthens, because LV pressure must climb higher to open the valve D. It disappears, because the mitral valve stays open until ejection Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The aortic valve opens only when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure. A higher starting aortic pressure means the ventricle must build more pressure first, so, all else equal, this phase lasts longer. 5. Ejection: why blood finally leaves As soon as LV pressure rises above aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens (on the right, the pulmonary valve opens the same way). Blood flows into the artery and ventricular volume begins to fall. Isovolumetric contraction is over; ejection has begun. Why is ejection rapid at first? At the moment the valve opens, the muscle is contracting strongly and the pressure difference pushing blood out is at its largest. Blood accelerates out quickly: rapid ejection. Ventricular pressure keeps rising for a while, peaking at about 120 mmHg in the lecture's example, even though volume is already falling. Pressure and volume measure different things and need not move together. On the volume curve, this is the steep part of the fall. Why does it then slow down? Two things shrink the pressure difference at once: The muscle weakens. As contraction passes its peak, ventricular force and pressure fall. The aorta fills. Blood entering the aorta stretches its elastic wall and raises aortic pressure. With the valve open, LV and aortic pressures now rise and fall almost together. ( → Arterial Blood Pressure, §2) With a smaller difference, outflow slows: reduced ejection, the gentler part of the volume fall. Late in this phase, ventricular pressure can dip slightly below aortic pressure while blood still flows forward. Moving blood carries momentum and takes time to stop ( Section 6). Source note: The lecture describes ejection as \"initially slow, then rapid\". Standard physiology, including the volume curve on every Wiggers diagram, shows the reverse: rapid ejection first, then reduced ejection. We teach the standard order. Meanwhile, the atria stay relaxed behind closed AV valves. Early in ejection, atrial pressure briefly dips, the x descent. The atrium is finishing its relaxation, and the contracting ventricle pulls the AV valve ring downward, enlarging the atrium. After that, returning venous blood fills the atrium steadily and its pressure rises again. So while the ventricles empty, the atria store the blood that will rush in once the ventricles relax. Electrically, the ventricles were activated before ejection began, and no new impulse is needed to keep it going. During the later part of ejection the ventricular muscle repolarises (the T wave on an ECG), which sets up the relaxation that follows. Repolarisation does not switch contraction off instantly; force declines over the following moments. When ejection ends, some blood remains: the end-systolic volume (ESV). Section 7 shows how EDV and ESV give the stroke volume. Understanding Check — Section 5 During early ejection, ventricular volume is falling, yet ventricular pressure is still rising. How can both happen at once? Reveal explanation Volume falls because the aortic valve is open and blood is leaving. Pressure rises because the muscle is still increasing its force faster than blood escapes. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling. 6. Closing the outflow valves and isovolumetric relaxation Why does the aortic valve close? Section 5 left blood still coasting forward on momentum, even though ventricular pressure had dipped below aortic pressure. That forward flow now slows to a stop and briefly reverses. The tiny backflow pushes the aortic cusps together and they seal (the rule from Section 2). The lecture calls this short interval, from the end of forward ejection to semilunar valve closure, protodiastole. Source note: The lecture times protodiastole from the moment \"ventricular pressure starts falling\". In our account, pressure begins to fall during reduced ejection, while blood is still leaving. We therefore date protodiastole from the end of forward flow, as most physiology texts do. Either way, it ends when the semilunar valves close. Aortic valve closure contributes the aortic component of the second heart sound ( S2). The pulmonary valve closes the same way, slightly later ( Section 10). On the aortic pressure trace, closure leaves a small dip and rebound, the dicrotic notch. The dip is the brief backflow; the rebound is the stretched aortic wall recoiling against the now-closed valve. It is not a second contraction. Why doesn't filling restart straight away? The aortic valve is now shut, but ventricular pressure is still above atrial pressure, so the mitral valve is also shut. Both valves are closed again, and the ventricle is once more a sealed chamber. Why does pressure fall so steeply? This is Section 4 in reverse. The muscle relaxes as calcium is pumped out of the cytoplasm, so its tension drops. Volume cannot change, so the falling tension shows up entirely as falling pressure. Pressure drops steeply while volume stays at ESV. This is isovolumetric relaxation. ( → Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §5) Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↓ Volume = ESV Meanwhile, the atria have kept filling behind closed AV valves since the x descent ( Section 5). Atrial pressure reaches its peak now: the v wave. No atrial contraction is involved; it is simply blood accumulating. When ventricular pressure finally falls below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens. The stored atrial blood rushes in, atrial pressure drops (the y descent), and rapid filling begins. We are back at Section 3. Electrically, ventricular repolarisation (the T wave) happened during late ejection, before most of this relaxation. The T wave, aortic valve closure and mitral valve opening are three separate moments, in that order, not one event. Understanding Check — Section 6 1. The aortic valve has just closed. Ventricular pressure is falling quickly, yet the mitral valve is still shut. Why? A. Atrial pressure is falling faster than ventricular pressure at this moment B. Ventricular pressure is still higher than left atrial pressure at this moment C. The mitral valve opens only when the next P wave triggers atrial systole D. Ventricular repolarisation must finish before either valve is able to move Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The mitral valve opens only when LV pressure falls below LA pressure. Until then, the ventricle relaxes with both valves closed. 2. The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises steadily during ventricular systole. Which wave is this, and why does it happen? Reveal explanation The v wave. Venous blood keeps entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed, so blood accumulates and pressure rises. When the AV valve opens, that stored blood flows into the ventricle. 7. How much blood does the ventricle pump? A ventricle never empties completely. EDV is the volume just before contraction; ESV is what remains after ejection. The difference is the stroke volume (SV), the volume ejected in one beat. Stroke volume SV = EDV − ESV Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = 80 mL Why is some blood always left behind? Ejection stops when the pressure difference and the blood's momentum run out (Sections 5 and 6), not when the chamber is empty. The remaining ESV is a reserve: a stronger contraction can eject some of it. The ejection fraction (EF) is the share of EDV that is ejected: Ejection fraction EF = (SV) / (EDV) × 100% Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 ≈ 61.5% The lecture uses EDV ≈ 130 mL, SV ≈ 70–90 mL and EF ≈ 65% as typical values; small differences between rounded examples are not contradictions. EF is the fraction of that ventricle's end-diastolic blood ejected, not a fraction of total blood volume. Because it compares what is ejected with what was available, EF is widely used clinically as a measure of how well the ventricle contracts. A low EF suggests weakened contraction. ( → Heart Failure Physiology) Two ways to raise stroke volume. Since SV = EDV − ESV, the ventricle can eject more by: filling more (a larger EDV), or emptying more (a smaller ESV), for example through stronger contraction. Chapter 7 explains what controls each. ( → Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §3) Now trace the volume through one beat. It rises during filling and reaches EDV after atrial systole. It stays flat during isovolumetric contraction, then falls during ejection, steeply then gradually. It stays flat at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation, then rises again when the mitral valve opens. Stroke volume and ejection fraction in one picture A ventricle filled to its end-diastolic volume of 130 mL. After ejection, 50 mL remains (end-systolic volume). The 80 mL that left is the stroke volume, which is 61.5% of 130 mL: the ejection fraction. Understanding Check — Section 7 EDV stays at 140 mL, but a drug lowers ESV from 70 mL to 50 mL. What happens to SV and EF? Reveal explanation Before: SV = 140 − 70 = 70 mL; EF = 70 ÷ 140 = 50%. After: SV = 140 − 50 = 90 mL; EF = 90 ÷ 140 ≈ 64.3%. Emptying more completely raises both SV and EF, even with the same filling. 8. The whole cycle on one map Use this after reading the explanation, to rebuild each step from its cause. Start with atrial systole, where the heartbeat begins. What changes? Immediate consequence Next phase SA node fires; atria depolarise Atria contract; final filling to EDV Atrial systole Ventricles depolarise; ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure Mitral valve closes (S1) Isovolumetric contraction Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Aortic valve opens Rapid, then reduced ejection Forward flow stops and briefly reverses ( protodiastole) Aortic valve closes (S2) Isovolumetric relaxation Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure Mitral valve opens Rapid filling, then diastasis SA node fires again Next atrial systole Next cycle Compare the two isovolumetric phases Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Muscle Developing force Relaxing Mitral and aortic valves Both closed Both closed Ventricular pressure Rising Falling Ventricular volume Constant at EDV Constant at ESV Atria Relaxed, filling; c wave Relaxed, full; v wave peaks Ends when Aortic valve opens Mitral valve opens Volume is constant in both because neither valve is open. Pressure moves in opposite directions because the muscle is building force in one and relaxing in the other. 9. Reading the Wiggers diagram one trace at a time A Wiggers diagram lines up everything you have just learned on one time axis: the ECG, atrial, ventricular and aortic pressures, ventricular volume and heart sounds. It looks crowded, but every curve follows the events above. We will build it one trace at a time for the left heart. How to read it. Time runs from left to right, and every trace shares the same clock. A vertical line drawn anywhere cuts all the traces at the same instant: it tells you what the ECG, pressures, volume and sounds are doing at that moment. The key insight: valves move where pressure curves cross. By the rule in Section 2, a valve opens or closes exactly when the pressures on its two sides swap order. So on the diagram: The mitral valve closes and opens where the ventricular and atrial pressure curves cross. The aortic valve opens and closes where the ventricular and aortic pressure curves cross. Find those four crossings and you have found the four valve events. Between them, the volume curve tells you whether blood is entering, leaving or neither. The Wiggers diagram, built one step at a time (left heart) One heartbeat plus the start of the next, with the ECG, left atrial, left ventricular and aortic pressures, left ventricular volume and heart sounds on one time axis. The four valve events are where pressure curves cross: mitral closure and opening where the ventricular and atrial curves cross, aortic opening and closure where the ventricular and aortic curves cross. Labels show the P, QRS and T waves; the a, c and v waves and x and y descents; the dicrotic notch; EDV and ESV; and S1 and S2. Schematic; values approximate. Step 1: Atrial systole. The SA node fires and the atria depolarise: the P wave. Shortly afterwards the atria contract, producing the a wave on the atrial pressure trace and topping up the ventricle to EDV. P wave → atrial contraction → a wave → EDV: related, but in sequence, not simultaneous. (In a stiff ventricle, this final push can produce an extra sound, S4.) Step 2: Ventricular contraction begins. After the AV nodal delay, ventricular activation gives the QRS complex. Ventricular pressure then rises and crosses atrial pressure: the mitral valve closes ( S1), and the bulging valve makes the c wave. With both valves shut, the volume trace is flat: isovolumetric contraction. Step 3: Ejection. Ventricular pressure crosses aortic pressure and the aortic valve opens. From here the LV and aortic pressure traces run almost together, because they are connected through the open valve. The volume trace falls steeply ( rapid ejection), then more gently (reduced ejection). Meanwhile atrial pressure dips in the x descent ( Section 5), then begins to climb. Step 4: Relaxation. The T wave appears during reduced ejection as the ventricles repolarise. Forward flow stops and briefly reverses, the aortic valve closes ( S2), and the dicrotic notch marks the aortic trace. Ventricular pressure drops steeply while volume stays flat at ESV; atrial pressure reaches the v wave. Step 5: Filling resumes. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, atrial pressure falls ( y descent) and the volume trace rises again. (Rapid inflow can sometimes produce an extra sound, S3.) Read it in four passes Find QRS and mitral closure. QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: isovolumetric contraction. Find aortic opening. Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now falls as ejection begins. Find aortic closure. Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and S2. A small dicrotic notch appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation. Find mitral reopening. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve rises as filling resumes. Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure? Wave Cause a wave Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure. c wave Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium. x descent Atrial relaxation, then the AV valve ring pulled down during ejection. v wave Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed. y descent AV valve opens and stored atrial blood empties into the ventricle. Related: full JVP waves and descents → Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse, §4 · ECG timing → Electrocardiography, §5 · S1 and S2 → Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §2 · S3 and S4 → Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §4 Watch the mechanism SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts. Play animation DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0. Loaded only when selected. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted. Understanding Check — Section 9 1. On a Wiggers diagram, ventricular pressure rises sharply while ventricular volume stays flat. Which event ends this phase? A. The mitral valve opens as ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure B. The T wave appears and the ventricle begins its electrical recovery C. The aortic valve closes, leaving the dicrotic notch on aortic pressure D. The aortic valve opens as ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The flat volume with rising pressure is isovolumetric contraction. It ends when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure and blood can leave. 2. The T wave has begun, yet the aortic valve is still open and blood is still leaving. Is this possible? Reveal explanation Yes. Repolarisation happens during reduced ejection. Electrical recovery starts the decline in force, but force and forward flow do not stop instantly. The aortic valve closes only when flow reverses at the end of ejection. 10. Right versus left heart, S2 splitting and heart rate Same rules, lower pressures. Both ventricles are activated in the same beat and follow the same valve rule. The right ventricle pumps into the low-resistance pulmonary circulation, reaching about 25 mmHg; the left reaches about 120 mmHg. Even so, over time both pump almost exactly the same volume per minute. The two circuits are connected in series, so whatever the right ventricle sends through the lungs must come back to the left. Higher pressure does not mean more blood. ( → Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §2) Why the right ventricle ejects first. The lecture notes that the left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet right ventricular ejection begins first. Both points are correct. The right ventricle only has to exceed about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery, versus about 80 mmHg in the aorta, so its isovolumetric phase (contracting with all valves shut, before ejection) is much shorter and the pulmonary valve opens first. The SA node does not send separate beats to each side. Why S2 has two parts. S2 is made of A2 (aortic closure) and P2 (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During expiration they are close together and often sound single. During inspiration, P2 moves later and the gap widens: physiological splitting of S2. Two effects delay P2: More right-heart filling. Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject. Lower pulmonary impedance. The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later. A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly. Why S2 splits on inspiration Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale. Source note: The lecture says the valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the inspiratory widening. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it. Related: listening for splitting → Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §3 · breathing and venous return → Veins and Venous Return, §5 What happens when heart rate rises? At 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 0.8 s; at 150 beats/min, only 0.4 s. Both phases shorten, but not equally. Why does diastole lose most of the time? How long systole lasts is tied to how long the ventricular action potential lasts. Faster contraction and repolarisation shorten it a little, giving quicker ejection, as the lecture notes. But diastole is simply whatever time is left before the next beat, so it absorbs most of the cut. That matters, because diastole is when the ventricles fill. At very high rates, or when the ventricle relaxes poorly, there may not be enough time to fill. EDV then falls, and stroke volume falls with it. A faster heart rate does not automatically mean less stroke volume, though. During exercise, greater venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can maintain or even raise it. The heart also cannot be driven into a sustained, tetanic contraction, because its long refractory period forces relaxation between beats. Cycle length = 60 ÷ heart rate. At 75 beats/min it is 0.80 s (800 ms). This tool calculates total cycle length only; it does not model the exact split between systole and diastole. Related: cardiac output and rate → Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §2 · refractory period → Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §6 Coronary blood flow. The left ventricle's own muscle receives most of its blood flow during diastole. During systole the contracting muscle squeezes the vessels running through it, especially the deeper ones. So marked tachycardia hits twice: it shortens the time for coronary perfusion just as the faster-working heart needs more oxygen. ( → Coronary Circulation, §3 and §5) Understanding Check — Section 10 During inspiration, P2 occurs later than during expiration. Which explanation is best? A. More right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance prolong RV ejection B. Inspiration slows conduction in the right bundle, delaying RV activation C. Pulmonary arterial pressure rises on inspiration, so RV ejection starts late D. Less blood returns to the right atrium, so the tricuspid valve closes later Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Both inspiratory effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so the pulmonary valve closes later and the A2–P2 gap widens. 11. Understanding Check Predict first, then reveal. Each scenario changes one thing. Work out what happens in the rest of the heart before opening the answer. Scenario 1: Contracting, but no blood leaving. The left ventricle has just started contracting. Its pressure is rising, but its volume is not changing. What are the valves and the left atrium doing? Reveal explanation This is isovolumetric contraction. The mitral valve has just shut, and the aortic valve has not opened yet, because ventricular pressure is still below aortic pressure. With both shut, blood can't get in or out. Meanwhile the left atrium is relaxed and filling with blood from the lungs. Contracting and ejecting are not the same thing. Scenario 2: Relaxing, but no blood entering. Ejection has ended and the aortic valve has shut. Ventricular pressure is falling, but volume is not changing. Why is no blood coming in? Reveal explanation This is isovolumetric relaxation. Ventricular pressure is still higher than atrial pressure, so the mitral valve stays shut. The atrium is full, at its v wave. Once ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and blood rushes in. Scenario 3: The atria stop contracting properly. Does ventricular filling stop? Reveal explanation No. Most filling is passive: blood flows in whenever atrial pressure is higher than ventricular pressure. Only the final top-up from atrial contraction is lost. That loss matters most when the ventricle is stiff or the heart rate is fast. Scenario 4: Heart rate doubles. The rate rises from 75 to 150 beats/min. What may happen to filling, stroke volume and blood flow to the heart muscle? Reveal explanation Each beat now lasts 0.4 s instead of 0.8 s, and most of the lost time comes out of diastole. So there is less time to fill the ventricle and less time for blood to flow into the left ventricle's own muscle. Stroke volume may fall, but not always: during exercise, more venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can protect it. Final questions (answer and explanation shown straight after each response) Q1. A patient's ventricle becomes stiff and relaxes poorly. Atrial contraction is normal. What happens to ventricular filling? A. Passive filling rises, so the atrial kick becomes unnecessary for filling B. Filling stops completely, because the mitral valve can no longer open C. Passive filling falls, so the atrial kick supplies a larger share of filling D. Filling is unchanged, because filling depends only on the SA node firing Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A stiff ventricle doesn't stretch easily. As blood enters, its pressure rises quickly and soon matches atrial pressure, so passive inflow stops early. The atria's final push then makes up a bigger part of the filling. This is why losing atrial contraction hurts these patients most. Q2. During diastasis, before the next P wave, what is happening electrically? A. SA nodal cells are slowly depolarising towards their next threshold B. A slow excitation wave is spreading through the ventricular muscle C. The AV node is firing repeatedly to sustain passive ventricular filling D. Purkinje fibres are repolarising the atria in preparation for systole Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Passive filling needs no electrical impulse; the pressure difference moves the blood. Meanwhile the SA node's pacemaker cells slowly drift towards threshold. When they fire, the P wave and atrial contraction follow. Q3. The left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet the pulmonary valve opens before the aortic valve. Why? A. The right ventricle contracts more strongly than the left ventricle does B. The impulse reaches the right ventricle first by a faster route C. The pulmonary valve has thinner cusps that open at any pressure D. The right ventricle only has to exceed a much lower arterial pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The right ventricle only needs to beat about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery; the left must beat about 80 mmHg in the aorta. The right ventricle reaches its target sooner, so its valve opens first. Q4. Ventricles X and Y eject the same stroke volume, but X starts with a larger end-diastolic volume. Which conclusion follows? A. Both have the same ejection fraction, because their stroke volumes match B. X has the lower ejection fraction, because SV is a smaller share of its EDV C. X has the lower end-systolic volume, because it started with more blood D. Both have the same end-systolic volume, because they ejected equal volumes Show answer and explanation Answer: B. EF = SV ÷ EDV. If SV is the same but EDV is bigger, the fraction is smaller. X also has the larger ESV, since ESV = EDV − SV. Q5. Late in ejection, left ventricular pressure falls slightly below aortic pressure, yet blood keeps leaving for a moment. Why? A. The left atrium contracts and pushes blood on through the ventricle B. The aortic cusps actively propel blood forward as they begin closing C. Blood already moving forward carries momentum that takes time to stop D. The mitral valve opens and adds atrial pressure behind the outflow Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Moving blood can't stop instantly. It slows down, then briefly flows backwards, and that backflow is what shuts the aortic valve. Q6. An arrhythmia raises a resting patient's heart rate from 75 to 150 beats/min. Which change is most likely? A. Systole and diastole both halve, so filling time is proportionally kept B. Systole shortens most, so ejection is incomplete but filling is protected C. Coronary perfusion time rises, because the heart relaxes more often D. Diastole shortens most, so filling and coronary perfusion time both fall Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Systole can only shorten a little, so diastole loses most of the time. At rest, without the boosts that exercise brings, less filling time can lower EDV and stroke volume. The left ventricle's muscle also gets less time to receive blood. Q7. Which pairing of an atrial pressure wave with its cause is correct? A. c wave: the closed AV valve bulging back towards the atrium B. v wave: the atrium contracting against a closed AV valve C. a wave: venous blood filling the atrium while the AV valve is shut D. y descent: the atrium relaxing while the AV valve stays closed Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The c wave comes from the contracting ventricle pushing the shut valve back. The a wave is the atrium contracting, the v wave is the atrium filling up, and the y descent happens when the AV valve opens and the atrium empties. Q8. End-diastolic volume is 150 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. What are the stroke volume and ejection fraction? A. 90 mL and 40% B. 90 mL and 60% C. 60 mL and 60% D. 60 mL and 40% Show answer and explanation Answer: B. SV = 150 − 60 = 90 mL. EF = 90 ÷ 150 × 100 = 60%. 12. Chapter Essentials The atria and ventricles take turns. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. While the ventricles contract, the atria relax and refill. For about 0.4 s of a 0.8 s beat, both are relaxed together. One chain drives every phase: electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes. Valves are passive. Each opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front, and shuts when that reverses. Papillary muscles stop the AV valves turning inside out; they do not open them. Filling is mostly passive: rapid filling, then slower diastasis, then the atrial kick (lecture: 70% passive, 30% atrial; at rest often 10–30% atrial). The kick matters more with a stiff ventricle or a fast heart rate. Isovolumetric contraction: all four valves are shut, so the contracting ventricle's pressure shoots up while its volume stays at EDV. Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves contributes to S1. Ejection starts only when ventricular pressure beats arterial pressure: about 80 mmHg in the aorta, 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery. It is rapid first, then reduced, as the muscle weakens and the aorta fills. Isovolumetric relaxation: forward flow briefly reverses and shuts the aortic and pulmonary valves (S2, dicrotic notch). With all valves shut, pressure falls while volume stays at ESV. It ends when the mitral and tricuspid valves open. Volumes: SV = EDV − ESV and EF = SV ÷ EDV × 100%. Stroke volume rises by filling more (bigger EDV) or emptying more (smaller ESV). Atrial pressure waves: a = atria contract; c = shut AV valves bulge back; x = atria relax and the valve ring is pulled down; v = atria fill behind shut valves; y = AV valves open and the atria empty. Electrical comes before mechanical: the P wave before atrial contraction, the QRS before ventricular contraction, and the T wave during reduced ejection, before the outflow valves shut. On a Wiggers diagram, every valve event is a point where two pressure curves cross. Right heart and heart rate: the right heart follows the same rules at lower pressures, so the right ventricle ejects first; on inspiration P2 is delayed and S2 splitting widens. At faster heart rates diastole loses most of the time, so very high rates cut filling and coronary perfusion time."},{"slug":"heart-sounds","title":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"18-19 and 27-34","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds","title":"1. Why does the heart produce sounds?"},{"id":"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2","title":"2. Why does S1 occur before S2?"},{"id":"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration","title":"3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?"},{"id":"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4","title":"4. What causes S3 and S4?"},{"id":"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-loudness","title":"5. Why do heart sounds change in loudness?"},{"id":"6-what-makes-a-murmur","title":"6. What makes a murmur?"},{"id":"7-predict-a-murmur-s-timing-without-memorising-a-list","title":"7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list"},{"id":"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve","title":"8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?"},{"id":"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere","title":"9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?"},{"id":"10-understanding-check","title":"10. Understanding Check"},{"id":"11-chapter-essentials","title":"11. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Sounds and Murmurs</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds\">1. Why does the heart produce sounds?</h2>\n<p>With each beat, a healthy heart makes two main sounds, heard through a stethoscope as <strong>&quot;lub-dub&quot;</strong>. Each one is made by a pair of valves shutting.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Sound</th>\n  <th>Valves that shut</th>\n  <th>When it happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"s1\">S1</span>, &quot;lub&quot;</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Mitral</strong> and <strong>tricuspid</strong>, the inlet valves between the atria and the ventricles</td>\n  <td>As the ventricles start to contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span>, &quot;dub&quot;</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Aortic</strong> and <strong>pulmonary</strong>, the outlet valves into the aorta and the pulmonary artery</td>\n  <td>As the ventricles stop ejecting and start to relax</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does a shutting valve make a sound?</strong> The lecture puts it simply: the sounds are made by the valves closing. But what you hear is not the leaflets clapping together. When a valve snaps shut, the blood moving against it stops suddenly. That sudden stop makes the blood, the tightened valve leaflets and the nearby heart and artery walls vibrate. It's the same reason a water pipe bangs when a tap is turned off quickly. The vibrations travel through the chest wall to the stethoscope.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is there no sound when a valve opens?</strong> A normal valve opens gently as pressure builds behind it, and blood simply starts to flow smoothly. Nothing is stopped suddenly, so there is nothing to hear.</p>\n<p><strong>What the sounds tell you about timing.</strong> S1 marks the start of ventricular contraction, and S2 marks its end. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S1 → S2</strong> is <strong><span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">ventricular systole</span></strong>: the ventricles contract and eject.</li>\n<li><strong>S2 → next S1</strong> is <strong>ventricular diastole</strong>: the ventricles relax and fill.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At a resting heart rate, diastole is the longer phase. So the silence after &quot;dub&quot; is longer than the gap between &quot;lub&quot; and &quot;dub&quot;. That is how you tell S1 from S2 when listening. At fast heart rates the two gaps become similar, so clinicians feel the carotid pulse while listening: the pulse comes just after S1. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"heart-sound-timeline\"><figcaption>Watch and listen to one heartbeat</figcaption><p>A marker moves across one heartbeat. At S1 the mitral and tricuspid valves light up as they shut; at S2 the aortic and pulmonary valves light up. The span from S1 to S2 is ventricular systole; the longer span after S2 is ventricular diastole. Play gives a lub-dub in time with the marker, and a heart-rate control (60–150 beats/min) shows diastole shrinking much more than systole as the rate rises. Schematic sounds and timings, not a recording.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Hear a real heart.</strong> Listen for lub, dub, then the longer pause.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource audio-resource\" data-audio-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/HROgg_Louder.ogg\" data-audio-mp3=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d3/HROgg_Louder.ogg/HROgg_Louder.ogg.mp3\" data-media-title=\"Amplified normal heart sounds at approximately 70 beats per minute\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">REAL HEART · AMPLIFIED RECORDING · ABOUT 19 SECONDS</span><h3>Normal S1–S2 sequence</h3><p>A real chest, recorded through a stethoscope. Headphones work best.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-audio\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Listen</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HROgg_Louder.ogg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on Wikimedia Commons</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">Original recording: James Heilman, MD (<a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HROgg.ogg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC0</a>); amplified version: Natural Philo (<a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>). Audio loads only when selected.</p></div>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the valve rule → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Cardiac Cycle, §2</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2\">2. Why does S1 occur before S2?</h2>\n<p>Both sounds follow the valve rule from Chapter 4. A valve <strong>opens</strong> when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it. It <strong>shuts</strong> the moment that difference reverses. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §2</a>)</em> Follow one beat. The left and right sides do the same thing at the same time; the right side just works at lower pressures.</p>\n<p><strong>S1: the inlet valves shut as contraction begins.</strong> The electrical signal spreads through the ventricles (the <span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span> on the ECG), and the ventricles start to contract. Within moments, pressure in each ventricle rises above the pressure in its atrium. Blood starts to push back towards the atria, and that push snaps the <strong>mitral</strong> and <strong>tricuspid</strong> valves shut: <strong>S1</strong>. The outlet valves are still shut as well, so for a moment the ventricles squeeze with every valve closed. This is <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>. S1 therefore marks the <strong>start of ventricular systole</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>S2: the outlet valves shut as ejection ends.</strong> The ventricles keep ejecting until their force fades. Their pressure then falls below the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. Blood briefly starts to flow back towards the ventricles, and this snaps the <strong>aortic</strong> and <strong>pulmonary</strong> valves shut: <strong>S2</strong>. The ventricles now relax with every valve closed again. This is isovolumetric relaxation. As the lecture puts it, S2 marks the <strong>end of ventricular systole, which is the beginning of diastole</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p>In one line: <strong>S1 is when the ventricle overtakes the atrium; S2 is when the artery overtakes the ventricle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How long are they, and why do they sound different?</strong> The lecture gives about <strong>0.15 s for S1</strong> and <strong>0.12 s for S2</strong>. S1 is the longer, lower &quot;lub&quot;. S2 is the shorter, sharper &quot;dub&quot;: the aortic and pulmonary valves, and the stretched artery walls behind them, are tighter, so they vibrate at a higher pitch.</p>\n<p><strong>Do both valves in a pair shut at exactly the same moment?</strong> Almost, but not quite. The lecture says they close at about the same time. For S1 the gap is too small to hear, so S1 sounds single. For S2 the gap can widen enough to hear as two parts. That is S2 splitting, the subject of the next section.</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> where S1 and S2 sit on the full timeline → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">Cardiac Cycle, §9</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration\">3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?</h2>\n<p><strong>Why S2 has two parts.</strong> S2 is made of <strong>A2</strong> (aortic closure) and <strong>P2</strong> (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During <strong>expiration</strong> they are close together and often sound single. During <strong>inspiration</strong>, P2 moves later and the gap widens: <strong><span data-term=\"s2-splitting\">physiological splitting of S2</span></strong>. Two effects delay P2:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>More right-heart filling.</strong> Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower pulmonary impedance.</strong> The expanding lungs lower the <span data-term=\"pulmonary-impedance\">impedance</span> (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly.</p>\n<p>These two effects are what the lecture means by &quot;venous return&quot; and &quot;reduces pulmonary resistance… thus longer time for blood flow&quot;.</p>\n<p><strong>Why &quot;physiological&quot;?</strong> Because it is normal. It happens in healthy people with every breath in, and it fades with every breath out. What matters is the <strong>change with breathing</strong>: the gap widens on inspiration and narrows on expiration. A split that stays wide during expiration as well is not normal. That is clinical material beyond this lecture.</p>\n<p><strong>Where and how to hear it.</strong> P2 is a soft sound, so listen at the <strong>pulmonary area</strong>: the second left intercostal space, beside the sternum (see <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve\">§8</a>). Ask the person to breathe slowly. On a breath in, the single &quot;dub&quot; becomes a quick &quot;da-dub&quot;. On a breath out, it merges back into one.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"s2-timing\"><figcaption>Why S2 splits on inspiration</figcaption><p>Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Hear it.</strong> A simulator recording at the pulmonary area. The simulator breathes quickly, so S2 changes from one sound to two and back every few beats. Listen for the &quot;dub&quot; becoming a short &quot;da-dub&quot;. <em>(Recording: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, &quot;split S2, transient&quot;, CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended.)</em></p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget sound-clips\" data-widget=\"sound-clips\" data-clips=\"split-s2\"><p>Recording: split S2 that comes and goes with breathing, at the pulmonary area.</p></div>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says the aortic and pulmonary valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the widening on inspiration. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it.</aside>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the full timing picture → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Cardiac Cycle, §10</a> · breathing and venous return → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return\">Veins and Venous Return, §5</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Listening at the second left intercostal space, you hear S2 as two sounds when the person breathes in and as one sound when they breathe out. What does this most likely show?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Delayed closure of the mitral valve because more blood fills the left heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Early closure of the pulmonary valve because less blood returns to the right heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Normal widening of the A2–P2 gap as the right ventricle ejects for longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A stiff right ventricle that delays the start of right ventricular ejection</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Inspiration fills the right ventricle more and lowers the opposition to flow in the lungs. Both effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so P2 comes later. The mitral valve has nothing to do with S2, and the gap closing again on expiration shows this is normal, not disease.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> At the instant S2 is heard over the aortic area, which pressure change has just happened?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above left atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below left atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above aortic pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below aortic pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The aortic valve shuts when the artery overtakes the ventricle. A is the moment of S1 (the mitral valve shuts). C is the aortic valve opening, which makes no sound. B is the mitral valve opening at the end of isovolumetric relaxation, which is also silent.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> During inspiration, why does blood keep flowing into the pulmonary artery after right ventricular pressure has peaked?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The tricuspid valve stays open later during inspiration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The expanded lung vessels offer less opposition to flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The pulmonary valve has fewer cusps than the aortic valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Right ventricular contraction starts later on inspiration</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Expanded lungs lower the impedance of the pulmonary vessels, so forward flow continues for longer and the pulmonary valve shuts later. The tricuspid valve must be shut while the right ventricle ejects. Both outflow valves have three cusps. Inspiration does not delay the electrical activation of the right ventricle.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4\">4. What causes S3 and S4?</h2>\n<p>S1 and S2 are valves shutting. S3 and S4 are different: no valve shuts. They are <strong>filling sounds</strong>, made in diastole as blood rushes into the ventricle and is then suddenly slowed. The lecture says the same: both are due to ventricular filling. What separates them is <strong>what drives the rush</strong>, and that puts them at opposite ends of diastole.</p>\n<p>Recall how a ventricle fills <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §3</a>)</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rapid filling.</strong> The mitral and tricuspid valves open, and blood stored in the atria pours into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"diastasis\">Diastasis</span>.</strong> Filling slows to a trickle.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"atrial-kick\">Atrial kick</span>.</strong> The atria contract and push in the last portion of blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"s3\">S3</span>: at the end of rapid filling, early in diastole.</strong> About 0.1–0.2 s after S2, the rush of early filling reaches the limit of how far the ventricle wall will easily stretch. The inflowing blood is suddenly slowed, and the ventricle wall and blood vibrate. The result is a low, dull sound just after S2: <strong>&quot;lub-dub-dum&quot;</strong>. Many students remember the rhythm as <em>Ken-TUC-ky</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When it is normal:</strong> in children, young adults, athletes and pregnant women. Their ventricles relax quickly and fill very fast.</li>\n<li><strong>When it suggests a problem:</strong> with age, early filling normally slows and S3 disappears. In an older adult, an S3 usually means the ventricle is being overfilled or is enlarged and failing. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure\">→ Heart Failure</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"s4\">S4</span>: during the atrial kick, late in diastole.</strong> Just before S1, the atria contract and push their blood into the ventricles. If the ventricle is <strong>stiff</strong>, for example thickened, it resists this final push. The blood is stopped abruptly and the wall vibrates. The result is a low sound just before S1: <strong>&quot;da-lub-dub&quot;</strong>, or <em>TEN-nes-see</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It depends entirely on atrial contraction.</strong> It comes just after the <span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span> and goes with the <span data-term=\"a-wave\">a wave</span> in the atria. So it is <strong>absent in atrial fibrillation</strong>, where the atria quiver instead of contracting together.</li>\n<li><strong>When it suggests a problem:</strong> an S4 you can actually hear with a stethoscope in an adult usually means a stiff ventricle.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>S3</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>S4</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>When</td>\n  <td>Early diastole, just after S2</td>\n  <td>Late diastole, just before S1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>What drives the rush</td>\n  <td>Passive rapid filling</td>\n  <td>Atrial contraction (the atrial kick)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Rhythm</td>\n  <td>lub-dub-<strong>dum</strong> (<em>Ken-TUC-ky</em>)</td>\n  <td><strong>da</strong>-lub-dub (<em>TEN-nes-see</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Heard in healthy people?</td>\n  <td>Often, in the young</td>\n  <td>Rarely heard with a stethoscope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>In an older adult it usually means</td>\n  <td>An overfilled or failing ventricle</td>\n  <td>A stiff ventricle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Needs atrial contraction?</td>\n  <td>No</td>\n  <td>Yes, so it is lost in atrial fibrillation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>How to listen.</strong> Both are very low-pitched. Use the <strong>bell</strong> of the stethoscope, pressed lightly, at the <strong>apex</strong>, with the person turned onto their <strong>left side</strong>. This brings the apex closer to the chest wall.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"filling-sounds\"><figcaption>Where S3 and S4 sit in filling</figcaption><p>Ventricular volume through one diastole: a steep rise (rapid filling), a flat stretch (diastasis), then a small final rise (atrial kick). S3 is marked where the steep rise suddenly levels off; S4 is marked on the atrial kick; S2 and the next S1 are marked at either end. Hear S3 and Hear S4 play the matching recording while the marker lights up. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture lists both S3 and S4 as normal sounds due to rapid ventricular filling. We agree that both are filling sounds. Standard physiology texts (e.g. Guyton and Hall) separate them by cause: S3 comes from rapid passive filling, S4 from atrial contraction. They also note that S3 is often normal in young people, while an audible S4 in an adult usually points to a stiff ventricle. We teach that distinction because it explains why S4 disappears in atrial fibrillation.</aside>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the filling phases → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">Cardiac Cycle, §3</a> · S3 and S4 on the Wiggers diagram → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">Cardiac Cycle, §9</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-loudness\">5. Why do heart sounds change in loudness?</h2>\n<p>Each sound is made by a valve snapping shut. So anything that makes the snap <strong>harder</strong> makes the sound <strong>louder</strong>. For S1 and S2, two different things control how hard the snap is.</p>\n<p><strong>S1 depends on how wide open the mitral and tricuspid valves are when contraction starts.</strong> The lecture links S1's loudness to how the ventricle fills. Think of a door: one that is already nearly shut closes quietly, but one flung shut from wide open bangs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slow heart rate → soft S1.</strong> Diastole is long, so the ventricles finish filling well before they contract. As the inflow slows, the cusps of the mitral and tricuspid valves drift up towards each other. The lecture calls these &quot;floating valves&quot;. When the ventricles finally contract, the cusps only have a short way to go, so they close gently.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast heart rate → loud S1.</strong> Diastole is short, so the ventricles contract while blood is still rushing in and the cusps are still wide apart. Rising ventricular pressure slams them shut from fully open.</li>\n<li><strong>A stronger contraction also gives a louder S1</strong>, for example during exercise. Ventricular pressure rises faster, so the cusps are driven shut harder.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"s1-loudness\"><figcaption>Why a slow heart has a soft S1</figcaption><p>Two panels of the mitral valve at the instant the ventricle starts to contract. Slow heart: the cusps are already close together and travel a short distance (soft S1). Fast heart: the cusps are still wide open and travel a long distance (loud S1). Play animates both closing at the same moment, with a quiet and a loud lub. Schematic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>S2 depends on the pressure in the artery behind the valve.</strong> The aortic and pulmonary valves are pushed shut by the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. The higher that pressure, the harder and quicker the valve is driven shut. This is the lecture's &quot;brisk closure&quot;.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High blood pressure in the aorta</strong> (systemic hypertension) → loud <strong>A2</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>High pressure in the pulmonary artery</strong> (pulmonary hypertension) → loud <strong>P2</strong>. It is heard best at the pulmonary area, the second left intercostal space.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What a stethoscope picks up also depends on the route to the chest wall.</strong> Sounds are quieter through a thick chest wall, fluid around the heart, or over-inflated lungs. A badly damaged, stiff valve may also barely move, so its closing sound becomes softer even though the disease is severe. So <strong>loudness alone never tells you how serious a problem is</strong>. It is one clue to put alongside timing and where the sound is heard.</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> heart rate and the length of diastole → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Cardiac Cycle, §10</a> · contractility → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=7-what-changes-contractility\">Cardiac Output, §7</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A patient's S4 disappears when they develop atrial fibrillation. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricle becomes less stiff once the heart rate rises</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The mitral valve no longer opens fully during diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Rapid filling ends before the atria have time to empty</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The atria no longer contract together to push the last blood in</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> S4 is made when the atrial kick pushes blood into a stiff ventricle. In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver instead of contracting together, so there is no kick and no S4. The ventricle is just as stiff as before.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> An S3 is heard in a healthy 19-year-old athlete. Which explanation fits best?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Atrial contraction is pushing blood into a stiff left ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fast early filling of a ventricle that relaxes quickly is suddenly slowed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The aortic and pulmonary valves are closing at clearly different times</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The mitral valve is closing late because the heart rate is very slow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> S3 comes at the end of rapid filling. Young, quickly relaxing ventricles fill very fast, so the sudden slowing can be heard, and that is normal. A describes S4. C describes split S2. D mixes up S3 with S1.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In a person with a slow resting heart rate, S1 is soft. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricle contracts less forcefully because it is overfilled</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The aortic valve shuts gently because aortic pressure is low</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The mitral and tricuspid cusps have nearly floated shut already</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The atria contract weakly, so less blood crosses the valves</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A long diastole lets filling finish, and the cusps drift close together before contraction starts. They only have a short way to travel, so they close softly. A fuller ventricle actually contracts more strongly. The aortic valve makes S2, not S1.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A loud P2 at the second left intercostal space most likely points to:<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised pressure in the pulmonary artery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Raised pressure in the right atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Faster flow through the tricuspid valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Lower resistance in the lung vessels</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The higher the pressure behind the pulmonary valve, the harder it is driven shut, so P2 gets louder. D is what happens on inspiration: it delays P2 rather than making it louder.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-what-makes-a-murmur\">6. What makes a murmur?</h2>\n<p><strong>Normal blood flow is silent.</strong> Blood normally moves in smooth layers, sliding past one another. This is <strong><span data-term=\"laminar-flow\">laminar flow</span></strong>, the lecture's &quot;streamlined&quot; flow. Nothing tumbles or collides, so there is nothing to hear.</p>\n<p>A <strong><span data-term=\"murmur\">murmur</span></strong> is the sound of blood flow that has become <strong><span data-term=\"turbulent-flow\">turbulent</span></strong>: swirling and tumbling instead of sliding in layers. The swirls shake the heart and vessel walls for as long as the turbulence lasts. So a murmur is a longer <strong>whooshing</strong> sound, not a short thud like S1 or S2.</p>\n<p><strong>What makes flow turbulent?</strong> Flow tips into turbulence when it is <strong>fast</strong>, when the blood is <strong>thin</strong> (low viscosity), or when it is forced through a <strong>sudden narrowing</strong>. The lecture sums this up with the Reynolds number: the higher it is, the more likely turbulence becomes. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">→ Haemodynamics, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>The heart can make flow turbulent in three ways.</strong> These follow the lecture's list.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A narrowed valve (<span data-term=\"stenosis\">stenosis</span>).</strong> When the valve should be open, it opens only partly. Blood is squeezed through the small opening as a fast jet, which becomes turbulent beyond the valve. This murmur is made by <strong>forward</strong> flow.</li>\n<li><strong>A leaking valve (<span data-term=\"regurgitation\">regurgitation</span>, the lecture's &quot;incompetence&quot;).</strong> When the valve should be shut, it does not close fully. Blood is driven <strong>backwards</strong> through the gap as a fast jet. This is the lecture's &quot;backflow&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast flow through normal valves (a &quot;hyperdynamic&quot; circulation).</strong> The valves are healthy, but blood moves fast enough, or is thin enough, to become turbulent anyway. The lecture's example is <strong>anaemia</strong>, where thin blood and a raised cardiac output combine. Exercise, fever and pregnancy can do the same. These <strong><span data-term=\"flow-murmur\">flow murmurs</span></strong> do not mean the valves are damaged.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"murmur-figure\"><figcaption>Three ways to make a murmur</figcaption><p>Four panels. Normal: a fully open valve with smooth, parallel arrows (laminar: silent). Stenosis: a narrowed opening with a fast jet that breaks into swirls beyond it (forward jet). Regurgitation: a valve that should be shut, with a thin jet leaking backwards into swirls (backward jet). High flow: a normal open valve with many long, fast arrows becoming swirls (normal valve, fast flow). In every murmur there is turbulence somewhere; the questions are where it is and which way the blood is going.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The key question for any murmur:</strong> which way is the abnormal blood flowing, and is that happening in systole or diastole? That decides when you hear it, which is the subject of <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=7-predict-a-murmur-s-timing-without-memorising-a-list\">§7</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Murmur or bruit?</strong> Both come from the same mechanism. A murmur is heard over the heart; a bruit is heard over a blood vessel (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere\">§9</a>).</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> laminar flow, turbulence and the Reynolds number → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">Haemodynamics, §5</a> · anaemia and viscosity → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells\">Haemodynamics, §7</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-predict-a-murmur-s-timing-without-memorising-a-list\">7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list</h2>\n<p>The lecture's tip is to <strong>group the valves that close together</strong>. The aortic and pulmonary valves behave as one pair, and the mitral and tricuspid valves as the other. Then you only need to know what each pair should be doing in each phase:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>During</th>\n  <th>Mitral and tricuspid (inlet valves)</th>\n  <th>Aortic and pulmonary (outlet valves)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ventricular systole</strong> (S1 → S2)</td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>shut</strong></td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>open</strong>, for ejection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ventricular diastole</strong> (S2 → S1)</td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>open</strong>, for filling</td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>shut</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For any faulty valve, ask one question: <strong>when is the faulty valve supposed to be open (for stenosis) or shut (for a leak)?</strong> That phase is when the abnormal flow happens, so that is when you hear the murmur.</p>\n<p><strong>Systolic murmurs (between S1 and S2)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aortic or pulmonary stenosis.</strong> These valves should be open while the ventricles eject. A narrowed opening squeezes forward flow into a fast jet, so the murmur is <strong>systolic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation.</strong> These valves should be shut while the ventricles contract. If they leak, the contracting ventricle drives blood back into the atrium. The leak starts as soon as contraction starts and lasts until relaxation, so the murmur can fill <strong>all of systole</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diastolic murmurs (between S2 and the next S1)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mitral or tricuspid stenosis.</strong> These valves should be open while the ventricles fill. A narrowed opening obstructs filling, so the murmur is <strong>diastolic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation.</strong> These valves should be shut while the ventricles relax. The high pressure in the artery drives blood back into the ventricle, starting <strong>right after S2</strong>, so the murmur is <strong>diastolic</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>At a glance</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Valve pair</th>\n  <th>Stenosis (forward jet)</th>\n  <th>Regurgitation (backward jet)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aortic / pulmonary</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Systolic</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Diastolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Mitral / tricuspid</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Diastolic</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Systolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The pattern is a cross: each lesion falls in the opposite phase to its partner. You never need to memorise it. If you forget, ask the question again.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"murmur-predictor\"><figcaption>Murmur predictor</figcaption><p>Pick a valve (aortic, pulmonary, mitral, tricuspid) and a fault (narrowed or leaking). The tool then shows, one step at a time, when that valve should be open or shut, which way the abnormal jet flows, the phase on an S1–S2–S1 timeline, and the answer: systolic or diastolic. Hear an example plays a recording typical of that lesion where one exists. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0.</p></figure>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"dBwr2GZCmQM\" data-media-title=\"Heart Sounds and Heart Murmurs — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · ABOUT 4 MINUTES</span><h3>Watch it all together</h3><p>Alila Medical Media, <em>Heart Sounds and Heart Murmurs</em>. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dBwr2GZCmQM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. When is the murmur heard?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. During systole, between S1 and S2, as the ventricle ejects</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. During diastole, starting just after S2, as the ventricle relaxes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Late in diastole only, when the atria push blood into the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Throughout the cycle, because aortic pressure is always higher</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The aortic valve should be shut while the ventricle relaxes. Aortic pressure is far above ventricular pressure then, so blood leaks back from the moment the valve closes (S2). In systole the valve is open anyway, so there is no backflow to hear.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur, while mitral regurgitation causes a systolic one?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Stenosis raises aortic pressure in diastole; a leak lowers left atrial pressure in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Stenosis is heard at the apex only in diastole; a leak is heard at the base only in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Stenosis blocks filling when the valve should be open; a leak lets blood back when it should be shut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Stenosis delays mitral closure into diastole; a leak moves aortic closure earlier into systole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The mitral valve should be open during filling (diastole), so a narrowed one causes a diastolic murmur. It should be shut during contraction (systole), so a leaking one causes a systolic murmur.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient with severe anaemia has a soft systolic murmur, but all four valves are normal on a scan. What is the most likely cause?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The mitral valve leaks because the left ventricle is overfilled</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The aortic valve narrows because red cells clump on its cusps</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Low blood viscosity delays closure of the pulmonary valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Thin blood and a high cardiac output make ejection flow turbulent</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> This is a flow murmur: normal valves, but blood that is thin and fast enough to become turbulent during ejection. It does not mean the valves are damaged.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve\">8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?</h2>\n<p>The lecture says each valve is best heard over its <strong>surface marking</strong>: the spot on the chest wall where that valve's sound comes through most clearly. These four spots are the <strong><span data-term=\"auscultation-areas\">auscultation areas</span></strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Area</th>\n  <th>Where on the chest</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aortic</strong></td>\n  <td>Second <strong>right</strong> intercostal space, beside the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pulmonary</strong></td>\n  <td>Second <strong>left</strong> intercostal space, beside the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Tricuspid</strong></td>\n  <td>Lower <strong>left</strong> border of the sternum (fourth or fifth intercostal space)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Mitral</strong></td>\n  <td>The <strong>apex</strong>: fifth left intercostal space, in line with the middle of the clavicle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration teaching-illustration-portrait\" id=\"cvs05-auscultation\">\n<img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/chapter05/auscultation-areas.jpg\" alt=\"Front view of the rib cage and sternum with four labelled listening points: aortic at the second right intercostal space beside the sternum, pulmonary at the second left, tricuspid at the lower left sternal border, and mitral at the apex in the fifth left intercostal space.\" width=\"850\" height=\"793\">\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Where to place the stethoscope for each valve.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Rib cage drawing from Gray's <em>Anatomy of the Human Body</em> (1918), public domain; listening-point labels added. Supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why aren't these spots directly over the valves?</strong> The four valves sit close together behind the sternum, packed into a small area. Each valve's sound travels <strong>in the direction its blood flows</strong>, and comes out where that chamber or vessel lies closest to the chest wall:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The aortic valve sits behind the left side of the sternum. Its sound is carried up the aorta, which comes nearest the surface at the <strong>right</strong> second intercostal space.</li>\n<li>The mitral valve also sits behind the sternum. Its sound is carried down through the left ventricle to the <strong>apex</strong>, where the ventricle touches the chest wall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A practical tip for telling S1 from S2:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S1 is loudest at the apex</strong>, where the mitral sound comes out.</li>\n<li><strong>S2 is loudest at the base</strong>, the aortic and pulmonary areas at the top of the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Moving the stethoscope between the two and hearing which sound gets louder tells you which is which.</p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget sound-clips\" data-widget=\"sound-clips\" data-clips=\"normal-apex,normal-base\"><p>Two recordings of the same normal heart sounds: at the apex, where S1 is louder, and at the base, where S2 is louder.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Bell or diaphragm?</strong> The <strong>diaphragm</strong>, pressed firmly, picks up higher-pitched sounds: S1, S2 and most murmurs. The <strong>bell</strong>, resting lightly, picks up low-pitched sounds such as S3, S4 and the rumble of mitral stenosis.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture slide gives \"AT PM\" beside the listening areas. We could not confirm what this abbreviation means, so we present the four standard areas without it. Valve surface positions follow standard anatomy texts.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere\">9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?</h2>\n<p>Turbulence is not limited to the heart valves. Wherever blood is forced through a narrowing, takes a short cut, or flows unusually fast, it can become turbulent and make a sound (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=6-what-makes-a-murmur\">§6</a>). Heard over a vessel, this sound is called a <strong><span data-term=\"bruit\">bruit</span></strong>. The lecture lists these examples:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Condition</th>\n  <th>Why the flow becomes turbulent</th>\n  <th>When it is heard</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</strong>: the fetal channel between the aorta and pulmonary artery stays open after birth</td>\n  <td>Aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure in <strong>both</strong> systole and diastole, so blood crosses all the time</td>\n  <td><strong>Continuous</strong>, through S2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</strong>: a hole between the ventricles</td>\n  <td>When the ventricles contract, left ventricular pressure far exceeds right, so blood jets through the hole</td>\n  <td><strong>Systolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arteriovenous (AV) fistula</strong>: a direct channel from an artery to a vein</td>\n  <td>High-pressure arterial blood rushes into a low-pressure vein, skipping the capillaries</td>\n  <td>Continuous bruit over the site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aneurysm</strong>: a ballooned section of artery</td>\n  <td>The sudden widening disturbs smooth flow (not every aneurysm makes a sound)</td>\n  <td>A bruit over the aneurysm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Goitre</strong> (enlarged thyroid) with high blood flow</td>\n  <td>Increased blood flow through the gland</td>\n  <td>A bruit over the thyroid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Anaemia</strong></td>\n  <td>Thin, fast blood (see §6)</td>\n  <td>A soft systolic flow murmur</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The rule is the same everywhere: <strong>find the pressure difference that drives the abnormal flow, and when it exists.</strong> That tells you when the sound is heard. The PDA murmur is continuous for exactly this reason: the pressure difference between the aorta and pulmonary artery never reverses.</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the ductus arteriosus and why it should close after birth → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/fetal?section=what-closes-after-delivery\">Fetal Circulation, \"What closes after delivery?\"</a> · bruits over narrowed arteries → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">Haemodynamics, §5</a> · Korotkoff sounds in blood-pressure measurement → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/blood-pressure?section=7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure\">Blood Pressure, §7</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-understanding-check\">10. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><em>Questions on the whole chapter. The earlier checks cover Sections 1–7 in detail.</em></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A patient has a patent ductus arteriosus, an open channel joining the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Why is its murmur continuous through systole and diastole?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ductus closes in systole and reopens in diastole each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure through the whole cycle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pulmonary pressure stays above aortic pressure through the whole cycle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Both the aortic and pulmonary valves leak during every diastole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Flow crosses the ductus whenever aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure, and in this patient that is true throughout the cycle. Because the driving difference never reverses, the turbulence and the sound never stop.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is the mitral valve best heard at the apex rather than directly over the valve?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The mitral valve actually lies beneath the fifth left intercostal space</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The sternum blocks all sound, so no valve can be heard over it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its sound is carried to where the left ventricle meets the chest wall</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The apex is the only area where the bell can be applied lightly</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The mitral valve sits behind the sternum, but its sound travels with the blood into the left ventricle and comes out at the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A 70-year-old has a stiff, thickened left ventricle. Which extra sound would you most expect, and why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. An S4 just before S1, from the atrial kick</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. An S3 just after S2, from rapid early filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A loud S1 at the apex, from floating cusps</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A split S2 on expiration, from late P2</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A stiff ventricle resists the final push of blood from the atria, which makes S4. An S3 points to an overfilled or failing ventricle. Floating cusps make S1 <em>soft</em>, not loud. A split that persists on expiration is not explained by stiffness.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Listening at the apex, you hear \"lub-dub\" with a long pause after \"dub\". Then you move to the second right intercostal space, and the second sound becomes the louder one. What have you confirmed?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The second sound is S1, because S1 is loudest over the aortic area</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The first sound is S1, because S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The patient has aortic regurgitation, because S2 is loud at the base</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The second sound is split, because it changes when you move position</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> S1 is loudest at the apex and S2 at the base. Together with the long pause after S2 (diastole), this confirms which sound is which. A louder S2 at the base is normal, not a sign of disease.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"11-chapter-essentials\">11. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>S1 (&quot;lub&quot;)</strong>: the mitral and tricuspid valves shutting as the ventricles start to contract. It marks the start of ventricular systole.</li>\n<li><strong>S2 (&quot;dub&quot;)</strong>: the aortic and pulmonary valves shutting as ejection ends. It marks the start of ventricular diastole.</li>\n<li><strong>The sound is not the leaflets clapping</strong>: it is the vibration caused when moving blood is suddenly stopped. Normal valves open silently.</li>\n<li><strong>S1 → S2 is systole; S2 → S1 is diastole.</strong> At rest the pause after S2 is longer. S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiological splitting of S2</strong>: on inspiration, more right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance delay P2, so A2 and P2 separate. They merge again on expiration.</li>\n<li><strong>S3</strong>: early diastole, at the end of rapid filling. Often normal in the young; in older adults it suggests an overfilled or failing ventricle.</li>\n<li><strong>S4</strong>: late diastole, from the atrial kick into a stiff ventricle. It needs atrial contraction, so it is absent in atrial fibrillation.</li>\n<li><strong>Loudness</strong>: a slow heart gives a soft S1 (the cusps have floated nearly shut). High aortic or pulmonary pressure gives a loud A2 or P2. Loudness alone does not measure severity.</li>\n<li><strong>Murmurs are turbulent flow</strong>: from a narrowed valve (forward jet), a leaking valve (backward jet), or fast, thin blood through normal valves (flow murmur, e.g. anaemia).</li>\n<li><strong>Timing rule</strong>: ask when the faulty valve should be open (stenosis) or shut (leak). Aortic/pulmonary stenosis and mitral/tricuspid regurgitation are <strong>systolic</strong>. Mitral/tricuspid stenosis and aortic/pulmonary regurgitation are <strong>diastolic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Auscultation areas</strong>: aortic at the second right intercostal space; pulmonary at the second left; tricuspid at the lower left sternal border; mitral at the apex. These are where each valve's sound comes out, not where the valves sit.</li>\n<li><strong>Bruits</strong> follow the same rule as murmurs: find the pressure difference driving the abnormal flow, and when it exists. A PDA is continuous because aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure all the time.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs 1. Why does the heart produce sounds? With each beat, a healthy heart makes two main sounds, heard through a stethoscope as \"lub-dub\". Each one is made by a pair of valves shutting. Sound Valves that shut When it happens S1, \"lub\" Mitral and tricuspid, the inlet valves between the atria and the ventricles As the ventricles start to contract S2, \"dub\" Aortic and pulmonary, the outlet valves into the aorta and the pulmonary artery As the ventricles stop ejecting and start to relax Why does a shutting valve make a sound? The lecture puts it simply: the sounds are made by the valves closing. But what you hear is not the leaflets clapping together. When a valve snaps shut, the blood moving against it stops suddenly. That sudden stop makes the blood, the tightened valve leaflets and the nearby heart and artery walls vibrate. It's the same reason a water pipe bangs when a tap is turned off quickly. The vibrations travel through the chest wall to the stethoscope. Why is there no sound when a valve opens? A normal valve opens gently as pressure builds behind it, and blood simply starts to flow smoothly. Nothing is stopped suddenly, so there is nothing to hear. What the sounds tell you about timing. S1 marks the start of ventricular contraction, and S2 marks its end. So: S1 → S2 is ventricular systole: the ventricles contract and eject. S2 → next S1 is ventricular diastole: the ventricles relax and fill. At a resting heart rate, diastole is the longer phase. So the silence after \"dub\" is longer than the gap between \"lub\" and \"dub\". That is how you tell S1 from S2 when listening. At fast heart rates the two gaps become similar, so clinicians feel the carotid pulse while listening: the pulse comes just after S1. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §1) Watch and listen to one heartbeat A marker moves across one heartbeat. At S1 the mitral and tricuspid valves light up as they shut; at S2 the aortic and pulmonary valves light up. The span from S1 to S2 is ventricular systole; the longer span after S2 is ventricular diastole. Play gives a lub-dub in time with the marker, and a heart-rate control (60–150 beats/min) shows diastole shrinking much more than systole as the rate rises. Schematic sounds and timings, not a recording. Hear a real heart. Listen for lub, dub, then the longer pause. REAL HEART · AMPLIFIED RECORDING · ABOUT 19 SECONDS Normal S1–S2 sequence A real chest, recorded through a stethoscope. Headphones work best. Listen Open on Wikimedia Commons Original recording: James Heilman, MD ( CC0); amplified version: Natural Philo ( CC BY-SA 3.0). Audio loads only when selected. Related: the valve rule → Cardiac Cycle, §2 2. Why does S1 occur before S2? Both sounds follow the valve rule from Chapter 4. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it. It shuts the moment that difference reverses. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §2) Follow one beat. The left and right sides do the same thing at the same time; the right side just works at lower pressures. S1: the inlet valves shut as contraction begins. The electrical signal spreads through the ventricles (the QRS complex on the ECG), and the ventricles start to contract. Within moments, pressure in each ventricle rises above the pressure in its atrium. Blood starts to push back towards the atria, and that push snaps the mitral and tricuspid valves shut: S1. The outlet valves are still shut as well, so for a moment the ventricles squeeze with every valve closed. This is isovolumetric contraction. S1 therefore marks the start of ventricular systole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §4) S2: the outlet valves shut as ejection ends. The ventricles keep ejecting until their force fades. Their pressure then falls below the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. Blood briefly starts to flow back towards the ventricles, and this snaps the aortic and pulmonary valves shut: S2. The ventricles now relax with every valve closed again. This is isovolumetric relaxation. As the lecture puts it, S2 marks the end of ventricular systole, which is the beginning of diastole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §6) In one line: S1 is when the ventricle overtakes the atrium; S2 is when the artery overtakes the ventricle. How long are they, and why do they sound different? The lecture gives about 0.15 s for S1 and 0.12 s for S2. S1 is the longer, lower \"lub\". S2 is the shorter, sharper \"dub\": the aortic and pulmonary valves, and the stretched artery walls behind them, are tighter, so they vibrate at a higher pitch. Do both valves in a pair shut at exactly the same moment? Almost, but not quite. The lecture says they close at about the same time. For S1 the gap is too small to hear, so S1 sounds single. For S2 the gap can widen enough to hear as two parts. That is S2 splitting, the subject of the next section. Related: where S1 and S2 sit on the full timeline → Cardiac Cycle, §9 3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration? Why S2 has two parts. S2 is made of A2 (aortic closure) and P2 (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During expiration they are close together and often sound single. During inspiration, P2 moves later and the gap widens: physiological splitting of S2. Two effects delay P2: More right-heart filling. Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject. Lower pulmonary impedance. The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later. A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly. These two effects are what the lecture means by \"venous return\" and \"reduces pulmonary resistance… thus longer time for blood flow\". Why \"physiological\"? Because it is normal. It happens in healthy people with every breath in, and it fades with every breath out. What matters is the change with breathing: the gap widens on inspiration and narrows on expiration. A split that stays wide during expiration as well is not normal. That is clinical material beyond this lecture. Where and how to hear it. P2 is a soft sound, so listen at the pulmonary area: the second left intercostal space, beside the sternum (see §8). Ask the person to breathe slowly. On a breath in, the single \"dub\" becomes a quick \"da-dub\". On a breath out, it merges back into one. Why S2 splits on inspiration Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale. Hear it. A simulator recording at the pulmonary area. The simulator breathes quickly, so S2 changes from one sound to two and back every few beats. Listen for the \"dub\" becoming a short \"da-dub\". (Recording: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, \"split S2, transient\", CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended.) Recording: split S2 that comes and goes with breathing, at the pulmonary area. Source note: The lecture says the aortic and pulmonary valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the widening on inspiration. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it. Related: the full timing picture → Cardiac Cycle, §10 · breathing and venous return → Veins and Venous Return, §5 Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Listening at the second left intercostal space, you hear S2 as two sounds when the person breathes in and as one sound when they breathe out. What does this most likely show? A. Delayed closure of the mitral valve because more blood fills the left heart B. Early closure of the pulmonary valve because less blood returns to the right heart C. Normal widening of the A2–P2 gap as the right ventricle ejects for longer D. A stiff right ventricle that delays the start of right ventricular ejection Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inspiration fills the right ventricle more and lowers the opposition to flow in the lungs. Both effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so P2 comes later. The mitral valve has nothing to do with S2, and the gap closing again on expiration shows this is normal, not disease. 2. At the instant S2 is heard over the aortic area, which pressure change has just happened? A. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above left atrial pressure B. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below left atrial pressure C. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above aortic pressure D. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below aortic pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The aortic valve shuts when the artery overtakes the ventricle. A is the moment of S1 (the mitral valve shuts). C is the aortic valve opening, which makes no sound. B is the mitral valve opening at the end of isovolumetric relaxation, which is also silent. 3. During inspiration, why does blood keep flowing into the pulmonary artery after right ventricular pressure has peaked? A. The tricuspid valve stays open later during inspiration B. The expanded lung vessels offer less opposition to flow C. The pulmonary valve has fewer cusps than the aortic valve D. Right ventricular contraction starts later on inspiration Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Expanded lungs lower the impedance of the pulmonary vessels, so forward flow continues for longer and the pulmonary valve shuts later. The tricuspid valve must be shut while the right ventricle ejects. Both outflow valves have three cusps. Inspiration does not delay the electrical activation of the right ventricle. 4. What causes S3 and S4? S1 and S2 are valves shutting. S3 and S4 are different: no valve shuts. They are filling sounds, made in diastole as blood rushes into the ventricle and is then suddenly slowed. The lecture says the same: both are due to ventricular filling. What separates them is what drives the rush, and that puts them at opposite ends of diastole. Recall how a ventricle fills ( → Cardiac Cycle, §3): Rapid filling. The mitral and tricuspid valves open, and blood stored in the atria pours into the ventricles. Diastasis. Filling slows to a trickle. Atrial kick. The atria contract and push in the last portion of blood. S3: at the end of rapid filling, early in diastole. About 0.1–0.2 s after S2, the rush of early filling reaches the limit of how far the ventricle wall will easily stretch. The inflowing blood is suddenly slowed, and the ventricle wall and blood vibrate. The result is a low, dull sound just after S2: \"lub-dub-dum\". Many students remember the rhythm as Ken-TUC-ky. When it is normal: in children, young adults, athletes and pregnant women. Their ventricles relax quickly and fill very fast. When it suggests a problem: with age, early filling normally slows and S3 disappears. In an older adult, an S3 usually means the ventricle is being overfilled or is enlarged and failing. ( → Heart Failure) S4: during the atrial kick, late in diastole. Just before S1, the atria contract and push their blood into the ventricles. If the ventricle is stiff, for example thickened, it resists this final push. The blood is stopped abruptly and the wall vibrates. The result is a low sound just before S1: \"da-lub-dub\", or TEN-nes-see. It depends entirely on atrial contraction. It comes just after the P wave and goes with the a wave in the atria. So it is absent in atrial fibrillation, where the atria quiver instead of contracting together. When it suggests a problem: an S4 you can actually hear with a stethoscope in an adult usually means a stiff ventricle. S3 S4 When Early diastole, just after S2 Late diastole, just before S1 What drives the rush Passive rapid filling Atrial contraction (the atrial kick) Rhythm lub-dub- dum ( Ken-TUC-ky) da -lub-dub ( TEN-nes-see) Heard in healthy people? Often, in the young Rarely heard with a stethoscope In an older adult it usually means An overfilled or failing ventricle A stiff ventricle Needs atrial contraction? No Yes, so it is lost in atrial fibrillation How to listen. Both are very low-pitched. Use the bell of the stethoscope, pressed lightly, at the apex, with the person turned onto their left side. This brings the apex closer to the chest wall. Where S3 and S4 sit in filling Ventricular volume through one diastole: a steep rise (rapid filling), a flat stretch (diastasis), then a small final rise (atrial kick). S3 is marked where the steep rise suddenly levels off; S4 is marked on the atrial kick; S2 and the next S1 are marked at either end. Hear S3 and Hear S4 play the matching recording while the marker lights up. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended. Source note: The lecture lists both S3 and S4 as normal sounds due to rapid ventricular filling. We agree that both are filling sounds. Standard physiology texts (e.g. Guyton and Hall) separate them by cause: S3 comes from rapid passive filling, S4 from atrial contraction. They also note that S3 is often normal in young people, while an audible S4 in an adult usually points to a stiff ventricle. We teach that distinction because it explains why S4 disappears in atrial fibrillation. Related: the filling phases → Cardiac Cycle, §3 · S3 and S4 on the Wiggers diagram → Cardiac Cycle, §9 5. Why do heart sounds change in loudness? Each sound is made by a valve snapping shut. So anything that makes the snap harder makes the sound louder. For S1 and S2, two different things control how hard the snap is. S1 depends on how wide open the mitral and tricuspid valves are when contraction starts. The lecture links S1's loudness to how the ventricle fills. Think of a door: one that is already nearly shut closes quietly, but one flung shut from wide open bangs. Slow heart rate → soft S1. Diastole is long, so the ventricles finish filling well before they contract. As the inflow slows, the cusps of the mitral and tricuspid valves drift up towards each other. The lecture calls these \"floating valves\". When the ventricles finally contract, the cusps only have a short way to go, so they close gently. Fast heart rate → loud S1. Diastole is short, so the ventricles contract while blood is still rushing in and the cusps are still wide apart. Rising ventricular pressure slams them shut from fully open. A stronger contraction also gives a louder S1, for example during exercise. Ventricular pressure rises faster, so the cusps are driven shut harder. Why a slow heart has a soft S1 Two panels of the mitral valve at the instant the ventricle starts to contract. Slow heart: the cusps are already close together and travel a short distance (soft S1). Fast heart: the cusps are still wide open and travel a long distance (loud S1). Play animates both closing at the same moment, with a quiet and a loud lub. Schematic. S2 depends on the pressure in the artery behind the valve. The aortic and pulmonary valves are pushed shut by the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. The higher that pressure, the harder and quicker the valve is driven shut. This is the lecture's \"brisk closure\". High blood pressure in the aorta (systemic hypertension) → loud A2. High pressure in the pulmonary artery (pulmonary hypertension) → loud P2. It is heard best at the pulmonary area, the second left intercostal space. What a stethoscope picks up also depends on the route to the chest wall. Sounds are quieter through a thick chest wall, fluid around the heart, or over-inflated lungs. A badly damaged, stiff valve may also barely move, so its closing sound becomes softer even though the disease is severe. So loudness alone never tells you how serious a problem is. It is one clue to put alongside timing and where the sound is heard. Related: heart rate and the length of diastole → Cardiac Cycle, §10 · contractility → Cardiac Output, §7 Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. A patient's S4 disappears when they develop atrial fibrillation. What is the best explanation? A. The ventricle becomes less stiff once the heart rate rises B. The mitral valve no longer opens fully during diastole C. Rapid filling ends before the atria have time to empty D. The atria no longer contract together to push the last blood in Show answer and explanation Answer: D. S4 is made when the atrial kick pushes blood into a stiff ventricle. In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver instead of contracting together, so there is no kick and no S4. The ventricle is just as stiff as before. 2. An S3 is heard in a healthy 19-year-old athlete. Which explanation fits best? A. Atrial contraction is pushing blood into a stiff left ventricle B. Fast early filling of a ventricle that relaxes quickly is suddenly slowed C. The aortic and pulmonary valves are closing at clearly different times D. The mitral valve is closing late because the heart rate is very slow Show answer and explanation Answer: B. S3 comes at the end of rapid filling. Young, quickly relaxing ventricles fill very fast, so the sudden slowing can be heard, and that is normal. A describes S4. C describes split S2. D mixes up S3 with S1. 3. In a person with a slow resting heart rate, S1 is soft. What is the best explanation? A. The ventricle contracts less forcefully because it is overfilled B. The aortic valve shuts gently because aortic pressure is low C. The mitral and tricuspid cusps have nearly floated shut already D. The atria contract weakly, so less blood crosses the valves Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A long diastole lets filling finish, and the cusps drift close together before contraction starts. They only have a short way to travel, so they close softly. A fuller ventricle actually contracts more strongly. The aortic valve makes S2, not S1. 4. A loud P2 at the second left intercostal space most likely points to: A. Raised pressure in the pulmonary artery B. Raised pressure in the right atrium C. Faster flow through the tricuspid valve D. Lower resistance in the lung vessels Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The higher the pressure behind the pulmonary valve, the harder it is driven shut, so P2 gets louder. D is what happens on inspiration: it delays P2 rather than making it louder. 6. What makes a murmur? Normal blood flow is silent. Blood normally moves in smooth layers, sliding past one another. This is laminar flow, the lecture's \"streamlined\" flow. Nothing tumbles or collides, so there is nothing to hear. A murmur is the sound of blood flow that has become turbulent: swirling and tumbling instead of sliding in layers. The swirls shake the heart and vessel walls for as long as the turbulence lasts. So a murmur is a longer whooshing sound, not a short thud like S1 or S2. What makes flow turbulent? Flow tips into turbulence when it is fast, when the blood is thin (low viscosity), or when it is forced through a sudden narrowing. The lecture sums this up with the Reynolds number: the higher it is, the more likely turbulence becomes. ( → Haemodynamics, §5) The heart can make flow turbulent in three ways. These follow the lecture's list. A narrowed valve ( stenosis). When the valve should be open, it opens only partly. Blood is squeezed through the small opening as a fast jet, which becomes turbulent beyond the valve. This murmur is made by forward flow. A leaking valve ( regurgitation, the lecture's \"incompetence\"). When the valve should be shut, it does not close fully. Blood is driven backwards through the gap as a fast jet. This is the lecture's \"backflow\". Fast flow through normal valves (a \"hyperdynamic\" circulation). The valves are healthy, but blood moves fast enough, or is thin enough, to become turbulent anyway. The lecture's example is anaemia, where thin blood and a raised cardiac output combine. Exercise, fever and pregnancy can do the same. These flow murmurs do not mean the valves are damaged. Three ways to make a murmur Four panels. Normal: a fully open valve with smooth, parallel arrows (laminar: silent). Stenosis: a narrowed opening with a fast jet that breaks into swirls beyond it (forward jet). Regurgitation: a valve that should be shut, with a thin jet leaking backwards into swirls (backward jet). High flow: a normal open valve with many long, fast arrows becoming swirls (normal valve, fast flow). In every murmur there is turbulence somewhere; the questions are where it is and which way the blood is going. The key question for any murmur: which way is the abnormal blood flowing, and is that happening in systole or diastole? That decides when you hear it, which is the subject of §7. Murmur or bruit? Both come from the same mechanism. A murmur is heard over the heart; a bruit is heard over a blood vessel ( §9). Related: laminar flow, turbulence and the Reynolds number → Haemodynamics, §5 · anaemia and viscosity → Haemodynamics, §7 7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list The lecture's tip is to group the valves that close together. The aortic and pulmonary valves behave as one pair, and the mitral and tricuspid valves as the other. Then you only need to know what each pair should be doing in each phase: During Mitral and tricuspid (inlet valves) Aortic and pulmonary (outlet valves) Ventricular systole (S1 → S2) Should be shut Should be open, for ejection Ventricular diastole (S2 → S1) Should be open, for filling Should be shut For any faulty valve, ask one question: when is the faulty valve supposed to be open (for stenosis) or shut (for a leak)? That phase is when the abnormal flow happens, so that is when you hear the murmur. Systolic murmurs (between S1 and S2) Aortic or pulmonary stenosis. These valves should be open while the ventricles eject. A narrowed opening squeezes forward flow into a fast jet, so the murmur is systolic. Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation. These valves should be shut while the ventricles contract. If they leak, the contracting ventricle drives blood back into the atrium. The leak starts as soon as contraction starts and lasts until relaxation, so the murmur can fill all of systole. Diastolic murmurs (between S2 and the next S1) Mitral or tricuspid stenosis. These valves should be open while the ventricles fill. A narrowed opening obstructs filling, so the murmur is diastolic. Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation. These valves should be shut while the ventricles relax. The high pressure in the artery drives blood back into the ventricle, starting right after S2, so the murmur is diastolic. At a glance Valve pair Stenosis (forward jet) Regurgitation (backward jet) Aortic / pulmonary Systolic Diastolic Mitral / tricuspid Diastolic Systolic The pattern is a cross: each lesion falls in the opposite phase to its partner. You never need to memorise it. If you forget, ask the question again. Murmur predictor Pick a valve (aortic, pulmonary, mitral, tricuspid) and a fault (narrowed or leaking). The tool then shows, one step at a time, when that valve should be open or shut, which way the abnormal jet flows, the phase on an S1–S2–S1 timeline, and the answer: systolic or diastolic. Hear an example plays a recording typical of that lesion where one exists. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. OPTIONAL VIDEO · ABOUT 4 MINUTES Watch it all together Alila Medical Media, Heart Sounds and Heart Murmurs. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. Understanding Check: Sections 6–7 1. A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. When is the murmur heard? A. During systole, between S1 and S2, as the ventricle ejects B. During diastole, starting just after S2, as the ventricle relaxes C. Late in diastole only, when the atria push blood into the ventricle D. Throughout the cycle, because aortic pressure is always higher Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The aortic valve should be shut while the ventricle relaxes. Aortic pressure is far above ventricular pressure then, so blood leaks back from the moment the valve closes (S2). In systole the valve is open anyway, so there is no backflow to hear. 2. Why does mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur, while mitral regurgitation causes a systolic one? A. Stenosis raises aortic pressure in diastole; a leak lowers left atrial pressure in systole B. Stenosis is heard at the apex only in diastole; a leak is heard at the base only in systole C. Stenosis blocks filling when the valve should be open; a leak lets blood back when it should be shut D. Stenosis delays mitral closure into diastole; a leak moves aortic closure earlier into systole Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The mitral valve should be open during filling (diastole), so a narrowed one causes a diastolic murmur. It should be shut during contraction (systole), so a leaking one causes a systolic murmur. 3. A patient with severe anaemia has a soft systolic murmur, but all four valves are normal on a scan. What is the most likely cause? A. The mitral valve leaks because the left ventricle is overfilled B. The aortic valve narrows because red cells clump on its cusps C. Low blood viscosity delays closure of the pulmonary valve D. Thin blood and a high cardiac output make ejection flow turbulent Show answer and explanation Answer: D. This is a flow murmur: normal valves, but blood that is thin and fast enough to become turbulent during ejection. It does not mean the valves are damaged. 8. Where do we listen to each heart valve? The lecture says each valve is best heard over its surface marking: the spot on the chest wall where that valve's sound comes through most clearly. These four spots are the auscultation areas: Area Where on the chest Aortic Second right intercostal space, beside the sternum Pulmonary Second left intercostal space, beside the sternum Tricuspid Lower left border of the sternum (fourth or fifth intercostal space) Mitral The apex: fifth left intercostal space, in line with the middle of the clavicle Figure 01. Where to place the stethoscope for each valve. Image credit Rib cage drawing from Gray's Anatomy of the Human Body (1918), public domain; listening-point labels added. Supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project. Why aren't these spots directly over the valves? The four valves sit close together behind the sternum, packed into a small area. Each valve's sound travels in the direction its blood flows, and comes out where that chamber or vessel lies closest to the chest wall: The aortic valve sits behind the left side of the sternum. Its sound is carried up the aorta, which comes nearest the surface at the right second intercostal space. The mitral valve also sits behind the sternum. Its sound is carried down through the left ventricle to the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall. A practical tip for telling S1 from S2: S1 is loudest at the apex, where the mitral sound comes out. S2 is loudest at the base, the aortic and pulmonary areas at the top of the heart. Moving the stethoscope between the two and hearing which sound gets louder tells you which is which. Two recordings of the same normal heart sounds: at the apex, where S1 is louder, and at the base, where S2 is louder. Bell or diaphragm? The diaphragm, pressed firmly, picks up higher-pitched sounds: S1, S2 and most murmurs. The bell, resting lightly, picks up low-pitched sounds such as S3, S4 and the rumble of mitral stenosis. Source note: The lecture slide gives \"AT PM\" beside the listening areas. We could not confirm what this abbreviation means, so we present the four standard areas without it. Valve surface positions follow standard anatomy texts. 9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere? Turbulence is not limited to the heart valves. Wherever blood is forced through a narrowing, takes a short cut, or flows unusually fast, it can become turbulent and make a sound ( §6). Heard over a vessel, this sound is called a bruit. The lecture lists these examples: Condition Why the flow becomes turbulent When it is heard Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA): the fetal channel between the aorta and pulmonary artery stays open after birth Aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure in both systole and diastole, so blood crosses all the time Continuous, through S2 Ventricular septal defect (VSD): a hole between the ventricles When the ventricles contract, left ventricular pressure far exceeds right, so blood jets through the hole Systolic Arteriovenous (AV) fistula: a direct channel from an artery to a vein High-pressure arterial blood rushes into a low-pressure vein, skipping the capillaries Continuous bruit over the site Aneurysm: a ballooned section of artery The sudden widening disturbs smooth flow (not every aneurysm makes a sound) A bruit over the aneurysm Goitre (enlarged thyroid) with high blood flow Increased blood flow through the gland A bruit over the thyroid Anaemia Thin, fast blood (see §6) A soft systolic flow murmur The rule is the same everywhere: find the pressure difference that drives the abnormal flow, and when it exists. That tells you when the sound is heard. The PDA murmur is continuous for exactly this reason: the pressure difference between the aorta and pulmonary artery never reverses. Related: the ductus arteriosus and why it should close after birth → Fetal Circulation, \"What closes after delivery?\" · bruits over narrowed arteries → Haemodynamics, §5 · Korotkoff sounds in blood-pressure measurement → Blood Pressure, §7 10. Understanding Check Questions on the whole chapter. The earlier checks cover Sections 1–7 in detail. 1. A patient has a patent ductus arteriosus, an open channel joining the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Why is its murmur continuous through systole and diastole? A. The ductus closes in systole and reopens in diastole each beat B. Aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure through the whole cycle C. Pulmonary pressure stays above aortic pressure through the whole cycle D. Both the aortic and pulmonary valves leak during every diastole Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Flow crosses the ductus whenever aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure, and in this patient that is true throughout the cycle. Because the driving difference never reverses, the turbulence and the sound never stop. 2. Why is the mitral valve best heard at the apex rather than directly over the valve? A. The mitral valve actually lies beneath the fifth left intercostal space B. The sternum blocks all sound, so no valve can be heard over it C. Its sound is carried to where the left ventricle meets the chest wall D. The apex is the only area where the bell can be applied lightly Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The mitral valve sits behind the sternum, but its sound travels with the blood into the left ventricle and comes out at the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall. 3. A 70-year-old has a stiff, thickened left ventricle. Which extra sound would you most expect, and why? A. An S4 just before S1, from the atrial kick B. An S3 just after S2, from rapid early filling C. A loud S1 at the apex, from floating cusps D. A split S2 on expiration, from late P2 Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A stiff ventricle resists the final push of blood from the atria, which makes S4. An S3 points to an overfilled or failing ventricle. Floating cusps make S1 soft, not loud. A split that persists on expiration is not explained by stiffness. 4. Listening at the apex, you hear \"lub-dub\" with a long pause after \"dub\". Then you move to the second right intercostal space, and the second sound becomes the louder one. What have you confirmed? A. The second sound is S1, because S1 is loudest over the aortic area B. The first sound is S1, because S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base C. The patient has aortic regurgitation, because S2 is loud at the base D. The second sound is split, because it changes when you move position Show answer and explanation Answer: B. S1 is loudest at the apex and S2 at the base. Together with the long pause after S2 (diastole), this confirms which sound is which. A louder S2 at the base is normal, not a sign of disease. 11. Chapter Essentials S1 (\"lub\"): the mitral and tricuspid valves shutting as the ventricles start to contract. It marks the start of ventricular systole. S2 (\"dub\"): the aortic and pulmonary valves shutting as ejection ends. It marks the start of ventricular diastole. The sound is not the leaflets clapping: it is the vibration caused when moving blood is suddenly stopped. Normal valves open silently. S1 → S2 is systole; S2 → S1 is diastole. At rest the pause after S2 is longer. S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base. Physiological splitting of S2: on inspiration, more right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance delay P2, so A2 and P2 separate. They merge again on expiration. S3: early diastole, at the end of rapid filling. Often normal in the young; in older adults it suggests an overfilled or failing ventricle. S4: late diastole, from the atrial kick into a stiff ventricle. It needs atrial contraction, so it is absent in atrial fibrillation. Loudness: a slow heart gives a soft S1 (the cusps have floated nearly shut). High aortic or pulmonary pressure gives a loud A2 or P2. Loudness alone does not measure severity. Murmurs are turbulent flow: from a narrowed valve (forward jet), a leaking valve (backward jet), or fast, thin blood through normal valves (flow murmur, e.g. anaemia). Timing rule: ask when the faulty valve should be open (stenosis) or shut (leak). Aortic/pulmonary stenosis and mitral/tricuspid regurgitation are systolic. Mitral/tricuspid stenosis and aortic/pulmonary regurgitation are diastolic. Auscultation areas: aortic at the second right intercostal space; pulmonary at the second left; tricuspid at the lower left sternal border; mitral at the apex. These are where each valve's sound comes out, not where the valves sit. Bruits follow the same rule as murmurs: find the pressure difference driving the abnormal flow, and when it exists. A PDA is continuous because aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure all the time."},{"slug":"ecg","title":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms","group":"Heart","source":"Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf","pages":"1-14; extra diagrams Circulation 3 35-38","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record","title":"1. What does an ECG actually record?"},{"id":"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards","title":"2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?"},{"id":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","title":"3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?"},{"id":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","title":"4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?"},{"id":"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events","title":"5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events"},{"id":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","title":"6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?"},{"id":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","title":"7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing"},{"id":"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary","title":"8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record\">1. What does an ECG actually record?</h2>\n<p>Every heartbeat is preceded by cardiac <strong>electrical activation</strong>. As myocardial cells depolarise and repolarise, electrical potential differences are produced and conducted through body tissues. Electrodes on the skin detect these differences. The <strong><span data-term=\"ecg\">electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG)</span></strong> is a recording of this electrical activity over time.</p>\n<p>An ECG <strong>does not directly measure</strong> contraction strength, pressure, stroke volume or cardiac output. For example, a QRS complex signifies <em>ventricular depolarisation</em>, not the actual volume of blood being ejected. Electrical activity can be present without an effective pulse.</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-concept-line\" aria-label=\"Electrical and mechanical sequence\"><span>Electrical activation</span><b>→</b><span>Myocardial contraction</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Blood movement</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why does this distinction matter?</strong> An ECG can show organised electrical complexes even if ventricular contraction produces little or no effective circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards\">2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?</h2>\n<p>An <strong>electrode</strong> is a physical contact on the patient's skin. A <strong>lead</strong> is a particular electrical view calculated from electrode potentials. Different leads view the same heart from different directions, so the same heartbeat can produce different shapes across leads.</p>\n<p>As activation spreads through the myocardium, we can represent its combined direction and magnitude by a changing electrical <strong><span data-term=\"ecg-vector\">vector</span></strong>. The ECG lead detects the part of that vector directed along its positive recording direction.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>A depolarisation wave travels…</th>\n  <th>Recorded tendency</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Towards</strong> a lead's positive direction</td>\n  <td>Positive (upward) deflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Away from</strong> its positive direction</td>\n  <td>Negative (downward) deflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Roughly <strong>perpendicular</strong></td>\n  <td>Small or biphasic deflection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For example, much of normal ventricular depolarisation is directed inferiorly and towards the patient's left. Since Lead II views the heart in that general direction, its QRS complex is often predominantly upright.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> These rules describe <strong>depolarisation</strong>. Repolarisation has a different electrical polarity: a repolarisation wave travelling towards a positive electrode tends to produce a negative deflection. The normal ventricular T wave can therefore be upright even though it represents repolarisation, because the spread and polarity of repolarisation differ from depolarisation.</p>\n<p>An upward deflection is <strong>not</strong> a drawing of the heart moving upwards—it is the electrical signal's projection onto that lead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads\">3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</h2>\n<p>The standard <strong>12-lead ECG</strong> uses <strong>10 physical electrodes</strong>: four on the limbs and six on the chest. Their potentials are combined to produce twelve electrical views.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lead group</th>\n  <th style=\"text-align:right\">Views</th>\n  <th>Names</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Bipolar limb</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">3</td>\n  <td>I, II, III</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Augmented limb</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">3</td>\n  <td>aVR, aVL, aVF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Precordial (chest)</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">6</td>\n  <td>V1–V6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>right-leg (RL)</strong> electrode mainly helps suppress electrical interference; it is not a thirteenth lead. The six limb leads observe the heart in the <strong>frontal plane</strong>, while the six chest leads add views in the <strong>horizontal plane</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The three bipolar limb leads</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lead</th>\n  <th>Negative electrode</th>\n  <th>Positive electrode</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>I</strong></td>\n  <td>Right arm (RA)</td>\n  <td>Left arm (LA)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>II</strong></td>\n  <td>Right arm (RA)</td>\n  <td>Left leg (LL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>III</strong></td>\n  <td>Left arm (LA)</td>\n  <td>Left leg (LL)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These directions form <strong>Einthoven's triangle</strong>. Their simultaneous voltages obey <strong>Einthoven's law</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Einthoven's law</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"V_II = V_I + V_III\">V_II = V_I + V_III</span></div>\n\n<p>For instance, if Lead I measures +0.5 mV and Lead III measures +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II should measure <strong>+0.8 mV</strong> (ignoring recording error).</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-einthoven\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" alt=\"Diagram of Einthoven triangle connecting arm and leg lead positions with bipolar Leads I, II and III.\" width=\"992\" height=\"744\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Kychot · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>The augmented limb leads</h3>\n<p><strong>aVR</strong> views towards the right arm, <strong>aVL</strong> towards the left arm and <strong>aVF</strong> towards the left leg/foot. Each uses one positive limb electrode and a reference derived from the other limb electrodes. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector usually moves away from aVR, so its QRS complex is commonly predominantly negative.</p>\n<h3>The six chest leads</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Electrode</th>\n  <th>Standard position</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V1</strong></td>\n  <td>Fourth right intercostal space, right sternal border</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V2</strong></td>\n  <td>Fourth left intercostal space, left sternal border</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V3</strong></td>\n  <td>Midway between V2 and V4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V4</strong></td>\n  <td>Fifth left intercostal space, midclavicular line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V5</strong></td>\n  <td>Anterior axillary line, horizontal with V4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V6</strong></td>\n  <td>Midaxillary line, horizontal with V4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For accurate placement, locate <strong>V4 before V3</strong>, then put V3 midway between V2 and V4. Incorrect placement can alter the tracing and create misleading findings.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-precordial\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" alt=\"Anterior torso and rib cage with six labelled ECG chest electrodes V1 to V6 in their correct anatomical positions.\" width=\"287\" height=\"252\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?</h2>\n<p>The ECG tracing is made up of <strong>waves</strong>, <strong>segments</strong> and <strong>intervals</strong>. These words are not interchangeable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wave:</strong> A deflection associated with a change in the heart's summed electrical activity (e.g. P or T).</li>\n<li><strong>Segment:</strong> The part <strong>between</strong> specified waves (e.g. PR segment or ST segment).</li>\n<li><strong>Interval:</strong> A measured span including <strong>one or more waves</strong> and often a segment (e.g. PR or QT interval).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-signal\" id=\"ecg-normal-wave\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" alt=\"Labeled normal ECG with P wave, QRS complex, T wave, PR segment and interval, ST segment and QT interval.\" width=\"512\" height=\"512\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>P wave — Atrial depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The SA node initiates an impulse, which spreads through atrial myocardium. The <strong>P wave represents atrial myocardial depolarisation</strong>, not merely the firing of the tiny SA node. Atrial contraction follows. Representative an illustrative normal P-wave duration of <strong>0.08–0.10 seconds</strong>.</p>\n<h3>PR interval — Time from atrial to ventricular depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>PR interval</strong> starts at the <strong>beginning of the P wave</strong> and ends at the <strong>beginning of QRS</strong>. It is normally about <strong>0.12–0.20 seconds</strong>. It includes atrial depolarisation and conduction through the AV node and His–Purkinje system up to the start of ventricular activation. <strong>The whole PR interval is not simply the AV nodal delay.</strong></p>\n<p>In contrast, the <strong>PR segment</strong> runs from the <strong>end</strong> of P to the <strong>start</strong> of QRS. Keep the starting points distinct when answering exam questions.</p>\n<h3>QRS complex — Ventricular depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>QRS complex</strong> represents ventricular depolarisation. Ventricular muscle mass is much greater than atrial mass, so its summed signal is typically larger than the P wave. The lecturer quotes a QRS duration of approximately <strong>0.06–0.10 seconds</strong>; a duration <strong>under 0.12 seconds</strong> is a common broad normal clinical criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Q</strong> is the first negative deflection before a positive R; <strong>R</strong> is a positive deflection; <strong>S</strong> is a negative deflection after an R. A particular lead need not show all three letters.</p>\n<p><strong>Why don't we see atrial repolarisation separately?</strong> It usually overlaps with ventricular depolarisation, and its smaller signal is masked by QRS—not absent.</p>\n<h3>ST segment — A nearly flat line during continued ventricular electrical activity</h3>\n<p>The <strong>ST segment</strong> extends from the <strong>end of QRS (J point)</strong> to the <strong>beginning of the T wave</strong>. During much of this interval, ventricular myocardial cells are in relatively similar depolarised states, so the lead often detects little net voltage difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Flat does not mean inactive.</strong> The ST segment roughly corresponds to the ventricular action-potential plateau, not to a period when the cells have returned to rest. ST elevation and depression can have clinical importance, including myocardial ischaemia, but require appropriate context.</p>\n<h3>T wave — Ventricular repolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>T wave</strong> reflects the spread of ventricular repolarisation. Repolarisation is not instantaneous and may give a broader waveform than QRS. The normal T-wave direction reflects both the spatial sequence of recovery and the polarity of repolarisation.</p>\n<h3>U wave — Sometimes present, origin uncertain</h3>\n<p>A small <strong>U wave</strong> may follow the T wave. The lecture calls it the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation; this is a traditional teaching description. <strong>Its precise source is still debated</strong>, so we should not present a specific proposed mechanism as settled fact.</p>\n<h3>QT interval — Ventricular activation through recovery</h3>\n<p>The <strong>QT interval</strong> extends from the <strong>start of QRS to the end of the T wave</strong>. It includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. Representative an illustrative range of <strong>0.2–0.4 seconds</strong>, noting that QT varies with heart rate. Its duration shortens or lengthens with heart rate, so a single fixed normal QT value is misleading.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplementary: heart-rate correction.</strong> Bazett's formula is a commonly taught approximation, although it is less reliable at unusually low or high heart rates.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Bazett's QT correction</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"QT_c = (QT) / (√(RR))\">QT_c = (QT) / (√(RR))</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Use QT and RR measurements in seconds; interpret the result in clinical context.</span></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events\">5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>ECG feature</th>\n  <th>Electrical event</th>\n  <th>Mechanical connection</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>P wave</td>\n  <td>Atrial depolarisation</td>\n  <td>Atrial contraction follows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>PR interval</td>\n  <td>Atrial-to-ventricular activation time</td>\n  <td>Includes the AV delay that supports final filling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>QRS complex</td>\n  <td>Ventricular depolarisation</td>\n  <td>Ventricular contraction follows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>ST segment</td>\n  <td>Much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised</td>\n  <td>Sustained ventricular contraction/ejection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>T wave</td>\n  <td>Ventricular repolarisation</td>\n  <td>Mechanical relaxation develops, but not instantaneously</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This connects directly with Chapter 04's Wiggers diagram: <strong>the ECG measures the electrical changes; the Wiggers diagram also shows pressure, volume and valve events</strong>. The electrical and mechanical curves are connected but are not interchangeable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage\">6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer's standard settings are <strong>25 mm/s</strong> (paper speed) and <strong>10 mm/mV</strong> (voltage gain).</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-calibration-mini\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"ECG paper calibration\"><div class=\"ecg-paper-sample\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><div><strong>At 25 mm/s</strong><p>1 small 1-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.04 s</strong><br>1 large 5-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.20 s</strong></p><p><strong>At 10 mm/mV:</strong> 1 small vertical square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong>; 1 large vertical square = <strong>0.5 mV</strong>.</p></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why is this true?</strong> At 25 mm/s, the paper moves 25 mm in one second. Each 1-mm horizontal square therefore spans <strong>1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s</strong>; five such squares span <strong>0.20 s</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse axes:</strong> Horizontal spacing measures time; vertical height measures voltage. A taller QRS does not mean a longer QRS duration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing\">7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing</h2>\n<p>For a <strong>regular rhythm</strong>, measure the R–R interval. Since 60 seconds make one minute:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">General relationship</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"HR = (60) / (RRin seconds)\">HR = (60) / (RRin seconds)</span></div>\n\n<p>At 25 mm/s, the common shortcuts follow from 1500 small or 300 large squares passing per minute:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">25 mm/s · regular rhythm</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"HR = (1500) / (N_small squares)\">HR = (1500) / (N_small squares)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves.</span></div>\n\n<h3>Worked example: the lecturer's 22 small squares</h3>\n<p>If an R–R interval spans <strong>22 small squares</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>RR duration = 22 × 0.04 s = 0.88 s</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Heart rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min</strong>, equivalently 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68.</p>\n<p><strong>Important observation about slide 14:</strong> Not all its R–R intervals appear equal. The calculation <strong>1500 ÷ 22</strong> gives the rate represented by <em>that particular interval</em>. It should not automatically be called the average rate for the whole irregular tracing.</p>\n<h3>What changes if the rhythm is irregular?</h3>\n<p>When R–R intervals vary, a single pair of R waves is insufficient to estimate the average. One simple method is to count ventricular complexes in a <strong>6-second strip</strong> and multiply by <strong>10</strong> (supplementary).</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> 8 QRS complexes in 6 seconds → approximate average <strong>80 beats/min</strong>.</p>\n<h3>What if paper speed is 50 mm/s?</h3>\n<p>Each small horizontal square then represents <strong>0.02 s</strong>, so the shortcuts become <strong>3000 ÷ small squares</strong> or <strong>600 ÷ large squares</strong>. Always check the paper speed before using a shortcut.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary\">8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)</h2>\n<p>The lecture's central focus is ECG fundamentals, not a full arrhythmia course. Still, an initial rhythm assessment connects the tracing to its purpose.</p>\n<p>Ask four questions: <strong>Is the ventricular rate appropriate? Are the R–R intervals regular? Are consistent P waves present before QRS? Is conduction through PR and QRS appropriate?</strong></p>\n<p>A normal sinus rhythm generally shows P waves of consistent shape, with an appropriate relationship between each P and the following QRS and a reasonably regular rhythm. For healthy adults at rest, <strong>60–100 beats/min</strong> is a common reference range, though slower rates can be normal in particular circumstances.</p>\n<h3>Recognition of AV conduction disturbance</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Pattern</th>\n  <th>Interpretation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>PR &gt;0.20 s</strong>, every P followed by QRS</td>\n  <td>First-degree AV block</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Some P waves <strong>not followed by QRS</strong></td>\n  <td>Second-degree AV block</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>P waves and QRS complexes follow <strong>independent rhythms</strong></td>\n  <td>Complete (third-degree) AV block</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong> These patterns concern how the atrial impulse is delayed or fails to reach ventricular myocardium. Their detailed subtypes are supplementary and require additional ECG practice.</p>\n<p>A normal-looking ECG cannot guarantee effective cardiac output: <strong>electrical activity does not prove adequate mechanical pumping</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. How can skin electrodes record activity generated inside the heart?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Cardiac depolarisation and repolarisation produce electrical potential differences that are conducted through body tissues. Electrodes detect differences between skin locations, so the ECG can record the combined electrical activity without touching the myocardium. It records electricity, not the strength of contraction.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. Why do ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Electrodes are recording contacts; leads are calculated electrical views.</strong> Four limb electrodes and six precordial electrodes provide three bipolar limb views, three augmented limb views and six chest views. The right-leg electrode helps reduce interference rather than producing another lead.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. If a depolarisation vector moves away from the positive direction of Lead II, what deflection is expected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Predominantly negative (downward).</strong> A lead displays the projection of the heart's electrical vector onto its positive recording direction. Depolarisation moving away from that direction generates a negative deflection; moving towards it produces a positive one.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Explain what Einthoven's law means if Leads I and III measure +0.5 and +0.3 mV at the same instant.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Lead II = +0.8 mV</strong>, because V<sub>II</sub> = V<sub>I</sub> + V<sub>III</sub>. This relationship follows from how the three bipolar limb leads use the right-arm, left-arm and left-leg electrode potentials. The values must be compared at the same instant.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. The QRS complex is larger than the P wave. Which electrical event does each represent, and why is the difference expected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>QRS is ventricular depolarisation; P is atrial depolarisation.</strong> The ventricles have greater muscle mass, so the summed surface electrical signal during ventricular activation is usually much larger. The small atrial repolarisation signal is normally hidden within QRS.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. A student measures the PR segment and calls it the PR interval. What is missing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The P wave itself.</strong> The PR segment starts at the end of P and ends at the beginning of QRS; the PR interval starts at the <em>beginning</em> of P. Therefore the PR interval includes atrial depolarisation plus subsequent conduction time, not just AV nodal delay.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. Why can the ST segment lie near the baseline while most ventricular cells remain depolarised?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>During much of the ST segment, ventricular regions are in relatively similar depolarised states. The lead therefore detects only a small net electrical potential difference. A flat segment does <strong>not</strong> indicate that the ventricle has become electrically inactive.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are separated by 22 small squares. Calculate the rate.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Each small horizontal square lasts 0.04 s. The RR interval is 22 × 0.04 = <strong>0.88 s</strong>. Rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ <strong>68 beats/min</strong> (also 1500 ÷ 22). If other R–R intervals differ, this is only the beat-to-beat rate for the chosen interval.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Why does the 1500 rule need adjustment at a paper speed of 50 mm/s?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The paper travels twice as far per second, so each small square lasts 0.02 s rather than 0.04 s. There are now 3000 small squares per minute; the corresponding regular-rhythm shortcut is <strong>3000 ÷ small squares</strong> (or 600 ÷ large squares).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. An irregular ECG has eight QRS complexes in six seconds. Estimate its average ventricular rate.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Approximately 80 beats/min.</strong> Eight beats in six seconds correspond to 8 × (60 ÷ 6) = 80 beats/min. For an irregular rhythm, counting complexes over a longer interval is more representative than applying the 1500 rule to one R–R interval.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Each P wave is followed by QRS, but the PR interval is 0.24 seconds. What pattern does this suggest?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>First-degree AV block.</strong> PR is prolonged beyond 0.20 s, yet each atrial impulse still conducts to the ventricles. The finding indicates delayed AV transmission, not dropped beats or complete AV dissociation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Can a regular ECG tracing prove that the ventricles are pumping enough blood?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> An ECG measures electrical activity. Electrical impulses may occur without effective myocardial contraction or sufficient stroke volume, so perfusion requires assessment of the pulse, blood pressure and clinical state.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>ECG:</strong> Records electrical potential differences detected at the body surface, not blood pressure or contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>Twelve leads, ten electrodes:</strong> Three bipolar limb, three augmented limb and six chest leads.</li>\n<li><strong>Vector rule:</strong> Depolarisation towards a positive lead produces a positive deflection; away produces a negative deflection.</li>\n<li><strong>Lead placement:</strong> I, II and III use limb-electrode differences; V1–V6 have specific chest landmarks.</li>\n<li><strong>P wave:</strong> Atrial myocardial depolarisation; <strong>QRS:</strong> ventricular depolarisation; <strong>T wave:</strong> ventricular repolarisation.</li>\n<li><strong>PR interval:</strong> Start of P to start of QRS, normally about 0.12–0.20 s; do not confuse with PR segment or pure AV nodal delay.</li>\n<li><strong>QRS duration:</strong> About 0.06–0.10 s in the lecturer's examples; often less than 0.12 s clinically.</li>\n<li><strong>ST segment:</strong> Ventricular myocardium remains largely depolarised; a flat tracing does not mean inactivity.</li>\n<li><strong>QT interval:</strong> Start of QRS to end of T; varies with heart rate. U-wave origin remains unsettled.</li>\n<li><strong>Calibration at 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV:</strong> Small square 0.04 s horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically; large square 0.20 s.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart rate:</strong> Regular rhythm HR = 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares at 25 mm/s; use longer strips to estimate irregular rhythms.</li>\n<li><strong>Basic rhythm assessment:</strong> Rate, regularity, P waves, PR and QRS; ECG electrical activity does not guarantee cardiac output.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms 1. What does an ECG actually record? Every heartbeat is preceded by cardiac electrical activation. As myocardial cells depolarise and repolarise, electrical potential differences are produced and conducted through body tissues. Electrodes on the skin detect these differences. The electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG) is a recording of this electrical activity over time. An ECG does not directly measure contraction strength, pressure, stroke volume or cardiac output. For example, a QRS complex signifies ventricular depolarisation, not the actual volume of blood being ejected. Electrical activity can be present without an effective pulse. Electrical activation → Myocardial contraction → Pressure changes → Blood movement Why does this distinction matter? An ECG can show organised electrical complexes even if ventricular contraction produces little or no effective circulation. 2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards? An electrode is a physical contact on the patient's skin. A lead is a particular electrical view calculated from electrode potentials. Different leads view the same heart from different directions, so the same heartbeat can produce different shapes across leads. As activation spreads through the myocardium, we can represent its combined direction and magnitude by a changing electrical vector. The ECG lead detects the part of that vector directed along its positive recording direction. A depolarisation wave travels… Recorded tendency Towards a lead's positive direction Positive (upward) deflection Away from its positive direction Negative (downward) deflection Roughly perpendicular Small or biphasic deflection For example, much of normal ventricular depolarisation is directed inferiorly and towards the patient's left. Since Lead II views the heart in that general direction, its QRS complex is often predominantly upright. Important: These rules describe depolarisation. Repolarisation has a different electrical polarity: a repolarisation wave travelling towards a positive electrode tends to produce a negative deflection. The normal ventricular T wave can therefore be upright even though it represents repolarisation, because the spread and polarity of repolarisation differ from depolarisation. An upward deflection is not a drawing of the heart moving upwards—it is the electrical signal's projection onto that lead. 3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? The standard 12-lead ECG uses 10 physical electrodes: four on the limbs and six on the chest. Their potentials are combined to produce twelve electrical views. Lead group Views Names Bipolar limb 3 I, II, III Augmented limb 3 aVR, aVL, aVF Precordial (chest) 6 V1–V6 The right-leg (RL) electrode mainly helps suppress electrical interference; it is not a thirteenth lead. The six limb leads observe the heart in the frontal plane, while the six chest leads add views in the horizontal plane. The three bipolar limb leads Lead Negative electrode Positive electrode I Right arm (RA) Left arm (LA) II Right arm (RA) Left leg (LL) III Left arm (LA) Left leg (LL) These directions form Einthoven's triangle. Their simultaneous voltages obey Einthoven's law: Einthoven's law V_II = V_I + V_III For instance, if Lead I measures +0.5 mV and Lead III measures +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II should measure +0.8 mV (ignoring recording error). Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions. Image credit Kychot · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. The augmented limb leads aVR views towards the right arm, aVL towards the left arm and aVF towards the left leg/foot. Each uses one positive limb electrode and a reference derived from the other limb electrodes. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector usually moves away from aVR, so its QRS complex is commonly predominantly negative. The six chest leads Electrode Standard position V1 Fourth right intercostal space, right sternal border V2 Fourth left intercostal space, left sternal border V3 Midway between V2 and V4 V4 Fifth left intercostal space, midclavicular line V5 Anterior axillary line, horizontal with V4 V6 Midaxillary line, horizontal with V4 For accurate placement, locate V4 before V3, then put V3 midway between V2 and V4. Incorrect placement can alter the tracing and create misleading findings. Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions. Image credit Jmarchn · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. 4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference? The ECG tracing is made up of waves, segments and intervals. These words are not interchangeable: Wave: A deflection associated with a change in the heart's summed electrical activity (e.g. P or T). Segment: The part between specified waves (e.g. PR segment or ST segment). Interval: A measured span including one or more waves and often a segment (e.g. PR or QT interval). Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals. Image credit Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · Wikimedia Commons · public domain. P wave — Atrial depolarisation The SA node initiates an impulse, which spreads through atrial myocardium. The P wave represents atrial myocardial depolarisation, not merely the firing of the tiny SA node. Atrial contraction follows. Representative an illustrative normal P-wave duration of 0.08–0.10 seconds. PR interval — Time from atrial to ventricular depolarisation The PR interval starts at the beginning of the P wave and ends at the beginning of QRS. It is normally about 0.12–0.20 seconds. It includes atrial depolarisation and conduction through the AV node and His–Purkinje system up to the start of ventricular activation. The whole PR interval is not simply the AV nodal delay. In contrast, the PR segment runs from the end of P to the start of QRS. Keep the starting points distinct when answering exam questions. QRS complex — Ventricular depolarisation The QRS complex represents ventricular depolarisation. Ventricular muscle mass is much greater than atrial mass, so its summed signal is typically larger than the P wave. The lecturer quotes a QRS duration of approximately 0.06–0.10 seconds; a duration under 0.12 seconds is a common broad normal clinical criterion. Q is the first negative deflection before a positive R; R is a positive deflection; S is a negative deflection after an R. A particular lead need not show all three letters. Why don't we see atrial repolarisation separately? It usually overlaps with ventricular depolarisation, and its smaller signal is masked by QRS—not absent. ST segment — A nearly flat line during continued ventricular electrical activity The ST segment extends from the end of QRS (J point) to the beginning of the T wave. During much of this interval, ventricular myocardial cells are in relatively similar depolarised states, so the lead often detects little net voltage difference. Flat does not mean inactive. The ST segment roughly corresponds to the ventricular action-potential plateau, not to a period when the cells have returned to rest. ST elevation and depression can have clinical importance, including myocardial ischaemia, but require appropriate context. T wave — Ventricular repolarisation The T wave reflects the spread of ventricular repolarisation. Repolarisation is not instantaneous and may give a broader waveform than QRS. The normal T-wave direction reflects both the spatial sequence of recovery and the polarity of repolarisation. U wave — Sometimes present, origin uncertain A small U wave may follow the T wave. The lecture calls it the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation; this is a traditional teaching description. Its precise source is still debated, so we should not present a specific proposed mechanism as settled fact. QT interval — Ventricular activation through recovery The QT interval extends from the start of QRS to the end of the T wave. It includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. Representative an illustrative range of 0.2–0.4 seconds, noting that QT varies with heart rate. Its duration shortens or lengthens with heart rate, so a single fixed normal QT value is misleading. Supplementary: heart-rate correction. Bazett's formula is a commonly taught approximation, although it is less reliable at unusually low or high heart rates. Bazett's QT correction QT_c = (QT) / (√(RR)) Use QT and RR measurements in seconds; interpret the result in clinical context. 5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events ECG feature Electrical event Mechanical connection P wave Atrial depolarisation Atrial contraction follows PR interval Atrial-to-ventricular activation time Includes the AV delay that supports final filling QRS complex Ventricular depolarisation Ventricular contraction follows ST segment Much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised Sustained ventricular contraction/ejection T wave Ventricular repolarisation Mechanical relaxation develops, but not instantaneously This connects directly with Chapter 04's Wiggers diagram: the ECG measures the electrical changes; the Wiggers diagram also shows pressure, volume and valve events. The electrical and mechanical curves are connected but are not interchangeable. 6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage? The lecturer's standard settings are 25 mm/s (paper speed) and 10 mm/mV (voltage gain). At 25 mm/s 1 small 1-mm horizontal square = 0.04 s 1 large 5-mm horizontal square = 0.20 s At 10 mm/mV: 1 small vertical square = 0.1 mV; 1 large vertical square = 0.5 mV. Why is this true? At 25 mm/s, the paper moves 25 mm in one second. Each 1-mm horizontal square therefore spans 1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s; five such squares span 0.20 s. Do not confuse axes: Horizontal spacing measures time; vertical height measures voltage. A taller QRS does not mean a longer QRS duration. 7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing For a regular rhythm, measure the R–R interval. Since 60 seconds make one minute: General relationship HR = (60) / (RRin seconds) At 25 mm/s, the common shortcuts follow from 1500 small or 300 large squares passing per minute: 25 mm/s · regular rhythm HR = (1500) / (N_small squares) Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves. Worked example: the lecturer's 22 small squares If an R–R interval spans 22 small squares: RR duration = 22 × 0.04 s = 0.88 s Heart rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min, equivalently 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68. Important observation about slide 14: Not all its R–R intervals appear equal. The calculation 1500 ÷ 22 gives the rate represented by that particular interval. It should not automatically be called the average rate for the whole irregular tracing. What changes if the rhythm is irregular? When R–R intervals vary, a single pair of R waves is insufficient to estimate the average. One simple method is to count ventricular complexes in a 6-second strip and multiply by 10 (supplementary). Example: 8 QRS complexes in 6 seconds → approximate average 80 beats/min. What if paper speed is 50 mm/s? Each small horizontal square then represents 0.02 s, so the shortcuts become 3000 ÷ small squares or 600 ÷ large squares. Always check the paper speed before using a shortcut. 8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary) The lecture's central focus is ECG fundamentals, not a full arrhythmia course. Still, an initial rhythm assessment connects the tracing to its purpose. Ask four questions: Is the ventricular rate appropriate? Are the R–R intervals regular? Are consistent P waves present before QRS? Is conduction through PR and QRS appropriate? A normal sinus rhythm generally shows P waves of consistent shape, with an appropriate relationship between each P and the following QRS and a reasonably regular rhythm. For healthy adults at rest, 60–100 beats/min is a common reference range, though slower rates can be normal in particular circumstances. Recognition of AV conduction disturbance Pattern Interpretation PR >0.20 s, every P followed by QRS First-degree AV block Some P waves not followed by QRS Second-degree AV block P waves and QRS complexes follow independent rhythms Complete (third-degree) AV block Mechanism: These patterns concern how the atrial impulse is delayed or fails to reach ventricular myocardium. Their detailed subtypes are supplementary and require additional ECG practice. A normal-looking ECG cannot guarantee effective cardiac output: electrical activity does not prove adequate mechanical pumping. Understanding Check 1. How can skin electrodes record activity generated inside the heart? Reveal explanation Cardiac depolarisation and repolarisation produce electrical potential differences that are conducted through body tissues. Electrodes detect differences between skin locations, so the ECG can record the combined electrical activity without touching the myocardium. It records electricity, not the strength of contraction. 2. Why do ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? Reveal explanation Electrodes are recording contacts; leads are calculated electrical views. Four limb electrodes and six precordial electrodes provide three bipolar limb views, three augmented limb views and six chest views. The right-leg electrode helps reduce interference rather than producing another lead. 3. If a depolarisation vector moves away from the positive direction of Lead II, what deflection is expected? Reveal explanation Predominantly negative (downward). A lead displays the projection of the heart's electrical vector onto its positive recording direction. Depolarisation moving away from that direction generates a negative deflection; moving towards it produces a positive one. 4. Explain what Einthoven's law means if Leads I and III measure +0.5 and +0.3 mV at the same instant. Reveal explanation Lead II = +0.8 mV, because V II = V I + V III. This relationship follows from how the three bipolar limb leads use the right-arm, left-arm and left-leg electrode potentials. The values must be compared at the same instant. 5. The QRS complex is larger than the P wave. Which electrical event does each represent, and why is the difference expected? Reveal explanation QRS is ventricular depolarisation; P is atrial depolarisation. The ventricles have greater muscle mass, so the summed surface electrical signal during ventricular activation is usually much larger. The small atrial repolarisation signal is normally hidden within QRS. 6. A student measures the PR segment and calls it the PR interval. What is missing? Reveal explanation The P wave itself. The PR segment starts at the end of P and ends at the beginning of QRS; the PR interval starts at the beginning of P. Therefore the PR interval includes atrial depolarisation plus subsequent conduction time, not just AV nodal delay. 7. Why can the ST segment lie near the baseline while most ventricular cells remain depolarised? Reveal explanation During much of the ST segment, ventricular regions are in relatively similar depolarised states. The lead therefore detects only a small net electrical potential difference. A flat segment does not indicate that the ventricle has become electrically inactive. 8. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are separated by 22 small squares. Calculate the rate. Reveal explanation Each small horizontal square lasts 0.04 s. The RR interval is 22 × 0.04 = 0.88 s. Rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min (also 1500 ÷ 22). If other R–R intervals differ, this is only the beat-to-beat rate for the chosen interval. 9. Why does the 1500 rule need adjustment at a paper speed of 50 mm/s? Reveal explanation The paper travels twice as far per second, so each small square lasts 0.02 s rather than 0.04 s. There are now 3000 small squares per minute; the corresponding regular-rhythm shortcut is 3000 ÷ small squares (or 600 ÷ large squares). 10. An irregular ECG has eight QRS complexes in six seconds. Estimate its average ventricular rate. Reveal explanation Approximately 80 beats/min. Eight beats in six seconds correspond to 8 × (60 ÷ 6) = 80 beats/min. For an irregular rhythm, counting complexes over a longer interval is more representative than applying the 1500 rule to one R–R interval. 11. Each P wave is followed by QRS, but the PR interval is 0.24 seconds. What pattern does this suggest? Reveal explanation First-degree AV block. PR is prolonged beyond 0.20 s, yet each atrial impulse still conducts to the ventricles. The finding indicates delayed AV transmission, not dropped beats or complete AV dissociation. 12. Can a regular ECG tracing prove that the ventricles are pumping enough blood? Reveal explanation No. An ECG measures electrical activity. Electrical impulses may occur without effective myocardial contraction or sufficient stroke volume, so perfusion requires assessment of the pulse, blood pressure and clinical state. Chapter Essentials ECG: Records electrical potential differences detected at the body surface, not blood pressure or contraction. Twelve leads, ten electrodes: Three bipolar limb, three augmented limb and six chest leads. Vector rule: Depolarisation towards a positive lead produces a positive deflection; away produces a negative deflection. Lead placement: I, II and III use limb-electrode differences; V1–V6 have specific chest landmarks. P wave: Atrial myocardial depolarisation; QRS: ventricular depolarisation; T wave: ventricular repolarisation. PR interval: Start of P to start of QRS, normally about 0.12–0.20 s; do not confuse with PR segment or pure AV nodal delay. QRS duration: About 0.06–0.10 s in the lecturer's examples; often less than 0.12 s clinically. ST segment: Ventricular myocardium remains largely depolarised; a flat tracing does not mean inactivity. QT interval: Start of QRS to end of T; varies with heart rate. U-wave origin remains unsettled. Calibration at 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV: Small square 0.04 s horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically; large square 0.20 s. Heart rate: Regular rhythm HR = 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares at 25 mm/s; use longer strips to estimate irregular rhythms. Basic rhythm assessment: Rate, regularity, P waves, PR and QRS; ECG electrical activity does not guarantee cardiac output."},{"slug":"cardiac-output","title":"Cardiac Output and Frank-Starling Law","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"35-51","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter","title":"1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?"},{"id":"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output","title":"2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?"},{"id":"3-what-determines-stroke-volume","title":"3. What determines stroke volume?"},{"id":"4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection","title":"4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?"},{"id":"5-how-do-we-read-frank-starling-curves","title":"5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?"},{"id":"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume","title":"6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?"},{"id":"7-what-changes-contractility","title":"7. What changes contractility?"},{"id":"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations","title":"8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?"},{"id":"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states","title":"9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?</h2>\n<p><strong>Cardiac output (CO)</strong> is the <strong>volume of blood ejected by one ventricle per unit time</strong>, usually expressed in <strong>litres per minute (L/min)</strong>. It tells us how much blood the heart delivers to a circulation—not how much blood is ejected in a single beat.</p>\n<p>Two quantities determine cardiac output:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart rate (HR):</strong> number of beats per minute.</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke volume (SV):</strong> blood ejected from one ventricle <em>per beat</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac output</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"CO = HR × SV\">CO = HR × SV</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = <strong>5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min</strong></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why multiply?</strong> Each beat sends out one stroke volume. If the ventricle pumps 70 mL each time and beats 75 times in one minute, the total volume ejected in that minute is 75 × 70 mL.</p>\n<p>A representative resting cardiac output is approximately <strong>5–5.5 L/min</strong>. This is a representative value, not a universal constant: cardiac output varies with body size, activity and physiological conditions.</p>\n<h3>Why should right and left cardiac outputs be approximately equal?</h3>\n<p>The pulmonary and systemic circulations are <strong>connected in series</strong>. Blood ejected by the right ventricle enters the lungs and ultimately returns to the left ventricle. If one side consistently pumped a greater volume per minute than the other, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. The two outputs therefore remain approximately equal <strong>over time</strong>, although small moment-to-moment differences can occur.</p>\n<h3>Cardiac index: allowing for body size</h3>\n<p>A person with a larger body generally needs more total blood flow than a smaller person. <strong>Cardiac index (CI)</strong> expresses cardiac output relative to <strong>body surface area (BSA)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac index</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"CI = (CO) / (BSA)\">CI = (CO) / (BSA)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: <strong>CI = 3.0 L/min/m²</strong>.</span></div>\n\n<p>Unlike CO, cardiac index is expressed as <strong>L min⁻¹ m⁻²</strong>. It is a size-adjusted measure, not a different kind of blood flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?</h2>\n<p>From <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>, increasing either HR or SV can raise output <strong>if the other factor remains unchanged</strong>. In a living heart, however, they are connected.</p>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation generally increases SA nodal firing (positive <strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">chronotropy</span></strong>) and makes ventricular contraction stronger (positive <strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">inotropy</span></strong>). These effects can raise both HR and SV.</p>\n<p>But <strong>a faster heartbeat does not always mean more blood pumped per minute</strong>. At very high rates, diastole becomes so short that ventricular filling decreases. Reduced filling can lower SV enough to offset the rise in HR.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> At 75 beats/min and 70 mL/beat, CO is <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. At 130 beats/min but only 35 mL/beat, CO is <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. Even though the heart beats faster, it pumps less blood per minute.</p>\n<div class=\"co-impact-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Two determinants of cardiac output\"><span><strong>HR ↑</strong><small>More beats per minute</small></span><span><strong>SV ↓ markedly</strong><small>Less blood each beat</small></span><span><strong>CO may ↓</strong><small>Depends on their product</small></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Reasoning rule:</strong> Never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone unless you know what happens to stroke volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-determines-stroke-volume\">3. What determines stroke volume?</h2>\n<p>From Chapter 04, <strong>SV = EDV − ESV</strong>: the amount ejected is the amount present at the end of filling minus the amount remaining after contraction.</p>\n<p>Three closely related factors determine how much is ejected:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Factor</th>\n  <th>The question it answers</th>\n  <th>Typical effect on SV, if other conditions are fixed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"preload\">Preload</span></strong></td>\n  <td>How stretched is the ventricle before contraction?</td>\n  <td>More preload → usually higher SV within physiological limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"afterload\">Afterload</span></strong></td>\n  <td>How difficult is it to eject blood?</td>\n  <td>More afterload → usually lower SV acutely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Contractility (inotropy)</strong></td>\n  <td>How strongly can the myocardium contract at the same loading?</td>\n  <td>Greater contractility → higher SV</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Preload — the starting stretch</h3>\n<p><strong>Preload</strong> refers to ventricular myocardial fibre stretch (and associated passive wall stress) <strong>at the end of diastole</strong>, just before contraction.</p>\n<p>More blood returning to a compliant ventricle usually increases <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong> and stretches its fibres. EDV is therefore a useful teaching representation of preload. End-diastolic pressure is another clinical surrogate, but <strong>volume and pressure are not interchangeable</strong>, particularly in a stiff ventricle.</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Gently stretching an elastic band before releasing it can help it recoil more forcefully. It is only an analogy: cardiac muscle is active tissue, and excessive stretch does not produce unlimited pumping.</p>\n<h3>Afterload — the resistance or load against ejection</h3>\n<p><strong>Afterload</strong> is the mechanical load the ventricle must overcome during ejection. On the left side it is related to <strong>aortic pressure, arterial properties and ventricular wall stress</strong>. Peripheral vascular resistance contributes to the load but is not identical to afterload.</p>\n<p>If aortic pressure rises suddenly while preload and contractility remain unchanged, the ventricle must generate more force before and during ejection; it will generally eject <strong>less blood in that beat</strong>, leaving a higher ESV.</p>\n<h3>Contractility — how capable the muscle is at a given load</h3>\n<p><strong>Contractility</strong> is the intrinsic ability of the myocardium to develop force <strong>when preload and afterload are held comparable</strong>. It depends strongly on intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling and the contractile machinery.</p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> A ventricle that contracts more forcefully <em>because it filled more</em> is using the Frank–Starling mechanism; that does <strong>not automatically mean its intrinsic contractility increased</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?</h2>\n<p>Imagine more blood returning through the veins. The extra blood enters a ventricle during diastole and stretches its muscle fibres before the next beat.</p>\n<p>Within the normal operating range, increased initial sarcomere length improves force development during the next contraction. This reflects <strong>length-dependent activation</strong>, including improved myofilament sensitivity to Ca²⁺ and more favourable interaction between contractile proteins.</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Frank Starling mechanism\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV and fibre stretch ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Force generated ↑</span><b>→</b><span>SV ↑</span></div>\n\n<p>Thus, the <strong>Frank–Starling law</strong> states that <strong>within physiological limits, the heart tends to eject more blood when its fibres are stretched more during filling</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this useful?</strong> It helps match the amount of blood ejected to the amount returning to the heart. If venous return increases, the ventricle can automatically increase SV without needing a new sympathetic command for every extra millilitre received.</p>\n<h3>Does greater preload always increase stroke volume?</h3>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> The relationship is not unlimited. At high filling levels the curve becomes flatter, so additional volume yields progressively less extra output. A severely failing ventricle may be particularly unresponsive to extra filling while pressure rises enough to cause congestion.</p>\n<p>Lecture slide 39 includes a <strong>dashed descending limb</strong> at extreme stretch. That portion is a conceptual teaching extension: ordinary intact human hearts do <strong>not normally operate on a descending Frank–Starling limb</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-we-read-frank-starling-curves\">5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?</h2>\n<p>The slides 39–41 show <strong>ventricular performance</strong> plotted against filling. The usual horizontal axis is EDV or a surrogate of preload; the vertical axis may be <strong>stroke volume</strong> or <strong>cardiac output</strong>, depending on the particular diagram.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure cardiac-output-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Frank–Starling curves — preload versus pumping performance</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 295\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic stroke-volume versus end-diastolic-volume graph showing increased, normal and reduced cardiac contractility curves.\"><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M99 229 C168 100 285 57 580 55\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 232 C188 181 275 148 580 148\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 235 C205 222 320 208 580 207\" stroke=\"#c39431\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"190\" y=\"273\">End-diastolic volume (preload) →</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"130\" transform=\"rotate(-90,22,130)\">Stroke volume</text><text x=\"387\" y=\"40\">Greater contractility</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"138\">Reference</text><text x=\"432\" y=\"196\">Lower contractility</text></g></svg><p><strong>Read across one curve:</strong> changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. <strong>Compare curves vertically:</strong> at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>A. Moving <strong>along</strong> the same curve</h3>\n<p>Suppose a person lies down after standing. Venous pooling in the legs decreases, so more blood returns to the heart, EDV increases and the ventricle moves <strong>rightwards and upwards along a curve</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is primarily a change in <strong>preload</strong>, without requiring the intrinsic contractility of the myocardium to change.</p>\n<h3>B. Shifting <strong>between</strong> curves</h3>\n<p>Now imagine two beats with the <strong>same EDV</strong>, but sympathetic stimulation has increased intracellular Ca²⁺ availability during the second beat. The second beat can eject a greater SV <strong>without additional stretch</strong>. This is a change in <strong>contractile state</strong>, represented by an <strong>upward function-curve shift</strong>.</p>\n<p>A fall in contractility, as in significant myocardial dysfunction, tends to produce a <strong>lower curve</strong>. Higher afterload can also lower ventricular performance at a given EDV, so <strong>a shifted curve is not proof of altered contractility unless afterload is accounted for</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The unusual terms: heterometric and homometric</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Term</th>\n  <th>Meaning</th>\n  <th>Typical example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Heterometric regulation</strong></td>\n  <td>Adjusting output through changes in the initial fibre length (preload)</td>\n  <td>Frank–Starling response to increased venous return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Homometric regulation</strong></td>\n  <td>Adjusting force independently of the initial fibre length</td>\n  <td>β₁-mediated increase in contractility at the same EDV</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Exam insight:</strong> <em>Heterometric</em> changes the starting length; <em>homometric</em> changes pumping performance <strong>without needing a length change</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume\">6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?</h2>\n<p><strong>EDV depends on how much blood returns, the time available to fill, and whether the ventricle can accommodate that blood.</strong> More venous return does not always translate into the same EDV change if ventricular compliance is impaired.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Often increases EDV</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Increased blood volume</td>\n  <td>More blood available to return to the heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Increased venous tone</td>\n  <td>Shifts blood from compliant venous reservoirs towards the heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Skeletal-muscle pump</td>\n  <td>Contracting muscles compress veins and promote flow through one-way valves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Respiratory pump</td>\n  <td>Breathing-related pressure changes can favour venous return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Effective atrial contraction</td>\n  <td>Completes late ventricular filling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Longer diastolic filling time</td>\n  <td>More time is available for blood to enter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Often reduces EDV or limits effective filling</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Haemorrhage / hypovolaemia</td>\n  <td>Less circulating blood and lower venous return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Prolonged standing</td>\n  <td>Gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pericardial effusion / tamponade</td>\n  <td>External pericardial pressure limits ventricular expansion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Reduced ventricular compliance</td>\n  <td>A stiff ventricle fills less readily for a given pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Severe tachycardia</td>\n  <td>Shortened diastole limits filling time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Myocardial infarction and infiltrative disease</strong>, including endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF), also deserve consideration. These can impair relaxation, compliance and/or pumping; their effect on EDV is <strong>not universally a simple decrease</strong>. The precise direction depends on the disease and stage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-contractility\">7. What changes contractility?</h2>\n<p>Sympathetic cardiac nerves release <strong>noradrenaline (norepinephrine)</strong>, while the adrenal medulla releases circulating <strong>adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> and some noradrenaline. These catecholamines stimulate <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong> in the heart.</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Beta adrenergic inotropic pathway\"><span>β₁ receptor</span><b>→</b><span>Gs / adenylyl cyclase</span><b>→</b><span>cAMP / PKA ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Ca²⁺ cycling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stronger contraction</span></div>\n\n<p>PKA signalling increases L-type Ca²⁺ entry and enhances Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, allowing greater actin–myosin interaction. This produces <strong>positive inotropy</strong>. The same signalling can speed Ca²⁺ reuptake, producing <strong>positive <span data-term=\"lusitropy\">lusitropy</span></strong> (faster relaxation).</p>\n<p>The lecture cites <strong>caffeine and theophylline</strong> (xanthines). By inhibiting phosphodiesterase activity at sufficiently high concentrations, such agents can reduce cAMP breakdown, but their physiological and clinical effects are dose- and context-dependent. Do not assume everyday caffeine intake produces a predictable large positive inotropic response.</p>\n<h3>What about the vagus nerve?</h3>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> vagal effects are strong for <strong>heart rate</strong> and AV nodal conduction, but the <strong>direct parasympathetic effect on ventricular contractility is relatively small</strong>. Any fall in cardiac output may occur largely through changes in HR and filling rather than a large direct weakening of ventricular myocardium.</p>\n<h3>What can depress inotropy?</h3>\n<p>Lecture slide 48 lists <strong>hypercapnia, hypoxia, acidosis, selected drugs and cardiac failure</strong>. Severe myocardial hypoxia and acidosis can interfere with Ca²⁺ handling and cellular energy availability, reducing force generation; myocardial injury or failure may also reduce contractility. Responses to individual drugs depend on their specific mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations\">8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?</h2>\n<div class=\"co-compare-grid\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Three ways stroke volume changes\"><div><strong>More venous return</strong><span>Preload ↑</span><small>Often moves <em>along</em> the curve → SV ↑</small></div><div><strong>Higher aortic pressure</strong><span>Afterload ↑</span><small>Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓</small></div><div><strong>β₁ stimulation</strong><span>Contractility ↑</span><small>More force at same EDV → SV ↑</small></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why are these comparisons useful?</strong> In a patient with blood loss, HR may rise through the sympathetic reflex, but low preload can still reduce SV and CO. In a patient with aortic stenosis, even a strong contraction faces a substantial outflow load. In a failing heart, giving more filling volume may only modestly increase SV if the ventricular function curve is flat.</p>\n<p>These examples show why no single determinant should be interpreted in isolation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states\">9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?</h2>\n<p>During <strong>dynamic exercise</strong>, several processes usually occur together: sympathetic stimulation increases HR and inotropy; the skeletal-muscle and respiratory pumps increase <strong>venous return</strong>; and arterioles supplying active muscle dilate. CO can therefore increase substantially by changes in <strong>both</strong> HR and SV.</p>\n<p><strong>Lecture clarification:</strong> Slide 49 contains an ambiguous downward-arrow description of venous return during exercise. For ordinary dynamic exercise, the expected effect of muscle and respiratory pumping is generally <strong>increased</strong>, not decreased, venous return. The diagrams on slides 50–51 also show increased venous return associated with the pumps.</p>\n<p>The lecture additionally mentions conditions associated with increased CO or cardiac demands: <strong>eating, high environmental temperature, pregnancy, anxiety/excitement, catecholamines and thyrotoxicosis</strong>. These conditions differ in mechanisms and magnitude; they are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical connection — shock:</strong> During major haemorrhage, venous return and preload decrease. Sympathetic tachycardia and vasoconstriction can help maintain arterial pressure temporarily, but this does not guarantee normal CO or adequate tissue perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. A person has a heart rate of 75 beats/min and stroke volume of 70 mL. Calculate CO and state the correct units.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>CO = HR × SV = 75 × 70 = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min.</strong> Heart rate counts beats per minute, while stroke volume gives the blood ejected per beat; multiplying cancels 'beats' and leaves volume per minute.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be approximately equal over time, even though they pump against different pressures?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The systemic and pulmonary circuits are arranged <strong>in series</strong>: what leaves the right heart eventually returns to the left heart. A persistent unequal flow would cause blood to accumulate in one circuit and deplete the other. The left ventricle produces higher pressure, but not a greater long-term flow rate.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, but SV falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to CO?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Initially, CO = 75 × 70 = <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. Afterwards CO = 130 × 35 = <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. Thus CO <strong>decreases</strong> despite tachycardia. Severe shortening of filling time can reduce SV enough to outweigh the increase in HR.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Two ventricular beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects a larger SV following sympathetic stimulation. Has preload necessarily increased?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Same EDV implies comparable filling volume, while β₁ stimulation can increase Ca²⁺ entry and SR release, raising intrinsic contractility. At similar preload and afterload, this produces greater force and a larger SV, reflecting a <strong>higher ventricular function curve</strong> rather than merely movement along the original curve.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. A patient lies down after standing for a long time. Explain the expected Frank–Starling response.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Standing promotes venous pooling. Lying down reduces the gravitational gradient, usually improving venous return and EDV. Extra ventricular filling stretches fibres, increasing force through <strong>length-dependent activation</strong> and generally increasing SV. This is primarily movement <strong>along</strong> a Frank–Starling curve.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. Why is EDV not an exact measure of preload in a very stiff ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>True preload concerns end-diastolic myocardial stretch and wall stress; EDV is only a surrogate. A stiff ventricle may develop high filling pressure with relatively little increase in volume or fibre stretch. Therefore identical EDVs or pressures need not imply identical myocardial preload across different ventricular properties.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. On a stroke-volume-versus-EDV graph, what distinguishes moving along one curve from shifting to a higher curve?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Along one curve:</strong> EDV changes, and the Frank–Starling length-dependent mechanism changes SV at the same functional state. <strong>Higher curve:</strong> at the same EDV, the ventricle ejects more blood, often because intrinsic contractility increased; reduced afterload can also improve performance, so loading conditions must be considered.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. An acute increase in aortic pressure occurs without an immediate change in preload or contractility. Predict the first-beat effect on SV.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>SV usually decreases.</strong> Higher aortic pressure increases the load opposing ventricular ejection. With initially unchanged preload and inotropy, the ventricle ejects less blood, leaving more residual ESV. Later compensatory changes can alter the result, but they are not part of the immediate first-beat prediction.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Why might a large intravenous fluid load fail to appreciably raise SV in a severely failing ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>In marked systolic dysfunction the Frank–Starling curve may be relatively <strong>flat</strong> at high filling levels. Additional filling can markedly increase pressures and congestion while producing only a small increase in force and SV. More preload therefore does not guarantee proportionately greater output.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. How can β₁ stimulation increase contraction strength and accelerate myocardial relaxation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>β₁ receptors activate Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA. Greater L-type Ca²⁺ influx and SR Ca²⁺ release strengthen contraction (<strong>positive inotropy</strong>), while enhanced SERCA-mediated Ca²⁺ reuptake accelerates relaxation (<strong>positive lusitropy</strong>). The two effects arise from different parts of the same signalling pathway.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why does dynamic exercise usually increase venous return rather than reduce it?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Contracting skeletal muscles compress veins, and one-way venous valves favour flow towards the heart. Respiratory pressure changes also support return. Combined with sympathetic venoconstriction, these mechanisms tend to move more blood back to the heart, increasing preload and helping sustain the higher CO required during exercise.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. A patient loses a large volume of blood, becomes tachycardic, yet develops poor tissue perfusion. Explain using CO, preload and compensation.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Haemorrhage reduces blood volume and venous return, causing <strong>lower preload and EDV</strong>. The reduction in SV can markedly reduce CO. Reflex sympathetic stimulation increases HR and vascular resistance, which may support arterial pressure temporarily; however, CO = HR × SV means a sufficiently large SV fall can overwhelm tachycardic compensation, leaving tissue perfusion inadequate.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cardiac output:</strong> CO = HR × SV, measured in L/min; right and left outputs are approximately equal over time.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac index:</strong> CI = CO/BSA, measured in L/min/m².</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke volume:</strong> SV = EDV − ESV; altered HR alone does not predict CO.</li>\n<li><strong>Preload:</strong> End-diastolic myocardial fibre stretch; EDV and filling pressure are imperfect surrogates.</li>\n<li><strong>Afterload:</strong> Mechanical load opposing ejection; acute increase generally lowers SV at unchanged preload/inotropy.</li>\n<li><strong>Contractility:</strong> Ability to develop force at comparable loading; β₁ stimulation increases inotropy through cAMP/PKA/Ca²⁺.</li>\n<li><strong>Frank–Starling law:</strong> Within physiological limits, increased filling and fibre length increase force and SV.</li>\n<li><strong>Curve interpretation:</strong> Changing preload moves <em>along</em> a curve; changing contractile state shifts performance between curves.</li>\n<li><strong>Heterometric regulation:</strong> Change in fibre starting length; <strong>homometric regulation:</strong> change in force without change in starting length.</li>\n<li><strong>Venous return and EDV:</strong> Supported by blood volume, venous tone, muscle pump and respiratory pump; restricted by pooling, tamponade and stiff ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Vagal influence:</strong> Dominant direct effects on HR and AV conduction; direct ventricular negative inotropy is small.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe dysfunction:</strong> A flat Frank–Starling curve means more filling may raise congestion more than SV.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise and haemorrhage:</strong> CO must be reasoned from changes in both HR and SV, not heart rate alone.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law 1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter? Cardiac output (CO) is the volume of blood ejected by one ventricle per unit time, usually expressed in litres per minute (L/min). It tells us how much blood the heart delivers to a circulation—not how much blood is ejected in a single beat. Two quantities determine cardiac output: Heart rate (HR): number of beats per minute. Stroke volume (SV): blood ejected from one ventricle per beat. Cardiac output CO = HR × SV Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min Why multiply? Each beat sends out one stroke volume. If the ventricle pumps 70 mL each time and beats 75 times in one minute, the total volume ejected in that minute is 75 × 70 mL. A representative resting cardiac output is approximately 5–5.5 L/min. This is a representative value, not a universal constant: cardiac output varies with body size, activity and physiological conditions. Why should right and left cardiac outputs be approximately equal? The pulmonary and systemic circulations are connected in series. Blood ejected by the right ventricle enters the lungs and ultimately returns to the left ventricle. If one side consistently pumped a greater volume per minute than the other, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. The two outputs therefore remain approximately equal over time, although small moment-to-moment differences can occur. Cardiac index: allowing for body size A person with a larger body generally needs more total blood flow than a smaller person. Cardiac index (CI) expresses cardiac output relative to body surface area (BSA). Cardiac index CI = (CO) / (BSA) For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: CI = 3.0 L/min/m². Unlike CO, cardiac index is expressed as L min⁻¹ m⁻². It is a size-adjusted measure, not a different kind of blood flow. 2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output? From CO = HR × SV, increasing either HR or SV can raise output if the other factor remains unchanged. In a living heart, however, they are connected. Sympathetic stimulation generally increases SA nodal firing (positive chronotropy) and makes ventricular contraction stronger (positive inotropy). These effects can raise both HR and SV. But a faster heartbeat does not always mean more blood pumped per minute. At very high rates, diastole becomes so short that ventricular filling decreases. Reduced filling can lower SV enough to offset the rise in HR. Example: At 75 beats/min and 70 mL/beat, CO is 5.25 L/min. At 130 beats/min but only 35 mL/beat, CO is 4.55 L/min. Even though the heart beats faster, it pumps less blood per minute. HR ↑ More beats per minute SV ↓ markedly Less blood each beat CO may ↓ Depends on their product Reasoning rule: Never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone unless you know what happens to stroke volume. 3. What determines stroke volume? From Chapter 04, SV = EDV − ESV: the amount ejected is the amount present at the end of filling minus the amount remaining after contraction. Three closely related factors determine how much is ejected: Factor The question it answers Typical effect on SV, if other conditions are fixed Preload How stretched is the ventricle before contraction? More preload → usually higher SV within physiological limits Afterload How difficult is it to eject blood? More afterload → usually lower SV acutely Contractility (inotropy) How strongly can the myocardium contract at the same loading? Greater contractility → higher SV Preload — the starting stretch Preload refers to ventricular myocardial fibre stretch (and associated passive wall stress) at the end of diastole, just before contraction. More blood returning to a compliant ventricle usually increases end-diastolic volume (EDV) and stretches its fibres. EDV is therefore a useful teaching representation of preload. End-diastolic pressure is another clinical surrogate, but volume and pressure are not interchangeable, particularly in a stiff ventricle. Analogy: Gently stretching an elastic band before releasing it can help it recoil more forcefully. It is only an analogy: cardiac muscle is active tissue, and excessive stretch does not produce unlimited pumping. Afterload — the resistance or load against ejection Afterload is the mechanical load the ventricle must overcome during ejection. On the left side it is related to aortic pressure, arterial properties and ventricular wall stress. Peripheral vascular resistance contributes to the load but is not identical to afterload. If aortic pressure rises suddenly while preload and contractility remain unchanged, the ventricle must generate more force before and during ejection; it will generally eject less blood in that beat, leaving a higher ESV. Contractility — how capable the muscle is at a given load Contractility is the intrinsic ability of the myocardium to develop force when preload and afterload are held comparable. It depends strongly on intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling and the contractile machinery. Important distinction: A ventricle that contracts more forcefully because it filled more is using the Frank–Starling mechanism; that does not automatically mean its intrinsic contractility increased. 4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection? Imagine more blood returning through the veins. The extra blood enters a ventricle during diastole and stretches its muscle fibres before the next beat. Within the normal operating range, increased initial sarcomere length improves force development during the next contraction. This reflects length-dependent activation, including improved myofilament sensitivity to Ca²⁺ and more favourable interaction between contractile proteins. Venous return ↑ → EDV and fibre stretch ↑ → Force generated ↑ → SV ↑ Thus, the Frank–Starling law states that within physiological limits, the heart tends to eject more blood when its fibres are stretched more during filling. Why is this useful? It helps match the amount of blood ejected to the amount returning to the heart. If venous return increases, the ventricle can automatically increase SV without needing a new sympathetic command for every extra millilitre received. Does greater preload always increase stroke volume? No. The relationship is not unlimited. At high filling levels the curve becomes flatter, so additional volume yields progressively less extra output. A severely failing ventricle may be particularly unresponsive to extra filling while pressure rises enough to cause congestion. Lecture slide 39 includes a dashed descending limb at extreme stretch. That portion is a conceptual teaching extension: ordinary intact human hearts do not normally operate on a descending Frank–Starling limb. 5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves? The slides 39–41 show ventricular performance plotted against filling. The usual horizontal axis is EDV or a surrogate of preload; the vertical axis may be stroke volume or cardiac output, depending on the particular diagram. Figure 01. Frank–Starling curves — preload versus pumping performance End-diastolic volume (preload) → Stroke volume Greater contractility Reference Lower contractility Read across one curve: changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. Compare curves vertically: at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements. A. Moving along the same curve Suppose a person lies down after standing. Venous pooling in the legs decreases, so more blood returns to the heart, EDV increases and the ventricle moves rightwards and upwards along a curve. This is primarily a change in preload, without requiring the intrinsic contractility of the myocardium to change. B. Shifting between curves Now imagine two beats with the same EDV, but sympathetic stimulation has increased intracellular Ca²⁺ availability during the second beat. The second beat can eject a greater SV without additional stretch. This is a change in contractile state, represented by an upward function-curve shift. A fall in contractility, as in significant myocardial dysfunction, tends to produce a lower curve. Higher afterload can also lower ventricular performance at a given EDV, so a shifted curve is not proof of altered contractility unless afterload is accounted for. The unusual terms: heterometric and homometric Term Meaning Typical example Heterometric regulation Adjusting output through changes in the initial fibre length (preload) Frank–Starling response to increased venous return Homometric regulation Adjusting force independently of the initial fibre length β₁-mediated increase in contractility at the same EDV Exam insight: Heterometric changes the starting length; homometric changes pumping performance without needing a length change. 6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume? EDV depends on how much blood returns, the time available to fill, and whether the ventricle can accommodate that blood. More venous return does not always translate into the same EDV change if ventricular compliance is impaired. Often increases EDV Why Increased blood volume More blood available to return to the heart Increased venous tone Shifts blood from compliant venous reservoirs towards the heart Skeletal-muscle pump Contracting muscles compress veins and promote flow through one-way valves Respiratory pump Breathing-related pressure changes can favour venous return Effective atrial contraction Completes late ventricular filling Longer diastolic filling time More time is available for blood to enter Often reduces EDV or limits effective filling Why Haemorrhage / hypovolaemia Less circulating blood and lower venous return Prolonged standing Gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs Pericardial effusion / tamponade External pericardial pressure limits ventricular expansion Reduced ventricular compliance A stiff ventricle fills less readily for a given pressure Severe tachycardia Shortened diastole limits filling time Myocardial infarction and infiltrative disease, including endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF), also deserve consideration. These can impair relaxation, compliance and/or pumping; their effect on EDV is not universally a simple decrease. The precise direction depends on the disease and stage. 7. What changes contractility? Sympathetic cardiac nerves release noradrenaline (norepinephrine), while the adrenal medulla releases circulating adrenaline (epinephrine) and some noradrenaline. These catecholamines stimulate β₁-adrenergic receptors in the heart. β₁ receptor → Gs / adenylyl cyclase → cAMP / PKA ↑ → Ca²⁺ cycling ↑ → Stronger contraction PKA signalling increases L-type Ca²⁺ entry and enhances Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, allowing greater actin–myosin interaction. This produces positive inotropy. The same signalling can speed Ca²⁺ reuptake, producing positive lusitropy (faster relaxation). The lecture cites caffeine and theophylline (xanthines). By inhibiting phosphodiesterase activity at sufficiently high concentrations, such agents can reduce cAMP breakdown, but their physiological and clinical effects are dose- and context-dependent. Do not assume everyday caffeine intake produces a predictable large positive inotropic response. What about the vagus nerve? Important distinction: vagal effects are strong for heart rate and AV nodal conduction, but the direct parasympathetic effect on ventricular contractility is relatively small. Any fall in cardiac output may occur largely through changes in HR and filling rather than a large direct weakening of ventricular myocardium. What can depress inotropy? Lecture slide 48 lists hypercapnia, hypoxia, acidosis, selected drugs and cardiac failure. Severe myocardial hypoxia and acidosis can interfere with Ca²⁺ handling and cellular energy availability, reducing force generation; myocardial injury or failure may also reduce contractility. Responses to individual drugs depend on their specific mechanism. 8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations? More venous return Preload ↑ Often moves along the curve → SV ↑ Higher aortic pressure Afterload ↑ Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓ β₁ stimulation Contractility ↑ More force at same EDV → SV ↑ Why are these comparisons useful? In a patient with blood loss, HR may rise through the sympathetic reflex, but low preload can still reduce SV and CO. In a patient with aortic stenosis, even a strong contraction faces a substantial outflow load. In a failing heart, giving more filling volume may only modestly increase SV if the ventricular function curve is flat. These examples show why no single determinant should be interpreted in isolation. 9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states? During dynamic exercise, several processes usually occur together: sympathetic stimulation increases HR and inotropy; the skeletal-muscle and respiratory pumps increase venous return; and arterioles supplying active muscle dilate. CO can therefore increase substantially by changes in both HR and SV. Lecture clarification: Slide 49 contains an ambiguous downward-arrow description of venous return during exercise. For ordinary dynamic exercise, the expected effect of muscle and respiratory pumping is generally increased, not decreased, venous return. The diagrams on slides 50–51 also show increased venous return associated with the pumps. The lecture additionally mentions conditions associated with increased CO or cardiac demands: eating, high environmental temperature, pregnancy, anxiety/excitement, catecholamines and thyrotoxicosis. These conditions differ in mechanisms and magnitude; they are not interchangeable. Clinical connection — shock: During major haemorrhage, venous return and preload decrease. Sympathetic tachycardia and vasoconstriction can help maintain arterial pressure temporarily, but this does not guarantee normal CO or adequate tissue perfusion. Understanding Check 1. A person has a heart rate of 75 beats/min and stroke volume of 70 mL. Calculate CO and state the correct units. Reveal explanation CO = HR × SV = 75 × 70 = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min. Heart rate counts beats per minute, while stroke volume gives the blood ejected per beat; multiplying cancels 'beats' and leaves volume per minute. 2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be approximately equal over time, even though they pump against different pressures? Reveal explanation The systemic and pulmonary circuits are arranged in series: what leaves the right heart eventually returns to the left heart. A persistent unequal flow would cause blood to accumulate in one circuit and deplete the other. The left ventricle produces higher pressure, but not a greater long-term flow rate. 3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, but SV falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to CO? Reveal explanation Initially, CO = 75 × 70 = 5.25 L/min. Afterwards CO = 130 × 35 = 4.55 L/min. Thus CO decreases despite tachycardia. Severe shortening of filling time can reduce SV enough to outweigh the increase in HR. 4. Two ventricular beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects a larger SV following sympathetic stimulation. Has preload necessarily increased? Reveal explanation No. Same EDV implies comparable filling volume, while β₁ stimulation can increase Ca²⁺ entry and SR release, raising intrinsic contractility. At similar preload and afterload, this produces greater force and a larger SV, reflecting a higher ventricular function curve rather than merely movement along the original curve. 5. A patient lies down after standing for a long time. Explain the expected Frank–Starling response. Reveal explanation Standing promotes venous pooling. Lying down reduces the gravitational gradient, usually improving venous return and EDV. Extra ventricular filling stretches fibres, increasing force through length-dependent activation and generally increasing SV. This is primarily movement along a Frank–Starling curve. 6. Why is EDV not an exact measure of preload in a very stiff ventricle? Reveal explanation True preload concerns end-diastolic myocardial stretch and wall stress; EDV is only a surrogate. A stiff ventricle may develop high filling pressure with relatively little increase in volume or fibre stretch. Therefore identical EDVs or pressures need not imply identical myocardial preload across different ventricular properties. 7. On a stroke-volume-versus-EDV graph, what distinguishes moving along one curve from shifting to a higher curve? Reveal explanation Along one curve: EDV changes, and the Frank–Starling length-dependent mechanism changes SV at the same functional state. Higher curve: at the same EDV, the ventricle ejects more blood, often because intrinsic contractility increased; reduced afterload can also improve performance, so loading conditions must be considered. 8. An acute increase in aortic pressure occurs without an immediate change in preload or contractility. Predict the first-beat effect on SV. Reveal explanation SV usually decreases. Higher aortic pressure increases the load opposing ventricular ejection. With initially unchanged preload and inotropy, the ventricle ejects less blood, leaving more residual ESV. Later compensatory changes can alter the result, but they are not part of the immediate first-beat prediction. 9. Why might a large intravenous fluid load fail to appreciably raise SV in a severely failing ventricle? Reveal explanation In marked systolic dysfunction the Frank–Starling curve may be relatively flat at high filling levels. Additional filling can markedly increase pressures and congestion while producing only a small increase in force and SV. More preload therefore does not guarantee proportionately greater output. 10. How can β₁ stimulation increase contraction strength and accelerate myocardial relaxation? Reveal explanation β₁ receptors activate Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA. Greater L-type Ca²⁺ influx and SR Ca²⁺ release strengthen contraction ( positive inotropy), while enhanced SERCA-mediated Ca²⁺ reuptake accelerates relaxation ( positive lusitropy). The two effects arise from different parts of the same signalling pathway. 11. Why does dynamic exercise usually increase venous return rather than reduce it? Reveal explanation Contracting skeletal muscles compress veins, and one-way venous valves favour flow towards the heart. Respiratory pressure changes also support return. Combined with sympathetic venoconstriction, these mechanisms tend to move more blood back to the heart, increasing preload and helping sustain the higher CO required during exercise. 12. A patient loses a large volume of blood, becomes tachycardic, yet develops poor tissue perfusion. Explain using CO, preload and compensation. Reveal explanation Haemorrhage reduces blood volume and venous return, causing lower preload and EDV. The reduction in SV can markedly reduce CO. Reflex sympathetic stimulation increases HR and vascular resistance, which may support arterial pressure temporarily; however, CO = HR × SV means a sufficiently large SV fall can overwhelm tachycardic compensation, leaving tissue perfusion inadequate. Chapter Essentials Cardiac output: CO = HR × SV, measured in L/min; right and left outputs are approximately equal over time. Cardiac index: CI = CO/BSA, measured in L/min/m². Stroke volume: SV = EDV − ESV; altered HR alone does not predict CO. Preload: End-diastolic myocardial fibre stretch; EDV and filling pressure are imperfect surrogates. Afterload: Mechanical load opposing ejection; acute increase generally lowers SV at unchanged preload/inotropy. Contractility: Ability to develop force at comparable loading; β₁ stimulation increases inotropy through cAMP/PKA/Ca²⁺. Frank–Starling law: Within physiological limits, increased filling and fibre length increase force and SV. Curve interpretation: Changing preload moves along a curve; changing contractile state shifts performance between curves. Heterometric regulation: Change in fibre starting length; homometric regulation: change in force without change in starting length. Venous return and EDV: Supported by blood volume, venous tone, muscle pump and respiratory pump; restricted by pooling, tamponade and stiff ventricles. Vagal influence: Dominant direct effects on HR and AV conduction; direct ventricular negative inotropy is small. Severe dysfunction: A flat Frank–Starling curve means more filling may raise congestion more than SV. Exercise and haemorrhage: CO must be reasoned from changes in both HR and SV, not heart rate alone."},{"slug":"co-measurement","title":"Cardiac Output Measurement and Work","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"52-54","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-measure-cardiac-output-instead-of-calculating-it-from-heart-rate-alone","title":"1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone?"},{"id":"2-fick-s-principle-build-the-formula-from-the-physical-idea","title":"2. Fick's principle — build the formula from the physical idea"},{"id":"3-where-do-the-oxygen-measurements-come-from","title":"3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from?"},{"id":"4-how-does-the-indicator-dilution-idea-measure-blood-flow-supplementary","title":"4. How does the indicator-dilution idea measure blood flow? (Supplementary)"},{"id":"5-what-does-it-mean-for-the-heart-to-perform-work","title":"5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work?"},{"id":"6-visualising-stroke-work-on-a-pressure-volume-loop","title":"6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop"},{"id":"7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand","title":"7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-measure-cardiac-output-instead-of-calculating-it-from-heart-rate-alone\">1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone?</h2>\n<p>From Chapter 07, <strong>cardiac output (CO) = heart rate × stroke volume</strong>. If we know both factors, we can calculate output. But in clinical situations, <strong>stroke volume often cannot be measured simply by counting a pulse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therefore, we need an independent way to estimate the volume of blood passing through a circulation each minute. The lecturer introduces <strong>Fick's principle</strong>, which uses oxygen exchange as a way to infer blood flow.</p>\n<p>The central idea is <strong>conservation of matter</strong>: oxygen used by a tissue must have been supplied by the blood entering it, minus the oxygen still present in blood leaving it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-fick-s-principle-build-the-formula-from-the-physical-idea\">2. Fick's principle — build the formula from the physical idea</h2>\n<p>Imagine one litre of arterial blood enters a group of tissues with a certain amount of oxygen. The venous blood leaves with less oxygen because cells have extracted some.</p>\n<p>Two observations determine total oxygen use:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>How much oxygen is removed from each litre of blood?</strong> This is the arterial–venous oxygen-content difference.</li>\n<li><strong>How many litres of blood flow through the tissues each minute?</strong> This is the blood flow rate, which equals systemic cardiac output in steady state.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The tissue oxygen-consumption rate must equal the product of these quantities.</p>\n<div class=\"fick-process\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fick oxygen extraction concept\"><span><strong>Arterial O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ carried into tissues</small></span><b>−</b><span><strong>Mixed venous O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ remaining after tissues</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>O₂ extracted</strong><small>Amount removed from each unit of blood</small></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick's oxygen principle</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Oxygen consumption equals cardiac output times the arterial minus mixed venous oxygen content\">V̇O₂ = CO × (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂)</span></div>\n\n<p>The relationship is easier to read in plain notation: <strong>O₂ consumption/min = blood flow/min × (arterial O₂ content − mixed venous O₂ content)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Solving for flow gives the equation used to measure cardiac output:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick cardiac output</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Cardiac output equals oxygen consumption divided by arterial minus mixed venous oxygen content\">CO = V̇O₂ / (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂)</span></div>\n\n<p>Here <strong>V̇O₂</strong> = systemic oxygen consumed per minute; <strong>CaO₂</strong> = arterial oxygen content per unit blood volume; and <strong>CvO₂</strong> = <strong>mixed venous</strong> oxygen content. Be careful: <strong>oxygen content</strong>, <strong>oxygen saturation</strong> and <strong>partial pressure of oxygen</strong> are different quantities.</p>\n<h3>Worked example — the lecturer's diagram</h3>\n<p>Suppose the body consumes <strong>250 mL O₂/min</strong>. Arterial blood contains <strong>0.20 mL O₂ per mL of blood</strong> and mixed venous blood contains <strong>0.15 mL O₂ per mL of blood</strong>.</p>\n<p>Arterial–venous difference:</p>\n<p><strong>0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood</strong>.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>CO = 250 mL O₂/min ÷ (0.05 mL O₂/mL blood) = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min.</strong></p>\n<p>The units matter. <em>Millilitres of O₂</em> cancel, leaving <strong>millilitres of blood per minute</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Why doesn't the answer use venous oxygen saturation alone?</h3>\n<p>Because Fick's equation needs the <strong>quantity of oxygen present per volume of blood</strong>, not merely the percentage of haemoglobin-binding sites occupied. Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin concentration, saturation and a smaller dissolved oxygen component. This is particularly important in anaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-where-do-the-oxygen-measurements-come-from\">3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer notes two sampling sites:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arterial oxygen content:</strong> A sample of systemic arterial blood provides an estimate of oxygen-rich blood entering tissues.</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed venous oxygen content:</strong> A blood sample from the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong>, before the blood passes through the lungs, reflects blood returning from multiple systemic tissues.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the pulmonary artery for venous blood?</strong> The right ventricle receives systemic venous return from different organs. By the time it reaches the pulmonary artery, this blood is relatively mixed, so it better represents the whole body's oxygen extraction than a sample from one arm vein.</p>\n<p><strong>Why not simply measure a finger oxygen saturation?</strong> Pulse oximetry estimates saturation; it does not measure mixed venous oxygen content or directly determine how many litres of blood the heart pumps each minute.</p>\n<h3>Direct versus estimated Fick</h3>\n<p>A <strong>direct Fick measurement</strong> involves measuring actual oxygen consumption alongside arterial and mixed venous oxygen contents. Some clinical estimates use a predicted oxygen-consumption rate instead; this is a further assumption and can introduce error.</p>\n<p>The Fick method is most reliable when measurements reflect a reasonably <strong>steady physiological state</strong>. Rapidly changing oxygen demand, significant shunts, sampling errors or inaccurate oxygen-consumption estimates can impair results.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-does-the-indicator-dilution-idea-measure-blood-flow-supplementary\">4. How does the indicator-dilution idea measure blood flow? (Supplementary)</h2>\n<p>Lecture slide 53 includes a <strong>conservation-of-mass diagram</strong>, which is the underlying logic of several flow-measurement techniques.</p>\n<p>If a known quantity of a detectable indicator is introduced into a moving stream of blood, the downstream concentration changes as it becomes dispersed. When appropriately sampled, the amount injected and the time–concentration pattern can be used to infer the flow rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why?</strong> In faster flow, a given indicator dose generally becomes more diluted and passes the sampling site more rapidly than in slower flow.</p>\n<div class=\"fick-process\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Indicator dilution sequence\"><span><strong>Known indicator dose</strong><small>Introduced upstream</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Mixed into blood</strong><small>Carried downstream</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Concentration over time</strong><small>Flow inferred from first pass</small></span></div>\n\n<p>For a simple first-pass indicator dilution model, <strong>flow is inversely related to the area under the indicator concentration–time curve</strong> after a known bolus. Actual measurements require appropriate calibration and handling of recirculation.</p>\n<h3>Thermodilution — using temperature as the indicator (Supplementary)</h3>\n<p>A clinical variation introduces a known bolus of saline at a known temperature via a pulmonary artery catheter's proximal port. A downstream <strong>thermistor</strong> detects how blood temperature changes over time.</p>\n<p>The recorded temperature–time response can be used to estimate flow. It uses a related indicator-dilution principle, but the indicator is a <strong>thermal change</strong>, not a dye.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Method</th>\n  <th>What is measured?</th>\n  <th>Important limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fick</strong></td>\n  <td>O₂ consumption and arterial–mixed venous O₂-content difference</td>\n  <td>Requires valid sampling and steady-state assumptions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Dye indicator dilution</strong></td>\n  <td>Concentration of injected tracer over time</td>\n  <td>Injection/sampling and recirculation affect the curve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Thermodilution</strong></td>\n  <td>Temperature change over time after injected saline</td>\n  <td>Catheter position, valve regurgitation and shunts may distort results</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These methods are <strong>supplementary</strong> to the three primary slides. Their purpose here is to show how the lecture's conservation principle extends to other cardiac-output measurements.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-does-it-mean-for-the-heart-to-perform-work\">5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work?</h2>\n<p>The heart does more than move blood: it must move that blood <strong>against pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>In mechanics, work can be expressed as <strong>force × distance</strong>. Because pressure is force per area, moving a volume of fluid against pressure also requires work.</p>\n<p>The lecture gives a simplified relationship for one ventricular beat:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Approximate external stroke work</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"SW ≈ SV × P\">SW ≈ SV × P</span><span class=\"equation-example\">P represents the relevant pressure generated against the outflow circulation, usually approximated from mean arterial pressure; external stroke work is more rigorously the ventricular pressure–volume loop area, so this product is only an estimate.</span></div>\n\n<p>In ordinary teaching examples, <strong>stroke volume × mean arterial pressure</strong> provides a practical approximation. More accurately, <strong>external stroke work is the area within the ventricular pressure–volume loop</strong>, accounting for the pressure throughout filling and ejection. The simple product is not an exact substitute for every loop shape.</p>\n<h3>Why does the left ventricle perform more pressure work than the right?</h3>\n<p>The ventricles normally eject <strong>approximately equal volumes per minute</strong>, but the left ventricle ejects against much higher systemic arterial pressure. Therefore, its external pressure work is greater.</p>\n<p><strong>Example (illustrative):</strong> If SV = 70 mL and mean arterial pressure = 100 mmHg:</p>\n<p><strong>SW ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL per beat ≈ 0.93 J per beat.</strong></p>\n<p>The value is an approximate measure of <em>external stroke work</em>, not the total chemical energy consumed by myocardium.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-visualising-stroke-work-on-a-pressure-volume-loop\">6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure work-pv-figure\" id=\"cvs08-pv\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 580 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic left ventricular pressure-volume loop with end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes marked, filling at low pressure, isovolumetric contraction, ejection and relaxation, and interior stroke-work area shaded.\"><defs><marker id=\"workloop-arrow\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L6 3 L0 6z\" fill=\"#2863dd\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"64\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"557\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M208 216 C270 226 374 228 441 216 L441 77 C389 35 314 43 208 86 Z\" fill=\"#93c5fd\" opacity=\".28\"/><path d=\"M208 216 C290 231 362 229 441 216\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M441 216 L441 77\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><path d=\"M441 77 C389 35 314 43 208 86\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><path d=\"M208 86 L208 216\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"310\" y=\"143\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"800\">Stroke work</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"159\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈ loop area</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"255\">ESV</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"270\">EDV</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"273\">Ventricular volume →</text><text x=\"21\" y=\"148\" transform=\"rotate(-90,21,148)\">Pressure</text><text x=\"458\" y=\"135\">Isovolumetric</text><text x=\"458\" y=\"150\">contraction</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"121\">Isovolumetric</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"136\">relaxation</text><text x=\"307\" y=\"37\">Ejection</text><text x=\"306\" y=\"235\">Filling</text></g></svg><p>Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume.</p></figure>\n\n<p>During <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>, pressure rises but ventricular volume does not change. During <strong>ejection</strong>, ventricular volume falls and the heart generates pressure to move blood into the artery. During <strong>isovolumetric relaxation</strong>, pressure falls while volume remains approximately at ESV.</p>\n<p>The area inside this loop represents <strong>external stroke work</strong>. The myocardium also spends energy maintaining electrical activity, cycling Ca²⁺ and sustaining internal tension, so <strong>stroke work is not identical to total myocardial oxygen consumption</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer lists three major determinants of myocardial work and oxygen consumption:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intramyocardial tension (wall stress).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Contractile state</strong> of the myocardium.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart rate.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>When pressure loading rises, the ventricular wall must develop greater tension to eject blood. Greater force development and more frequent contractions usually increase myocardial metabolic demand and oxygen consumption.</p>\n<p>The lecturer states that <strong>pressure work is more oxygen-demanding than volume work</strong> and uses <strong>aortic stenosis</strong> (pressure loading) compared with <strong>aortic incompetence / regurgitation</strong> (volume loading) as examples.</p>\n<p><strong>How to interpret this safely:</strong> Pressure overload often increases wall stress and myocardial oxygen demand substantially, but it would be incorrect to assume that <em>all</em> patients with aortic stenosis have more angina than <em>all</em> patients with aortic regurgitation. Severity, coronary blood supply and other diseases matter.</p>\n<h3>Why does the heart need increased coronary flow during exercise?</h3>\n<p>The myocardium extracts a large fraction of oxygen from coronary blood even at rest. When the heart works harder, it cannot increase oxygen supply sufficiently by extraction alone. <strong>Coronary blood flow must increase</strong> to deliver more oxygen.</p>\n<p>This explains the connection between exertion, increased myocardial work and angina when the oxygen supply cannot match demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> The Fick calculation uses oxygen consumption by the <strong>whole body</strong> to measure systemic CO. Myocardial oxygen consumption is the heart's <strong>own</strong> metabolic demand; these are different quantities, even though both use oxygen-extraction principles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why can the Fick method calculate CO without directly measuring stroke volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Fick's principle uses <strong>conservation of oxygen</strong>. Systemic oxygen consumed each minute equals blood flow each minute multiplied by the oxygen removed from each unit of blood. Therefore cardiac output can be found by dividing oxygen consumption by the arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference, without measuring SV beat by beat.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. Oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min, arterial content 0.20 mL O₂/mL blood and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂/mL blood. Calculate CO.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The content difference is <strong>0.05 mL O₂/mL blood</strong>. CO = 250 ÷ 0.05 = <strong>5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min</strong>. The oxygen units cancel, leaving volume of blood per minute.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Why is mixed venous blood ideally sampled from the pulmonary artery rather than a peripheral vein?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The pulmonary artery contains venous return from many systemic organs after mixing in the right heart. A peripheral vein reflects extraction in a particular region and may not represent whole-body oxygen content. The Fick equation for total systemic flow needs a representative <strong>mixed venous</strong> sample.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. A person has normal arterial oxygen saturation but very low haemoglobin. Can saturation alone be substituted for arterial oxygen content in the Fick formula?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Saturation is a fraction of haemoglobin sites occupied by oxygen, whereas oxygen content is the <em>actual amount of oxygen per unit blood</em>. With low haemoglobin, oxygen content can be reduced despite high saturation, so substituting saturation alone makes the calculation invalid.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. A dye bolus is introduced into a blood stream. Why does the downstream concentration–time pattern contain information about flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The injected dye represents a known amount of indicator. Its concentration is diluted and transported by moving blood. With suitable sampling and a first-pass analysis, the indicator's total time–concentration exposure is inversely related to flow. Recirculation must be recognised rather than included as if it were the original pass.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. What is the principal measurable signal in thermodilution?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Temperature change over time.</strong> A known bolus of saline mixes with blood, and a downstream thermistor measures the resulting thermal signal. The measured pattern is used to estimate cardiac output using indicator-dilution principles. It does not directly count ventricular contractions or measure SV.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. Two ventricles eject equal stroke volumes, but one ejects against much higher mean arterial pressure. Which performs more external pressure work?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The ventricle pumping against <strong>higher pressure</strong> performs more approximate external stroke work: SW ≈ SV × mean ejection pressure. This is why the left ventricle normally performs more pressure work than the right despite similar long-term flow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. A ventricle ejects 70 mL against an approximate mean arterial pressure of 100 mmHg. Estimate external stroke work.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>SW ≈ 70 × 100 = <strong>7,000 mmHg·mL per beat</strong>, which is approximately <strong>0.93 J per beat</strong>. This is an external-work approximation; the myocardium consumes additional metabolic energy for processes not captured by this product.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Why does the pressure–volume loop have an area even though both isovolumetric phases involve no change in volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The isovolumetric phases change pressure at almost constant volume. Between them, <strong>ejection and filling</strong> occur at different pressures. The enclosed area between those pressure–volume paths represents the external mechanical work associated with one cardiac cycle.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why must coronary blood flow increase when myocardial work rises substantially?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Cardiac muscle already extracts a high proportion of the oxygen delivered by coronary blood at rest. When heart rate, force or wall tension increases, myocardial oxygen demand rises. Further extraction alone cannot meet the increase, so <strong>coronary flow must rise</strong> to sustain oxygen delivery.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Does a greater external stroke work always imply the same proportional increase in myocardial oxygen consumption?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> External work is only part of total myocardial energetic demand. Intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling, cross-bridge activation, basal metabolism, wall stress and contractile state also consume energy. The relation between external work and oxygen consumption therefore depends on cardiac conditions.</p></details>\n\n<h3>Additional application and mechanism checks</h3>\n<p><strong>Supplementary 1. How would an erroneously small arterial–venous oxygen-content difference affect a calculated Fick cardiac output at fixed oxygen consumption?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>CO equals oxygen consumption divided by the arterial–mixed venous content difference. Underestimating the denominator overestimates calculated output, so accurate content measurement is essential.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fick's principle:</strong> Whole-body oxygen consumption equals blood flow × arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference.</li>\n<li><strong>Fick cardiac output:</strong> CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂), with consistent units.</li>\n<li><strong>Sampling:</strong> Systemic arterial blood supplies arterial content; pulmonary artery blood supplies mixed venous content.</li>\n<li><strong>Content versus saturation:</strong> Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin and saturation; they are not interchangeable.</li>\n<li><strong>Steady state:</strong> Accuracy is limited when oxygen use, mixing or sampling assumptions are invalid.</li>\n<li><strong>Indicator dilution (supplementary):</strong> Known injected marker and concentration–time trace can estimate flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Thermodilution (supplementary):</strong> Uses a temperature–time response after injected saline.</li>\n<li><strong>External stroke work:</strong> Approximately SV × mean arterial pressure; more accurately the enclosed pressure–volume loop area.</li>\n<li><strong>Left versus right ventricular work:</strong> Similar blood volume ejected, but left side faces much higher pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Myocardial O₂ demand:</strong> Strongly influenced by wall tension, contractility and heart rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure loading:</strong> Often increases myocardial demand markedly; clinical comparisons need context.</li>\n<li><strong>Coronary flow:</strong> Increased myocardial oxygen demand usually requires increased coronary blood flow.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart 1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone? From Chapter 07, cardiac output (CO) = heart rate × stroke volume. If we know both factors, we can calculate output. But in clinical situations, stroke volume often cannot be measured simply by counting a pulse. Therefore, we need an independent way to estimate the volume of blood passing through a circulation each minute. The lecturer introduces Fick's principle, which uses oxygen exchange as a way to infer blood flow. The central idea is conservation of matter: oxygen used by a tissue must have been supplied by the blood entering it, minus the oxygen still present in blood leaving it. 2. Fick's principle — build the formula from the physical idea Imagine one litre of arterial blood enters a group of tissues with a certain amount of oxygen. The venous blood leaves with less oxygen because cells have extracted some. Two observations determine total oxygen use: How much oxygen is removed from each litre of blood? This is the arterial–venous oxygen-content difference. How many litres of blood flow through the tissues each minute? This is the blood flow rate, which equals systemic cardiac output in steady state. The tissue oxygen-consumption rate must equal the product of these quantities. Arterial O₂ content O₂ carried into tissues − Mixed venous O₂ content O₂ remaining after tissues → O₂ extracted Amount removed from each unit of blood Fick's oxygen principle V̇O₂ = CO × (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂) The relationship is easier to read in plain notation: O₂ consumption/min = blood flow/min × (arterial O₂ content − mixed venous O₂ content). Solving for flow gives the equation used to measure cardiac output: Fick cardiac output CO = V̇O₂ / (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂) Here V̇O₂ = systemic oxygen consumed per minute; CaO₂ = arterial oxygen content per unit blood volume; and CvO₂ = mixed venous oxygen content. Be careful: oxygen content, oxygen saturation and partial pressure of oxygen are different quantities. Worked example — the lecturer's diagram Suppose the body consumes 250 mL O₂/min. Arterial blood contains 0.20 mL O₂ per mL of blood and mixed venous blood contains 0.15 mL O₂ per mL of blood. Arterial–venous difference: 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood. Then: CO = 250 mL O₂/min ÷ (0.05 mL O₂/mL blood) = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min. The units matter. Millilitres of O₂ cancel, leaving millilitres of blood per minute. Why doesn't the answer use venous oxygen saturation alone? Because Fick's equation needs the quantity of oxygen present per volume of blood, not merely the percentage of haemoglobin-binding sites occupied. Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin concentration, saturation and a smaller dissolved oxygen component. This is particularly important in anaemia. 3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from? The lecturer notes two sampling sites: Arterial oxygen content: A sample of systemic arterial blood provides an estimate of oxygen-rich blood entering tissues. Mixed venous oxygen content: A blood sample from the pulmonary artery, before the blood passes through the lungs, reflects blood returning from multiple systemic tissues. Why the pulmonary artery for venous blood? The right ventricle receives systemic venous return from different organs. By the time it reaches the pulmonary artery, this blood is relatively mixed, so it better represents the whole body's oxygen extraction than a sample from one arm vein. Why not simply measure a finger oxygen saturation? Pulse oximetry estimates saturation; it does not measure mixed venous oxygen content or directly determine how many litres of blood the heart pumps each minute. Direct versus estimated Fick A direct Fick measurement involves measuring actual oxygen consumption alongside arterial and mixed venous oxygen contents. Some clinical estimates use a predicted oxygen-consumption rate instead; this is a further assumption and can introduce error. The Fick method is most reliable when measurements reflect a reasonably steady physiological state. Rapidly changing oxygen demand, significant shunts, sampling errors or inaccurate oxygen-consumption estimates can impair results. 4. How does the indicator-dilution idea measure blood flow? (Supplementary) Lecture slide 53 includes a conservation-of-mass diagram, which is the underlying logic of several flow-measurement techniques. If a known quantity of a detectable indicator is introduced into a moving stream of blood, the downstream concentration changes as it becomes dispersed. When appropriately sampled, the amount injected and the time–concentration pattern can be used to infer the flow rate. Why? In faster flow, a given indicator dose generally becomes more diluted and passes the sampling site more rapidly than in slower flow. Known indicator dose Introduced upstream → Mixed into blood Carried downstream → Concentration over time Flow inferred from first pass For a simple first-pass indicator dilution model, flow is inversely related to the area under the indicator concentration–time curve after a known bolus. Actual measurements require appropriate calibration and handling of recirculation. Thermodilution — using temperature as the indicator (Supplementary) A clinical variation introduces a known bolus of saline at a known temperature via a pulmonary artery catheter's proximal port. A downstream thermistor detects how blood temperature changes over time. The recorded temperature–time response can be used to estimate flow. It uses a related indicator-dilution principle, but the indicator is a thermal change, not a dye. Method What is measured? Important limitation Fick O₂ consumption and arterial–mixed venous O₂-content difference Requires valid sampling and steady-state assumptions Dye indicator dilution Concentration of injected tracer over time Injection/sampling and recirculation affect the curve Thermodilution Temperature change over time after injected saline Catheter position, valve regurgitation and shunts may distort results These methods are supplementary to the three primary slides. Their purpose here is to show how the lecture's conservation principle extends to other cardiac-output measurements. 5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work? The heart does more than move blood: it must move that blood against pressure. In mechanics, work can be expressed as force × distance. Because pressure is force per area, moving a volume of fluid against pressure also requires work. The lecture gives a simplified relationship for one ventricular beat: Approximate external stroke work SW ≈ SV × P P represents the relevant pressure generated against the outflow circulation, usually approximated from mean arterial pressure; external stroke work is more rigorously the ventricular pressure–volume loop area, so this product is only an estimate. In ordinary teaching examples, stroke volume × mean arterial pressure provides a practical approximation. More accurately, external stroke work is the area within the ventricular pressure–volume loop, accounting for the pressure throughout filling and ejection. The simple product is not an exact substitute for every loop shape. Why does the left ventricle perform more pressure work than the right? The ventricles normally eject approximately equal volumes per minute, but the left ventricle ejects against much higher systemic arterial pressure. Therefore, its external pressure work is greater. Example (illustrative): If SV = 70 mL and mean arterial pressure = 100 mmHg: SW ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL per beat ≈ 0.93 J per beat. The value is an approximate measure of external stroke work, not the total chemical energy consumed by myocardium. 6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop Figure 01. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work Stroke work ≈ loop area ESV EDV Ventricular volume → Pressure Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Ejection Filling Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume. During isovolumetric contraction, pressure rises but ventricular volume does not change. During ejection, ventricular volume falls and the heart generates pressure to move blood into the artery. During isovolumetric relaxation, pressure falls while volume remains approximately at ESV. The area inside this loop represents external stroke work. The myocardium also spends energy maintaining electrical activity, cycling Ca²⁺ and sustaining internal tension, so stroke work is not identical to total myocardial oxygen consumption. 7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand? The lecturer lists three major determinants of myocardial work and oxygen consumption: Intramyocardial tension (wall stress). Contractile state of the myocardium. Heart rate. When pressure loading rises, the ventricular wall must develop greater tension to eject blood. Greater force development and more frequent contractions usually increase myocardial metabolic demand and oxygen consumption. The lecturer states that pressure work is more oxygen-demanding than volume work and uses aortic stenosis (pressure loading) compared with aortic incompetence / regurgitation (volume loading) as examples. How to interpret this safely: Pressure overload often increases wall stress and myocardial oxygen demand substantially, but it would be incorrect to assume that all patients with aortic stenosis have more angina than all patients with aortic regurgitation. Severity, coronary blood supply and other diseases matter. Why does the heart need increased coronary flow during exercise? The myocardium extracts a large fraction of oxygen from coronary blood even at rest. When the heart works harder, it cannot increase oxygen supply sufficiently by extraction alone. Coronary blood flow must increase to deliver more oxygen. This explains the connection between exertion, increased myocardial work and angina when the oxygen supply cannot match demand. Important: The Fick calculation uses oxygen consumption by the whole body to measure systemic CO. Myocardial oxygen consumption is the heart's own metabolic demand; these are different quantities, even though both use oxygen-extraction principles. Understanding Check 1. Why can the Fick method calculate CO without directly measuring stroke volume? Reveal explanation Fick's principle uses conservation of oxygen. Systemic oxygen consumed each minute equals blood flow each minute multiplied by the oxygen removed from each unit of blood. Therefore cardiac output can be found by dividing oxygen consumption by the arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference, without measuring SV beat by beat. 2. Oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min, arterial content 0.20 mL O₂/mL blood and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂/mL blood. Calculate CO. Reveal explanation The content difference is 0.05 mL O₂/mL blood. CO = 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min. The oxygen units cancel, leaving volume of blood per minute. 3. Why is mixed venous blood ideally sampled from the pulmonary artery rather than a peripheral vein? Reveal explanation The pulmonary artery contains venous return from many systemic organs after mixing in the right heart. A peripheral vein reflects extraction in a particular region and may not represent whole-body oxygen content. The Fick equation for total systemic flow needs a representative mixed venous sample. 4. A person has normal arterial oxygen saturation but very low haemoglobin. Can saturation alone be substituted for arterial oxygen content in the Fick formula? Reveal explanation No. Saturation is a fraction of haemoglobin sites occupied by oxygen, whereas oxygen content is the actual amount of oxygen per unit blood. With low haemoglobin, oxygen content can be reduced despite high saturation, so substituting saturation alone makes the calculation invalid. 5. A dye bolus is introduced into a blood stream. Why does the downstream concentration–time pattern contain information about flow? Reveal explanation The injected dye represents a known amount of indicator. Its concentration is diluted and transported by moving blood. With suitable sampling and a first-pass analysis, the indicator's total time–concentration exposure is inversely related to flow. Recirculation must be recognised rather than included as if it were the original pass. 6. What is the principal measurable signal in thermodilution? Reveal explanation Temperature change over time. A known bolus of saline mixes with blood, and a downstream thermistor measures the resulting thermal signal. The measured pattern is used to estimate cardiac output using indicator-dilution principles. It does not directly count ventricular contractions or measure SV. 7. Two ventricles eject equal stroke volumes, but one ejects against much higher mean arterial pressure. Which performs more external pressure work? Reveal explanation The ventricle pumping against higher pressure performs more approximate external stroke work: SW ≈ SV × mean ejection pressure. This is why the left ventricle normally performs more pressure work than the right despite similar long-term flow. 8. A ventricle ejects 70 mL against an approximate mean arterial pressure of 100 mmHg. Estimate external stroke work. Reveal explanation SW ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL per beat, which is approximately 0.93 J per beat. This is an external-work approximation; the myocardium consumes additional metabolic energy for processes not captured by this product. 9. Why does the pressure–volume loop have an area even though both isovolumetric phases involve no change in volume? Reveal explanation The isovolumetric phases change pressure at almost constant volume. Between them, ejection and filling occur at different pressures. The enclosed area between those pressure–volume paths represents the external mechanical work associated with one cardiac cycle. 10. Why must coronary blood flow increase when myocardial work rises substantially? Reveal explanation Cardiac muscle already extracts a high proportion of the oxygen delivered by coronary blood at rest. When heart rate, force or wall tension increases, myocardial oxygen demand rises. Further extraction alone cannot meet the increase, so coronary flow must rise to sustain oxygen delivery. 11. Does a greater external stroke work always imply the same proportional increase in myocardial oxygen consumption? Reveal explanation No. External work is only part of total myocardial energetic demand. Intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling, cross-bridge activation, basal metabolism, wall stress and contractile state also consume energy. The relation between external work and oxygen consumption therefore depends on cardiac conditions. Additional application and mechanism checks Supplementary 1. How would an erroneously small arterial–venous oxygen-content difference affect a calculated Fick cardiac output at fixed oxygen consumption? Reveal explanation CO equals oxygen consumption divided by the arterial–mixed venous content difference. Underestimating the denominator overestimates calculated output, so accurate content measurement is essential. Chapter Essentials Fick's principle: Whole-body oxygen consumption equals blood flow × arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference. Fick cardiac output: CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂), with consistent units. Sampling: Systemic arterial blood supplies arterial content; pulmonary artery blood supplies mixed venous content. Content versus saturation: Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin and saturation; they are not interchangeable. Steady state: Accuracy is limited when oxygen use, mixing or sampling assumptions are invalid. Indicator dilution (supplementary): Known injected marker and concentration–time trace can estimate flow. Thermodilution (supplementary): Uses a temperature–time response after injected saline. External stroke work: Approximately SV × mean arterial pressure; more accurately the enclosed pressure–volume loop area. Left versus right ventricular work: Similar blood volume ejected, but left side faces much higher pressure. Myocardial O₂ demand: Strongly influenced by wall tension, contractility and heart rate. Pressure loading: Often increases myocardial demand markedly; clinical comparisons need context. Coronary flow: Increased myocardial oxygen demand usually requires increased coronary blood flow."},{"slug":"hemodynamics","title":"Blood Flow and Hemodynamics","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"55-74","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-propels-blood-around-the-circulation","title":"1. What propels blood around the circulation?"},{"id":"2-what-actually-determines-flow","title":"2. What actually determines flow?"},{"id":"3-why-do-different-vessels-have-different-roles","title":"3. Why do different vessels have different roles?"},{"id":"4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries","title":"4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries?"},{"id":"5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number","title":"5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number"},{"id":"6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow","title":"6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow?"},{"id":"7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells","title":"7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells"},{"id":"8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels","title":"8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels?"},{"id":"9-how-can-blood-flow-be-measured","title":"9. How can blood flow be measured?"},{"id":"10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries","title":"10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Blood Flow and Hemodynamics</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-propels-blood-around-the-circulation\">1. What propels blood around the circulation?</h2>\n<p>The heart supplies the energy that establishes a pressure gradient. Blood moves through <strong>arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins → heart</strong>.</p>\n<p>Additional mechanisms include <strong>elastic recoil</strong> of large arteries, the <strong>skeletal-muscle pump</strong> compressing veins and the <strong>respiratory pump</strong> using changing intrathoracic pressures. These assist blood flow at different points; they do not replace the heart as its main pressure source.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does flow continue during ventricular diastole?</strong> Large elastic arteries stretch during systole, storing energy; their recoil helps sustain pressure and forward flow between ejections. One-way valves in many veins help prevent backward flow during muscle compression.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-actually-determines-flow\">2. What actually determines flow?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>effective perfusion pressure</strong> is the difference between upstream and downstream vascular pressure. For the same resistance, a larger pressure difference produces greater flow.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Pressure, flow and resistance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = (ΔP) / (R) R = (ΔP) / (Q)\">Q = (ΔP) / (R) R = (ΔP) / (Q)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> A pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a system whose resistance is 20 mmHg·min/L produces flow of <strong>4 L/min</strong>. This is an arithmetic illustration, not a specific organ's reference flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> High pressure at one point does not, by itself, guarantee high flow. Blood flow depends on the <strong>pressure drop across the pathway</strong> and its resistance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-do-different-vessels-have-different-roles\">3. Why do different vessels have different roles?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Segment</th>\n  <th>Special structure</th>\n  <th>Primary role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Large arteries</strong></td>\n  <td>Elastic walls</td>\n  <td>Store pressure energy in systole and recoil in diastole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arterioles</strong></td>\n  <td>Relatively abundant smooth muscle</td>\n  <td>Regulate resistance and organ perfusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Capillaries</strong></td>\n  <td>Very thin endothelial walls</td>\n  <td>Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Veins</strong></td>\n  <td>Distensible thin walls, often valves</td>\n  <td>Store blood and support its return</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why are arterioles called resistance vessels?</strong> Small muscular arterioles can change their radius rapidly. Even slight constriction can strongly increase resistance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why are veins called capacitance vessels?</strong> Their high compliance lets them accommodate substantial extra blood with a comparatively modest rise in pressure. Noradrenergic venoconstriction can shift blood from venous reservoirs towards the heart.</p>\n<p>Key structures include capillary sphincters, endothelial gaps and fenestrations. <strong>Careful correction:</strong> microscopic gaps and fenestrations are not <em>millimetres</em> wide as the slide's unit suggests. Not every capillary has its own sphincter; local control varies with tissue. These details receive fuller treatment in the microcirculation chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymphatic overview:</strong> Lymph vessels collect excess interstitial fluid and proteins, have valves and often traverse lymph nodes before large lymphatic ducts return fluid near the subclavian–internal jugular venous junctions. Detailed lymphatic physiology follows in its dedicated chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries\">4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries?</h2>\n<p><strong>Volume flow (Q)</strong> is the quantity of blood per unit time; <strong>velocity (v)</strong> is the distance travelled per unit time. They are different measurements.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Continuity relation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = vA v = (Q) / (A)\">Q = vA v = (Q) / (A)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The crucial word is TOTAL.</strong> Each capillary is narrow, but there are enormously many in parallel. Their combined cross-sectional area is far larger than that of the aorta.</p>\n<p>Therefore, at comparable overall volume flow, mean velocity decreases as blood travels from the aorta towards capillary beds, then increases again towards larger veins as the total area falls. Slow capillary flow also permits more time for exchange.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number</h2>\n<p>In ideal <strong>laminar flow</strong>, fluid travels in organised layers. The layer touching the vessel wall is slowest because of friction at the wall, while the centre moves faster. This gives the familiar smooth, approximately parabolic velocity profile in a straight tube.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure hemo-flow-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 540 165\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An idealised vessel with multiple flow arrows, the longest arrows at the centre and shortest nearest the vessel wall.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"34\" width=\"465\" height=\"99\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9f1ff\" stroke=\"#8caccb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g stroke=\"#2162be\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M89 49H179m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 72H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 96H446m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 118H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"41\" y=\"22\">Near the wall: slow</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"22\">Near centre: fast</text><text x=\"41\" y=\"153\">Original schematic — not a measured flow profile</text></g></svg><p>The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Turbulent or disturbed flow</strong> has changing local velocity directions, often generating audible sounds such as bruits or murmurs. It is made more likely by high velocity, sudden changes in vessel geometry and lower blood viscosity.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Reynolds number (Re)</strong> helps describe the tendency for flow disturbances to become turbulent:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Reynolds number</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Re = (ρvD) / (η)\">Re = (ρvD) / (η)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity.</span></div>\n\n<p>Higher Reynolds number means a <strong>greater likelihood</strong> of turbulent flow, not a guarantee. The vessel's curvature, pulsatility, branching and stenoses also matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Two different symbols:</strong> <strong>R</strong> denotes vascular resistance in the flow law; <strong>Re</strong> denotes <em>Reynolds number</em>, which is dimensionless. Despite their similar symbols, they are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Anaemia:</strong> Lower viscosity and a compensatory increase in flow can promote a systolic flow murmur without structural valve disease. <strong>Local narrowing:</strong> Velocity may accelerate through a stenosis, creating disturbed downstream flow and a vascular bruit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow\">6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Hagen–Poiseuille equation</strong> describes ideal steady laminar flow through a long, straight, rigid circular tube:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Hagen–Poiseuille equation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = (πΔPr^4) / (8ηL)\">Q = (πΔPr^4) / (8ηL)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity.</span></div>\n\n<p>Combined with <strong>R = ΔP/Q</strong>, this gives:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ideal tube resistance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"R = (8ηL) / (πr^4)\">R = (8ηL) / (πr^4)</span></div>\n\n<p>The key relationship is <strong>R ∝ 1/r⁴</strong>. When radius decreases, resistance increases dramatically.</p>\n<div class=\"hemo-radius-impact\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Effect of radius on ideal vessel resistance\"><div><strong>Radius halved</strong><span>Resistance ×16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16</small></div><div><strong>Radius doubled</strong><span>Resistance ÷16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold</small></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Worked mechanism:</strong> If the radius changes from r to r/2, the new resistance is <strong>2⁴ = 16</strong> times larger, <em>provided length and viscosity remain unchanged</em>. With a fixed pressure difference, Q = ΔP/R means flow falls to one-sixteenth.</p>\n<p>These relationships are <strong>mathematical aids</strong>, not exact universal predictions: blood is non-Newtonian, vessels are compliant rather than rigid, and vascular tone and pressures change in living people.</p>\n<h3>Does a faster jet at a stenosis mean total blood flow increases?</h3>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> At the narrowed throat, cross-sectional area decreases and velocity can rise locally, even while the stenosis increases resistance and reduces overall blood flow. A high <em>local speed</em> must not be confused with a high <em>volume flow rate</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells\">7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells</h2>\n<p><strong>Viscosity</strong> describes internal resistance to flow. Blood viscosity depends on <strong>haematocrit, plasma proteins, temperature, and red-cell deformation or aggregation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most important implications are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polycythaemia:</strong> More red cells generally increase viscosity, raising viscous resistance and cardiac work.</li>\n<li><strong>Anaemia:</strong> Viscosity is often lower, but reduced oxygen-carrying capacity can increase cardiac-output demand and produce a flow murmur.</li>\n<li><strong>Abnormal red-cell rigidity:</strong> Conditions such as sickle-cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis can alter rheology.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the very smallest vessels, the apparent viscosity differs from predictions for blood in large rigid tubes because cells change their distribution and deformation. Therefore, slide 71's suggestion that viscosity has little effect in all small vessels should be treated as a <strong>context-dependent approximation</strong>, not a universal statement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels?</h2>\n<p>The vascular network contains vessels <strong>in series</strong> (one after another) and <strong>in parallel</strong> (multiple paths serving different tissues).</p>\n<p>With vessels in series, pressure drops add. With many open pathways in parallel, the overall resistance is lower than that of a single pathway alone.</p>\n<p>This explains an apparent puzzle: each capillary has a tiny radius, but there are very many capillaries <strong>in parallel</strong>. Arterioles, with their active muscular control of diameter, account for a large part of the important pressure drop and resistance regulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Physiological application:</strong> Constricting arterioles reduces flow into a tissue at the same perfusion pressure. Dilation lowers local resistance and can increase flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-how-can-blood-flow-be-measured\">9. How can blood flow be measured?</h2>\n<p>Three techniques are commonly used to measure flow, but they detect different physical signals:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Method</th>\n  <th>What it measures or detects</th>\n  <th>How it relates to flow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Electromagnetic flowmeter</strong></td>\n  <td>Voltage induced by conducting blood in a magnetic field</td>\n  <td>Used to estimate blood movement through the field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Doppler ultrasound flowmeter</strong></td>\n  <td>Frequency shift from moving blood cells</td>\n  <td>Estimates velocity along the ultrasound beam</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Plethysmography</strong></td>\n  <td>Changes in tissue or limb volume</td>\n  <td>Can estimate blood inflow with appropriate experimental conditions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> Doppler primarily estimates <strong>velocity</strong>, not volume flow on its own. To estimate total flow, an estimate of the vessel's area and the velocity profile is also required.</p>\n<p>The lecture mentions a historical <strong>arm-to-tongue circulation time around 15 seconds</strong>. This is an illustrative transit-time estimate, not a fixed physiological constant for every person.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries\">10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries?</h2>\n<p><strong>Vascular compliance</strong> describes how much volume a vessel can accommodate for a given pressure change.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Compliance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)\">C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)</span></div>\n\n<p>Large veins are more compliant than arteries. Their volume can rise considerably before venous pressure rises by the same amount. This is why veins are called <strong>capacitance vessels</strong>, whereas arterioles are commonly described as <strong>resistance vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>An approximate resting blood-volume distribution places a large share in the venous system. The listed percentages are <strong>illustrative, not an exact complete partition</strong>; their values do not add to 100% and vary with physiological conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does venoconstriction help cardiac output?</strong> When the venous system becomes less distensible, stored blood can be redirected towards the heart. This can increase venous return, end-diastolic volume and stroke volume through the Frank–Starling mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why does blood keep moving through the arteries during ventricular diastole?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Large elastic arteries stretch during ventricular systole and store pressure energy. Their recoil during diastole sustains arterial pressure and the pressure gradient towards smaller vessels, so blood can keep moving between ventricular ejections.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. An organ has an upstream-to-downstream pressure difference of 80 mmHg and a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L. What is its flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Q = ΔP/R = 80/20 = 4 L/min.</strong> The pressure difference must be measured across the pathway; pressure at one end by itself does not establish the flow rate. The numbers are for arithmetic practice, not a named organ.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Why is mean capillary blood velocity low despite each capillary having a narrow lumen?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The capillary system has an enormous number of vessels in <strong>parallel</strong>. Their <strong>combined</strong> cross-sectional area is much greater than that of the aorta. Because v = Q/A, the same overall volume flow moving through a much larger combined area has a lower average velocity.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Why does ideal laminar flow have a greater velocity in the centre than beside the wall?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Fluid in immediate contact with the vessel wall moves slowly because of viscous boundary effects. Adjacent layers drag on one another, forming a velocity gradient. Flow towards the centre is less constrained and therefore faster. Real blood can depart from the ideal profile because it contains cells.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. A vessel's radius falls to half its original value at unchanged ΔP, viscosity and length. Predict resistance and flow.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Hagen–Poiseuille gives <strong>R ∝ 1/r⁴</strong>. Halving the radius therefore increases the predicted resistance by <strong>16 times</strong>. With unchanged perfusion pressure and Q = ΔP/R, volume flow decreases to <strong>1/16</strong> of its previous value, under ideal-tube assumptions.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. A stenosis produces a very high velocity at its narrowest point. Must total blood flow through the artery have increased?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Local velocity is volume flow divided by local area, so speed can rise as the cross-sectional area narrows even if volume flow remains the same or decreases. The narrowing may also raise resistance and reduce total flow, depending on the pressure gradient.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. Why may anaemia produce a systolic flow murmur even with normal valves?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Anaemia often reduces blood viscosity and increases cardiac output to compensate for reduced oxygen-carrying capacity. Higher flow velocity and reduced viscosity can increase the tendency towards disturbed flow, producing audible vibrations without a structurally abnormal valve.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. Distinguish vascular resistance R from the Reynolds number Re.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Resistance R</strong> relates the pressure difference across a pathway to the resulting volume flow. <strong>Reynolds number Re</strong> is dimensionless and compares effects of density, velocity, diameter and viscosity to assess the tendency towards turbulent flow. The two quantities are not equivalent.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Why are arterioles the main adjustable resistance vessels instead of capillaries?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Arterioles have smooth muscle capable of rapidly changing their radius and therefore flow resistance. Although individual capillaries are narrow, many operate in parallel and their overall equivalent resistance is reduced. Changing arteriolar tone strongly influences organ perfusion and systemic resistance.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. What does Doppler ultrasound directly estimate, and what else would be needed to estimate volume flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Doppler detects frequency shifts from moving blood cells and estimates a component of <strong>velocity</strong>. Volume flow also depends on the cross-sectional area and the velocity profile across the vessel, so estimating Q requires more information than a single Doppler velocity reading.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why do veins store more additional blood than arteries for a similar rise in pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Veins have a greater <strong>compliance (C = ΔV/ΔP)</strong>. They accommodate a larger volume increase for a given pressure rise, acting as blood reservoirs. Arterioles instead use their muscular walls to regulate resistance.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Polycythaemia and anaemia may both increase cardiac workload. How do their mechanisms differ?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>In <strong>polycythaemia</strong>, greater haematocrit usually increases viscosity and can raise flow resistance. In <strong>anaemia</strong>, reduced oxygen-carrying capacity often demands greater cardiac output to deliver enough oxygen, producing increased volume flow and workload even when viscosity is lower.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Flow:</strong> Q = ΔP/R; a pressure <em>difference</em>, not isolated vessel pressure, drives net flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Diastolic arterial flow:</strong> Elastic recoil helps maintain forward flow between ventricular ejections.</li>\n<li><strong>Vessel functions:</strong> Elastic arteries, resistance arterioles, exchange capillaries and capacitance veins have distinct roles.</li>\n<li><strong>Velocity versus flow:</strong> Q = vA; the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is enormous.</li>\n<li><strong>Laminar flow:</strong> Fastest near the centre and slowest by the wall in the ideal model.</li>\n<li><strong>Reynolds number:</strong> Re = ρvD/η, a dimensionless measure of turbulence tendency, not vascular resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Hagen–Poiseuille:</strong> Q = πΔPr⁴/(8ηL) for ideal-tube flow; R = 8ηL/(πr⁴).</li>\n<li><strong>Radius effect:</strong> Halving radius increases ideal resistance sixteenfold at unchanged other factors.</li>\n<li><strong>Arteriolar control:</strong> Small changes in muscular-vessel radius substantially regulate blood flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Viscosity:</strong> Depends on haematocrit, plasma components and red-cell properties; blood is not an ideal fluid.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement:</strong> Electromagnetic, Doppler and plethysmographic techniques detect different flow-related signals.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance:</strong> C = ΔV/ΔP; veins accommodate large volume changes at comparatively low pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Small-vessel and lymphatic physiology:</strong> Introduced here, developed in subsequent chapters.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Blood Flow and Hemodynamics 1. What propels blood around the circulation? The heart supplies the energy that establishes a pressure gradient. Blood moves through arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins → heart. Additional mechanisms include elastic recoil of large arteries, the skeletal-muscle pump compressing veins and the respiratory pump using changing intrathoracic pressures. These assist blood flow at different points; they do not replace the heart as its main pressure source. Why does flow continue during ventricular diastole? Large elastic arteries stretch during systole, storing energy; their recoil helps sustain pressure and forward flow between ejections. One-way valves in many veins help prevent backward flow during muscle compression. 2. What actually determines flow? The effective perfusion pressure is the difference between upstream and downstream vascular pressure. For the same resistance, a larger pressure difference produces greater flow. Pressure, flow and resistance Q = (ΔP) / (R) R = (ΔP) / (Q) Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance. Example: A pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a system whose resistance is 20 mmHg·min/L produces flow of 4 L/min. This is an arithmetic illustration, not a specific organ's reference flow. Important: High pressure at one point does not, by itself, guarantee high flow. Blood flow depends on the pressure drop across the pathway and its resistance. 3. Why do different vessels have different roles? Segment Special structure Primary role Large arteries Elastic walls Store pressure energy in systole and recoil in diastole Arterioles Relatively abundant smooth muscle Regulate resistance and organ perfusion Capillaries Very thin endothelial walls Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid Veins Distensible thin walls, often valves Store blood and support its return Why are arterioles called resistance vessels? Small muscular arterioles can change their radius rapidly. Even slight constriction can strongly increase resistance. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? Their high compliance lets them accommodate substantial extra blood with a comparatively modest rise in pressure. Noradrenergic venoconstriction can shift blood from venous reservoirs towards the heart. Key structures include capillary sphincters, endothelial gaps and fenestrations. Careful correction: microscopic gaps and fenestrations are not millimetres wide as the slide's unit suggests. Not every capillary has its own sphincter; local control varies with tissue. These details receive fuller treatment in the microcirculation chapter. Lymphatic overview: Lymph vessels collect excess interstitial fluid and proteins, have valves and often traverse lymph nodes before large lymphatic ducts return fluid near the subclavian–internal jugular venous junctions. Detailed lymphatic physiology follows in its dedicated chapter. 4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries? Volume flow (Q) is the quantity of blood per unit time; velocity (v) is the distance travelled per unit time. They are different measurements. Continuity relation Q = vA v = (Q) / (A) A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered. The crucial word is TOTAL. Each capillary is narrow, but there are enormously many in parallel. Their combined cross-sectional area is far larger than that of the aorta. Therefore, at comparable overall volume flow, mean velocity decreases as blood travels from the aorta towards capillary beds, then increases again towards larger veins as the total area falls. Slow capillary flow also permits more time for exchange. 5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number In ideal laminar flow, fluid travels in organised layers. The layer touching the vessel wall is slowest because of friction at the wall, while the centre moves faster. This gives the familiar smooth, approximately parabolic velocity profile in a straight tube. Figure 01. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile Near the wall: slow Near centre: fast Original schematic — not a measured flow profile The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic. Turbulent or disturbed flow has changing local velocity directions, often generating audible sounds such as bruits or murmurs. It is made more likely by high velocity, sudden changes in vessel geometry and lower blood viscosity. The Reynolds number (Re) helps describe the tendency for flow disturbances to become turbulent: Reynolds number Re = (ρvD) / (η) ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity. Higher Reynolds number means a greater likelihood of turbulent flow, not a guarantee. The vessel's curvature, pulsatility, branching and stenoses also matter. Two different symbols: R denotes vascular resistance in the flow law; Re denotes Reynolds number, which is dimensionless. Despite their similar symbols, they are not interchangeable. Anaemia: Lower viscosity and a compensatory increase in flow can promote a systolic flow murmur without structural valve disease. Local narrowing: Velocity may accelerate through a stenosis, creating disturbed downstream flow and a vascular bruit. 6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow? The Hagen–Poiseuille equation describes ideal steady laminar flow through a long, straight, rigid circular tube: Hagen–Poiseuille equation Q = (πΔPr^4) / (8ηL) r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity. Combined with R = ΔP/Q, this gives: Ideal tube resistance R = (8ηL) / (πr^4) The key relationship is R ∝ 1/r⁴. When radius decreases, resistance increases dramatically. Radius halved Resistance ×16 At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16 Radius doubled Resistance ÷16 At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold Worked mechanism: If the radius changes from r to r/2, the new resistance is 2⁴ = 16 times larger, provided length and viscosity remain unchanged. With a fixed pressure difference, Q = ΔP/R means flow falls to one-sixteenth. These relationships are mathematical aids, not exact universal predictions: blood is non-Newtonian, vessels are compliant rather than rigid, and vascular tone and pressures change in living people. Does a faster jet at a stenosis mean total blood flow increases? No. At the narrowed throat, cross-sectional area decreases and velocity can rise locally, even while the stenosis increases resistance and reduces overall blood flow. A high local speed must not be confused with a high volume flow rate. 7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells Viscosity describes internal resistance to flow. Blood viscosity depends on haematocrit, plasma proteins, temperature, and red-cell deformation or aggregation. The most important implications are: Polycythaemia: More red cells generally increase viscosity, raising viscous resistance and cardiac work. Anaemia: Viscosity is often lower, but reduced oxygen-carrying capacity can increase cardiac-output demand and produce a flow murmur. Abnormal red-cell rigidity: Conditions such as sickle-cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis can alter rheology. In the very smallest vessels, the apparent viscosity differs from predictions for blood in large rigid tubes because cells change their distribution and deformation. Therefore, slide 71's suggestion that viscosity has little effect in all small vessels should be treated as a context-dependent approximation, not a universal statement. 8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels? The vascular network contains vessels in series (one after another) and in parallel (multiple paths serving different tissues). With vessels in series, pressure drops add. With many open pathways in parallel, the overall resistance is lower than that of a single pathway alone. This explains an apparent puzzle: each capillary has a tiny radius, but there are very many capillaries in parallel. Arterioles, with their active muscular control of diameter, account for a large part of the important pressure drop and resistance regulation. Physiological application: Constricting arterioles reduces flow into a tissue at the same perfusion pressure. Dilation lowers local resistance and can increase flow. 9. How can blood flow be measured? Three techniques are commonly used to measure flow, but they detect different physical signals: Method What it measures or detects How it relates to flow Electromagnetic flowmeter Voltage induced by conducting blood in a magnetic field Used to estimate blood movement through the field Doppler ultrasound flowmeter Frequency shift from moving blood cells Estimates velocity along the ultrasound beam Plethysmography Changes in tissue or limb volume Can estimate blood inflow with appropriate experimental conditions Key distinction: Doppler primarily estimates velocity, not volume flow on its own. To estimate total flow, an estimate of the vessel's area and the velocity profile is also required. The lecture mentions a historical arm-to-tongue circulation time around 15 seconds. This is an illustrative transit-time estimate, not a fixed physiological constant for every person. 10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries? Vascular compliance describes how much volume a vessel can accommodate for a given pressure change. Compliance C = (ΔV) / (ΔP) Large veins are more compliant than arteries. Their volume can rise considerably before venous pressure rises by the same amount. This is why veins are called capacitance vessels, whereas arterioles are commonly described as resistance vessels. An approximate resting blood-volume distribution places a large share in the venous system. The listed percentages are illustrative, not an exact complete partition; their values do not add to 100% and vary with physiological conditions. Why does venoconstriction help cardiac output? When the venous system becomes less distensible, stored blood can be redirected towards the heart. This can increase venous return, end-diastolic volume and stroke volume through the Frank–Starling mechanism. Understanding Check 1. Why does blood keep moving through the arteries during ventricular diastole? Reveal explanation Large elastic arteries stretch during ventricular systole and store pressure energy. Their recoil during diastole sustains arterial pressure and the pressure gradient towards smaller vessels, so blood can keep moving between ventricular ejections. 2. An organ has an upstream-to-downstream pressure difference of 80 mmHg and a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L. What is its flow? Reveal explanation Q = ΔP/R = 80/20 = 4 L/min. The pressure difference must be measured across the pathway; pressure at one end by itself does not establish the flow rate. The numbers are for arithmetic practice, not a named organ. 3. Why is mean capillary blood velocity low despite each capillary having a narrow lumen? Reveal explanation The capillary system has an enormous number of vessels in parallel. Their combined cross-sectional area is much greater than that of the aorta. Because v = Q/A, the same overall volume flow moving through a much larger combined area has a lower average velocity. 4. Why does ideal laminar flow have a greater velocity in the centre than beside the wall? Reveal explanation Fluid in immediate contact with the vessel wall moves slowly because of viscous boundary effects. Adjacent layers drag on one another, forming a velocity gradient. Flow towards the centre is less constrained and therefore faster. Real blood can depart from the ideal profile because it contains cells. 5. A vessel's radius falls to half its original value at unchanged ΔP, viscosity and length. Predict resistance and flow. Reveal explanation Hagen–Poiseuille gives R ∝ 1/r⁴. Halving the radius therefore increases the predicted resistance by 16 times. With unchanged perfusion pressure and Q = ΔP/R, volume flow decreases to 1/16 of its previous value, under ideal-tube assumptions. 6. A stenosis produces a very high velocity at its narrowest point. Must total blood flow through the artery have increased? Reveal explanation No. Local velocity is volume flow divided by local area, so speed can rise as the cross-sectional area narrows even if volume flow remains the same or decreases. The narrowing may also raise resistance and reduce total flow, depending on the pressure gradient. 7. Why may anaemia produce a systolic flow murmur even with normal valves? Reveal explanation Anaemia often reduces blood viscosity and increases cardiac output to compensate for reduced oxygen-carrying capacity. Higher flow velocity and reduced viscosity can increase the tendency towards disturbed flow, producing audible vibrations without a structurally abnormal valve. 8. Distinguish vascular resistance R from the Reynolds number Re. Reveal explanation Resistance R relates the pressure difference across a pathway to the resulting volume flow. Reynolds number Re is dimensionless and compares effects of density, velocity, diameter and viscosity to assess the tendency towards turbulent flow. The two quantities are not equivalent. 9. Why are arterioles the main adjustable resistance vessels instead of capillaries? Reveal explanation Arterioles have smooth muscle capable of rapidly changing their radius and therefore flow resistance. Although individual capillaries are narrow, many operate in parallel and their overall equivalent resistance is reduced. Changing arteriolar tone strongly influences organ perfusion and systemic resistance. 10. What does Doppler ultrasound directly estimate, and what else would be needed to estimate volume flow? Reveal explanation Doppler detects frequency shifts from moving blood cells and estimates a component of velocity. Volume flow also depends on the cross-sectional area and the velocity profile across the vessel, so estimating Q requires more information than a single Doppler velocity reading. 11. Why do veins store more additional blood than arteries for a similar rise in pressure? Reveal explanation Veins have a greater compliance (C = ΔV/ΔP). They accommodate a larger volume increase for a given pressure rise, acting as blood reservoirs. Arterioles instead use their muscular walls to regulate resistance. 12. Polycythaemia and anaemia may both increase cardiac workload. How do their mechanisms differ? Reveal explanation In polycythaemia, greater haematocrit usually increases viscosity and can raise flow resistance. In anaemia, reduced oxygen-carrying capacity often demands greater cardiac output to deliver enough oxygen, producing increased volume flow and workload even when viscosity is lower. Chapter Essentials Flow: Q = ΔP/R; a pressure difference, not isolated vessel pressure, drives net flow. Diastolic arterial flow: Elastic recoil helps maintain forward flow between ventricular ejections. Vessel functions: Elastic arteries, resistance arterioles, exchange capillaries and capacitance veins have distinct roles. Velocity versus flow: Q = vA; the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is enormous. Laminar flow: Fastest near the centre and slowest by the wall in the ideal model. Reynolds number: Re = ρvD/η, a dimensionless measure of turbulence tendency, not vascular resistance. Hagen–Poiseuille: Q = πΔPr⁴/(8ηL) for ideal-tube flow; R = 8ηL/(πr⁴). Radius effect: Halving radius increases ideal resistance sixteenfold at unchanged other factors. Arteriolar control: Small changes in muscular-vessel radius substantially regulate blood flow. Viscosity: Depends on haematocrit, plasma components and red-cell properties; blood is not an ideal fluid. Measurement: Electromagnetic, Doppler and plethysmographic techniques detect different flow-related signals. Compliance: C = ΔV/ΔP; veins accommodate large volume changes at comparatively low pressure. Small-vessel and lymphatic physiology: Introduced here, developed in subsequent chapters."},{"slug":"blood-pressure","title":"Arterial Blood Pressure","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"76-83","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-exactly-is-arterial-blood-pressure","title":"1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure?"},{"id":"2-why-doesn-t-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole","title":"2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole?"},{"id":"3-why-do-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure-depend-on-more-than-heart-rate","title":"3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate?"},{"id":"4-what-is-pulse-pressure","title":"4. What is pulse pressure?"},{"id":"5-what-is-mean-arterial-pressure-and-why-isn-t-it-simply-the-midpoint","title":"5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint?"},{"id":"6-why-does-measured-bp-depend-on-whether-the-arm-is-above-or-below-the-heart","title":"6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart?"},{"id":"7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure","title":"7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure?"},{"id":"8-how-should-bp-actually-be-measured-accurately","title":"8. How should BP actually be measured accurately?"},{"id":"9-why-does-pressure-decrease-as-blood-travels-through-the-circulation","title":"9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Blood Pressure</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-exactly-is-arterial-blood-pressure\">1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure?</h2>\n<p><strong>Arterial blood pressure</strong> is the lateral pressure exerted by circulating blood against the arterial walls. It changes throughout each heartbeat because the left ventricle ejects blood intermittently.</p>\n<p>Two characteristic values are normally reported:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Measurement</th>\n  <th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Systolic blood pressure (SBP)</strong></td>\n  <td>Highest arterial pressure reached during ventricular systole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Diastolic blood pressure (DBP)</strong></td>\n  <td>Lowest arterial pressure before the next ventricular ejection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A reading such as <strong>120/70 mmHg</strong> means systolic pressure is 120 mmHg and diastolic pressure is 70 mmHg. These are <strong>illustrative numbers from the lecture</strong>, not an instruction to diagnose everyone's BP against one fixed value.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does pressure oscillate?</strong> Ventricular ejection causes a rise in aortic blood volume and pressure. Between ejections, arterial pressure gradually falls because blood continues escaping into the smaller vessels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-doesn-t-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole\">2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole?</h2>\n<p>At first glance, this seems puzzling. During diastole the aortic valve is closed, so no new blood is being ejected into the aorta. Yet blood still moves through the systemic arteries.</p>\n<p>This is the <strong>Windkessel effect</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In <strong>systole</strong>, the ventricle ejects blood into the aorta. Elastic arterial walls <strong>stretch</strong> and temporarily store part of the pressure energy.</li>\n<li>In <strong>diastole</strong>, those walls <strong>recoil</strong>, releasing stored energy as blood continues flowing downstream, even with the aortic valve closed.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure bp-windkessel-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Windkessel mechanism — elastic arterial energy storage and recoil</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two original arterial diagrams: during systole the large artery widens as blood enters and the wall expands; during diastole the artery recoils and sustains forward flow with the aortic valve closed.\"><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\"><text x=\"15\" y=\"21\">Systole</text><text x=\"15\" y=\"105\">Diastole</text></g><g stroke=\"#bd515a\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#fce1e0\"><path d=\"M124 42 C200 23 290 23 357 42 L475 42 L475 81 L357 81 C290 100 200 100 124 81 Z\"/><path d=\"M124 118 C219 112 290 112 357 118 L475 118 L475 143 L357 143 C290 149 219 149 124 143 Z\"/></g><g stroke=\"#2162be\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M147 60H447m-13 -8 13 8 -13 8\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M147 131H447m-13 -8 13 8 -13 8\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11.5\"><text x=\"488\" y=\"64\">Flow →</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"137\">Flow →</text><text x=\"213\" y=\"34\">Artery expands</text><text x=\"213\" y=\"160\">Artery recoils</text></g></svg><p>Original schematic showing that arterial elasticity converts intermittent ventricular ejection into a more continuous downstream flow. Not drawn to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<p>This does <strong>not</strong> mean arterial pressure remains constant. It still declines during diastole; recoil merely slows the decline and preserves forward flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical connection:</strong> A stiffer aorta stretches less for a given stroke volume, so systolic pressure and pulse pressure may rise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-do-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure-depend-on-more-than-heart-rate\">3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate?</h2>\n<p>Blood pressure reflects the interaction between <strong>cardiac output, vascular resistance, circulating volume and arterial compliance</strong>.</p>\n<p>A useful steady-state systemic relationship is:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Mean pressure and flow</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR\">MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR</span><span class=\"equation-example\">MAP = mean arterial pressure; CVP = central venous pressure; SVR = systemic vascular resistance.</span></div>\n\n<p>If CVP is small relative to MAP, a common approximation is <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong>. Therefore an increased heart rate is not sufficient by itself to establish what BP will do. Stroke volume, resistance and compensation also matter.</p>\n<p>For example, during haemorrhage, sympathetic vasoconstriction may help preserve BP even while circulating blood volume and cardiac output are falling. <strong>Maintained BP does not guarantee adequate tissue perfusion.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-is-pulse-pressure\">4. What is pulse pressure?</h2>\n<p><strong>Pulse pressure (PP)</strong> is the difference between systolic and diastolic arterial pressure.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Pulse pressure</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"PP = SBP − DBP\">PP = SBP − DBP</span><span class=\"equation-example\">For 120/70 mmHg: <strong>PP = 50 mmHg</strong>.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why does the pulse occur?</strong> Each ventricular ejection raises arterial pressure and expands the arterial wall. A pressure wave travels through the arteries; it is not the same thing as a particular bolus of blood travelling at the speed of the palpable pulse.</p>\n<p>Pulse pressure is influenced by <strong>stroke volume and arterial compliance</strong>, among other factors. A greater SV tends to expand the aorta more; a stiffer artery develops a larger pressure rise for the same added blood volume. Thus <strong>a wide pulse pressure is not automatically proof of increased stroke volume</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-is-mean-arterial-pressure-and-why-isn-t-it-simply-the-midpoint\">5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint?</h2>\n<p><strong>Mean arterial pressure (MAP)</strong> is the <strong>average arterial pressure over an entire cardiac cycle</strong>. At usual resting heart rates, the heart typically spends more time in diastole than systole. The mean pressure therefore lies closer to the diastolic value than to the arithmetic midpoint.</p>\n<p>The approximation is:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Approximate mean arterial pressure</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"MAP ≈ DBP + (1) / (3)(SBP − DBP)\">MAP ≈ DBP + (1) / (3)(SBP − DBP)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">At ordinary resting heart rates: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure.</span></div>\n\n<p>For <strong>120/70 mmHg</strong>, pulse pressure is 50 mmHg, so <strong>MAP ≈ 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this an estimate?</strong> The exact average depends on the <em>shape and duration</em> of the arterial pressure waveform. When heart rate or waveform shape changes markedly, the one-third approximation becomes less reliable. True mean arterial pressure is the time average across the cycle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-does-measured-bp-depend-on-whether-the-arm-is-above-or-below-the-heart\">6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart?</h2>\n<p><strong>Gravity</strong> matters: pressure readings must be interpreted relative to heart level.</p>\n<p>Fluid pressure in a vertical column changes according to the height difference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Hydrostatic pressure change</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"ΔP = ρgh\">ΔP = ρgh</span><span class=\"equation-example\">ρ = fluid density; g = gravitational acceleration; h = vertical height difference.</span></div>\n\n<p>For whole blood, this corresponds to <strong>about 0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre</strong> of vertical displacement.</p>\n<div class=\"bp-height-rules\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Arm height measurement bias\"><div><strong>Arm 20 cm below heart</strong><span>About +15–16 mmHg</span><small>Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading higher</small></div><div><strong>Arm 20 cm above heart</strong><span>About −15–16 mmHg</span><small>Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading lower</small></div></div>\n\n<p>These are approximate physics examples for the height component, not predictions of a person's entire measured BP response to changing posture.</p>\n<p><strong>Why support the arm?</strong> If the person holds the arm up using their own muscles, isometric exertion can itself alter BP. So the arm should rest on a support <strong>at heart level</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure\">7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure?</h2>\n<p>The traditional <strong>auscultatory method</strong> uses an upper-arm cuff, pressure gauge and stethoscope over the <strong>brachial artery</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Phase A — Cuff pressure higher than systolic pressure</h3>\n<p>The cuff compresses the brachial artery sufficiently to stop flow beneath it. <strong>No Korotkoff sounds are heard</strong>, because blood is not passing through the compressed segment.</p>\n<h3>Phase B — Cuff pressure falls just below systolic pressure</h3>\n<p>During each systolic peak, arterial pressure is now high enough to force some blood through the partially compressed artery. Flow becomes rapid and disturbed, generating the first characteristic tapping sounds.</p>\n<p><strong>The first consistent Korotkoff sound (Phase I) marks systolic pressure.</strong></p>\n<h3>Phase C — Cuff pressure lies between systolic and diastolic pressures</h3>\n<p>The artery opens during part of the cardiac cycle but is still compressed during another part. Sounds continue and may change in pitch, loudness or character as cuff pressure decreases.</p>\n<h3>Phase D — Cuff pressure falls below diastolic pressure</h3>\n<p>The artery can remain open throughout the cycle, so the sounds ultimately disappear as the obstruction is relieved.</p>\n<p><strong>Disappearance of Korotkoff sounds (Phase V) generally marks diastolic pressure in adults.</strong> In some circumstances, muffling (Phase IV) may be used rather than disappearance.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Cuff pressure</th>\n  <th>Arterial state</th>\n  <th>What is heard?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Above SBP</strong></td>\n  <td>Artery occluded</td>\n  <td>Silence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Just below SBP</strong></td>\n  <td>Intermittent systolic flow begins</td>\n  <td><strong>First tapping sound = SBP</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Between SBP and DBP</strong></td>\n  <td>Partially compressed artery</td>\n  <td>Korotkoff sounds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Below DBP</strong></td>\n  <td>Flow no longer significantly obstructed</td>\n  <td><strong>Sounds disappear = DBP</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why are these sounds not ordinary S1 and S2?</strong> They are generated locally in the <em>partly compressed brachial artery</em> during cuff deflation, rather than by closing heart valves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-how-should-bp-actually-be-measured-accurately\">8. How should BP actually be measured accurately?</h2>\n<p>Blood pressure can be assessed through <strong>auscultation, palpation, direct arterial cannulation and manometry</strong>. For a conventional manual cuff measurement:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Let the patient <strong>rest quietly</strong>, ideally for at least five minutes, with back supported and feet flat when seated; avoid talking during measurement.</li>\n<li>Use a <strong>correctly sized cuff</strong> on the bare upper arm, supported at <strong>heart level</strong>.</li>\n<li>Palpate the brachial or radial pulse to estimate systolic pressure and help avoid missing an auscultatory gap.</li>\n<li>Inflate sufficiently above the palpated systolic level, then <strong>deflate gradually</strong> (about 2 mmHg per second) while listening over the brachial artery.</li>\n<li>Record the <strong>first consistent Korotkoff sound</strong> as systolic and the <strong>disappearance</strong> as diastolic for most adult measurements.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>What does palpation alone tell you?</h3>\n<p>A palpated return of the radial pulse during cuff deflation estimates <strong>systolic pressure</strong>. It <strong>cannot reliably identify diastolic pressure</strong>, because palpation does not provide the necessary information about the Korotkoff sound phases.</p>\n<h3>Why do cuff sizes matter?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Technique error</th>\n  <th>Typical direction of error</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Cuff too small</strong> for the arm</td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>high</strong> reading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Cuff too large</strong> for the arm</td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>low</strong> reading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Arm hangs <strong>below</strong> heart level</td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>high</strong> reading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Arm held <strong>above</strong> heart level</td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>low</strong> reading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Talking, unsupported arm or recent exertion</td>\n  <td>Can alter the reading, often upward</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The original lecture explains the importance of larger cuff sizes for larger arms and appropriately sized cuffs for children. The error is about the <strong>cuff dimensions relative to arm circumference</strong>, not merely body weight.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplementary:</strong> Automated oscillometric monitors estimate BP using cuff-pressure oscillations and validated algorithms. Their displayed values are not derived by a human listening for Korotkoff sounds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-why-does-pressure-decrease-as-blood-travels-through-the-circulation\">9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation?</h2>\n<p>The mean pressure does not remain constant from the aorta to the venae cavae. Some pressure energy is lost as blood flows against resistance.</p>\n<p>The largest mean pressure fall in the systemic circulation usually occurs across the <strong>arterioles</strong>, consistent with their role as resistance vessels.</p>\n<div class=\"bp-drop-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Main locations of systemic pressure decrease\"><span><strong>Aorta / arteries</strong><small>High pressure · strong pulses</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Arterioles</strong><small>Large pressure drop</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Capillaries / veins</strong><small>Lower pressure · damped pulses</small></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why do pulses diminish in the microcirculation?</strong> Pulsatile energy is progressively damped as blood passes through elastic vessels and resistance pathways. Capillary pressure and velocity also differ from aortic values.</p>\n<p>The fall in mean pressure is <strong>not evenly distributed</strong> across all segments. Arteriolar tone can therefore affect both the amount of blood reaching tissues and the systemic pressure upstream.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why does blood keep flowing through arteries during diastole, even though the aortic valve is closed?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>During ventricular systole, the aorta and large elastic arteries stretch and store energy. During diastole, their <strong>elastic recoil</strong> maintains an arterial-to-peripheral pressure gradient, permitting continuing forward flow. Pressure declines but does not immediately become zero when ejection stops.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. A student's BP is 120/70 mmHg. Calculate pulse pressure and approximate MAP.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>PP = 120 − 70 = 50 mmHg.</strong> At an ordinary resting heart rate, <strong>MAP ≈ DBP + PP/3 = 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg</strong>. MAP is the average over time, so this is an approximation rather than the simple midpoint of SBP and DBP.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Why is the mean arterial pressure closer to diastolic than systolic pressure at usual resting heart rates?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>At typical resting rates, the cardiac cycle spends more time in diastole than at the highest systolic pressure. MAP is a <strong>time-weighted average</strong> across the waveform, so the longer diastolic interval gives the lower pressure more weight. The exact result varies with waveform and heart rate.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. A person's upper-arm cuff is positioned 20 cm below heart level. What approximate hydrostatic error would you expect?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The hydrostatic component is about <strong>0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre</strong> for whole blood. At 20 cm below heart level, the observed pressure may be approximately <strong>15–16 mmHg higher</strong> because of that height difference. The arm should be supported at heart level for a comparable reading.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. Why can a very small BP cuff produce a falsely high blood pressure reading?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>A cuff too small for the upper-arm circumference does not transmit its indicated pressure uniformly or effectively enough to compress the artery. The observer may need to inflate to a <strong>higher recorded cuff pressure</strong> to occlude the vessel, so the displayed BP can be falsely elevated.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure. Why are Korotkoff sounds initially absent?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The cuff has compressed the brachial artery so that blood cannot pass beneath it. Without intermittent blood flow through the partly compressed segment, there is no local sound-generating vibration. The first tapping sounds emerge when cuff pressure falls just below the systolic peak.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. What physiological event causes the first audible Korotkoff sound during deflation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>When cuff pressure falls just below the arterial <strong>systolic pressure</strong>, blood begins to pass through the partly compressed brachial artery during each pressure peak. The resulting disturbed flow and vessel vibration produce the first tapping sounds (Phase I), which estimate SBP.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. Why does the disappearance of Korotkoff sounds generally indicate diastolic pressure in adults?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Once cuff pressure falls sufficiently below diastolic arterial pressure, the artery can remain open throughout the cycle rather than becoming intermittently compressed. The flow-related sounds disappear (Phase V), which is commonly used as the adult DBP endpoint.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Why can't palpation alone determine both systolic and diastolic pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Palpation can detect when the distal pulse returns as cuff pressure falls, providing a useful estimate of <strong>systolic pressure</strong>. It does not identify the pattern or disappearance of Korotkoff sounds, so it cannot reliably determine <strong>diastolic pressure</strong> by this method.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why can a stiff aorta have a wider pulse pressure than a more compliant aorta at the same stroke volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>A compliant aorta expands more when the same blood volume enters it, limiting the pressure increase. A stiffer aorta expands less, so the same stroke volume can produce a larger systolic pressure rise and often a <strong>wider pulse pressure</strong>. Stroke volume and compliance both influence the result.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. A patient with severe blood loss develops tachycardia and vasoconstriction, yet tissue perfusion is poor. Why might BP be misleading?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Haemorrhage lowers venous return, EDV and stroke volume. Reflex tachycardia and increased systemic vascular resistance can temporarily support mean arterial pressure, but <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong> may still be too low to meet tissue oxygen needs. A near-normal pressure does not ensure adequate blood flow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why is the largest fall in systemic mean pressure often across arterioles rather than uniformly across all vessels?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Arterioles have relatively narrow lumens and active smooth muscle that regulates resistance. They are a major resistance segment, so a substantial fraction of the perfusion-pressure difference is expended across them. This allows arteriolar tone to govern local flow and strongly influence upstream arterial pressure.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Arterial pressure:</strong> SBP is the systolic peak and DBP the minimum pressure before the next ejection.</li>\n<li><strong>Windkessel effect:</strong> Elastic artery expansion during systole and recoil in diastole maintain forward flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Mean pressure and resistance:</strong> MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR; heart rate alone does not determine BP.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulse pressure:</strong> PP = SBP − DBP; affected by stroke volume and arterial compliance.</li>\n<li><strong>Mean arterial pressure:</strong> MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓PP at usual resting heart rates; it is a time average, not an arithmetic midpoint.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity:</strong> Hydrostatic pressure changes by approximately 0.77–0.78 mmHg for each centimetre of vertical displacement of whole blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Cuff at heart level:</strong> Below heart → falsely high; above heart → falsely low hydrostatic contribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Korotkoff Phase I:</strong> First tapping sound during cuff deflation estimates SBP.</li>\n<li><strong>Korotkoff Phase V:</strong> Disappearance of sounds generally estimates adult DBP.</li>\n<li><strong>Palpation:</strong> Can estimate systolic but not reliably diastolic pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Cuff sizing:</strong> Too small commonly reads falsely high; too large commonly reads falsely low.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement quality:</strong> Quiet rest, supported arm, bare upper arm, correct cuff and gradual deflation matter.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure through vessels:</strong> Largest mean pressure drop occurs across resistance arterioles; pulsations progressively dampen.</li>\n<li><strong>Clinical reasoning:</strong> Maintained pressure does not guarantee normal cardiac output or tissue oxygen delivery.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Arterial Blood Pressure 1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure? Arterial blood pressure is the lateral pressure exerted by circulating blood against the arterial walls. It changes throughout each heartbeat because the left ventricle ejects blood intermittently. Two characteristic values are normally reported: Measurement What it means Systolic blood pressure (SBP) Highest arterial pressure reached during ventricular systole Diastolic blood pressure (DBP) Lowest arterial pressure before the next ventricular ejection A reading such as 120/70 mmHg means systolic pressure is 120 mmHg and diastolic pressure is 70 mmHg. These are illustrative numbers from the lecture, not an instruction to diagnose everyone's BP against one fixed value. Why does pressure oscillate? Ventricular ejection causes a rise in aortic blood volume and pressure. Between ejections, arterial pressure gradually falls because blood continues escaping into the smaller vessels. 2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole? At first glance, this seems puzzling. During diastole the aortic valve is closed, so no new blood is being ejected into the aorta. Yet blood still moves through the systemic arteries. This is the Windkessel effect: In systole, the ventricle ejects blood into the aorta. Elastic arterial walls stretch and temporarily store part of the pressure energy. In diastole, those walls recoil, releasing stored energy as blood continues flowing downstream, even with the aortic valve closed. Figure 01. Windkessel mechanism — elastic arterial energy storage and recoil Systole Diastole Flow → Flow → Artery expands Artery recoils Original schematic showing that arterial elasticity converts intermittent ventricular ejection into a more continuous downstream flow. Not drawn to scale. This does not mean arterial pressure remains constant. It still declines during diastole; recoil merely slows the decline and preserves forward flow. Clinical connection: A stiffer aorta stretches less for a given stroke volume, so systolic pressure and pulse pressure may rise. 3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate? Blood pressure reflects the interaction between cardiac output, vascular resistance, circulating volume and arterial compliance. A useful steady-state systemic relationship is: Mean pressure and flow MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR MAP = mean arterial pressure; CVP = central venous pressure; SVR = systemic vascular resistance. If CVP is small relative to MAP, a common approximation is MAP ≈ CO × SVR. Therefore an increased heart rate is not sufficient by itself to establish what BP will do. Stroke volume, resistance and compensation also matter. For example, during haemorrhage, sympathetic vasoconstriction may help preserve BP even while circulating blood volume and cardiac output are falling. Maintained BP does not guarantee adequate tissue perfusion. 4. What is pulse pressure? Pulse pressure (PP) is the difference between systolic and diastolic arterial pressure. Pulse pressure PP = SBP − DBP For 120/70 mmHg: PP = 50 mmHg. Why does the pulse occur? Each ventricular ejection raises arterial pressure and expands the arterial wall. A pressure wave travels through the arteries; it is not the same thing as a particular bolus of blood travelling at the speed of the palpable pulse. Pulse pressure is influenced by stroke volume and arterial compliance, among other factors. A greater SV tends to expand the aorta more; a stiffer artery develops a larger pressure rise for the same added blood volume. Thus a wide pulse pressure is not automatically proof of increased stroke volume. 5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint? Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is the average arterial pressure over an entire cardiac cycle. At usual resting heart rates, the heart typically spends more time in diastole than systole. The mean pressure therefore lies closer to the diastolic value than to the arithmetic midpoint. The approximation is: Approximate mean arterial pressure MAP ≈ DBP + (1) / (3)(SBP − DBP) At ordinary resting heart rates: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure. For 120/70 mmHg, pulse pressure is 50 mmHg, so MAP ≈ 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg. Why is this an estimate? The exact average depends on the shape and duration of the arterial pressure waveform. When heart rate or waveform shape changes markedly, the one-third approximation becomes less reliable. True mean arterial pressure is the time average across the cycle. 6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart? Gravity matters: pressure readings must be interpreted relative to heart level. Fluid pressure in a vertical column changes according to the height difference: Hydrostatic pressure change ΔP = ρgh ρ = fluid density; g = gravitational acceleration; h = vertical height difference. For whole blood, this corresponds to about 0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre of vertical displacement. Arm 20 cm below heart About +15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading higher Arm 20 cm above heart About −15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading lower These are approximate physics examples for the height component, not predictions of a person's entire measured BP response to changing posture. Why support the arm? If the person holds the arm up using their own muscles, isometric exertion can itself alter BP. So the arm should rest on a support at heart level. 7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure? The traditional auscultatory method uses an upper-arm cuff, pressure gauge and stethoscope over the brachial artery. Phase A — Cuff pressure higher than systolic pressure The cuff compresses the brachial artery sufficiently to stop flow beneath it. No Korotkoff sounds are heard, because blood is not passing through the compressed segment. Phase B — Cuff pressure falls just below systolic pressure During each systolic peak, arterial pressure is now high enough to force some blood through the partially compressed artery. Flow becomes rapid and disturbed, generating the first characteristic tapping sounds. The first consistent Korotkoff sound (Phase I) marks systolic pressure. Phase C — Cuff pressure lies between systolic and diastolic pressures The artery opens during part of the cardiac cycle but is still compressed during another part. Sounds continue and may change in pitch, loudness or character as cuff pressure decreases. Phase D — Cuff pressure falls below diastolic pressure The artery can remain open throughout the cycle, so the sounds ultimately disappear as the obstruction is relieved. Disappearance of Korotkoff sounds (Phase V) generally marks diastolic pressure in adults. In some circumstances, muffling (Phase IV) may be used rather than disappearance. Cuff pressure Arterial state What is heard? Above SBP Artery occluded Silence Just below SBP Intermittent systolic flow begins First tapping sound = SBP Between SBP and DBP Partially compressed artery Korotkoff sounds Below DBP Flow no longer significantly obstructed Sounds disappear = DBP Why are these sounds not ordinary S1 and S2? They are generated locally in the partly compressed brachial artery during cuff deflation, rather than by closing heart valves. 8. How should BP actually be measured accurately? Blood pressure can be assessed through auscultation, palpation, direct arterial cannulation and manometry. For a conventional manual cuff measurement: Let the patient rest quietly, ideally for at least five minutes, with back supported and feet flat when seated; avoid talking during measurement. Use a correctly sized cuff on the bare upper arm, supported at heart level. Palpate the brachial or radial pulse to estimate systolic pressure and help avoid missing an auscultatory gap. Inflate sufficiently above the palpated systolic level, then deflate gradually (about 2 mmHg per second) while listening over the brachial artery. Record the first consistent Korotkoff sound as systolic and the disappearance as diastolic for most adult measurements. What does palpation alone tell you? A palpated return of the radial pulse during cuff deflation estimates systolic pressure. It cannot reliably identify diastolic pressure, because palpation does not provide the necessary information about the Korotkoff sound phases. Why do cuff sizes matter? Technique error Typical direction of error Cuff too small for the arm Falsely high reading Cuff too large for the arm Falsely low reading Arm hangs below heart level Falsely high reading Arm held above heart level Falsely low reading Talking, unsupported arm or recent exertion Can alter the reading, often upward The original lecture explains the importance of larger cuff sizes for larger arms and appropriately sized cuffs for children. The error is about the cuff dimensions relative to arm circumference, not merely body weight. Supplementary: Automated oscillometric monitors estimate BP using cuff-pressure oscillations and validated algorithms. Their displayed values are not derived by a human listening for Korotkoff sounds. 9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation? The mean pressure does not remain constant from the aorta to the venae cavae. Some pressure energy is lost as blood flows against resistance. The largest mean pressure fall in the systemic circulation usually occurs across the arterioles, consistent with their role as resistance vessels. Aorta / arteries High pressure · strong pulses → Arterioles Large pressure drop → Capillaries / veins Lower pressure · damped pulses Why do pulses diminish in the microcirculation? Pulsatile energy is progressively damped as blood passes through elastic vessels and resistance pathways. Capillary pressure and velocity also differ from aortic values. The fall in mean pressure is not evenly distributed across all segments. Arteriolar tone can therefore affect both the amount of blood reaching tissues and the systemic pressure upstream. Understanding Check 1. Why does blood keep flowing through arteries during diastole, even though the aortic valve is closed? Reveal explanation During ventricular systole, the aorta and large elastic arteries stretch and store energy. During diastole, their elastic recoil maintains an arterial-to-peripheral pressure gradient, permitting continuing forward flow. Pressure declines but does not immediately become zero when ejection stops. 2. A student's BP is 120/70 mmHg. Calculate pulse pressure and approximate MAP. Reveal explanation PP = 120 − 70 = 50 mmHg. At an ordinary resting heart rate, MAP ≈ DBP + PP/3 = 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg. MAP is the average over time, so this is an approximation rather than the simple midpoint of SBP and DBP. 3. Why is the mean arterial pressure closer to diastolic than systolic pressure at usual resting heart rates? Reveal explanation At typical resting rates, the cardiac cycle spends more time in diastole than at the highest systolic pressure. MAP is a time-weighted average across the waveform, so the longer diastolic interval gives the lower pressure more weight. The exact result varies with waveform and heart rate. 4. A person's upper-arm cuff is positioned 20 cm below heart level. What approximate hydrostatic error would you expect? Reveal explanation The hydrostatic component is about 0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre for whole blood. At 20 cm below heart level, the observed pressure may be approximately 15–16 mmHg higher because of that height difference. The arm should be supported at heart level for a comparable reading. 5. Why can a very small BP cuff produce a falsely high blood pressure reading? Reveal explanation A cuff too small for the upper-arm circumference does not transmit its indicated pressure uniformly or effectively enough to compress the artery. The observer may need to inflate to a higher recorded cuff pressure to occlude the vessel, so the displayed BP can be falsely elevated. 6. A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure. Why are Korotkoff sounds initially absent? Reveal explanation The cuff has compressed the brachial artery so that blood cannot pass beneath it. Without intermittent blood flow through the partly compressed segment, there is no local sound-generating vibration. The first tapping sounds emerge when cuff pressure falls just below the systolic peak. 7. What physiological event causes the first audible Korotkoff sound during deflation? Reveal explanation When cuff pressure falls just below the arterial systolic pressure, blood begins to pass through the partly compressed brachial artery during each pressure peak. The resulting disturbed flow and vessel vibration produce the first tapping sounds (Phase I), which estimate SBP. 8. Why does the disappearance of Korotkoff sounds generally indicate diastolic pressure in adults? Reveal explanation Once cuff pressure falls sufficiently below diastolic arterial pressure, the artery can remain open throughout the cycle rather than becoming intermittently compressed. The flow-related sounds disappear (Phase V), which is commonly used as the adult DBP endpoint. 9. Why can't palpation alone determine both systolic and diastolic pressure? Reveal explanation Palpation can detect when the distal pulse returns as cuff pressure falls, providing a useful estimate of systolic pressure. It does not identify the pattern or disappearance of Korotkoff sounds, so it cannot reliably determine diastolic pressure by this method. 10. Why can a stiff aorta have a wider pulse pressure than a more compliant aorta at the same stroke volume? Reveal explanation A compliant aorta expands more when the same blood volume enters it, limiting the pressure increase. A stiffer aorta expands less, so the same stroke volume can produce a larger systolic pressure rise and often a wider pulse pressure. Stroke volume and compliance both influence the result. 11. A patient with severe blood loss develops tachycardia and vasoconstriction, yet tissue perfusion is poor. Why might BP be misleading? Reveal explanation Haemorrhage lowers venous return, EDV and stroke volume. Reflex tachycardia and increased systemic vascular resistance can temporarily support mean arterial pressure, but CO = HR × SV may still be too low to meet tissue oxygen needs. A near-normal pressure does not ensure adequate blood flow. 12. Why is the largest fall in systemic mean pressure often across arterioles rather than uniformly across all vessels? Reveal explanation Arterioles have relatively narrow lumens and active smooth muscle that regulates resistance. They are a major resistance segment, so a substantial fraction of the perfusion-pressure difference is expended across them. This allows arteriolar tone to govern local flow and strongly influence upstream arterial pressure. Chapter Essentials Arterial pressure: SBP is the systolic peak and DBP the minimum pressure before the next ejection. Windkessel effect: Elastic artery expansion during systole and recoil in diastole maintain forward flow. Mean pressure and resistance: MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR; heart rate alone does not determine BP. Pulse pressure: PP = SBP − DBP; affected by stroke volume and arterial compliance. Mean arterial pressure: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓PP at usual resting heart rates; it is a time average, not an arithmetic midpoint. Gravity: Hydrostatic pressure changes by approximately 0.77–0.78 mmHg for each centimetre of vertical displacement of whole blood. Cuff at heart level: Below heart → falsely high; above heart → falsely low hydrostatic contribution. Korotkoff Phase I: First tapping sound during cuff deflation estimates SBP. Korotkoff Phase V: Disappearance of sounds generally estimates adult DBP. Palpation: Can estimate systolic but not reliably diastolic pressure. Cuff sizing: Too small commonly reads falsely high; too large commonly reads falsely low. Measurement quality: Quiet rest, supported arm, bare upper arm, correct cuff and gradual deflation matter. Pressure through vessels: Largest mean pressure drop occurs across resistance arterioles; pulsations progressively dampen. Clinical reasoning: Maintained pressure does not guarantee normal cardiac output or tissue oxygen delivery."},{"slug":"pulses","title":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"22-26","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-an-arterial-pulse","title":"1. What is an arterial pulse?"},{"id":"2-what-do-we-assess-when-palpating-a-pulse","title":"2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse?"},{"id":"3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck","title":"3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck?"},{"id":"4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents","title":"4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents?"},{"id":"5-how-do-you-distinguish-the-jvp-from-the-carotid-pulse","title":"5. How do you distinguish the JVP from the carotid pulse?"},{"id":"6-how-is-the-jvp-estimated-at-the-bedside","title":"6. How is the JVP estimated at the bedside?"},{"id":"7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important","title":"7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important?"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-an-arterial-pulse\">1. What is an arterial pulse?</h2>\n<p>Each time the left ventricle ejects, it pushes a fresh volume of blood into an aorta that is already full. The aortic wall stretches and the pressure inside it rises. That stretch and pressure rise travel down the arteries as a <strong><span data-term=\"pulse-wave\">wave</span></strong>, like a ripple running along a tight hose. When the wave passes under your finger at the wrist, the artery wall bulges briefly: that bulge is the <strong>pulse</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"pulse-mechanism\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"How an arterial pulse arises\"><span>LV ejection</span><b>→</b><span>Aortic pressure ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Arterial wall expands</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure wave travels</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The pulse is the wave, not the blood.</strong> As the lecture stresses, the pulse does not depend on how fast the blood is moving. The wave races ahead: about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, and faster further out. It reaches the wrist in roughly a tenth of a second. The blood itself moves far more slowly, well under 1 m/s on average in the aorta. So when you feel the pulse, the blood that left the heart in that beat is still nowhere near your wrist.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does the wave speed up?</strong> A stiff tube passes a pressure wave on faster than a stretchy one, because a stiff wall barely gives, so the pressure change is handed on quickly. The lecture gives two examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The wave gets faster further from the heart.</strong> The smaller arteries are stiffer than the stretchy aorta.</li>\n<li><strong>The wave gets faster with age.</strong> The arteries become more rigid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In both cases it is the wave that speeds up, not the blood.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Wave speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall): about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, 7–10 m/s in large branches, and faster in small arteries.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-what-do-we-assess-when-palpating-a-pulse\">2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse?</h2>\n<p>The lecture lists five features:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Feature</th>\n  <th>What you check</th>\n  <th>What it tells you</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Rate</strong></td>\n  <td>Beats per minute</td>\n  <td>How many beats are reaching the arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Volume</strong></td>\n  <td>How big the bulge under your finger is</td>\n  <td>Mainly the <span data-term=\"pulse-pressure\">pulse pressure</span>: the rise from diastolic to systolic pressure, which grows with <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Strength</strong></td>\n  <td>How firmly it pushes against your finger</td>\n  <td>How much pressure is in the artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Character</strong></td>\n  <td>The shape of each beat: how fast it rises and falls</td>\n  <td>How the left ventricle ejects, and how the aortic valve is working</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Location</strong></td>\n  <td>Which artery (radial, carotid, femoral…), and whether both sides match</td>\n  <td>A weak or missing pulse on one side points to a blockage on that side</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Whether the rhythm is <strong>regular</strong> is also worth noting.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can the wrist count fewer beats than the heart makes?</strong> In a very fast or irregular rhythm, some beats come so soon that the ventricle has hardly filled. Those beats eject too little blood to make a pulse you can feel. The pulse at the wrist can then be slower than the heart rate counted with a stethoscope. This difference is a <strong><span data-term=\"pulse-deficit\">pulse deficit</span></strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck\">3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck?</h2>\n<p>There is no valve between the right atrium and the large veins of the neck. So, as the lecture explains, pressure changes in the right atrium pass straight up into the neck veins, and you can see them. The <strong><span data-term=\"jvp\">internal jugular vein</span></strong> acts like a gauge attached to the right atrium: the higher the right atrial pressure, the higher up the neck the column of blood reaches.</p>\n<p><strong>Pressure moves; the blood doesn't have to.</strong> A pressure change travels up the column of blood even while the blood itself keeps flowing down towards the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can you see it but not feel it?</strong> Venous pressure is only a few mmHg, far too low to push against a finger. Even light pressure squashes the vein flat. What you see is a soft, flickering movement of the skin. The lecture notes that the JVP is not palpable.</p>\n<p><strong>Breathing changes it.</strong> Breathing in lowers the pressure inside the chest and draws blood into the heart, so the level in the neck <strong>falls on inspiration</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> the arterial pulse is the left ventricle's ejection wave. The JVP shows the right atrium's pressure changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> You feel the radial pulse about a tenth of a second after the left ventricle ejects. What has travelled to the wrist in that time?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The blood ejected in that beat, moving at about 8 m/s</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A pressure wave travelling along the artery walls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A nerve signal from the heart to the arterial muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Sound from the closing aortic valve, carried by the blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The ejection starts a pressure and stretch wave that travels along the arteries far faster than the blood itself moves. You feel the wave, not the blood.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does the pulse wave travel faster in an elderly person?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Their blood is thicker, so it carries pressure better</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their heart ejects blood at a higher speed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Their stiffer arteries pass the pressure change on faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Their arteries are wider, so blood meets less resistance</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A stiff wall hardly stretches, so it hands the pressure change on quickly. Arteries stiffen with age, so the wave speeds up, even though the blood does not.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can the JVP be seen but not felt?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Venous pressure is too low to push against a finger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The jugular vein lies too deep to be touched</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The JVP only appears when the patient breathes in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Venous blood flows away from the heart in the neck</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Pressure in the neck veins is only a few mmHg. Any pressure from a finger squashes the vein, so you can only see the movement.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents\">4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents?</h2>\n<p>In one heartbeat, the pressure in the right atrium rises three times (the <strong>a</strong>, <strong>c</strong> and <strong>v</strong> waves) and falls twice (the <strong>x</strong> and <strong>y</strong> descents). Each one follows from what the right atrium and the tricuspid valve are doing. They are the same atrial waves you meet on the <span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §9</a>)</em></p>\n<p>In order through one beat:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a wave: the right atrium contracts.</strong> At the end of ventricular diastole, just after the <span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span>, the atrium squeezes and its pressure rises. There is no valve between the atrium and the great veins, so, as the lecture notes, venous inflow briefly stops and a little blood is pushed back up the veins.</li>\n<li><strong>c wave: the closed tricuspid valve bulges back.</strong> As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve so it bulges back into the atrium. The atrium itself is not contracting.</li>\n<li><strong>x descent: the atrium relaxes and is stretched open.</strong> The atrium relaxes after its contraction. As the right ventricle ejects, it also pulls the tricuspid valve ring downwards, making the atrium bigger. Pressure falls.</li>\n<li><strong>v wave: the atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut.</strong> Blood keeps returning from the body throughout ventricular systole. With the tricuspid valve shut, it can only collect in the atrium, so pressure rises slowly. The atrium is not contracting.</li>\n<li><strong>y descent: the tricuspid valve opens.</strong> When right ventricular pressure falls below right atrial pressure, the valve opens and the stored blood rushes into the ventricle. Atrial pressure drops.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Wave</th>\n  <th>What causes it</th>\n  <th>When in the cycle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>a</strong></td>\n  <td>The atrium contracts</td>\n  <td>End of ventricular diastole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>c</strong></td>\n  <td>The closed tricuspid valve bulges back</td>\n  <td>Start of ventricular contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>x</strong></td>\n  <td>The atrium relaxes, and the valve ring is pulled down</td>\n  <td>Ventricular ejection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>v</strong></td>\n  <td>The atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut</td>\n  <td>Late ventricular systole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>y</strong></td>\n  <td>The tricuspid valve opens and the atrium empties</td>\n  <td>Early ventricular diastole</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>A memory hook:</strong> <strong>a</strong> for <strong>a</strong>trial contraction, <strong>c</strong> for the <strong>c</strong>losed valve bulging, <strong>v</strong> for <strong>v</strong>enous filling.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"jvp-explorer\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>JVP wave explorer</figcaption><p>Replaces the static curve. It shows one JVP trace, with the P wave, QRS, S1 and S2 marked above it, and a small right heart beside it. Next/Back steps through a → c → x → v → y. At each step the matching part of the trace is highlighted, and the heart shows what is happening: the atrium contracting, relaxing or filling; the tricuspid valve shut, bulging or opening; blood moving or collecting. One plain sentence underneath matches the bullet above. Schematic, not a patient tracing.</p></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-you-distinguish-the-jvp-from-the-carotid-pulse\">5. How do you distinguish the JVP from the carotid pulse?</h2>\n<p>Both pulsations sit side by side in the neck, so they are easy to confuse.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Jugular venous pulse</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Carotid arterial pulse</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Can you feel it?</td>\n  <td>No: pressure is too low</td>\n  <td>Yes: a firm thrust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>What it looks like</td>\n  <td>A soft flicker with more than one movement per beat</td>\n  <td>One sharp outward thrust per beat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>When the patient sits up</td>\n  <td>The level falls</td>\n  <td>No change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>On breathing in</td>\n  <td>The level falls</td>\n  <td>No regular change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Light pressure at the base of the neck</td>\n  <td>It disappears (the vein is squashed)</td>\n  <td>It continues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>What it shows</td>\n  <td>Right atrial pressure</td>\n  <td>Left ventricular ejection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Confusing the two leads to wrong conclusions about right atrial pressure.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture states that the JVP is not palpable and falls during inspiration. The other bedside differences are standard clinical teaching.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which JVP wave appears while the right atrium is filling and the tricuspid valve is shut?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The a wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The c wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The v wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The y descent</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> During ventricular systole the tricuspid valve is shut, so returning blood collects in the atrium and its pressure rises slowly: the v wave. The atrium is not contracting.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What causes the c wave?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The right atrium contracting at the end of diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The shut tricuspid valve bulging back into the atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood rushing into the ventricle as the valve opens</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The atrium filling while the ventricle ejects</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve back towards the atrium, giving a small rise in atrial pressure. A is the a wave, C the y descent, and D the v wave.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> At what moment does the y descent begin?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. When the pulmonary valve opens</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. When the right atrium contracts</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. When the tricuspid valve closes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. When the tricuspid valve opens</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Once the tricuspid valve opens, the blood stored in the atrium pours into the ventricle and atrial pressure falls.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A neck pulsation can be felt easily, shows one outward thrust per beat, and doesn't change when the patient sits up. What is it?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The carotid pulse, from the left ventricle's ejection</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The JVP, raised high by right-sided heart failure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A giant v wave from tricuspid regurgitation</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The JVP, seen with the patient lying unusually flat</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A pulsation you can feel, with a single thrust and no change with position, is arterial. The JVP cannot be felt, has more than one movement per beat, and changes with posture and breathing.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-how-is-the-jvp-estimated-at-the-bedside\">6. How is the JVP estimated at the bedside?</h2>\n<p>The lecture's method:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Recline the patient at about 45°</strong>, with the head turned slightly to the left, and look at the right internal jugular vein.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjust the angle until you can see the top of the pulsating column.</strong> Sit the patient more upright if the neck veins are very full, or lie them flatter if the column is low.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the <span data-term=\"sternal-angle\">sternal angle</span></strong>, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, level with the second rib.</li>\n<li><strong>Measure the vertical height</strong> from the sternal angle up to the top of the column.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why measure vertically?</strong> The pressure in a column of fluid depends only on its vertical height, not on how long or slanted it is.</p>\n<p><strong>Why from the sternal angle?</strong> It sits about 5 cm above the middle of the right atrium in most positions, so it makes a fixed reference point.</p>\n<p><strong>Normal:</strong> the top of the column is no more than about <strong>3–4 cm</strong> above the sternal angle. Higher than that means raised right atrial pressure.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs11-jvp-measurement\">\n<img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/chapter11/jvp-measurement.jpg\" alt=\"An elderly patient reclined with the head turned to the left. A clear ruler stands upright on the sternal angle, and a thin metal pointer is held level from the top of the visible venous column in the neck across to the ruler, where the height is read.\" width=\"1110\" height=\"599\">\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Measuring the JVP at the bedside.</strong> (1) The pointer is held level with the top of the venous column. (2) A ruler stands vertically on the sternal angle. (3) Where the level pointer crosses the ruler gives the JVP height in cm.<details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Bedside photograph supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project; numbered labels added. Original source to be confirmed.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture gives the 45° position, sitting upright if very engorged, and measuring from the sternum. The normal value (no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle) and the 5 cm offset are standard clinical teaching; the offset varies between people.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important\">7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important?</h2>\n<p>The lecture links three conditions to the JVP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right-sided heart failure: a raised JVP.</strong> The right heart cannot pump on all the blood returning to it. Pressure backs up into the right atrium and the neck veins, so the column sits high.</li>\n<li><strong>Tricuspid <span data-term=\"regurgitation\">regurgitation</span>: a large wave during systole.</strong> The leaky tricuspid valve lets the contracting right ventricle push blood back into the atrium, on top of the normal venous filling. The rise during systole becomes large. The lecture calls it a &quot;big c wave&quot;; because it joins the c and v waves, it is usually described as a giant <strong>v</strong> (or <strong>cv</strong>) wave.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete <span data-term=\"heart-block\">heart block</span>: giant (&quot;cannon&quot;) a waves.</strong> The atria and ventricles beat independently. Sometimes the atrium contracts while the tricuspid valve is shut, because the ventricle is contracting at the same moment. The blood cannot go forward, so the pressure spikes up the neck. This only happens on the beats where the timing collides, so the <span data-term=\"cannon-a-wave\">giant a waves</span> come and go. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §8</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration teaching-illustration-portrait\" id=\"cvs11-distended-neck-veins\">\n<img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/chapter11/distended-neck-veins.jpg\" alt=\"Side view of a woman lying almost flat with her face covered. A thick, swollen vein bulges along the side of her neck from above the collarbone up towards the angle of the jaw.\" width=\"625\" height=\"615\">\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. A very high JVP.</strong> Grossly distended neck veins in a patient lying almost flat. Right atrial pressure is so high that the venous column reaches the angle of the jaw. (Cause in this patient: cardiac tamponade, where fluid around the heart stops it filling.)<details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Photograph believed to be by Ferencga, Wikimedia Commons; supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project. Licence to be confirmed.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> a giant <strong>a</strong> wave is the <strong>atrium contracting against a shut valve</strong>. A giant <strong>v</strong> wave is the <strong>ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve</strong>.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture describes a \"big C wave\" in tricuspid incompetence. In practice, the regurgitant wave merges the c and v waves and is usually called a giant v or cv wave.</aside>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"l_rXk8GflL0\" data-media-title=\"JVP video 1\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO 1 OF 3</span><h3>JVP video 1</h3><p>A video chosen and watched by the DOMiNANCE team. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l_rXk8GflL0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© the video's creator, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"PecnLMSAAlw\" data-youtube-start=\"481\" data-media-title=\"JVP video 2\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO 2 OF 3</span><h3>JVP video 2</h3><p>A video chosen and watched by the DOMiNANCE team. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PecnLMSAAlw&amp;t=481s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© the video's creator, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"Z4yRBhlK0uY\" data-youtube-start=\"23\" data-media-title=\"JVP video 3\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO 3 OF 3</span><h3>JVP video 3</h3><p>A video chosen and watched by the DOMiNANCE team. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z4yRBhlK0uY&amp;t=23s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© the video's creator, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why is the JVP height measured vertically from the sternal angle?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The sternal angle lies directly over the tricuspid valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The jugular vein runs straight up from the sternal angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pressure in a column of fluid depends on its vertical height</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Measuring along the neck would include the carotid pulse</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Only vertical height sets the pressure in a fluid column. The sternal angle is used because it sits a fairly constant distance above the right atrium.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient's JVP is 8 cm above the sternal angle, and their ankles are swollen. What does this most suggest?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. High right atrial pressure from right-sided heart failure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low right atrial pressure from blood loss</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A stiff aorta raising the speed of the pulse wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Tricuspid stenosis reducing the return of venous blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A column well above 3–4 cm means raised right atrial pressure. With swollen ankles, this fits a failing right heart that cannot pump on the blood returning to it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In complete heart block, giant \"a\" waves appear in the neck only now and then. Why only sometimes?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The atria contract only when the ventricles are relaxed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Only some atrial beats fall while the tricuspid valve is shut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The vagus nerve blocks every other atrial contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Giant a waves appear only during deep inspiration</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The atria and ventricles beat independently, so only now and then does an atrial contraction land while the ventricle is contracting and the tricuspid valve is shut. Those beats give the giant waves.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> How does the giant wave of tricuspid regurgitation differ from a giant (\"cannon\") a wave?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It comes from the atrium contracting against a shut valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It appears only in diastole, while the tricuspid valve is open</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It can be felt as an outward thrust, like the carotid pulse</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It comes from the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> In tricuspid regurgitation the ventricle drives blood back into the atrium during systole. A cannon a wave is the atrium itself contracting against a shut valve.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The arterial pulse is a pressure wave</strong>, started by left ventricular ejection. It travels far faster than the blood itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Stiffer arteries carry the wave faster:</strong> further from the heart, and with age.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulse features (lecture):</strong> rate, volume, strength, character and location, plus rhythm. A pulse deficit means some beats are too weak to reach the wrist.</li>\n<li><strong>The JVP shows right atrial pressure</strong>, because no valve separates the right atrium from the neck veins. It can be seen but not felt, and it falls on inspiration.</li>\n<li><strong>a</strong> = the atrium contracts; <strong>c</strong> = the closed tricuspid valve bulges back; <strong>x</strong> = the atrium relaxes and the valve ring is pulled down; <strong>v</strong> = the atrium fills with the valve shut; <strong>y</strong> = the valve opens and the atrium empties.</li>\n<li><strong>JVP versus carotid:</strong> the JVP is not palpable, flickers more than once per beat, and changes with position, breathing and light pressure. The carotid is a single palpable thrust.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement:</strong> recline at about 45° (adjust as needed) and measure the vertical height above the sternal angle. Normal is no more than about 3–4 cm.</li>\n<li><strong>A raised JVP</strong> means high right atrial pressure, as in right-sided heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Giant v (cv) wave:</strong> tricuspid regurgitation, the ventricle pushing blood back.</li>\n<li><strong>Giant (cannon) a wave:</strong> complete heart block, the atrium contracting against a shut tricuspid valve.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse 1. What is an arterial pulse? Each time the left ventricle ejects, it pushes a fresh volume of blood into an aorta that is already full. The aortic wall stretches and the pressure inside it rises. That stretch and pressure rise travel down the arteries as a wave, like a ripple running along a tight hose. When the wave passes under your finger at the wrist, the artery wall bulges briefly: that bulge is the pulse. LV ejection → Aortic pressure ↑ → Arterial wall expands → Pressure wave travels The pulse is the wave, not the blood. As the lecture stresses, the pulse does not depend on how fast the blood is moving. The wave races ahead: about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, and faster further out. It reaches the wrist in roughly a tenth of a second. The blood itself moves far more slowly, well under 1 m/s on average in the aorta. So when you feel the pulse, the blood that left the heart in that beat is still nowhere near your wrist. Why does the wave speed up? A stiff tube passes a pressure wave on faster than a stretchy one, because a stiff wall barely gives, so the pressure change is handed on quickly. The lecture gives two examples: The wave gets faster further from the heart. The smaller arteries are stiffer than the stretchy aorta. The wave gets faster with age. The arteries become more rigid. In both cases it is the wave that speeds up, not the blood. Source note: Wave speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall): about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, 7–10 m/s in large branches, and faster in small arteries. 2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse? The lecture lists five features: Feature What you check What it tells you Rate Beats per minute How many beats are reaching the arteries Volume How big the bulge under your finger is Mainly the pulse pressure: the rise from diastolic to systolic pressure, which grows with stroke volume Strength How firmly it pushes against your finger How much pressure is in the artery Character The shape of each beat: how fast it rises and falls How the left ventricle ejects, and how the aortic valve is working Location Which artery (radial, carotid, femoral…), and whether both sides match A weak or missing pulse on one side points to a blockage on that side Whether the rhythm is regular is also worth noting. Why can the wrist count fewer beats than the heart makes? In a very fast or irregular rhythm, some beats come so soon that the ventricle has hardly filled. Those beats eject too little blood to make a pulse you can feel. The pulse at the wrist can then be slower than the heart rate counted with a stethoscope. This difference is a pulse deficit. 3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck? There is no valve between the right atrium and the large veins of the neck. So, as the lecture explains, pressure changes in the right atrium pass straight up into the neck veins, and you can see them. The internal jugular vein acts like a gauge attached to the right atrium: the higher the right atrial pressure, the higher up the neck the column of blood reaches. Pressure moves; the blood doesn't have to. A pressure change travels up the column of blood even while the blood itself keeps flowing down towards the heart. Why can you see it but not feel it? Venous pressure is only a few mmHg, far too low to push against a finger. Even light pressure squashes the vein flat. What you see is a soft, flickering movement of the skin. The lecture notes that the JVP is not palpable. Breathing changes it. Breathing in lowers the pressure inside the chest and draws blood into the heart, so the level in the neck falls on inspiration. Key distinction: the arterial pulse is the left ventricle's ejection wave. The JVP shows the right atrium's pressure changes. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. You feel the radial pulse about a tenth of a second after the left ventricle ejects. What has travelled to the wrist in that time? A. The blood ejected in that beat, moving at about 8 m/s B. A pressure wave travelling along the artery walls C. A nerve signal from the heart to the arterial muscle D. Sound from the closing aortic valve, carried by the blood Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The ejection starts a pressure and stretch wave that travels along the arteries far faster than the blood itself moves. You feel the wave, not the blood. 2. Why does the pulse wave travel faster in an elderly person? A. Their blood is thicker, so it carries pressure better B. Their heart ejects blood at a higher speed C. Their stiffer arteries pass the pressure change on faster D. Their arteries are wider, so blood meets less resistance Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A stiff wall hardly stretches, so it hands the pressure change on quickly. Arteries stiffen with age, so the wave speeds up, even though the blood does not. 3. Why can the JVP be seen but not felt? A. Venous pressure is too low to push against a finger B. The jugular vein lies too deep to be touched C. The JVP only appears when the patient breathes in D. Venous blood flows away from the heart in the neck Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pressure in the neck veins is only a few mmHg. Any pressure from a finger squashes the vein, so you can only see the movement. 4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents? In one heartbeat, the pressure in the right atrium rises three times (the a, c and v waves) and falls twice (the x and y descents). Each one follows from what the right atrium and the tricuspid valve are doing. They are the same atrial waves you meet on the Wiggers diagram. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §9) In order through one beat: a wave: the right atrium contracts. At the end of ventricular diastole, just after the P wave, the atrium squeezes and its pressure rises. There is no valve between the atrium and the great veins, so, as the lecture notes, venous inflow briefly stops and a little blood is pushed back up the veins. c wave: the closed tricuspid valve bulges back. As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve so it bulges back into the atrium. The atrium itself is not contracting. x descent: the atrium relaxes and is stretched open. The atrium relaxes after its contraction. As the right ventricle ejects, it also pulls the tricuspid valve ring downwards, making the atrium bigger. Pressure falls. v wave: the atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut. Blood keeps returning from the body throughout ventricular systole. With the tricuspid valve shut, it can only collect in the atrium, so pressure rises slowly. The atrium is not contracting. y descent: the tricuspid valve opens. When right ventricular pressure falls below right atrial pressure, the valve opens and the stored blood rushes into the ventricle. Atrial pressure drops. Wave What causes it When in the cycle a The atrium contracts End of ventricular diastole c The closed tricuspid valve bulges back Start of ventricular contraction x The atrium relaxes, and the valve ring is pulled down Ventricular ejection v The atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut Late ventricular systole y The tricuspid valve opens and the atrium empties Early ventricular diastole A memory hook: a for a trial contraction, c for the c losed valve bulging, v for v enous filling. JVP wave explorer Replaces the static curve. It shows one JVP trace, with the P wave, QRS, S1 and S2 marked above it, and a small right heart beside it. Next/Back steps through a → c → x → v → y. At each step the matching part of the trace is highlighted, and the heart shows what is happening: the atrium contracting, relaxing or filling; the tricuspid valve shut, bulging or opening; blood moving or collecting. One plain sentence underneath matches the bullet above. Schematic, not a patient tracing. 5. How do you distinguish the JVP from the carotid pulse? Both pulsations sit side by side in the neck, so they are easy to confuse. Jugular venous pulse Carotid arterial pulse Can you feel it? No: pressure is too low Yes: a firm thrust What it looks like A soft flicker with more than one movement per beat One sharp outward thrust per beat When the patient sits up The level falls No change On breathing in The level falls No regular change Light pressure at the base of the neck It disappears (the vein is squashed) It continues What it shows Right atrial pressure Left ventricular ejection Confusing the two leads to wrong conclusions about right atrial pressure. Source note: The lecture states that the JVP is not palpable and falls during inspiration. The other bedside differences are standard clinical teaching. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. Which JVP wave appears while the right atrium is filling and the tricuspid valve is shut? A. The a wave B. The c wave C. The v wave D. The y descent Show answer and explanation Answer: C. During ventricular systole the tricuspid valve is shut, so returning blood collects in the atrium and its pressure rises slowly: the v wave. The atrium is not contracting. 2. What causes the c wave? A. The right atrium contracting at the end of diastole B. The shut tricuspid valve bulging back into the atrium C. Blood rushing into the ventricle as the valve opens D. The atrium filling while the ventricle ejects Show answer and explanation Answer: B. As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve back towards the atrium, giving a small rise in atrial pressure. A is the a wave, C the y descent, and D the v wave. 3. At what moment does the y descent begin? A. When the pulmonary valve opens B. When the right atrium contracts C. When the tricuspid valve closes D. When the tricuspid valve opens Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Once the tricuspid valve opens, the blood stored in the atrium pours into the ventricle and atrial pressure falls. 4. A neck pulsation can be felt easily, shows one outward thrust per beat, and doesn't change when the patient sits up. What is it? A. The carotid pulse, from the left ventricle's ejection B. The JVP, raised high by right-sided heart failure C. A giant v wave from tricuspid regurgitation D. The JVP, seen with the patient lying unusually flat Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A pulsation you can feel, with a single thrust and no change with position, is arterial. The JVP cannot be felt, has more than one movement per beat, and changes with posture and breathing. 6. How is the JVP estimated at the bedside? The lecture's method: Recline the patient at about 45°, with the head turned slightly to the left, and look at the right internal jugular vein. Adjust the angle until you can see the top of the pulsating column. Sit the patient more upright if the neck veins are very full, or lie them flatter if the column is low. Find the sternal angle, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, level with the second rib. Measure the vertical height from the sternal angle up to the top of the column. Why measure vertically? The pressure in a column of fluid depends only on its vertical height, not on how long or slanted it is. Why from the sternal angle? It sits about 5 cm above the middle of the right atrium in most positions, so it makes a fixed reference point. Normal: the top of the column is no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle. Higher than that means raised right atrial pressure. Figure 01. Measuring the JVP at the bedside. (1) The pointer is held level with the top of the venous column. (2) A ruler stands vertically on the sternal angle. (3) Where the level pointer crosses the ruler gives the JVP height in cm. Image credit Bedside photograph supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project; numbered labels added. Original source to be confirmed. Source note: The lecture gives the 45° position, sitting upright if very engorged, and measuring from the sternum. The normal value (no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle) and the 5 cm offset are standard clinical teaching; the offset varies between people. 7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important? The lecture links three conditions to the JVP: Right-sided heart failure: a raised JVP. The right heart cannot pump on all the blood returning to it. Pressure backs up into the right atrium and the neck veins, so the column sits high. Tricuspid regurgitation: a large wave during systole. The leaky tricuspid valve lets the contracting right ventricle push blood back into the atrium, on top of the normal venous filling. The rise during systole becomes large. The lecture calls it a \"big c wave\"; because it joins the c and v waves, it is usually described as a giant v (or cv) wave. Complete heart block: giant (\"cannon\") a waves. The atria and ventricles beat independently. Sometimes the atrium contracts while the tricuspid valve is shut, because the ventricle is contracting at the same moment. The blood cannot go forward, so the pressure spikes up the neck. This only happens on the beats where the timing collides, so the giant a waves come and go. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §8) Figure 02. A very high JVP. Grossly distended neck veins in a patient lying almost flat. Right atrial pressure is so high that the venous column reaches the angle of the jaw. (Cause in this patient: cardiac tamponade, where fluid around the heart stops it filling.) Image credit Photograph believed to be by Ferencga, Wikimedia Commons; supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project. Licence to be confirmed. Key distinction: a giant a wave is the atrium contracting against a shut valve. A giant v wave is the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve. Source note: The lecture describes a \"big C wave\" in tricuspid incompetence. In practice, the regurgitant wave merges the c and v waves and is usually called a giant v or cv wave. OPTIONAL VIDEO 1 OF 3 JVP video 1 A video chosen and watched by the DOMiNANCE team. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © the video's creator, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. OPTIONAL VIDEO 2 OF 3 JVP video 2 A video chosen and watched by the DOMiNANCE team. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © the video's creator, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. OPTIONAL VIDEO 3 OF 3 JVP video 3 A video chosen and watched by the DOMiNANCE team. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © the video's creator, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. Understanding Check 1. Why is the JVP height measured vertically from the sternal angle? A. The sternal angle lies directly over the tricuspid valve B. The jugular vein runs straight up from the sternal angle C. Pressure in a column of fluid depends on its vertical height D. Measuring along the neck would include the carotid pulse Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Only vertical height sets the pressure in a fluid column. The sternal angle is used because it sits a fairly constant distance above the right atrium. 2. A patient's JVP is 8 cm above the sternal angle, and their ankles are swollen. What does this most suggest? A. High right atrial pressure from right-sided heart failure B. Low right atrial pressure from blood loss C. A stiff aorta raising the speed of the pulse wave D. Tricuspid stenosis reducing the return of venous blood Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A column well above 3–4 cm means raised right atrial pressure. With swollen ankles, this fits a failing right heart that cannot pump on the blood returning to it. 3. In complete heart block, giant \"a\" waves appear in the neck only now and then. Why only sometimes? A. The atria contract only when the ventricles are relaxed B. Only some atrial beats fall while the tricuspid valve is shut C. The vagus nerve blocks every other atrial contraction D. Giant a waves appear only during deep inspiration Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The atria and ventricles beat independently, so only now and then does an atrial contraction land while the ventricle is contracting and the tricuspid valve is shut. Those beats give the giant waves. 4. How does the giant wave of tricuspid regurgitation differ from a giant (\"cannon\") a wave? A. It comes from the atrium contracting against a shut valve B. It appears only in diastole, while the tricuspid valve is open C. It can be felt as an outward thrust, like the carotid pulse D. It comes from the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve Show answer and explanation Answer: D. In tricuspid regurgitation the ventricle drives blood back into the atrium during systole. A cannon a wave is the atrium itself contracting against a shut valve. 8. Chapter Essentials The arterial pulse is a pressure wave, started by left ventricular ejection. It travels far faster than the blood itself. Stiffer arteries carry the wave faster: further from the heart, and with age. Pulse features (lecture): rate, volume, strength, character and location, plus rhythm. A pulse deficit means some beats are too weak to reach the wrist. The JVP shows right atrial pressure, because no valve separates the right atrium from the neck veins. It can be seen but not felt, and it falls on inspiration. a = the atrium contracts; c = the closed tricuspid valve bulges back; x = the atrium relaxes and the valve ring is pulled down; v = the atrium fills with the valve shut; y = the valve opens and the atrium empties. JVP versus carotid: the JVP is not palpable, flickers more than once per beat, and changes with position, breathing and light pressure. The carotid is a single palpable thrust. Measurement: recline at about 45° (adjust as needed) and measure the vertical height above the sternal angle. Normal is no more than about 3–4 cm. A raised JVP means high right atrial pressure, as in right-sided heart failure. Giant v (cv) wave: tricuspid regurgitation, the ventricle pushing blood back. Giant (cannon) a wave: complete heart block, the atrium contracting against a shut tricuspid valve."},{"slug":"venous-return","title":"Veins and Venous Return","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"43-44 and 73-75 and 97-98","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-venous-return-and-why-is-it-essential","title":"1. What is venous return, and why is it essential?"},{"id":"2-why-are-veins-called-capacitance-vessels","title":"2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels?"},{"id":"3-what-pressure-actually-drives-venous-return","title":"3. What pressure actually drives venous return?"},{"id":"4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work","title":"4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work?"},{"id":"5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return","title":"5. How does breathing help venous return?"},{"id":"6-what-does-central-venous-pressure-tell-us","title":"6. What does central venous pressure tell us?"},{"id":"7-what-happens-to-veins-in-the-head-when-standing","title":"7. What happens to veins in the head when standing?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Veins and Venous Return</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-venous-return-and-why-is-it-essential\">1. What is venous return, and why is it essential?</h2>\n<p><strong>Venous return (VR)</strong> is the <strong>volume of blood returning to the right atrium per unit time</strong>. In a closed circulation at steady state, venous return must equal <strong>cardiac output (CO)</strong>: the heart cannot pump more blood out indefinitely than the circulation brings back.</p>\n<p>A stronger heart cannot maintain normal stroke volume when it has too little blood to fill it. This is the link to the Frank–Starling law in Chapter 07:</p>\n<div class=\"vr-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Venous return to stroke volume\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Right-heart filling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stroke volume ↑</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why doesn't increased return immediately increase the left ventricular output?</strong> Blood first passes through the right heart and lungs; effects on left ventricular filling follow after this transit. The two outputs become approximately equal over time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-are-veins-called-capacitance-vessels\">2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels?</h2>\n<p>Compared with thick-walled arteries, large veins generally have <strong>thin, compliant, distensible walls</strong>. They can hold a substantial amount of blood while pressure remains relatively low.</p>\n<p>The lecture contrasts a filling artery with a filling vein: the artery's pressure rises more sharply for a given added volume, while the vein can accommodate far more volume with a small initial pressure rise.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Vascular compliance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)\">C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">The higher C of veins permits greater volume storage for a given pressure rise.</span></div>\n\n<p>A representative resting distribution places roughly <strong>half of circulating blood in the systemic venous compartment</strong>. These percentages are approximations and do not form an exact complete partition.</p>\n<h3>Why does venoconstriction increase available central blood?</h3>\n<p>Noradrenergic sympathetic stimulation contracts venous smooth muscle. Veins become less distensible and hold less blood at a given pressure. Blood may shift out of peripheral venous reservoirs towards the heart, increasing the pressure driving venous return.</p>\n<p><strong>This is not identical to arteriolar constriction.</strong> Arterioles chiefly adjust resistance to organ perfusion; venoconstriction chiefly influences <strong>venous capacitance and filling</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-pressure-actually-drives-venous-return\">3. What pressure actually drives venous return?</h2>\n<p>Venous blood normally returns towards the right atrium because its upstream filling pressure exceeds downstream right atrial pressure.</p>\n<p>One useful conceptual model expresses this relation as:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Venous-return relation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"VR = (P_msf − P_RA) / (R_VR)\">VR = (P_msf − P_RA) / (R_VR)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Pmsf = mean systemic filling pressure; PRA = right atrial pressure; RVR = resistance to venous return.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Mean systemic filling pressure</strong> is a conceptual pressure determined by the amount of stressed blood volume and vascular properties; it is not simply the measured pressure in an ordinary peripheral vein while the heart is pumping.</p>\n<p>At unchanged upstream filling pressure and resistance, <strong>a higher right atrial pressure narrows the gradient and can reduce venous return</strong>. Conversely, increasing stressed blood volume or venous tone can increase upstream filling pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> This model is simplified; the great veins may partially collapse when intrathoracic pressure becomes sufficiently negative, so lowering right atrial pressure does not increase venous return without limit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work\">4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work?</h2>\n<p>Veins in the limbs commonly contain <strong>one-way valves</strong>. When skeletal muscles contract around deep veins, the veins are compressed and blood is pushed onward, usually towards the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure vr-pump-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Skeletal-muscle pump and one-way venous valves</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 540 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original diagram of a vertical leg vein, one-way valves and surrounding muscle compression moving venous blood upwards towards the heart.\"><rect x=\"230\" y=\"16\" width=\"82\" height=\"128\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#d7eafe\" stroke=\"#4381c1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M230 55 Q271 65 273 79 Q273 65 312 55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#174f92\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M230 107 Q271 117 273 132 Q273 117 312 107\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#174f92\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M273 114V23 M264 35 L273 23 L282 35\" stroke=\"#1463bd\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g stroke=\"#e38c38\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M172 80H222 M213 71 L222 80 L213 89\"/><path d=\"M371 80H318 M327 71 L318 80 L327 89\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"163\" y=\"64\">Muscle</text><text x=\"163\" y=\"104\">squeezes</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"64\">Muscle</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"104\">squeezes</text><text x=\"276\" y=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\">To heart ↑</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"149\">Valves favour one-way upward movement during compression</text></g></svg><p>Illustration of the principle, not a scale drawing. Valves limit backward flow when muscle pressure is released.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>Why do soldiers subtly exercise their legs while standing?</h3>\n<p>When a person stands motionless, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in leg veins and promotes <strong>venous pooling</strong>. Less blood is immediately available to return to the heart. The resulting fall in preload can reduce stroke volume and may produce light-headedness or fainting.</p>\n<p>Repeated <strong>calf contractions, toe raises or marching in place</strong> compress veins and improve return. This is the physiological reason that standing at attention can be more difficult than walking or subtly contracting the leg muscles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return\">5. How does breathing help venous return?</h2>\n<p>During normal spontaneous <strong>inspiration</strong>, intrathoracic pressure becomes more negative. The thoracic venous/atrial pressure may decrease, promoting return from extrathoracic veins to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diaphragm</strong> also changes abdominal pressure, helping generate a pressure gradient along the venae cavae. The effect varies with posture, volume status and the breathing pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Important qualification:</strong> Positive-pressure ventilation can change these dynamics; “inspiration always increases venous return” is too absolute. Also, right-heart filling may increase before a similar effect is transmitted to the left heart.</p>\n<p>The lecture also mentions arterial pulsation compressing adjacent veins, which can assist movement locally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-does-central-venous-pressure-tell-us\">6. What does central venous pressure tell us?</h2>\n<p><strong>Central venous pressure (CVP)</strong> usually refers to pressure near the right atrium or great thoracic veins. It varies through the cardiac cycle and with respiration, reflecting right-sided filling pressures.</p>\n<p>Traditional teaching gives low pressures near the right atrium, including approximately <strong>4.6 mmHg</strong> in its example, but a single absolute value is <strong>not a universal normal</strong>. Actual readings depend on the reference level, respiratory phase, volume status, venous return and right ventricular function.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can CVP rise when cardiac output falls?</strong> If the right ventricle cannot empty effectively, blood may accumulate upstream and right atrial pressure may increase even while forward flow decreases. CVP is therefore not a direct measurement of circulating blood volume or cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-happens-to-veins-in-the-head-when-standing\">7. What happens to veins in the head when standing?</h2>\n<p>Gravity changes venous pressure depending on height relative to the heart. Thin-walled extracranial veins may partially collapse when the surrounding pressure exceeds the pressure inside.</p>\n<p>Traditional teaching contrasts these with <strong>dural venous sinuses</strong>, whose walls are held open by dural attachments. If an open sinus is exposed while the internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, <strong>air may enter the venous circulation</strong>, potentially causing a venous air embolism.</p>\n<p>This is a specific clinical risk of exposed low-pressure noncollapsible veins, not evidence that blood normally flows backwards from the brain to the heart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why must venous return equal cardiac output in steady state?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The systemic and pulmonary circulations form a closed series circuit. Blood returning to the right heart must eventually be ejected through the pulmonary and systemic circuits. Persistent differences would cause progressive accumulation or depletion of blood in a compartment, so long-term <strong>VR = CO</strong>.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. Why can't a very strong heart maintain normal output during major haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Haemorrhage reduces circulating volume and the pressure driving venous return. The ventricle receives less blood, so <strong>EDV and stroke volume fall</strong>. Increased intrinsic contractility cannot fully compensate for the absence of adequate filling.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Explain the difference between venous compliance and arteriolar resistance.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Compliance</strong> is volume change per pressure change; veins can store large volumes at low pressure. <strong>Resistance</strong> determines the pressure drop required for a particular blood flow; arterioles change their radius to control organ perfusion. The two vascular properties serve different functions.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Why can sympathetic venoconstriction increase venous return even if total blood volume has not changed?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Venoconstriction reduces venous capacitance and mobilises blood stored in peripheral veins. This increases the stressed volume and may increase mean systemic filling pressure, strengthening the gradient towards the right atrium without adding new blood.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. What does the difference between mean systemic filling pressure and right atrial pressure represent?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>It represents a conceptual <strong>pressure gradient driving systemic venous return</strong>. Venous return is greater at the same resistance when that gradient increases. Mean systemic filling pressure is not simply the pressure measured in a peripheral vein during ongoing circulation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. Why may a rise in right atrial pressure reduce venous return if upstream filling pressure remains fixed?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The pressure difference moving blood towards the right atrium becomes smaller. From the simplified relation VR = (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR, a higher downstream right atrial pressure reduces the gradient. Severe rises may reflect right-heart pumping failure as well.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. Why does subtle calf contraction help someone who is standing still for a long time?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Standing promotes gravitational pooling in leg veins, which can reduce venous return and EDV. Calf contractions compress the veins while one-way valves favour upward flow. This mobilises pooled blood towards the heart, improving preload and helping prevent faintness.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. How does inspiration ordinarily support right-heart filling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Spontaneous inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, while abdominal and thoracic pressure changes favour movement of blood towards the chest. This can increase systemic venous return to the right heart. Positive-pressure ventilation or unusual pressures may change the effect.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Does an elevated CVP always mean the patient has excess circulating blood volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> CVP measures pressure near the right atrium and also depends on ventricular function, intrathoracic pressure and venous tone. Right-heart dysfunction can elevate CVP through upstream congestion despite inadequate forward flow, so CVP is not simply a blood-volume gauge.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why can the left ventricular stroke volume change after, rather than exactly when, venous return to the right atrium increases?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>New venous return first enters the right atrium and ventricle, then passes through the pulmonary circulation before reaching the left heart. The right-heart filling response therefore precedes the corresponding change in left ventricular EDV and ejection.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why can a dural venous sinus admit air when opened above heart level?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Pressure may become subatmospheric in veins above the heart. Ordinary thin-walled veins can collapse, but dural sinuses are held open by surrounding tissue. If such a sinus is cut while internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, air may be drawn into it, causing a dangerous venous air embolism.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why might a person feel faint after suddenly standing, even with a normal heart?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Gravity temporarily shifts blood into dependent veins. Reduced immediate venous return lowers preload, stroke volume and sometimes arterial pressure until autonomic reflexes and muscle pumping compensate. This is a filling and circulation problem, not proof that the heart muscle is intrinsically weak.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Venous return:</strong> Blood reaching the right atrium per minute; it equals cardiac output at steady state.</li>\n<li><strong>Veins:</strong> High-compliance capacitance reservoirs, unlike resistance-regulating arterioles.</li>\n<li><strong>Venous tone:</strong> Sympathetic venoconstriction can mobilise stored blood and increase central filling.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure gradient:</strong> VR ≈ (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR in a simplified model.</li>\n<li><strong>Mean systemic filling pressure:</strong> Conceptual upstream filling pressure, not an ordinary peripheral venous reading.</li>\n<li><strong>Skeletal-muscle pump:</strong> Contracting leg muscles compress veins and valves limit backward flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Standing:</strong> Gravity promotes venous pooling, reducing venous return and potentially stroke volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory pump:</strong> Spontaneous inspiration usually helps right-heart venous return.</li>\n<li><strong>CVP:</strong> Pressure close to the right atrium; influenced by right-heart function and intrathoracic pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>CVP is not blood volume:</strong> High right atrial pressure can accompany poor forward cardiac output.</li>\n<li><strong>Dural venous sinuses:</strong> Remain open despite subatmospheric pressure, creating an air-embolism risk if exposed.</li>\n<li><strong>Filling and output:</strong> Strong contraction cannot fully compensate for insufficient ventricular filling.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Veins and Venous Return 1. What is venous return, and why is it essential? Venous return (VR) is the volume of blood returning to the right atrium per unit time. In a closed circulation at steady state, venous return must equal cardiac output (CO): the heart cannot pump more blood out indefinitely than the circulation brings back. A stronger heart cannot maintain normal stroke volume when it has too little blood to fill it. This is the link to the Frank–Starling law in Chapter 07: Venous return ↑ → Right-heart filling ↑ → EDV ↑ → Stroke volume ↑ Why doesn't increased return immediately increase the left ventricular output? Blood first passes through the right heart and lungs; effects on left ventricular filling follow after this transit. The two outputs become approximately equal over time. 2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? Compared with thick-walled arteries, large veins generally have thin, compliant, distensible walls. They can hold a substantial amount of blood while pressure remains relatively low. The lecture contrasts a filling artery with a filling vein: the artery's pressure rises more sharply for a given added volume, while the vein can accommodate far more volume with a small initial pressure rise. Vascular compliance C = (ΔV) / (ΔP) The higher C of veins permits greater volume storage for a given pressure rise. A representative resting distribution places roughly half of circulating blood in the systemic venous compartment. These percentages are approximations and do not form an exact complete partition. Why does venoconstriction increase available central blood? Noradrenergic sympathetic stimulation contracts venous smooth muscle. Veins become less distensible and hold less blood at a given pressure. Blood may shift out of peripheral venous reservoirs towards the heart, increasing the pressure driving venous return. This is not identical to arteriolar constriction. Arterioles chiefly adjust resistance to organ perfusion; venoconstriction chiefly influences venous capacitance and filling. 3. What pressure actually drives venous return? Venous blood normally returns towards the right atrium because its upstream filling pressure exceeds downstream right atrial pressure. One useful conceptual model expresses this relation as: Venous-return relation VR = (P_msf − P_RA) / (R_VR) Pmsf = mean systemic filling pressure; PRA = right atrial pressure; RVR = resistance to venous return. Mean systemic filling pressure is a conceptual pressure determined by the amount of stressed blood volume and vascular properties; it is not simply the measured pressure in an ordinary peripheral vein while the heart is pumping. At unchanged upstream filling pressure and resistance, a higher right atrial pressure narrows the gradient and can reduce venous return. Conversely, increasing stressed blood volume or venous tone can increase upstream filling pressure. Limitation: This model is simplified; the great veins may partially collapse when intrathoracic pressure becomes sufficiently negative, so lowering right atrial pressure does not increase venous return without limit. 4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work? Veins in the limbs commonly contain one-way valves. When skeletal muscles contract around deep veins, the veins are compressed and blood is pushed onward, usually towards the heart. Figure 01. Skeletal-muscle pump and one-way venous valves Muscle squeezes Muscle squeezes To heart ↑ Valves favour one-way upward movement during compression Illustration of the principle, not a scale drawing. Valves limit backward flow when muscle pressure is released. Why do soldiers subtly exercise their legs while standing? When a person stands motionless, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in leg veins and promotes venous pooling. Less blood is immediately available to return to the heart. The resulting fall in preload can reduce stroke volume and may produce light-headedness or fainting. Repeated calf contractions, toe raises or marching in place compress veins and improve return. This is the physiological reason that standing at attention can be more difficult than walking or subtly contracting the leg muscles. 5. How does breathing help venous return? During normal spontaneous inspiration, intrathoracic pressure becomes more negative. The thoracic venous/atrial pressure may decrease, promoting return from extrathoracic veins to the right side of the heart. The diaphragm also changes abdominal pressure, helping generate a pressure gradient along the venae cavae. The effect varies with posture, volume status and the breathing pattern. Important qualification: Positive-pressure ventilation can change these dynamics; “inspiration always increases venous return” is too absolute. Also, right-heart filling may increase before a similar effect is transmitted to the left heart. The lecture also mentions arterial pulsation compressing adjacent veins, which can assist movement locally. 6. What does central venous pressure tell us? Central venous pressure (CVP) usually refers to pressure near the right atrium or great thoracic veins. It varies through the cardiac cycle and with respiration, reflecting right-sided filling pressures. Traditional teaching gives low pressures near the right atrium, including approximately 4.6 mmHg in its example, but a single absolute value is not a universal normal. Actual readings depend on the reference level, respiratory phase, volume status, venous return and right ventricular function. Why can CVP rise when cardiac output falls? If the right ventricle cannot empty effectively, blood may accumulate upstream and right atrial pressure may increase even while forward flow decreases. CVP is therefore not a direct measurement of circulating blood volume or cardiac output. 7. What happens to veins in the head when standing? Gravity changes venous pressure depending on height relative to the heart. Thin-walled extracranial veins may partially collapse when the surrounding pressure exceeds the pressure inside. Traditional teaching contrasts these with dural venous sinuses, whose walls are held open by dural attachments. If an open sinus is exposed while the internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, air may enter the venous circulation, potentially causing a venous air embolism. This is a specific clinical risk of exposed low-pressure noncollapsible veins, not evidence that blood normally flows backwards from the brain to the heart. Understanding Check 1. Why must venous return equal cardiac output in steady state? Reveal explanation The systemic and pulmonary circulations form a closed series circuit. Blood returning to the right heart must eventually be ejected through the pulmonary and systemic circuits. Persistent differences would cause progressive accumulation or depletion of blood in a compartment, so long-term VR = CO. 2. Why can't a very strong heart maintain normal output during major haemorrhage? Reveal explanation Haemorrhage reduces circulating volume and the pressure driving venous return. The ventricle receives less blood, so EDV and stroke volume fall. Increased intrinsic contractility cannot fully compensate for the absence of adequate filling. 3. Explain the difference between venous compliance and arteriolar resistance. Reveal explanation Compliance is volume change per pressure change; veins can store large volumes at low pressure. Resistance determines the pressure drop required for a particular blood flow; arterioles change their radius to control organ perfusion. The two vascular properties serve different functions. 4. Why can sympathetic venoconstriction increase venous return even if total blood volume has not changed? Reveal explanation Venoconstriction reduces venous capacitance and mobilises blood stored in peripheral veins. This increases the stressed volume and may increase mean systemic filling pressure, strengthening the gradient towards the right atrium without adding new blood. 5. What does the difference between mean systemic filling pressure and right atrial pressure represent? Reveal explanation It represents a conceptual pressure gradient driving systemic venous return. Venous return is greater at the same resistance when that gradient increases. Mean systemic filling pressure is not simply the pressure measured in a peripheral vein during ongoing circulation. 6. Why may a rise in right atrial pressure reduce venous return if upstream filling pressure remains fixed? Reveal explanation The pressure difference moving blood towards the right atrium becomes smaller. From the simplified relation VR = (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR, a higher downstream right atrial pressure reduces the gradient. Severe rises may reflect right-heart pumping failure as well. 7. Why does subtle calf contraction help someone who is standing still for a long time? Reveal explanation Standing promotes gravitational pooling in leg veins, which can reduce venous return and EDV. Calf contractions compress the veins while one-way valves favour upward flow. This mobilises pooled blood towards the heart, improving preload and helping prevent faintness. 8. How does inspiration ordinarily support right-heart filling? Reveal explanation Spontaneous inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, while abdominal and thoracic pressure changes favour movement of blood towards the chest. This can increase systemic venous return to the right heart. Positive-pressure ventilation or unusual pressures may change the effect. 9. Does an elevated CVP always mean the patient has excess circulating blood volume? Reveal explanation No. CVP measures pressure near the right atrium and also depends on ventricular function, intrathoracic pressure and venous tone. Right-heart dysfunction can elevate CVP through upstream congestion despite inadequate forward flow, so CVP is not simply a blood-volume gauge. 10. Why can the left ventricular stroke volume change after, rather than exactly when, venous return to the right atrium increases? Reveal explanation New venous return first enters the right atrium and ventricle, then passes through the pulmonary circulation before reaching the left heart. The right-heart filling response therefore precedes the corresponding change in left ventricular EDV and ejection. 11. Why can a dural venous sinus admit air when opened above heart level? Reveal explanation Pressure may become subatmospheric in veins above the heart. Ordinary thin-walled veins can collapse, but dural sinuses are held open by surrounding tissue. If such a sinus is cut while internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, air may be drawn into it, causing a dangerous venous air embolism. 12. Why might a person feel faint after suddenly standing, even with a normal heart? Reveal explanation Gravity temporarily shifts blood into dependent veins. Reduced immediate venous return lowers preload, stroke volume and sometimes arterial pressure until autonomic reflexes and muscle pumping compensate. This is a filling and circulation problem, not proof that the heart muscle is intrinsically weak. Chapter Essentials Venous return: Blood reaching the right atrium per minute; it equals cardiac output at steady state. Veins: High-compliance capacitance reservoirs, unlike resistance-regulating arterioles. Venous tone: Sympathetic venoconstriction can mobilise stored blood and increase central filling. Pressure gradient: VR ≈ (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR in a simplified model. Mean systemic filling pressure: Conceptual upstream filling pressure, not an ordinary peripheral venous reading. Skeletal-muscle pump: Contracting leg muscles compress veins and valves limit backward flow. Standing: Gravity promotes venous pooling, reducing venous return and potentially stroke volume. Respiratory pump: Spontaneous inspiration usually helps right-heart venous return. CVP: Pressure close to the right atrium; influenced by right-heart function and intrathoracic pressure. CVP is not blood volume: High right atrial pressure can accompany poor forward cardiac output. Dural venous sinuses: Remain open despite subatmospheric pressure, creating an air-embolism risk if exposed. Filling and output: Strong contraction cannot fully compensate for insufficient ventricular filling."},{"slug":"microcirculation","title":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"57-60 and 84-89","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-is-the-microcirculation-necessary","title":"1. Why is the microcirculation necessary?"},{"id":"2-what-is-a-capillary-wall-made-of","title":"2. What is a capillary wall made of?"},{"id":"3-how-do-substances-actually-cross-the-capillary-wall","title":"3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall?"},{"id":"4-what-decides-which-capillaries-receive-more-blood","title":"4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood?"},{"id":"5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood","title":"5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood?"},{"id":"6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding","title":"6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding"},{"id":"7-what-changes-microvascular-filtration-or-permeability","title":"7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-is-the-microcirculation-necessary\">1. Why is the microcirculation necessary?</h2>\n<p>The heart delivers blood to organs, but <strong>cells do not receive oxygen directly from the aorta</strong>. Oxygen and nutrients must cross the tiny exchange vessels close to each tissue cell.</p>\n<p>The <strong>microcirculation</strong> includes small arterioles, metarterioles or transitional vessels where present, capillaries and venules. Its tasks are to distribute blood locally, exchange substances and regulate interstitial fluid.</p>\n<div class=\"micro-path\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Microcirculatory sequence\"><span>Small arteriole</span><b>→</b><span>Capillary exchange</span><b>→</b><span>Venule</span><b>→</b><span>Venous return</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why are capillaries suitable?</strong> Their thin walls and the very large combined capillary surface area bring flowing blood close to cells. Blood moves relatively slowly because the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is large.</p>\n<p>An important point is that capillaries contain only a <strong>small fraction of total blood volume, around 5% in a representative estimate</strong>, despite their importance for tissue exchange.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-is-a-capillary-wall-made-of\">2. What is a capillary wall made of?</h2>\n<p>A typical capillary is lined by <strong>one layer of endothelial cells</strong> supported by specialised extracellular matrix called the <strong>basal lamina (part of the basement membrane)</strong>. Pericytes may lie externally and help support microvascular stability.</p>\n<p><strong>Important histology distinction:</strong> A capillary does <strong>not</strong> possess a typical three-coat wall with a substantial smooth-muscle tunica media, unlike an artery. A capillary also does <strong>not</strong> need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane.</p>\n<h3>Three principal capillary patterns</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Type</th>\n  <th>Structure</th>\n  <th>Common examples</th>\n  <th>Exchange implication</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Continuous</strong></td>\n  <td>Relatively uninterrupted endothelial lining</td>\n  <td>Muscle, skin, CNS</td>\n  <td>Controlled passage; CNS barrier especially restrictive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fenestrated</strong></td>\n  <td>Endothelial pores/fenestrae</td>\n  <td>Intestinal villi, endocrine organs, renal glomeruli</td>\n  <td>Rapid exchange of water and small solutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Discontinuous / sinusoidal</strong></td>\n  <td>Wider gaps and discontinuities</td>\n  <td>Liver, spleen, bone marrow</td>\n  <td>Exchange of larger materials, specialised by tissue</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>A note on units:</strong> Capillary gaps and fenestrations are microscopic. These openings are microscopic, often measured in <strong>nanometres</strong>, not millimetres. Treat the printed units as erroneous, while keeping the structural distinction the slide intended.</p>\n<p><strong>Why do fenestrations help?</strong> A porous endothelium provides lower resistance to selected water-soluble materials crossing the vessel. But renal filtration still depends on additional specialised layers, including the glomerular basement membrane and podocyte filtration slits; pores alone do not determine the whole barrier.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-do-substances-actually-cross-the-capillary-wall\">3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall?</h2>\n<p>The lecture names <strong>diffusion, filtration, fenestrations and vesicular transport</strong>. These are not all the same kind of process.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Mechanism</th>\n  <th>What moves?</th>\n  <th>Main driving factor</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Diffusion</strong></td>\n  <td>O₂, CO₂ and many dissolved solutes</td>\n  <td>Concentration or partial-pressure differences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Intercellular routes/fenestrae</strong></td>\n  <td>Water and selected small hydrophilic substances</td>\n  <td>Barrier structure plus relevant gradients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Vesicular transport</strong></td>\n  <td>Selected macromolecular cargo</td>\n  <td>Active endothelial transport mechanisms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Bulk filtration</strong></td>\n  <td>Water and associated dissolved materials</td>\n  <td>Hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Why can oxygen cross efficiently?</h3>\n<p>Oxygen diffuses down its <strong>partial-pressure gradient</strong> from capillary blood into metabolically active tissue. Carbon dioxide tends to diffuse in the opposite direction because cells produce it.</p>\n<p>The short diffusion distance and extensive capillary surface area enable exchange. <strong>Diffusion is not the same as fluid being forcibly filtered across the wall.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-decides-which-capillaries-receive-more-blood\">4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood?</h2>\n<p>Tissues require more delivery when they become active. Local metabolic changes can dilate resistance vessels and increase the number of well-perfused microvascular pathways.</p>\n<p>Classical descriptions include <strong>metarterioles and precapillary sphincters</strong>. Such structures are useful classical teaching concepts but vary across vascular beds; not every capillary has an identical anatomical sphincter.</p>\n<p><strong>Applied example:</strong> During exercise, active skeletal muscle produces metabolites that promote local dilation. More blood is delivered into exchange networks where oxygen demand is high.</p>\n<p>An important point is that capillary pressure may be estimated from the external force required to occlude a capillary. It is a <strong>method-dependent estimate</strong>, not a direct measure of all capillary pressures everywhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood?</h2>\n<p>Two families of pressure forces influence movement of water across a microvascular wall:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hydrostatic pressure:</strong> Fluid pressure; inside a capillary it usually tends to push water outward.</li>\n<li><strong>Oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure:</strong> Due mainly to proteins; plasma proteins generally oppose excessive outward water movement by attracting or retaining water.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The classic balance uses four named quantities:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Quantity</th>\n  <th>Location</th>\n  <th>Typical direction in simple model</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pc</strong></td>\n  <td>Capillary hydrostatic pressure</td>\n  <td>Promotes outward filtration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pi</strong></td>\n  <td>Interstitial hydrostatic pressure</td>\n  <td>May oppose or favour filtration, depending on sign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>πc</strong></td>\n  <td>Plasma capillary oncotic pressure</td>\n  <td>Opposes outward filtration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>πi</strong></td>\n  <td>Interstitial oncotic pressure</td>\n  <td>Favours outward filtration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure micro-force-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Hydrostatic and oncotic influences on capillary filtration</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 570 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original schematic capillary containing plasma proteins, with an outward hydrostatic arrow from blood to tissue and an opposing inward plasma oncotic arrow.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"51\" width=\"490\" height=\"76\" rx=\"30\" fill=\"#f5d8df\" stroke=\"#b66b80\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g fill=\"#ae536c\"><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"327\" cy=\"88\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"458\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/></g><path d=\"M239 77 V14 M230 27 L239 14 L248 27\" stroke=\"#d27c26\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M389 14 V75 M380 62 L389 75 L398 62\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"166\" y=\"13\">Pc: outward</text><text x=\"400\" y=\"13\">Plasma oncotic: inward</text><text x=\"167\" y=\"96\">Capillary blood</text><text x=\"181\" y=\"151\">Interstitial tissue space</text></g></svg><p>A conceptual comparison of two important forces. Actual net filtration also depends on interstitial forces, surface area, permeability and the endothelial glycocalyx.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why does plasma albumin matter?</strong> Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. Low albumin weakens the oncotic opposition to filtration, increasing oedema risk if lymphatic drainage cannot keep up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\">6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding</h2>\n<p>The slides 85–88 teach hydrostatic and oncotic gradients and give an example of <strong>24 litres filtered per day, 85% reabsorbed into capillaries and 15% returned by lymph</strong>. This is the <strong>classic textbook model</strong> and explains the intended arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Modern physiological clarification:</strong> The <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> is a protein-rich surface layer that affects effective filtration and protein gradients immediately beneath it. In many continuous capillary beds, a low level of <strong>net filtration persists during steady state</strong>, including towards the venous end. Much of the filtered fluid is returned through <strong>lymphatics</strong>, rather than assuming a universal large sustained venous-end reabsorption.</p>\n<p><strong>What should a student learn?</strong> Understand the lecturer's four pressure forces and numerical illustration, but also know that the 85%/15% split <strong>is not a universal modern physiological rule</strong>. Transient absorption after sudden pressure changes and some specialised tissues are exceptions.</p>\n<p>This distinction prevents a major misconception: the lymphatic system is not merely an optional back-up after routine large venous reabsorption.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-microvascular-filtration-or-permeability\">7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability?</h2>\n<p>Local vessel conditions alter exchange:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arteriolar dilation:</strong> May increase blood delivery and capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging filtration under some conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Venous congestion:</strong> Increases downstream and often capillary hydrostatic pressure, promoting filtration.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased endothelial permeability:</strong> Histamine, bradykinin and substance P are examples named by the lecturer; inflammatory conditions can increase fluid/protein escape.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased surface area:</strong> Perfusion of more capillaries can increase total exchange capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical link:</strong> Inflammation causes microvascular endothelial changes, enabling more protein and fluid to escape into tissues. Venous obstruction may raise fluid filtration without requiring an initially leaky capillary barrier. These are different causes of swelling.</p>\n<p>The next two chapters distinguish <strong>excessive tissue-fluid accumulation (oedema)</strong> from <strong>inadequate lymphatic clearance</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why can capillaries exchange substances efficiently even though their walls are so thin?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Their thin endothelial lining and short diffusion distance bring blood close to tissue fluid. Numerous capillaries provide a large total surface area, while relatively low mean velocity creates time for exchange. Thinness is therefore an <strong>advantage for transport</strong>, not evidence that capillaries are ineffective vessels.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. Do capillaries need a thick connective-tissue subendothelial layer to have a basement membrane?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. It is not the same thing as a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer, and a typical capillary lacks the muscular middle coat of an artery.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Why are fenestrated capillaries useful in endocrine organs and the intestinal villi?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Endothelial fenestrations permit relatively rapid water and small-solute exchange, suited to tissues with high exchange requirements. Their barrier properties still depend on other structural features and the tissue involved.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Why is the lecture's “mm” unit for endothelial fenestrations unsuitable?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Fenestrations are tiny pores in microscopic endothelial cells, commonly described on the <strong>nanometre scale</strong>. A pore several millimetres wide would dwarf the cell and vessel. The lecture intends to describe small permeable openings, not literal millimetre-sized gaps.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. What drives oxygen diffusion from capillaries into active tissues?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Tissue cells consume oxygen, lowering local tissue oxygen partial pressure. Oxygen then diffuses from the relatively higher partial pressure in capillary blood towards lower tissue partial pressure. This is <strong>diffusion down a partial-pressure gradient</strong>, not bulk filtration of the entire blood volume.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. Distinguish diffusion from filtration in one physiological example each.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Diffusion</strong> transfers individual molecules such as O₂ down a concentration/partial-pressure difference. <strong>Filtration</strong> moves bulk water across the microvascular barrier because of hydrostatic and oncotic forces. Solutes may accompany filtered fluid, but the driving principles differ.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. If capillary hydrostatic pressure increases with other factors unchanged, what happens to filtration?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Outward filtration generally increases.</strong> Hydrostatic pressure within the capillary pushes fluid towards the interstitium. Extra filtered fluid must be removed by lymphatic drainage or it may accumulate as oedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. Why does a severe fall in plasma albumin promote oedema?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Albumin contributes importantly to plasma colloid osmotic pressure. When it falls, the oncotic opposition to outward capillary filtration weakens. If net filtration exceeds lymphatic return, interstitial fluid accumulates.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Does every capillary have an identical precapillary sphincter controlling whether it opens?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Precapillary sphincters appear in classic microvascular models and some anatomical beds, but the degree and arrangement of local smooth-muscle control vary between tissues. Arteriolar dilation and local endothelial/metabolic mechanisms can change perfusion without a universal sphincter at every capillary.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why does the revised Starling model place greater importance on lymphatic drainage than the classic 85% reabsorption diagram?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The endothelial glycocalyx modifies the effective oncotic gradient. In many tissues, slight net filtration persists even near venular ends at steady state, rather than sustained reabsorption of most filtered fluid. The <strong>lymphatic system</strong> returns this filtered fluid and proteins to blood.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. How may histamine increase tissue swelling without an initial increase in systemic arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Histamine can increase microvascular permeability, allowing more fluid and plasma proteins to cross into tissue. These local barrier changes can increase filtration and interstitial protein effects even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why can capillary recruitment improve exchange in active muscle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>When resistance vessels dilate and more exchange paths are adequately perfused, effective capillary surface area and blood delivery increase. This brings oxygenated blood close to more active fibres and facilitates diffusion in response to tissue demand.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Microcirculation:</strong> Small arterioles, capillaries and venules coordinate local flow and tissue exchange.</li>\n<li><strong>Capillary structure:</strong> Thin endothelium, basal lamina and possible pericytes; no typical muscular tunica media.</li>\n<li><strong>Types:</strong> Continuous, fenestrated and sinusoidal/discontinuous vessels differ in permeability.</li>\n<li><strong>Transport:</strong> Diffusion, fenestrae/paracellular routes, vesicular movement and bulk filtration are distinct.</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion:</strong> O₂ and CO₂ move mainly along partial-pressure gradients.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrostatic Pc:</strong> Usually promotes outward filtration; <strong>plasma oncotic πc</strong> usually opposes it.</li>\n<li><strong>Other forces:</strong> Interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures influence net fluid movement.</li>\n<li><strong>Classic lecture model:</strong> 24 L/day filtered, 85% reabsorbed, 15% by lymph — a historical illustrative model, not a universal modern rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Revised Starling concept:</strong> Endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients modify filtration, often leaving steady-state net filtration.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic removal:</strong> Returns excess interstitial fluid and proteins to circulating blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Capillary perfusion:</strong> Arteriolar tone, local activity and vascular geometry govern exchange-path access.</li>\n<li><strong>Permeability mediators:</strong> Histamine, bradykinin and substance P can increase endothelial leakage and swelling.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange 1. Why is the microcirculation necessary? The heart delivers blood to organs, but cells do not receive oxygen directly from the aorta. Oxygen and nutrients must cross the tiny exchange vessels close to each tissue cell. The microcirculation includes small arterioles, metarterioles or transitional vessels where present, capillaries and venules. Its tasks are to distribute blood locally, exchange substances and regulate interstitial fluid. Small arteriole → Capillary exchange → Venule → Venous return Why are capillaries suitable? Their thin walls and the very large combined capillary surface area bring flowing blood close to cells. Blood moves relatively slowly because the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is large. An important point is that capillaries contain only a small fraction of total blood volume, around 5% in a representative estimate, despite their importance for tissue exchange. 2. What is a capillary wall made of? A typical capillary is lined by one layer of endothelial cells supported by specialised extracellular matrix called the basal lamina (part of the basement membrane). Pericytes may lie externally and help support microvascular stability. Important histology distinction: A capillary does not possess a typical three-coat wall with a substantial smooth-muscle tunica media, unlike an artery. A capillary also does not need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane. Three principal capillary patterns Type Structure Common examples Exchange implication Continuous Relatively uninterrupted endothelial lining Muscle, skin, CNS Controlled passage; CNS barrier especially restrictive Fenestrated Endothelial pores/fenestrae Intestinal villi, endocrine organs, renal glomeruli Rapid exchange of water and small solutes Discontinuous / sinusoidal Wider gaps and discontinuities Liver, spleen, bone marrow Exchange of larger materials, specialised by tissue A note on units: Capillary gaps and fenestrations are microscopic. These openings are microscopic, often measured in nanometres, not millimetres. Treat the printed units as erroneous, while keeping the structural distinction the slide intended. Why do fenestrations help? A porous endothelium provides lower resistance to selected water-soluble materials crossing the vessel. But renal filtration still depends on additional specialised layers, including the glomerular basement membrane and podocyte filtration slits; pores alone do not determine the whole barrier. 3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall? The lecture names diffusion, filtration, fenestrations and vesicular transport. These are not all the same kind of process. Mechanism What moves? Main driving factor Diffusion O₂, CO₂ and many dissolved solutes Concentration or partial-pressure differences Intercellular routes/fenestrae Water and selected small hydrophilic substances Barrier structure plus relevant gradients Vesicular transport Selected macromolecular cargo Active endothelial transport mechanisms Bulk filtration Water and associated dissolved materials Hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences Why can oxygen cross efficiently? Oxygen diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient from capillary blood into metabolically active tissue. Carbon dioxide tends to diffuse in the opposite direction because cells produce it. The short diffusion distance and extensive capillary surface area enable exchange. Diffusion is not the same as fluid being forcibly filtered across the wall. 4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood? Tissues require more delivery when they become active. Local metabolic changes can dilate resistance vessels and increase the number of well-perfused microvascular pathways. Classical descriptions include metarterioles and precapillary sphincters. Such structures are useful classical teaching concepts but vary across vascular beds; not every capillary has an identical anatomical sphincter. Applied example: During exercise, active skeletal muscle produces metabolites that promote local dilation. More blood is delivered into exchange networks where oxygen demand is high. An important point is that capillary pressure may be estimated from the external force required to occlude a capillary. It is a method-dependent estimate, not a direct measure of all capillary pressures everywhere. 5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood? Two families of pressure forces influence movement of water across a microvascular wall: Hydrostatic pressure: Fluid pressure; inside a capillary it usually tends to push water outward. Oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure: Due mainly to proteins; plasma proteins generally oppose excessive outward water movement by attracting or retaining water. The classic balance uses four named quantities: Quantity Location Typical direction in simple model Pc Capillary hydrostatic pressure Promotes outward filtration Pi Interstitial hydrostatic pressure May oppose or favour filtration, depending on sign πc Plasma capillary oncotic pressure Opposes outward filtration πi Interstitial oncotic pressure Favours outward filtration Figure 01. Hydrostatic and oncotic influences on capillary filtration Pc: outward Plasma oncotic: inward Capillary blood Interstitial tissue space A conceptual comparison of two important forces. Actual net filtration also depends on interstitial forces, surface area, permeability and the endothelial glycocalyx. Why does plasma albumin matter? Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. Low albumin weakens the oncotic opposition to filtration, increasing oedema risk if lymphatic drainage cannot keep up. 6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding The slides 85–88 teach hydrostatic and oncotic gradients and give an example of 24 litres filtered per day, 85% reabsorbed into capillaries and 15% returned by lymph. This is the classic textbook model and explains the intended arithmetic. Modern physiological clarification: The endothelial glycocalyx is a protein-rich surface layer that affects effective filtration and protein gradients immediately beneath it. In many continuous capillary beds, a low level of net filtration persists during steady state, including towards the venous end. Much of the filtered fluid is returned through lymphatics, rather than assuming a universal large sustained venous-end reabsorption. What should a student learn? Understand the lecturer's four pressure forces and numerical illustration, but also know that the 85%/15% split is not a universal modern physiological rule. Transient absorption after sudden pressure changes and some specialised tissues are exceptions. This distinction prevents a major misconception: the lymphatic system is not merely an optional back-up after routine large venous reabsorption. 7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability? Local vessel conditions alter exchange: Arteriolar dilation: May increase blood delivery and capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging filtration under some conditions. Venous congestion: Increases downstream and often capillary hydrostatic pressure, promoting filtration. Increased endothelial permeability: Histamine, bradykinin and substance P are examples named by the lecturer; inflammatory conditions can increase fluid/protein escape. Increased surface area: Perfusion of more capillaries can increase total exchange capacity. Clinical link: Inflammation causes microvascular endothelial changes, enabling more protein and fluid to escape into tissues. Venous obstruction may raise fluid filtration without requiring an initially leaky capillary barrier. These are different causes of swelling. The next two chapters distinguish excessive tissue-fluid accumulation (oedema) from inadequate lymphatic clearance. Understanding Check 1. Why can capillaries exchange substances efficiently even though their walls are so thin? Reveal explanation Their thin endothelial lining and short diffusion distance bring blood close to tissue fluid. Numerous capillaries provide a large total surface area, while relatively low mean velocity creates time for exchange. Thinness is therefore an advantage for transport, not evidence that capillaries are ineffective vessels. 2. Do capillaries need a thick connective-tissue subendothelial layer to have a basement membrane? Reveal explanation No. The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. It is not the same thing as a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer, and a typical capillary lacks the muscular middle coat of an artery. 3. Why are fenestrated capillaries useful in endocrine organs and the intestinal villi? Reveal explanation Endothelial fenestrations permit relatively rapid water and small-solute exchange, suited to tissues with high exchange requirements. Their barrier properties still depend on other structural features and the tissue involved. 4. Why is the lecture's “mm” unit for endothelial fenestrations unsuitable? Reveal explanation Fenestrations are tiny pores in microscopic endothelial cells, commonly described on the nanometre scale. A pore several millimetres wide would dwarf the cell and vessel. The lecture intends to describe small permeable openings, not literal millimetre-sized gaps. 5. What drives oxygen diffusion from capillaries into active tissues? Reveal explanation Tissue cells consume oxygen, lowering local tissue oxygen partial pressure. Oxygen then diffuses from the relatively higher partial pressure in capillary blood towards lower tissue partial pressure. This is diffusion down a partial-pressure gradient, not bulk filtration of the entire blood volume. 6. Distinguish diffusion from filtration in one physiological example each. Reveal explanation Diffusion transfers individual molecules such as O₂ down a concentration/partial-pressure difference. Filtration moves bulk water across the microvascular barrier because of hydrostatic and oncotic forces. Solutes may accompany filtered fluid, but the driving principles differ. 7. If capillary hydrostatic pressure increases with other factors unchanged, what happens to filtration? Reveal explanation Outward filtration generally increases. Hydrostatic pressure within the capillary pushes fluid towards the interstitium. Extra filtered fluid must be removed by lymphatic drainage or it may accumulate as oedema. 8. Why does a severe fall in plasma albumin promote oedema? Reveal explanation Albumin contributes importantly to plasma colloid osmotic pressure. When it falls, the oncotic opposition to outward capillary filtration weakens. If net filtration exceeds lymphatic return, interstitial fluid accumulates. 9. Does every capillary have an identical precapillary sphincter controlling whether it opens? Reveal explanation No. Precapillary sphincters appear in classic microvascular models and some anatomical beds, but the degree and arrangement of local smooth-muscle control vary between tissues. Arteriolar dilation and local endothelial/metabolic mechanisms can change perfusion without a universal sphincter at every capillary. 10. Why does the revised Starling model place greater importance on lymphatic drainage than the classic 85% reabsorption diagram? Reveal explanation The endothelial glycocalyx modifies the effective oncotic gradient. In many tissues, slight net filtration persists even near venular ends at steady state, rather than sustained reabsorption of most filtered fluid. The lymphatic system returns this filtered fluid and proteins to blood. 11. How may histamine increase tissue swelling without an initial increase in systemic arterial pressure? Reveal explanation Histamine can increase microvascular permeability, allowing more fluid and plasma proteins to cross into tissue. These local barrier changes can increase filtration and interstitial protein effects even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen. 12. Why can capillary recruitment improve exchange in active muscle? Reveal explanation When resistance vessels dilate and more exchange paths are adequately perfused, effective capillary surface area and blood delivery increase. This brings oxygenated blood close to more active fibres and facilitates diffusion in response to tissue demand. Chapter Essentials Microcirculation: Small arterioles, capillaries and venules coordinate local flow and tissue exchange. Capillary structure: Thin endothelium, basal lamina and possible pericytes; no typical muscular tunica media. Types: Continuous, fenestrated and sinusoidal/discontinuous vessels differ in permeability. Transport: Diffusion, fenestrae/paracellular routes, vesicular movement and bulk filtration are distinct. Diffusion: O₂ and CO₂ move mainly along partial-pressure gradients. Hydrostatic Pc: Usually promotes outward filtration; plasma oncotic πc usually opposes it. Other forces: Interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures influence net fluid movement. Classic lecture model: 24 L/day filtered, 85% reabsorbed, 15% by lymph — a historical illustrative model, not a universal modern rule. Revised Starling concept: Endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients modify filtration, often leaving steady-state net filtration. Lymphatic removal: Returns excess interstitial fluid and proteins to circulating blood. Capillary perfusion: Arteriolar tone, local activity and vascular geometry govern exchange-path access. Permeability mediators: Histamine, bradykinin and substance P can increase endothelial leakage and swelling."},{"slug":"edema","title":"Formation of Oedema","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"89-95","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-exactly-is-oedema","title":"1. What exactly is oedema?"},{"id":"2-what-physical-forces-govern-filtration","title":"2. What physical forces govern filtration?"},{"id":"3-five-routes-to-the-same-outcome-excess-interstitial-fluid","title":"3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid"},{"id":"4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema","title":"4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema?"},{"id":"5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema","title":"5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema?"},{"id":"6-how-does-inflammation-increase-oedema","title":"6. How does inflammation increase oedema?"},{"id":"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-persistent-swelling","title":"7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling?"},{"id":"8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different","title":"8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different?"},{"id":"9-which-location-of-oedema-suggests-which-mechanism","title":"9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Formation of Oedema</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-exactly-is-oedema\">1. What exactly is oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Oedema</strong> is the <strong>abnormal accumulation of excess fluid in tissue interstitial spaces</strong>. It is not simply the presence of fluid outside blood vessels: cells normally live surrounded by a regulated volume of interstitial fluid.</p>\n<p>The important balance is between:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microvascular filtration:</strong> Fluid leaving blood vessels into tissue.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic drainage:</strong> Fluid and proteins being returned from tissues to the venous circulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"edema-balance\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fluid balance causes oedema\"><span><strong>Filtration ↑</strong><small>More fluid entering tissue</small></span><b>+</b><span><strong>Drainage insufficient</strong><small>Less fluid leaving tissue</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Oedema</strong><small>Fluid accumulates</small></span></div>\n\n<p>When filtration exceeds the removal capacity of lymphatic vessels, interstitial fluid accumulates. Oedema may therefore occur even if the heart is still beating and blood is continuing to circulate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-physical-forces-govern-filtration\">2. What physical forces govern filtration?</h2>\n<p>The principal factors include <strong>capillary hydrostatic pressure, interstitial hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, interstitial oncotic pressure, filtration coefficient, pre-/postcapillary resistance and capillary surface area</strong>.</p>\n<p>A conventional form of the Starling equation summarises these:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Traditional Starling filtration model</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)]\">J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)]</span></div>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Symbol</th>\n  <th>Meaning</th>\n  <th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Jv</strong></td>\n  <td>Net fluid flux</td>\n  <td>Positive value represents net outward filtration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Kf</strong></td>\n  <td>Filtration coefficient</td>\n  <td>Reflects permeability and filtering surface area</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pc</strong></td>\n  <td>Capillary hydrostatic pressure</td>\n  <td>Drives fluid out of capillaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pi</strong></td>\n  <td>Interstitial hydrostatic pressure</td>\n  <td>Can oppose outward filtration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>πc</strong></td>\n  <td>Capillary plasma oncotic pressure</td>\n  <td>Opposes outward filtration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>πi</strong></td>\n  <td>Interstitial oncotic pressure</td>\n  <td>Can promote outward filtration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>σ</strong></td>\n  <td>Protein reflection coefficient</td>\n  <td>How effectively the barrier restricts protein passage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why is albumin important?</strong> Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. If plasma protein concentration falls, its normal opposition to fluid loss becomes weaker.</p>\n<h3>Important refinement: endothelial glycocalyx</h3>\n<p>The classic model assumes a simple balance between capillary and bulk interstitial proteins. Modern research shows the <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> affects the relevant oncotic gradient immediately beneath the endothelial surface.</p>\n<p>In many tissues, steady-state filtration persists along much of the capillary bed; a large universal reabsorption at the venous end is <strong>not</strong> expected. Lymphatics provide the main ongoing return path for filtered fluid.</p>\n<p>The lecture's <strong>24 L/day, 85% capillary reabsorption and 15% lymphatic return</strong> is a <strong>historical illustrative model</strong>, not an exact modern description for all tissues. Some transient reabsorption and specialised tissue exceptions remain possible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-five-routes-to-the-same-outcome-excess-interstitial-fluid\">3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Mechanism</th>\n  <th>Physiological change</th>\n  <th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Higher Pc</strong></td>\n  <td>More hydrostatic pressure pushes water outward</td>\n  <td>Venous obstruction, heart failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Lower πc</strong></td>\n  <td>Weaker plasma oncotic opposition to water loss</td>\n  <td>Liver disease, renal protein loss, malnutrition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Higher permeability/Kf</strong></td>\n  <td>Vessel barrier allows more fluid and protein escape</td>\n  <td>Inflammation, burns, allergy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Impaired lymph drainage</strong></td>\n  <td>Filtered fluid and proteins cannot return adequately</td>\n  <td>Filariasis, lymph node removal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Salt and water retention</strong></td>\n  <td>More ECF volume and often higher microvascular pressure</td>\n  <td>Renal dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Key reasoning:</strong> Different diseases can all create swelling, but through <strong>different failed parts of the fluid-balance system</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\">4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer lists increased venous pressure, venous obstruction, venous valve incompetence, prolonged standing and heart failure.</p>\n<p>If pressure at the venous end of a capillary bed rises, the pressure gradient across its resistance vessels changes, often raising <strong>capillary hydrostatic pressure Pc</strong>. More fluid is pushed into the interstitium.</p>\n<p><strong>Example — standing still:</strong> Gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent leg veins. Without the calf-muscle pump, venous pooling persists and capillary filtration rises. Ankles can swell even though the heart's intrinsic muscle is normal.</p>\n<p><strong>Example — cardiac failure:</strong> Elevated systemic venous pressure may produce dependent peripheral oedema; elevated pulmonary venous pressure may produce pulmonary oedema, impairing lung gas exchange.</p>\n<h3>Why can arteriolar dilation or venular constriction affect filtration?</h3>\n<p>A simplified microvascular pressure system has resistance <strong>before</strong> and <strong>after</strong> the capillary bed. Reduced precapillary resistance or increased postcapillary resistance can raise capillary pressure relative to surrounding tissue.</p>\n<p>The lecturer explicitly lists <strong>arteriolar dilation and venular constriction</strong> as factors that can increase filtration. Their effect depends on the wider microvascular context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\">5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema?</h2>\n<p>Plasma proteins help maintain <strong>plasma oncotic pressure</strong>. If albumin falls markedly, the oncotic pressure opposing capillary filtration decreases.</p>\n<p>Three important examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Liver disease:</strong> Reduced albumin synthesis in significant hepatic dysfunction.</li>\n<li><strong>Kidney disease:</strong> Excess urinary protein loss in conditions such as nephrotic syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe malnutrition:</strong> Inadequate protein availability and related metabolic effects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Albumin ↓ → plasma oncotic pressure ↓ → filtration easier relative to retention → interstitial fluid may accumulate if lymphatic return is exceeded.</p>\n<p>The lecture also mentions <strong>osmotically active substances accumulating in tissues</strong>. Increased effective interstitial oncotic forces can draw or retain more water in the tissue environment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-inflammation-increase-oedema\">6. How does inflammation increase oedema?</h2>\n<p>The lecture names <strong>histamine, bradykinin and substance P</strong> among agents that increase permeability.</p>\n<p>Inflammatory signals change endothelial barrier properties, allowing greater fluid movement and, in some vascular beds, more protein escape. This can increase <strong>Kf</strong> and alter the effective oncotic balance, promoting local tissue swelling.</p>\n<p>This is not identical to swelling caused purely by raised venous pressure: the <strong>barrier itself</strong> has changed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-persistent-swelling\">7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling?</h2>\n<p>Even when capillary pressure is fairly normal, some fluid and proteins continually reach the interstitium. Lymphatic vessels normally return these materials to the venous circulation.</p>\n<p>If lymphatic channels are damaged or obstructed, drainage cannot match filtration. Protein-rich fluid accumulates and chronic inflammation and tissue remodelling can follow.</p>\n<p>The principal factors include <strong>filarial infection (elephantiasis)</strong> and <strong>radical mastectomy</strong> affecting lymph drainage as examples. Details are developed in Chapter 15.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different\">8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different?</h2>\n<p><strong>Pitting oedema</strong> means sustained finger pressure produces a temporary indentation. Mobile interstitial fluid is displaced by the pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-pitting oedema</strong> may involve tissue changes such as fibrosis, protein deposition or altered connective-tissue properties, so an indentation is less easily produced.</p>\n<p><strong>Important qualification:</strong> Early lymphoedema can <strong>pit</strong>. Longstanding lymphoedema may become firm and non-pitting as fibrosis develops. Therefore, the lecturer's short “lymphoedema — not pitting” statement describes a common <strong>late pattern</strong>, not every stage.</p>\n<p>Clinical signs alone should not be used to determine a diagnosis without considering the full presentation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-which-location-of-oedema-suggests-which-mechanism\">9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Pattern</th>\n  <th>A physiological possibility</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Dependent ankle swelling after standing</strong></td>\n  <td>Gravity-related venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure rise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pulmonary oedema</strong></td>\n  <td>Raised pulmonary microvascular pressure and/or permeability, impairing gas exchange</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Generalised swelling with low albumin</strong></td>\n  <td>Reduced plasma oncotic opposition to filtration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Chronic swelling after lymph-node removal</strong></td>\n  <td>Impaired regional lymphatic drainage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are <strong>mechanistic associations</strong>, not exclusive diagnoses. For example, more than one oedema mechanism can coexist in heart or kidney disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Central takeaway:</strong> The visible swelling is the outcome. The important question is <strong>which fluid-balance mechanism failed?</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why doesn't a healthy person normally develop continually increasing tissue swelling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Microvascular filtration supplies some interstitial fluid, but lymphatic vessels return the excess fluid and escaped proteins to the venous circulation. Normally drainage matches ongoing net filtration sufficiently to prevent progressive interstitial accumulation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. A patient has venous obstruction in one leg. Which Starling force is most likely increased?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc).</strong> Obstruction increases pressure upstream in venules and capillaries, pushing more fluid into tissue. Swelling develops when the increase in filtration exceeds local lymphatic capacity.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Why might prolonged motionless standing produce ankle oedema?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Gravity raises venous pressure in dependent legs. This elevates capillary hydrostatic pressure and filtration. Lack of calf-muscle pumping also reduces venous emptying, favouring interstitial fluid accumulation at the ankles.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Why can advanced liver disease cause oedema through a different mechanism from venous obstruction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Severe liver disease may reduce albumin synthesis, lowering <strong>plasma oncotic pressure</strong>. Fluid is then less strongly retained within capillaries. Venous obstruction, by contrast, chiefly increases hydrostatic pressure; either may cause excess filtration and oedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. How can nephrotic protein loss produce swelling despite apparently normal arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Heavy urinary protein loss can reduce circulating albumin, decreasing plasma colloid osmotic pressure. This favours greater net fluid escape from microvessels; additional renal sodium and water retention may worsen the swelling.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. A patient develops red, warm swelling around an inflamed area. What barrier mechanism can produce this?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Inflammatory mediators such as histamine and bradykinin can increase endothelial permeability. More water and some proteins enter local tissues, raising filtration and changing the effective oncotic conditions. This is <strong>increased permeability</strong>, not merely pressure loading.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. What does the capillary filtration coefficient Kf represent?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Kf</strong> represents the combined effect of exchange surface area and hydraulic permeability of the microvascular barrier. A larger Kf permits more volume movement for a given effective pressure difference, potentially increasing filtration and swelling.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. Why is the classic statement that 85% of filtered fluid is always reabsorbed into venous capillaries misleading?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The revised Starling principle accounts for the endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients. In many continuous capillary beds, low net filtration persists at steady state instead of large sustained venous-end reabsorption. <strong>Lymphatic return</strong> is central to fluid balance. The percentage is a historical illustrative model, not universal physiology.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. Why might early lymphoedema be pitting while longstanding lymphoedema is often non-pitting?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Early obstruction produces relatively mobile accumulated tissue fluid that may be displaced by finger pressure. Chronic lymphatic failure can cause tissue inflammation, fibrosis and skin thickening, making the swollen tissue firmer and less readily indented.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Compare oedema caused by heart failure with oedema caused by lymph-node removal.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Heart failure may elevate systemic or pulmonary venous pressure, increasing capillary hydrostatic filtration. Lymph-node removal can damage lymphatic return pathways, reducing clearance even if filtration initially remains near normal. Both ultimately produce excess interstitial fluid through different primary mechanisms.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why can left-sided cardiac failure interfere with breathing by producing oedema?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Impaired left-heart forward function may elevate pulmonary venous and capillary pressures, raising fluid filtration into the lung interstitium and potentially alveoli. This increases diffusion distance and disrupts gas exchange, contributing to breathlessness.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why is renal sodium/water retention able to worsen oedema in more than one disease?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Retention expands extracellular fluid volume and can increase vascular filling and capillary hydrostatic pressure. If plasma oncotic pressure is low, venous pressure is high or lymph drainage is impaired, the additional retained fluid can further exceed tissue-fluid clearance capacity.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Oedema:</strong> Abnormal excess interstitial fluid caused by filtration exceeding clearance or reduced lymph drainage.</li>\n<li><strong>Traditional Starling model:</strong> Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)].</li>\n<li><strong>Pc:</strong> Capillary hydrostatic pressure favours outward fluid movement.</li>\n<li><strong>Plasma πc:</strong> Mainly albumin-derived oncotic pressure opposes outward filtration.</li>\n<li><strong>Kf:</strong> Hydraulic permeability × filtering surface; inflammatory changes can raise fluid leakage.</li>\n<li><strong>Raised venous pressure:</strong> From obstruction, heart failure or standing increases capillary filtration.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduced plasma albumin:</strong> May follow liver disease, kidney protein loss or malnutrition.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflammation:</strong> Histamine, bradykinin and other mediators can increase endothelial permeability.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic dysfunction:</strong> Impaired fluid/protein return causes local or regional swelling.</li>\n<li><strong>Revised Starling model:</strong> Glycocalyx-mediated gradients and lymphatic return revise universal venous-end reabsorption assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Pitting:</strong> Early fluid-rich swelling can pit; advanced lymphoedema may become non-pitting with fibrosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Dependent, pulmonary, generalised and post-surgical patterns suggest different mechanisms.</li>\n<li><strong>Excess salt/water:</strong> Can amplify swelling through expanded ECF and increased microvascular pressures.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Formation of Oedema 1. What exactly is oedema? Oedema is the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid in tissue interstitial spaces. It is not simply the presence of fluid outside blood vessels: cells normally live surrounded by a regulated volume of interstitial fluid. The important balance is between: Microvascular filtration: Fluid leaving blood vessels into tissue. Lymphatic drainage: Fluid and proteins being returned from tissues to the venous circulation. Filtration ↑ More fluid entering tissue + Drainage insufficient Less fluid leaving tissue → Oedema Fluid accumulates When filtration exceeds the removal capacity of lymphatic vessels, interstitial fluid accumulates. Oedema may therefore occur even if the heart is still beating and blood is continuing to circulate. 2. What physical forces govern filtration? The principal factors include capillary hydrostatic pressure, interstitial hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, interstitial oncotic pressure, filtration coefficient, pre-/postcapillary resistance and capillary surface area. A conventional form of the Starling equation summarises these: Traditional Starling filtration model J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)] Symbol Meaning Why it matters Jv Net fluid flux Positive value represents net outward filtration Kf Filtration coefficient Reflects permeability and filtering surface area Pc Capillary hydrostatic pressure Drives fluid out of capillaries Pi Interstitial hydrostatic pressure Can oppose outward filtration πc Capillary plasma oncotic pressure Opposes outward filtration πi Interstitial oncotic pressure Can promote outward filtration σ Protein reflection coefficient How effectively the barrier restricts protein passage Why is albumin important? Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. If plasma protein concentration falls, its normal opposition to fluid loss becomes weaker. Important refinement: endothelial glycocalyx The classic model assumes a simple balance between capillary and bulk interstitial proteins. Modern research shows the endothelial glycocalyx affects the relevant oncotic gradient immediately beneath the endothelial surface. In many tissues, steady-state filtration persists along much of the capillary bed; a large universal reabsorption at the venous end is not expected. Lymphatics provide the main ongoing return path for filtered fluid. The lecture's 24 L/day, 85% capillary reabsorption and 15% lymphatic return is a historical illustrative model, not an exact modern description for all tissues. Some transient reabsorption and specialised tissue exceptions remain possible. 3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid Mechanism Physiological change Example Higher Pc More hydrostatic pressure pushes water outward Venous obstruction, heart failure Lower πc Weaker plasma oncotic opposition to water loss Liver disease, renal protein loss, malnutrition Higher permeability/Kf Vessel barrier allows more fluid and protein escape Inflammation, burns, allergy Impaired lymph drainage Filtered fluid and proteins cannot return adequately Filariasis, lymph node removal Salt and water retention More ECF volume and often higher microvascular pressure Renal dysfunction Key reasoning: Different diseases can all create swelling, but through different failed parts of the fluid-balance system. 4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema? The lecturer lists increased venous pressure, venous obstruction, venous valve incompetence, prolonged standing and heart failure. If pressure at the venous end of a capillary bed rises, the pressure gradient across its resistance vessels changes, often raising capillary hydrostatic pressure Pc. More fluid is pushed into the interstitium. Example — standing still: Gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent leg veins. Without the calf-muscle pump, venous pooling persists and capillary filtration rises. Ankles can swell even though the heart's intrinsic muscle is normal. Example — cardiac failure: Elevated systemic venous pressure may produce dependent peripheral oedema; elevated pulmonary venous pressure may produce pulmonary oedema, impairing lung gas exchange. Why can arteriolar dilation or venular constriction affect filtration? A simplified microvascular pressure system has resistance before and after the capillary bed. Reduced precapillary resistance or increased postcapillary resistance can raise capillary pressure relative to surrounding tissue. The lecturer explicitly lists arteriolar dilation and venular constriction as factors that can increase filtration. Their effect depends on the wider microvascular context. 5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema? Plasma proteins help maintain plasma oncotic pressure. If albumin falls markedly, the oncotic pressure opposing capillary filtration decreases. Three important examples: Liver disease: Reduced albumin synthesis in significant hepatic dysfunction. Kidney disease: Excess urinary protein loss in conditions such as nephrotic syndrome. Severe malnutrition: Inadequate protein availability and related metabolic effects. Mechanism: Albumin ↓ → plasma oncotic pressure ↓ → filtration easier relative to retention → interstitial fluid may accumulate if lymphatic return is exceeded. The lecture also mentions osmotically active substances accumulating in tissues. Increased effective interstitial oncotic forces can draw or retain more water in the tissue environment. 6. How does inflammation increase oedema? The lecture names histamine, bradykinin and substance P among agents that increase permeability. Inflammatory signals change endothelial barrier properties, allowing greater fluid movement and, in some vascular beds, more protein escape. This can increase Kf and alter the effective oncotic balance, promoting local tissue swelling. This is not identical to swelling caused purely by raised venous pressure: the barrier itself has changed. 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling? Even when capillary pressure is fairly normal, some fluid and proteins continually reach the interstitium. Lymphatic vessels normally return these materials to the venous circulation. If lymphatic channels are damaged or obstructed, drainage cannot match filtration. Protein-rich fluid accumulates and chronic inflammation and tissue remodelling can follow. The principal factors include filarial infection (elephantiasis) and radical mastectomy affecting lymph drainage as examples. Details are developed in Chapter 15. 8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different? Pitting oedema means sustained finger pressure produces a temporary indentation. Mobile interstitial fluid is displaced by the pressure. Non-pitting oedema may involve tissue changes such as fibrosis, protein deposition or altered connective-tissue properties, so an indentation is less easily produced. Important qualification: Early lymphoedema can pit. Longstanding lymphoedema may become firm and non-pitting as fibrosis develops. Therefore, the lecturer's short “lymphoedema — not pitting” statement describes a common late pattern, not every stage. Clinical signs alone should not be used to determine a diagnosis without considering the full presentation. 9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism? Pattern A physiological possibility Dependent ankle swelling after standing Gravity-related venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure rise Pulmonary oedema Raised pulmonary microvascular pressure and/or permeability, impairing gas exchange Generalised swelling with low albumin Reduced plasma oncotic opposition to filtration Chronic swelling after lymph-node removal Impaired regional lymphatic drainage These are mechanistic associations, not exclusive diagnoses. For example, more than one oedema mechanism can coexist in heart or kidney disease. Central takeaway: The visible swelling is the outcome. The important question is which fluid-balance mechanism failed? Understanding Check 1. Why doesn't a healthy person normally develop continually increasing tissue swelling? Reveal explanation Microvascular filtration supplies some interstitial fluid, but lymphatic vessels return the excess fluid and escaped proteins to the venous circulation. Normally drainage matches ongoing net filtration sufficiently to prevent progressive interstitial accumulation. 2. A patient has venous obstruction in one leg. Which Starling force is most likely increased? Reveal explanation Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc). Obstruction increases pressure upstream in venules and capillaries, pushing more fluid into tissue. Swelling develops when the increase in filtration exceeds local lymphatic capacity. 3. Why might prolonged motionless standing produce ankle oedema? Reveal explanation Gravity raises venous pressure in dependent legs. This elevates capillary hydrostatic pressure and filtration. Lack of calf-muscle pumping also reduces venous emptying, favouring interstitial fluid accumulation at the ankles. 4. Why can advanced liver disease cause oedema through a different mechanism from venous obstruction? Reveal explanation Severe liver disease may reduce albumin synthesis, lowering plasma oncotic pressure. Fluid is then less strongly retained within capillaries. Venous obstruction, by contrast, chiefly increases hydrostatic pressure; either may cause excess filtration and oedema. 5. How can nephrotic protein loss produce swelling despite apparently normal arterial pressure? Reveal explanation Heavy urinary protein loss can reduce circulating albumin, decreasing plasma colloid osmotic pressure. This favours greater net fluid escape from microvessels; additional renal sodium and water retention may worsen the swelling. 6. A patient develops red, warm swelling around an inflamed area. What barrier mechanism can produce this? Reveal explanation Inflammatory mediators such as histamine and bradykinin can increase endothelial permeability. More water and some proteins enter local tissues, raising filtration and changing the effective oncotic conditions. This is increased permeability, not merely pressure loading. 7. What does the capillary filtration coefficient Kf represent? Reveal explanation Kf represents the combined effect of exchange surface area and hydraulic permeability of the microvascular barrier. A larger Kf permits more volume movement for a given effective pressure difference, potentially increasing filtration and swelling. 8. Why is the classic statement that 85% of filtered fluid is always reabsorbed into venous capillaries misleading? Reveal explanation The revised Starling principle accounts for the endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients. In many continuous capillary beds, low net filtration persists at steady state instead of large sustained venous-end reabsorption. Lymphatic return is central to fluid balance. The percentage is a historical illustrative model, not universal physiology. 9. Why might early lymphoedema be pitting while longstanding lymphoedema is often non-pitting? Reveal explanation Early obstruction produces relatively mobile accumulated tissue fluid that may be displaced by finger pressure. Chronic lymphatic failure can cause tissue inflammation, fibrosis and skin thickening, making the swollen tissue firmer and less readily indented. 10. Compare oedema caused by heart failure with oedema caused by lymph-node removal. Reveal explanation Heart failure may elevate systemic or pulmonary venous pressure, increasing capillary hydrostatic filtration. Lymph-node removal can damage lymphatic return pathways, reducing clearance even if filtration initially remains near normal. Both ultimately produce excess interstitial fluid through different primary mechanisms. 11. Why can left-sided cardiac failure interfere with breathing by producing oedema? Reveal explanation Impaired left-heart forward function may elevate pulmonary venous and capillary pressures, raising fluid filtration into the lung interstitium and potentially alveoli. This increases diffusion distance and disrupts gas exchange, contributing to breathlessness. 12. Why is renal sodium/water retention able to worsen oedema in more than one disease? Reveal explanation Retention expands extracellular fluid volume and can increase vascular filling and capillary hydrostatic pressure. If plasma oncotic pressure is low, venous pressure is high or lymph drainage is impaired, the additional retained fluid can further exceed tissue-fluid clearance capacity. Chapter Essentials Oedema: Abnormal excess interstitial fluid caused by filtration exceeding clearance or reduced lymph drainage. Traditional Starling model: Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]. Pc: Capillary hydrostatic pressure favours outward fluid movement. Plasma πc: Mainly albumin-derived oncotic pressure opposes outward filtration. Kf: Hydraulic permeability × filtering surface; inflammatory changes can raise fluid leakage. Raised venous pressure: From obstruction, heart failure or standing increases capillary filtration. Reduced plasma albumin: May follow liver disease, kidney protein loss or malnutrition. Inflammation: Histamine, bradykinin and other mediators can increase endothelial permeability. Lymphatic dysfunction: Impaired fluid/protein return causes local or regional swelling. Revised Starling model: Glycocalyx-mediated gradients and lymphatic return revise universal venous-end reabsorption assumptions. Pitting: Early fluid-rich swelling can pit; advanced lymphoedema may become non-pitting with fibrosis. Distribution: Dependent, pulmonary, generalised and post-surgical patterns suggest different mechanisms. Excess salt/water: Can amplify swelling through expanded ECF and increased microvascular pressures."},{"slug":"lymphatics","title":"Lymphatic Circulation","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"60 and 94-95; Regional Circulation 47-48","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-lymphatic-vessels-if-we-already-have-veins","title":"1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins?"},{"id":"2-how-does-lymph-enter-a-lymphatic-capillary","title":"2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary?"},{"id":"3-how-can-lymph-move-when-there-is-no-central-lymphatic-heart","title":"3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart?"},{"id":"4-where-does-lymph-finally-return-to-blood","title":"4. Where does lymph finally return to blood?"},{"id":"5-why-is-the-intestinal-lymphatic-system-important-in-fat-absorption","title":"5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption?"},{"id":"6-how-does-lymphatic-drainage-protect-tissue-fluid-balance","title":"6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance?"},{"id":"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-lymphoedema","title":"7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema?"},{"id":"8-what-happens-if-lymphatic-function-is-lost","title":"8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Lymphatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-lymphatic-vessels-if-we-already-have-veins\">1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins?</h2>\n<p>Blood capillaries continuously exchange substances and filter some water into surrounding tissues. If every bit of fluid and escaped protein stayed there, tissues would swell progressively.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lymphatic system</strong> collects excess <strong>interstitial fluid, proteins and selected particles</strong> and returns them to the venous circulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymph</strong> is fluid inside lymphatic vessels. It develops from interstitial fluid that has entered lymphatic capillaries; its composition varies with the tissue drained.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Lymphatic return route\"><span>Capillary filtration</span><b>→</b><span>Interstitial fluid</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph capillaries</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph vessels and ducts</span><b>→</b><span>Venous blood</span></div>\n\n<p>A key principle is that lymphatic drainage handles fluid left after microvascular exchange. Under the <strong>revised Starling principle</strong>, lymphatic return is a major normal steady-state route for filtered fluid, not merely a back-up for a universal 85% venous-end reabsorption process.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-how-does-lymph-enter-a-lymphatic-capillary\">2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary?</h2>\n<p><strong>Initial lymphatic capillaries</strong> begin as <strong>blind-ended, thin-walled vessels</strong> in tissues. Their endothelial cells overlap and are connected with specialised anchoring structures to surrounding tissue.</p>\n<p>When local interstitial pressure exceeds pressure inside the initial lymphatic, overlapping endothelial margins can separate to admit <strong>fluid, proteins and some particles</strong>. The flap-like arrangement restricts reverse leakage when pressure changes.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure lymph-entry-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. From capillary filtration to a one-way lymphatic entry route</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 590 185\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original diagram of a blood capillary above an interstitial space, with arrows showing fluid filtration into a blind-ended green lymphatic capillary and one-way lymph movement towards a collecting vessel.\"><rect x=\"38\" y=\"28\" width=\"370\" height=\"40\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"#efc9cc\" stroke=\"#bb626e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g fill=\"#ac4857\"><circle cx=\"108\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"196\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"326\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/></g><path d=\"M220 73 V113m-7 -10 7 10 7 -10\" stroke=\"#d38428\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M36 145 Q37 121 70 121 L407 121 L407 166 L70 166 Q37 166 36 145 Z\" fill=\"#d9f3e6\" stroke=\"#3b9474\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M104 145H360m-10 -8 10 8 -10 8\" stroke=\"#16805e\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><g font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\"><text x=\"43\" y=\"17\">Blood capillary</text><text x=\"237\" y=\"94\">Filtered fluid ↓</text><text x=\"65\" y=\"112\">Interstitial space</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"174\">Initial lymphatic capillary →</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"144\">To larger</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"159\">lymphatics</text></g></svg><p>Original conceptual schematic based on the lecturer's lymphatic motion/pressure-gradient diagram. It illustrates fluid entry and one-way transport, not exact vessel anatomy.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why is this structure useful?</strong> It can admit material that cannot simply diffuse rapidly back into a blood capillary, including interstitial proteins.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-can-lymph-move-when-there-is-no-central-lymphatic-heart\">3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart?</h2>\n<p>Lymph flows along a low-pressure system. Several mechanisms promote its movement:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One-way valves:</strong> Limit backward flow when external compression changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Contracting collecting lymphatics:</strong> Smooth-muscle segments rhythmically compress lymph.</li>\n<li><strong>Skeletal-muscle movement:</strong> Surrounding muscle compression moves lymph along valved channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory pump:</strong> Changing thoracic and abdominal pressures help direct flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjacent tissue/arterial movement:</strong> Can also contribute to mechanical propulsion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer lists all these components, including pumping by the lymphatic walls and the pressure relationships where the major ducts empty into veins.</p>\n<p><strong>A useful comparison:</strong> Venous return and lymphatic flow both rely partly on valves and muscle/respiratory pumping, but lymphatics begin <strong>blindly in the interstitium</strong> rather than as continuations of blood capillaries within a closed blood vessel circuit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-where-does-lymph-finally-return-to-blood\">4. Where does lymph finally return to blood?</h2>\n<p>The lymphatic system has major collecting pathways:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Pathway</th>\n  <th>Main drainage territory</th>\n  <th>Typical return location</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Thoracic duct</strong></td>\n  <td>Most of body, including both lower limbs, abdomen and left upper body</td>\n  <td>Left venous angle near left internal jugular–subclavian junction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Right lymphatic duct</strong></td>\n  <td>Right side of head/neck, right upper limb, right part of thorax</td>\n  <td>Right venous angle near right internal jugular–subclavian junction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are standard drainage territories; <strong>normal anatomical variants</strong> occur.</p>\n<p>The mechanism involves lymph entering the right and left subclavian venous regions. More precise anatomy places the usual openings at or near the <strong>venous angles</strong>.</p>\n<h3>What role do lymph nodes play?</h3>\n<p>Lymph travelling through regional collecting vessels often passes through <strong>lymph nodes</strong>. These structures expose lymph-borne materials to immune cells and contribute to immune surveillance.</p>\n<p>Lymph nodes are <strong>not</strong> the structures that create the main pumping force. Fluid movement primarily depends on lymphatic vessel contractions, pressure gradients, movement and valves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-is-the-intestinal-lymphatic-system-important-in-fat-absorption\">5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer names fatty acids and cholesterol absorption as lymphatic functions. A major intestinal lymphatic capillary inside each villus is a <strong>lacteal</strong>.</p>\n<p>After digestion, many long-chain dietary fatty acids and monoacylglycerols are taken up by enterocytes, reassembled into triglycerides and packaged, together with cholesterol and other lipids, into <strong>chylomicrons</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Intestinal lipid absorption through lymphatics\"><span>Long-chain dietary lipids</span><b>→</b><span>Enterocyte chylomicrons</span><b>→</b><span>Villus lacteal</span><b>→</b><span>Mesenteric lymph</span><b>→</b><span>Thoracic duct → Blood</span></div>\n\n<p>Why take the lymphatic route? Chylomicrons are relatively large lipid-rich particles and preferentially enter the lacteal system rather than directly entering ordinary intestinal blood capillaries.</p>\n<p><strong>Important exception:</strong> Not every absorbed fat follows this path. Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the <strong>portal circulation</strong> directly, while long-chain lipids are especially associated with chylomicron transport.</p>\n<p>The lipid-rich lymph leaving the intestine is called <strong>chyle</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-lymphatic-drainage-protect-tissue-fluid-balance\">6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance?</h2>\n<p>In many tissues there is continuing <strong>net capillary filtration</strong>. The lymphatics collect fluid and proteins that escape into the interstitium and return them to blood.</p>\n<p>If filtration suddenly increases, lymphatic return can often rise to compensate for a while. <strong>Oedema develops when the total demand exceeds the available drainage capacity, or when lymphatic outflow is obstructed.</strong></p>\n<p>This gives two routes to swelling:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Problem</th>\n  <th>What fails?</th>\n  <th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Excess filtration</strong></td>\n  <td>Too much fluid enters tissue for the drainage capacity</td>\n  <td>Venous congestion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Reduced lymphatic clearance</strong></td>\n  <td>Normal or increased filtration cannot be removed</td>\n  <td>Filarial damage or surgical disruption</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The relationship is especially important when interpreting the lecture's classic <strong>24 L/day and 85%/15%</strong> example: those percentages are a historical illustration, whereas modern understanding gives lymphatics a central role in ongoing normal tissue-fluid balance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-lymphoedema\">7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema?</h2>\n<p>Representative two examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filarial worm infection:</strong> Parasites and associated inflammation can damage lymphatic vessels, producing chronic swelling and sometimes elephantiasis.</li>\n<li><strong>Radical mastectomy/axillary lymph-node surgery:</strong> Removing or damaging lymphatic channels may impair drainage from the affected upper limb or chest wall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other conditions, including malignancy and congenital lymphatic abnormalities, can also interfere with lymph flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Impaired drainage → interstitial fluid and protein accumulation → persistent swelling → chronic inflammatory changes and fibrosis if longstanding.</p>\n<h3>Is lymphoedema always non-pitting?</h3>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> The lecture says “not pitting,” describing a common advanced appearance. Early lymphoedema can be <strong>soft and pitting</strong>, because fluid is still mobile. With chronicity, tissue thickening and fibrosis can produce <strong>firm, less-pitting or non-pitting</strong> swelling.</p>\n<p>Pitting alone does not distinguish all lymphatic from all venous causes. The duration, distribution and clinical context matter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-what-happens-if-lymphatic-function-is-lost\">8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost?</h2>\n<p>Three broad consequences follow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluid balance:</strong> Less interstitial fluid and protein are returned, causing swelling.</li>\n<li><strong>Immune function:</strong> Lymph transport through nodes and immune surveillance are disrupted.</li>\n<li><strong>Nutrient transport:</strong> Damaged intestinal lacteals can impair delivery of long-chain dietary lipids via chylomicrons.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Key structures include lymphatics as helping bodily fluid balance and intestinal absorption. The more precise mechanism is <strong>return of interstitial fluid and transport of fat-rich chylomicrons</strong>, rather than lymphatic vessels directly performing the kidney's renal water reabsorption.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why can a person develop oedema even if their blood vessels are structurally intact?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Some fluid is normally filtered from capillaries into tissue. If the lymphatic drainage pathway is obstructed or damaged, the fluid and escaped proteins cannot return adequately to venous blood. They accumulate as <strong>oedema</strong> even when the blood-vessel walls are not newly damaged.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. How do initial lymphatic capillaries admit interstitial fluid without simply leaking it back out?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Initial lymphatics have overlapping endothelial flaps that can open when interstitial pressure favours entry. Their specialised arrangement helps restrict reverse leakage when internal pressure rises. Collecting lymphatics then have additional valves favouring one-way flow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. Why can lymph move upwards from the legs without a dedicated central lymph pump?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>One-way valves combine with <strong>intrinsic lymphatic smooth-muscle contractions</strong> and external skeletal-muscle and respiratory movements. Each compression episode helps drive lymph onward while valves limit backflow. A separate lymphatic heart is unnecessary.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Name the usual territories drained by the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The <strong>thoracic duct</strong> drains most of the body, including the lower limbs, abdomen and left upper regions. The <strong>right lymphatic duct</strong> commonly drains the right upper quadrant: right head/neck, upper limb and thorax. Both usually empty near their corresponding internal jugular–subclavian venous angles.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. Why are lymph nodes important even though they do not serve as the main lymphatic pumps?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Lymph nodes expose material transported in lymph to immune cells and help organise immune surveillance. Fluid movement mainly comes from pressure gradients, intrinsic lymphatic contractions and external pumps rather than active pumping by the nodes themselves.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>6. How are most long-chain dietary lipids delivered to systemic blood after absorption?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Enterocytes absorb digestion products, resynthesise triglycerides and package many long-chain lipids into <strong>chylomicrons</strong>. These enter villous <strong>lacteals</strong>, travel through intestinal lymphatic vessels and the thoracic duct, and ultimately enter systemic venous blood.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>7. Must all absorbed fatty acids pass through the lymphatic lacteals?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the portal circulation directly. The lymphatic chylomicron route is especially important for long-chain dietary lipids and other fat-soluble materials packaged into these particles.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>8. How is chyle different from ordinary tissue lymph?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Chyle</strong> is intestinal lymph enriched in dietary lipid-rich chylomicrons after a meal. Lymph from other tissues varies in composition but usually does not contain the same high concentration of absorbed intestinal chylomicrons.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>9. How can filarial infection cause the severe chronic swelling associated with elephantiasis?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Filarial infection and the associated inflammatory response can damage or obstruct lymphatic pathways. Protein-rich interstitial fluid accumulates and prolonged inflammation stimulates tissue remodelling and fibrosis. Severe chronic disease may produce thickened, enlarged limbs and skin changes.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why might an arm swell after axillary lymph-node dissection?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Dissection can remove or damage lymphatic channels draining that arm. Normal tissue filtration continues but clearance becomes inadequate, causing regional lymphoedema. The swelling is due primarily to <strong>impaired lymph drainage</strong>, not necessarily reduced cardiac output.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why is it incorrect to say that all lymphoedema is non-pitting?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Early lymphoedema may consist largely of mobile interstitial fluid, leaving an indentation after pressure. With chronic disease, inflammation and fibrosis make the tissue firmer and often non-pitting. The lecturer's non-pitting description mainly fits advanced stages.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. What is the important modern correction to the classical claim that only 15% of filtered capillary fluid is removed by lymphatics?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The revised Starling principle recognises the endothelial glycocalyx and persistent modest net filtration in many capillary beds at steady state. <strong>Lymphatic drainage is the principal continuous return route for much of that filtered fluid</strong>; the lecturer's 85%/15% calculation is historical illustrative teaching, not a universal proportion.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lymphatics:</strong> Return interstitial fluid and proteins to venous circulation; also transport immune cells/material.</li>\n<li><strong>Initial capillaries:</strong> Blind-ended and thin-walled, with specialised endothelial flaps admitting tissue fluid.</li>\n<li><strong>Collecting vessels:</strong> Valves and contractile smooth-muscle segments favour one-way lymph flow.</li>\n<li><strong>External pumps:</strong> Skeletal-muscle movement and respiration assist lymph propulsion.</li>\n<li><strong>Thoracic duct:</strong> Usually drains most of the body into the left venous angle.</li>\n<li><strong>Right lymphatic duct:</strong> Usually drains the right upper quadrant into the right venous angle.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph nodes:</strong> Sites of filtration and immune surveillance, not the main lymph-propelling pumps.</li>\n<li><strong>Lacteals:</strong> Intestinal lymphatics receiving chylomicrons formed from long-chain dietary lipids.</li>\n<li><strong>Chyle:</strong> Lipid-enriched intestinal lymph.</li>\n<li><strong>Steady-state fluid balance:</strong> Lymphatics continually return filtered fluid and proteins; excess filtration or reduced drainage causes oedema.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphoedema causes:</strong> Filarial injury, lymphatic surgery, malignancy and developmental disorders.</li>\n<li><strong>Pitting:</strong> Early lymphoedema may pit; chronic fibrotic lymphoedema may become non-pitting.</li>\n<li><strong>Transport consequences:</strong> Lymph dysfunction affects tissue fluid balance, immune surveillance and intestinal dietary-lipid transport.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Lymphatic Circulation 1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins? Blood capillaries continuously exchange substances and filter some water into surrounding tissues. If every bit of fluid and escaped protein stayed there, tissues would swell progressively. The lymphatic system collects excess interstitial fluid, proteins and selected particles and returns them to the venous circulation. Lymph is fluid inside lymphatic vessels. It develops from interstitial fluid that has entered lymphatic capillaries; its composition varies with the tissue drained. Capillary filtration → Interstitial fluid → Lymph capillaries → Lymph vessels and ducts → Venous blood A key principle is that lymphatic drainage handles fluid left after microvascular exchange. Under the revised Starling principle, lymphatic return is a major normal steady-state route for filtered fluid, not merely a back-up for a universal 85% venous-end reabsorption process. 2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary? Initial lymphatic capillaries begin as blind-ended, thin-walled vessels in tissues. Their endothelial cells overlap and are connected with specialised anchoring structures to surrounding tissue. When local interstitial pressure exceeds pressure inside the initial lymphatic, overlapping endothelial margins can separate to admit fluid, proteins and some particles. The flap-like arrangement restricts reverse leakage when pressure changes. Figure 01. From capillary filtration to a one-way lymphatic entry route Blood capillary Filtered fluid ↓ Interstitial space Initial lymphatic capillary → To larger lymphatics Original conceptual schematic based on the lecturer's lymphatic motion/pressure-gradient diagram. It illustrates fluid entry and one-way transport, not exact vessel anatomy. Why is this structure useful? It can admit material that cannot simply diffuse rapidly back into a blood capillary, including interstitial proteins. 3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart? Lymph flows along a low-pressure system. Several mechanisms promote its movement: One-way valves: Limit backward flow when external compression changes. Contracting collecting lymphatics: Smooth-muscle segments rhythmically compress lymph. Skeletal-muscle movement: Surrounding muscle compression moves lymph along valved channels. Respiratory pump: Changing thoracic and abdominal pressures help direct flow. Adjacent tissue/arterial movement: Can also contribute to mechanical propulsion. The lecturer lists all these components, including pumping by the lymphatic walls and the pressure relationships where the major ducts empty into veins. A useful comparison: Venous return and lymphatic flow both rely partly on valves and muscle/respiratory pumping, but lymphatics begin blindly in the interstitium rather than as continuations of blood capillaries within a closed blood vessel circuit. 4. Where does lymph finally return to blood? The lymphatic system has major collecting pathways: Pathway Main drainage territory Typical return location Thoracic duct Most of body, including both lower limbs, abdomen and left upper body Left venous angle near left internal jugular–subclavian junction Right lymphatic duct Right side of head/neck, right upper limb, right part of thorax Right venous angle near right internal jugular–subclavian junction These are standard drainage territories; normal anatomical variants occur. The mechanism involves lymph entering the right and left subclavian venous regions. More precise anatomy places the usual openings at or near the venous angles. What role do lymph nodes play? Lymph travelling through regional collecting vessels often passes through lymph nodes. These structures expose lymph-borne materials to immune cells and contribute to immune surveillance. Lymph nodes are not the structures that create the main pumping force. Fluid movement primarily depends on lymphatic vessel contractions, pressure gradients, movement and valves. 5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption? The lecturer names fatty acids and cholesterol absorption as lymphatic functions. A major intestinal lymphatic capillary inside each villus is a lacteal. After digestion, many long-chain dietary fatty acids and monoacylglycerols are taken up by enterocytes, reassembled into triglycerides and packaged, together with cholesterol and other lipids, into chylomicrons. Long-chain dietary lipids → Enterocyte chylomicrons → Villus lacteal → Mesenteric lymph → Thoracic duct → Blood Why take the lymphatic route? Chylomicrons are relatively large lipid-rich particles and preferentially enter the lacteal system rather than directly entering ordinary intestinal blood capillaries. Important exception: Not every absorbed fat follows this path. Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the portal circulation directly, while long-chain lipids are especially associated with chylomicron transport. The lipid-rich lymph leaving the intestine is called chyle. 6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance? In many tissues there is continuing net capillary filtration. The lymphatics collect fluid and proteins that escape into the interstitium and return them to blood. If filtration suddenly increases, lymphatic return can often rise to compensate for a while. Oedema develops when the total demand exceeds the available drainage capacity, or when lymphatic outflow is obstructed. This gives two routes to swelling: Problem What fails? Example Excess filtration Too much fluid enters tissue for the drainage capacity Venous congestion Reduced lymphatic clearance Normal or increased filtration cannot be removed Filarial damage or surgical disruption The relationship is especially important when interpreting the lecture's classic 24 L/day and 85%/15% example: those percentages are a historical illustration, whereas modern understanding gives lymphatics a central role in ongoing normal tissue-fluid balance. 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema? Representative two examples: Filarial worm infection: Parasites and associated inflammation can damage lymphatic vessels, producing chronic swelling and sometimes elephantiasis. Radical mastectomy/axillary lymph-node surgery: Removing or damaging lymphatic channels may impair drainage from the affected upper limb or chest wall. Other conditions, including malignancy and congenital lymphatic abnormalities, can also interfere with lymph flow. Mechanism: Impaired drainage → interstitial fluid and protein accumulation → persistent swelling → chronic inflammatory changes and fibrosis if longstanding. Is lymphoedema always non-pitting? No. The lecture says “not pitting,” describing a common advanced appearance. Early lymphoedema can be soft and pitting, because fluid is still mobile. With chronicity, tissue thickening and fibrosis can produce firm, less-pitting or non-pitting swelling. Pitting alone does not distinguish all lymphatic from all venous causes. The duration, distribution and clinical context matter. 8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost? Three broad consequences follow: Fluid balance: Less interstitial fluid and protein are returned, causing swelling. Immune function: Lymph transport through nodes and immune surveillance are disrupted. Nutrient transport: Damaged intestinal lacteals can impair delivery of long-chain dietary lipids via chylomicrons. Key structures include lymphatics as helping bodily fluid balance and intestinal absorption. The more precise mechanism is return of interstitial fluid and transport of fat-rich chylomicrons, rather than lymphatic vessels directly performing the kidney's renal water reabsorption. Understanding Check 1. Why can a person develop oedema even if their blood vessels are structurally intact? Reveal explanation Some fluid is normally filtered from capillaries into tissue. If the lymphatic drainage pathway is obstructed or damaged, the fluid and escaped proteins cannot return adequately to venous blood. They accumulate as oedema even when the blood-vessel walls are not newly damaged. 2. How do initial lymphatic capillaries admit interstitial fluid without simply leaking it back out? Reveal explanation Initial lymphatics have overlapping endothelial flaps that can open when interstitial pressure favours entry. Their specialised arrangement helps restrict reverse leakage when internal pressure rises. Collecting lymphatics then have additional valves favouring one-way flow. 3. Why can lymph move upwards from the legs without a dedicated central lymph pump? Reveal explanation One-way valves combine with intrinsic lymphatic smooth-muscle contractions and external skeletal-muscle and respiratory movements. Each compression episode helps drive lymph onward while valves limit backflow. A separate lymphatic heart is unnecessary. 4. Name the usual territories drained by the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct. Reveal explanation The thoracic duct drains most of the body, including the lower limbs, abdomen and left upper regions. The right lymphatic duct commonly drains the right upper quadrant: right head/neck, upper limb and thorax. Both usually empty near their corresponding internal jugular–subclavian venous angles. 5. Why are lymph nodes important even though they do not serve as the main lymphatic pumps? Reveal explanation Lymph nodes expose material transported in lymph to immune cells and help organise immune surveillance. Fluid movement mainly comes from pressure gradients, intrinsic lymphatic contractions and external pumps rather than active pumping by the nodes themselves. 6. How are most long-chain dietary lipids delivered to systemic blood after absorption? Reveal explanation Enterocytes absorb digestion products, resynthesise triglycerides and package many long-chain lipids into chylomicrons. These enter villous lacteals, travel through intestinal lymphatic vessels and the thoracic duct, and ultimately enter systemic venous blood. 7. Must all absorbed fatty acids pass through the lymphatic lacteals? Reveal explanation No. Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the portal circulation directly. The lymphatic chylomicron route is especially important for long-chain dietary lipids and other fat-soluble materials packaged into these particles. 8. How is chyle different from ordinary tissue lymph? Reveal explanation Chyle is intestinal lymph enriched in dietary lipid-rich chylomicrons after a meal. Lymph from other tissues varies in composition but usually does not contain the same high concentration of absorbed intestinal chylomicrons. 9. How can filarial infection cause the severe chronic swelling associated with elephantiasis? Reveal explanation Filarial infection and the associated inflammatory response can damage or obstruct lymphatic pathways. Protein-rich interstitial fluid accumulates and prolonged inflammation stimulates tissue remodelling and fibrosis. Severe chronic disease may produce thickened, enlarged limbs and skin changes. 10. Why might an arm swell after axillary lymph-node dissection? Reveal explanation Dissection can remove or damage lymphatic channels draining that arm. Normal tissue filtration continues but clearance becomes inadequate, causing regional lymphoedema. The swelling is due primarily to impaired lymph drainage, not necessarily reduced cardiac output. 11. Why is it incorrect to say that all lymphoedema is non-pitting? Reveal explanation Early lymphoedema may consist largely of mobile interstitial fluid, leaving an indentation after pressure. With chronic disease, inflammation and fibrosis make the tissue firmer and often non-pitting. The lecturer's non-pitting description mainly fits advanced stages. 12. What is the important modern correction to the classical claim that only 15% of filtered capillary fluid is removed by lymphatics? Reveal explanation The revised Starling principle recognises the endothelial glycocalyx and persistent modest net filtration in many capillary beds at steady state. Lymphatic drainage is the principal continuous return route for much of that filtered fluid; the lecturer's 85%/15% calculation is historical illustrative teaching, not a universal proportion. Chapter Essentials Lymphatics: Return interstitial fluid and proteins to venous circulation; also transport immune cells/material. Initial capillaries: Blind-ended and thin-walled, with specialised endothelial flaps admitting tissue fluid. Collecting vessels: Valves and contractile smooth-muscle segments favour one-way lymph flow. External pumps: Skeletal-muscle movement and respiration assist lymph propulsion. Thoracic duct: Usually drains most of the body into the left venous angle. Right lymphatic duct: Usually drains the right upper quadrant into the right venous angle. Lymph nodes: Sites of filtration and immune surveillance, not the main lymph-propelling pumps. Lacteals: Intestinal lymphatics receiving chylomicrons formed from long-chain dietary lipids. Chyle: Lipid-enriched intestinal lymph. Steady-state fluid balance: Lymphatics continually return filtered fluid and proteins; excess filtration or reduced drainage causes oedema. Lymphoedema causes: Filarial injury, lymphatic surgery, malignancy and developmental disorders. Pitting: Early lymphoedema may pit; chronic fibrotic lymphoedema may become non-pitting. Transport consequences: Lymph dysfunction affects tissue fluid balance, immune surveillance and intestinal dietary-lipid transport."},{"slug":"local-regulation","title":"Local Control of Blood Flow","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"1-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-local-control","title":"1. Why does a tissue need local control?"},{"id":"2-autoregulation-keeping-blood-flow-relatively-steady","title":"2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady"},{"id":"3-two-complementary-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","title":"3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic"},{"id":"4-active-versus-reactive-hyperaemia","title":"4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia"},{"id":"5-what-does-the-endothelium-contribute","title":"5. What does the endothelium contribute?"},{"id":"6-laplace-s-principle-pressure-radius-tension-and-stress","title":"6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress"},{"id":"7-bringing-local-and-whole-body-control-together","title":"7. Bringing local and whole-body control together"},{"id":"more-connections-worth-understanding","title":"More connections worth understanding"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"references-and-scope-notes","title":"References and scope notes"}],"html":"<h1>Local Control of Blood Flow</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-local-control\">1. Why does a tissue need local control?</h2>\n<p>The heart generates flow, but every organ must receive a supply matched to its moment-to-moment needs. During exercise, contracting muscles require more oxygen and nutrient delivery; meanwhile the brain needs fairly stable perfusion despite ordinary blood-pressure fluctuations. <strong>Local blood-flow control</strong> means a vascular bed adjusts the calibre of its own resistance vessels, especially small arteries and arterioles.</p>\n<p>The starting relationship is <strong>Q = ΔP / R</strong>, where Q is volume flow (for example mL/min), ΔP is the pressure difference across a vascular bed (mmHg), and R is vascular resistance (mmHg·min/mL). If pressure rises at unchanged resistance, flow rises. If local arterioles constrict enough to raise resistance, that rise in flow can be opposed.</p>\n<p>In an ideal rigid tube with laminar flow, <strong>R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)</strong> (Poiseuille relationship). η is dynamic viscosity, L is length and r is radius. Living vessels are not rigid tubes, but the fourth-power dependence explains why even a modest arteriolar radius change can strongly affect resistance. Halving radius would increase idealized resistance <strong>16-fold</strong> when the other variables are unchanged; do not treat this as an exact prediction for an intact circulation.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Local flow adjustment\"><span>Tissue activity or perfusion pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Local sensing</span><b>→</b><span>Arteriolar tone changes</span><b>→</b><span>Resistance and flow adjust</span></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-autoregulation-keeping-blood-flow-relatively-steady\">2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady</h2>\n<p><strong>Autoregulation</strong> is the ability of an organ to maintain relatively constant blood flow across a <em>range</em> of perfusion pressures by changing local vascular resistance. It does not mean perfect flow constancy, and it fails outside the organ's effective autoregulatory range. Cerebral and renal circulations are especially important examples; coronary regulation also strongly matches flow to metabolic demand.</p>\n<p>If perfusion pressure suddenly rises, flow initially tends to increase. Local arterioles then <strong>constrict</strong>, increasing resistance to counter the rise. If pressure falls moderately, they tend to <strong>dilate</strong> and lower resistance to defend flow. This is a local response and need not wait for a change in the heart's output.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Disturbance</th>\n  <th>Initial passive tendency</th>\n  <th>Compensatory change</th>\n  <th>Intended result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Increased perfusion pressure</td>\n  <td>Flow rises</td>\n  <td>Arteriolar constriction increases R</td>\n  <td>Flow moves back toward baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Decreased perfusion pressure</td>\n  <td>Flow falls</td>\n  <td>Arteriolar dilation decreases R</td>\n  <td>Flow moves back toward baseline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> If pressure becomes too low for further dilation, blood flow falls despite maximal compensation. Thus autoregulation is not a guarantee against shock or severe hypotension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-two-complementary-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic\">3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic</h2>\n<h3>Myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch</h3>\n<p>An arteriole experiences <strong>transmural pressure = intravascular pressure − surrounding tissue pressure</strong>. Higher transmural pressure distends its wall, activating vascular smooth-muscle signalling that increases contraction. Constriction reduces radius and raises resistance. Lower pressure reduces wall stretch, permitting relaxation and dilation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this useful?</strong> Without the active response, increased pressure would push extra blood through vulnerable small vessels. Myogenic autoregulation helps moderate flow variation and microvascular pressure, notably in cerebral and renal beds.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 140\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Higher transmural pressure stretches an arteriole, triggering smooth-muscle contraction, raising resistance and opposing a large rise in flow\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e9f3fd\" stroke=\"#4d84aa\"/><rect x=\"224\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e4f4ea\" stroke=\"#4e9b74\"/><rect x=\"443\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2d6\" stroke=\"#b88933\"/><g fill=\"#20324b\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"91\" y=\"57\">Pressure ↑</text><text x=\"91\" y=\"77\">Wall stretch ↑</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"57\">Smooth muscle</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"77\">contracts</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"57\">Radius ↓; R ↑</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"77\">Flow rise opposed</text></g><path d=\"M181 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7M400 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7\" stroke=\"#527895\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></svg><p>Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>Metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs</h3>\n<p>When metabolism increases or perfusion is inadequate, local vasodilator influences rise. Depending on the tissue, these may include <strong>adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, extracellular K⁺ and reduced tissue oxygen tension</strong>. The relative importance of each signal differs among organs. Dilation lowers local resistance and improves delivery; increasing flow can also wash out accumulated signals.</p>\n<p>Example: A vigorously contracting skeletal muscle consumes oxygen faster and produces metabolites. Its small resistance vessels dilate; muscle flow increases <strong>even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Distinguish the triggers:</strong> Myogenic control principally senses <strong>vessel stretch/pressure</strong>, whereas metabolic control senses <strong>local supply relative to demand</strong>. Both can act simultaneously; this is not an either/or choice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-active-versus-reactive-hyperaemia\">4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia</h2>\n<p><strong>Hyperaemia</strong> means increased blood flow.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Active hyperaemia</th>\n  <th>Reactive hyperaemia</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Occurs <strong>during increased tissue activity</strong></td>\n  <td>Occurs <strong>after a period of reduced or interrupted flow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Example: working skeletal muscle</td>\n  <td>Example: increased hand flow after releasing a temporary occlusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Increased metabolism promotes dilation</td>\n  <td>Ischaemia-associated metabolites, myogenic changes and other signals contribute; release restores driving pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Flow rises to meet ongoing demand</td>\n  <td>A temporary overshoot helps repay the supply deficit and dissipate vasodilator influences</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A useful sequence for <strong>reactive hyperaemia</strong> is: brief arterial occlusion → oxygen delivery falls and vasodilator influences develop → obstruction is released → previously dilated vascular bed receives a transient flow surge → flow gradually returns toward baseline. The magnitude and duration depend on the degree and length of occlusion and on the tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse these with autoregulation.</strong> Autoregulation describes relative <strong>stability</strong> of flow when perfusion pressure changes; hyperaemia describes an <strong>increase</strong> in flow in response to activity or prior underperfusion. Some underlying mechanisms overlap.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-does-the-endothelium-contribute\">5. What does the endothelium contribute?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>endothelium</strong> is more than a passive lining. It senses shear stress and chemical signals and releases substances that modify underlying smooth-muscle tone.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nitric oxide (NO):</strong> endothelial NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates <strong>soluble guanylyl cyclase</strong>, raises <strong>cGMP</strong>, and promotes relaxation. Shear stress from flowing blood can stimulate endothelial NO production.</li>\n<li><strong>Endothelin-1:</strong> a powerful vasoconstrictor peptide acting principally via smooth-muscle ETᴀ receptors; endothelial ETʙ signalling can have different effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Prostacyclin (PGI₂):</strong> an endothelial vasodilator that also inhibits platelet aggregation.</li>\n<li><strong>Histamine and bradykinin:</strong> can promote endothelial-dependent vasodilation, and in suitable vascular beds increase permeability; their effects vary with receptor and tissue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Application:</strong> Endothelial dysfunction can reduce NO bioavailability, contributing to impaired vasodilation. It should not be equated with the complete disappearance of myogenic or metabolic control.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-laplace-s-principle-pressure-radius-tension-and-stress\">6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress</h2>\n<p>One useful relationship is Laplace's law near myogenic regulation. It is important to specify <strong>which geometry</strong> and <strong>which quantity</strong> a formula describes.</p>\n<p>For an ideal <strong>thin-walled cylindrical vessel</strong>, circumferential <em>wall tension per unit axial length</em> is approximately <strong>T = Ptm × r</strong>, where Ptm is transmural pressure and r is internal radius. For average circumferential <strong>wall stress</strong>, <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r / h</strong>, with wall thickness h. For an ideal thin-walled <em>spherical</em> membrane, <strong>T = Ptm × r / 2</strong>. These idealizations are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p>At a fixed pressure and thickness, larger vessel radius implies greater average circumferential stress. However, <strong>Laplace's relationship does not by itself explain myogenic signalling</strong>: living vascular smooth muscle detects deformation and activates ion-channel and contractile pathways. Real arteries have finite wall thickness and active, nonlinear walls.</p>\n<p><strong>Numerical reasoning:</strong> Two thin-walled cylindrical vessels have equal Ptm and h. If one has twice the radius, its estimated wall tension and stress are each twice as large. It would be incorrect to say resistance also doubles: resistance responds oppositely and much more strongly to radius in the simplified Poiseuille model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-bringing-local-and-whole-body-control-together\">7. Bringing local and whole-body control together</h2>\n<p>Local regulation operates alongside the autonomic nervous system and circulating hormones. In exercise, <strong>active skeletal muscle</strong> can show powerful metabolic vasodilation even while sympathetic activity increases elsewhere. This is one reason blood supply can be redistributed to working tissue rather than rising equally in every organ.</p>\n<p>A fall in systemic pressure can trigger <strong>baroreflex sympathetic vasoconstriction</strong>, while critically underperfused tissues simultaneously accumulate local dilator signals. Which effect dominates depends on organ, severity, receptors, and physiological state. The next chapters examine the systemic side of this interaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical application:</strong> If systemic shock drives perfusion pressure below the lower autoregulatory limit, local dilation cannot maintain normal cerebral or renal flow. This connects local autoregulation directly to shock and organ dysfunction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"more-connections-worth-understanding\">More connections worth understanding</h2>\n<p>The lecture lists rising temperature, low pH, increased carbon dioxide, potassium, lactate, cardiac ADP, bradykinin, substance P, histamine H2 signalling and ANP among local vasodilator-associated influences. Effects vary by tissue and receptor. It lists serotonin, cooling and angiotensin among constrictor influences. The two-curvature Laplace form P = T(1/R1 + 1/R2) is not mathematically equivalent to the slide's later expression P = T/(R1 + R2); that second expression must not be taught as a rearrangement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01.</strong> Perfusion pressure through a vascular bed doubles while its resistance remains unchanged. What happens to flow initially, and what local adjustment can defend its baseline?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>From Q = ΔP/R, flow initially doubles if R is fixed. Autoregulation can increase arteriolar resistance through constriction, opposing the rise. A perfect twofold adjustment is an idealization; real responses vary by tissue and pressure range.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02.</strong> Why are arterioles more effective local flow regulators than large veins?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Arterioles contain actively controlled vascular smooth muscle and supply a large share of systemic vascular resistance. Small radius changes alter resistance substantially. Veins primarily serve as capacitance vessels and modulate venous return rather than controlling each tissue's entry resistance.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03.</strong> When a renal resistance arteriole experiences increased transmural pressure, why may its smooth muscle contract rather than simply remain stretched?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Stretch activates a myogenic smooth-muscle response that increases tone. A smaller radius raises resistance and helps counter an otherwise excessive pressure-driven rise in flow. The response is active, not merely elastic recoil.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04.</strong> A student says myogenic and metabolic autoregulation are the same because both change arteriolar radius. What is missing?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The initiating signals differ. Myogenic regulation responds chiefly to pressure-driven vessel stretch. Metabolic regulation responds to a mismatch between local perfusion and metabolic demand, via signals such as adenosine, H⁺ and K⁺. They can cooperate in the same vascular bed.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05.</strong> During a sprint, muscle perfusion increases even though arterial pressure barely changes. Explain the local mechanism.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Working muscle increases its metabolic demand. Local vasodilator signals lower arteriolar resistance; therefore flow can rise at nearly constant driving pressure according to Q = ΔP/R. This is active hyperaemia.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06.</strong> Blood flow surges briefly after a pressure cuff is released. Is this active hyperaemia, reactive hyperaemia or autoregulation?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reactive hyperaemia. Temporary occlusion reduced oxygen supply and fostered vasodilator influences; reopening permits a transient overshoot before signals dissipate. The defining feature is increased flow after preceding underperfusion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07.</strong> Does adenosine have identical importance in every vascular bed? Explain.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Local metabolic mediators differ by organ and context. Adenosine is influential in some settings, especially coronary metabolic regulation, while combinations of oxygen, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, endothelial and myogenic influences contribute elsewhere. Avoid memorising a universal single-metabolite explanation.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08.</strong> Trace the intracellular pathway by which endothelial NO relaxes adjacent vascular smooth muscle.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Endothelial NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and promoting smooth-muscle relaxation. This increases vessel radius, decreases resistance and can increase flow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09.</strong> Why can endothelin-1 and nitric oxide have opposite effects on local flow?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>They affect contractile tone differently: endothelin-1 typically stimulates smooth-muscle contraction, whereas NO promotes cGMP-dependent relaxation. The final response depends on their relative signalling and the receptors of the vascular bed.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10.</strong> Two ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessels face equal transmural pressure. One has double the radius and the same wall thickness. Compare estimated wall stress.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Average circumferential stress is approximately σθ = Ptm × r/h. With equal pressure and thickness, doubling radius doubles estimated stress. This is distinct from Poiseuille resistance, which decreases sharply as radius increases in the ideal rigid-tube model.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11.</strong> Why can autoregulation not fully protect the brain during profound haemorrhagic shock?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Autoregulation is effective only over a bounded pressure range. Below its lower limit, resistance vessels cannot dilate sufficiently to preserve flow; critically reduced perfusion pressure then leads to inadequate cerebral blood delivery despite local compensation.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12.</strong> A patient exercising has sympathetic activation, yet blood flow rises in active muscle. Is there a contradiction?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Sympathetic vasoconstrictor influence and local metabolic vasodilation act together. In active muscle, local demand-related dilation can strongly attenuate sympathetic constriction, so that perfusion increases while flow in less active beds may be restricted.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Tissue flow depends on pressure difference and vascular resistance: <strong>Q = ΔP/R</strong>.</li>\n<li>Arteriolar calibre is a major adjustable determinant of local resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> defends relatively stable flow only within a limited pressure range.</li>\n<li>Increased vessel stretch often produces <strong>myogenic constriction</strong>.</li>\n<li>A tissue with increased metabolic demand tends to develop <strong>metabolic vasodilation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Active hyperaemia</strong> accompanies increased tissue activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong> follows temporary underperfusion.</li>\n<li>Endothelial <strong>NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → cGMP</strong> promotes smooth-muscle relaxation.</li>\n<li><strong>Endothelin-1</strong> commonly promotes vasoconstriction; endothelial responses are context-dependent.</li>\n<li>Cylindrical wall tension <strong>T ≈ Ptm × r</strong> and wall stress <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r/h</strong> require their assumptions.</li>\n<li>Local and systemic controls may compete or cooperate in one vascular bed.</li>\n<li>Severe shock can push perfusion below the effective autoregulatory range.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"references-and-scope-notes\">References and scope notes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lecturer scope:</strong> CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, pp. 1–11 (local regulation, myogenic/metabolic theories, Laplace and mediators). Slide numbering/source mapping should be rechecked against the exact uploaded lecture PDF before merging.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference physiology:</strong> Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>, chapters on local and humoral control of tissue blood flow; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>, vascular smooth-muscle and regional-flow control.</li>\n<li><strong>Supplementary precision:</strong> Autoregulatory ranges are tissue-specific; mediator examples are not universal; Laplace equations refer to ideal geometries; Poiseuille's law is an explanatory approximation in biological vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Local Control of Blood Flow 1. Why does a tissue need local control? The heart generates flow, but every organ must receive a supply matched to its moment-to-moment needs. During exercise, contracting muscles require more oxygen and nutrient delivery; meanwhile the brain needs fairly stable perfusion despite ordinary blood-pressure fluctuations. Local blood-flow control means a vascular bed adjusts the calibre of its own resistance vessels, especially small arteries and arterioles. The starting relationship is Q = ΔP / R, where Q is volume flow (for example mL/min), ΔP is the pressure difference across a vascular bed (mmHg), and R is vascular resistance (mmHg·min/mL). If pressure rises at unchanged resistance, flow rises. If local arterioles constrict enough to raise resistance, that rise in flow can be opposed. In an ideal rigid tube with laminar flow, R = 8ηL/(πr⁴) (Poiseuille relationship). η is dynamic viscosity, L is length and r is radius. Living vessels are not rigid tubes, but the fourth-power dependence explains why even a modest arteriolar radius change can strongly affect resistance. Halving radius would increase idealized resistance 16-fold when the other variables are unchanged; do not treat this as an exact prediction for an intact circulation. Tissue activity or perfusion pressure changes → Local sensing → Arteriolar tone changes → Resistance and flow adjust 2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady Autoregulation is the ability of an organ to maintain relatively constant blood flow across a range of perfusion pressures by changing local vascular resistance. It does not mean perfect flow constancy, and it fails outside the organ's effective autoregulatory range. Cerebral and renal circulations are especially important examples; coronary regulation also strongly matches flow to metabolic demand. If perfusion pressure suddenly rises, flow initially tends to increase. Local arterioles then constrict, increasing resistance to counter the rise. If pressure falls moderately, they tend to dilate and lower resistance to defend flow. This is a local response and need not wait for a change in the heart's output. Disturbance Initial passive tendency Compensatory change Intended result Increased perfusion pressure Flow rises Arteriolar constriction increases R Flow moves back toward baseline Decreased perfusion pressure Flow falls Arteriolar dilation decreases R Flow moves back toward baseline Limitation: If pressure becomes too low for further dilation, blood flow falls despite maximal compensation. Thus autoregulation is not a guarantee against shock or severe hypotension. 3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic Myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch An arteriole experiences transmural pressure = intravascular pressure − surrounding tissue pressure. Higher transmural pressure distends its wall, activating vascular smooth-muscle signalling that increases contraction. Constriction reduces radius and raises resistance. Lower pressure reduces wall stretch, permitting relaxation and dilation. Why is this useful? Without the active response, increased pressure would push extra blood through vulnerable small vessels. Myogenic autoregulation helps moderate flow variation and microvascular pressure, notably in cerebral and renal beds. Figure 01. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure Pressure ↑ Wall stretch ↑ Smooth muscle contracts Radius ↓; R ↑ Flow rise opposed Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response. Metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs When metabolism increases or perfusion is inadequate, local vasodilator influences rise. Depending on the tissue, these may include adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, extracellular K⁺ and reduced tissue oxygen tension. The relative importance of each signal differs among organs. Dilation lowers local resistance and improves delivery; increasing flow can also wash out accumulated signals. Example: A vigorously contracting skeletal muscle consumes oxygen faster and produces metabolites. Its small resistance vessels dilate; muscle flow increases even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen. Distinguish the triggers: Myogenic control principally senses vessel stretch/pressure, whereas metabolic control senses local supply relative to demand. Both can act simultaneously; this is not an either/or choice. 4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia Hyperaemia means increased blood flow. Active hyperaemia Reactive hyperaemia Occurs during increased tissue activity Occurs after a period of reduced or interrupted flow Example: working skeletal muscle Example: increased hand flow after releasing a temporary occlusion Increased metabolism promotes dilation Ischaemia-associated metabolites, myogenic changes and other signals contribute; release restores driving pressure Flow rises to meet ongoing demand A temporary overshoot helps repay the supply deficit and dissipate vasodilator influences A useful sequence for reactive hyperaemia is: brief arterial occlusion → oxygen delivery falls and vasodilator influences develop → obstruction is released → previously dilated vascular bed receives a transient flow surge → flow gradually returns toward baseline. The magnitude and duration depend on the degree and length of occlusion and on the tissue. Do not confuse these with autoregulation. Autoregulation describes relative stability of flow when perfusion pressure changes; hyperaemia describes an increase in flow in response to activity or prior underperfusion. Some underlying mechanisms overlap. 5. What does the endothelium contribute? The endothelium is more than a passive lining. It senses shear stress and chemical signals and releases substances that modify underlying smooth-muscle tone. Nitric oxide (NO): endothelial NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raises cGMP, and promotes relaxation. Shear stress from flowing blood can stimulate endothelial NO production. Endothelin-1: a powerful vasoconstrictor peptide acting principally via smooth-muscle ETᴀ receptors; endothelial ETʙ signalling can have different effects. Prostacyclin (PGI₂): an endothelial vasodilator that also inhibits platelet aggregation. Histamine and bradykinin: can promote endothelial-dependent vasodilation, and in suitable vascular beds increase permeability; their effects vary with receptor and tissue. Application: Endothelial dysfunction can reduce NO bioavailability, contributing to impaired vasodilation. It should not be equated with the complete disappearance of myogenic or metabolic control. 6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress One useful relationship is Laplace's law near myogenic regulation. It is important to specify which geometry and which quantity a formula describes. For an ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessel, circumferential wall tension per unit axial length is approximately T = Ptm × r, where Ptm is transmural pressure and r is internal radius. For average circumferential wall stress, σθ ≈ Ptm × r / h, with wall thickness h. For an ideal thin-walled spherical membrane, T = Ptm × r / 2. These idealizations are not interchangeable. At a fixed pressure and thickness, larger vessel radius implies greater average circumferential stress. However, Laplace's relationship does not by itself explain myogenic signalling: living vascular smooth muscle detects deformation and activates ion-channel and contractile pathways. Real arteries have finite wall thickness and active, nonlinear walls. Numerical reasoning: Two thin-walled cylindrical vessels have equal Ptm and h. If one has twice the radius, its estimated wall tension and stress are each twice as large. It would be incorrect to say resistance also doubles: resistance responds oppositely and much more strongly to radius in the simplified Poiseuille model. 7. Bringing local and whole-body control together Local regulation operates alongside the autonomic nervous system and circulating hormones. In exercise, active skeletal muscle can show powerful metabolic vasodilation even while sympathetic activity increases elsewhere. This is one reason blood supply can be redistributed to working tissue rather than rising equally in every organ. A fall in systemic pressure can trigger baroreflex sympathetic vasoconstriction, while critically underperfused tissues simultaneously accumulate local dilator signals. Which effect dominates depends on organ, severity, receptors, and physiological state. The next chapters examine the systemic side of this interaction. Clinical application: If systemic shock drives perfusion pressure below the lower autoregulatory limit, local dilation cannot maintain normal cerebral or renal flow. This connects local autoregulation directly to shock and organ dysfunction. More connections worth understanding The lecture lists rising temperature, low pH, increased carbon dioxide, potassium, lactate, cardiac ADP, bradykinin, substance P, histamine H2 signalling and ANP among local vasodilator-associated influences. Effects vary by tissue and receptor. It lists serotonin, cooling and angiotensin among constrictor influences. The two-curvature Laplace form P = T(1/R1 + 1/R2) is not mathematically equivalent to the slide's later expression P = T/(R1 + R2); that second expression must not be taught as a rearrangement. Understanding Checks 01. Perfusion pressure through a vascular bed doubles while its resistance remains unchanged. What happens to flow initially, and what local adjustment can defend its baseline? Show explanation From Q = ΔP/R, flow initially doubles if R is fixed. Autoregulation can increase arteriolar resistance through constriction, opposing the rise. A perfect twofold adjustment is an idealization; real responses vary by tissue and pressure range. 02. Why are arterioles more effective local flow regulators than large veins? Show explanation Arterioles contain actively controlled vascular smooth muscle and supply a large share of systemic vascular resistance. Small radius changes alter resistance substantially. Veins primarily serve as capacitance vessels and modulate venous return rather than controlling each tissue's entry resistance. 03. When a renal resistance arteriole experiences increased transmural pressure, why may its smooth muscle contract rather than simply remain stretched? Show explanation Stretch activates a myogenic smooth-muscle response that increases tone. A smaller radius raises resistance and helps counter an otherwise excessive pressure-driven rise in flow. The response is active, not merely elastic recoil. 04. A student says myogenic and metabolic autoregulation are the same because both change arteriolar radius. What is missing? Show explanation The initiating signals differ. Myogenic regulation responds chiefly to pressure-driven vessel stretch. Metabolic regulation responds to a mismatch between local perfusion and metabolic demand, via signals such as adenosine, H⁺ and K⁺. They can cooperate in the same vascular bed. 05. During a sprint, muscle perfusion increases even though arterial pressure barely changes. Explain the local mechanism. Show explanation Working muscle increases its metabolic demand. Local vasodilator signals lower arteriolar resistance; therefore flow can rise at nearly constant driving pressure according to Q = ΔP/R. This is active hyperaemia. 06. Blood flow surges briefly after a pressure cuff is released. Is this active hyperaemia, reactive hyperaemia or autoregulation? Show explanation Reactive hyperaemia. Temporary occlusion reduced oxygen supply and fostered vasodilator influences; reopening permits a transient overshoot before signals dissipate. The defining feature is increased flow after preceding underperfusion. 07. Does adenosine have identical importance in every vascular bed? Explain. Show explanation No. Local metabolic mediators differ by organ and context. Adenosine is influential in some settings, especially coronary metabolic regulation, while combinations of oxygen, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, endothelial and myogenic influences contribute elsewhere. Avoid memorising a universal single-metabolite explanation. 08. Trace the intracellular pathway by which endothelial NO relaxes adjacent vascular smooth muscle. Show explanation Endothelial NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and promoting smooth-muscle relaxation. This increases vessel radius, decreases resistance and can increase flow. 09. Why can endothelin-1 and nitric oxide have opposite effects on local flow? Show explanation They affect contractile tone differently: endothelin-1 typically stimulates smooth-muscle contraction, whereas NO promotes cGMP-dependent relaxation. The final response depends on their relative signalling and the receptors of the vascular bed. 10. Two ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessels face equal transmural pressure. One has double the radius and the same wall thickness. Compare estimated wall stress. Show explanation Average circumferential stress is approximately σθ = Ptm × r/h. With equal pressure and thickness, doubling radius doubles estimated stress. This is distinct from Poiseuille resistance, which decreases sharply as radius increases in the ideal rigid-tube model. 11. Why can autoregulation not fully protect the brain during profound haemorrhagic shock? Show explanation Autoregulation is effective only over a bounded pressure range. Below its lower limit, resistance vessels cannot dilate sufficiently to preserve flow; critically reduced perfusion pressure then leads to inadequate cerebral blood delivery despite local compensation. 12. A patient exercising has sympathetic activation, yet blood flow rises in active muscle. Is there a contradiction? Show explanation No. Sympathetic vasoconstrictor influence and local metabolic vasodilation act together. In active muscle, local demand-related dilation can strongly attenuate sympathetic constriction, so that perfusion increases while flow in less active beds may be restricted. Chapter Essentials Tissue flow depends on pressure difference and vascular resistance: Q = ΔP/R. Arteriolar calibre is a major adjustable determinant of local resistance. Autoregulation defends relatively stable flow only within a limited pressure range. Increased vessel stretch often produces myogenic constriction. A tissue with increased metabolic demand tends to develop metabolic vasodilation. Active hyperaemia accompanies increased tissue activity. Reactive hyperaemia follows temporary underperfusion. Endothelial NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → cGMP promotes smooth-muscle relaxation. Endothelin-1 commonly promotes vasoconstriction; endothelial responses are context-dependent. Cylindrical wall tension T ≈ Ptm × r and wall stress σθ ≈ Ptm × r/h require their assumptions. Local and systemic controls may compete or cooperate in one vascular bed. Severe shock can push perfusion below the effective autoregulatory range. References and scope notes Lecturer scope: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, pp. 1–11 (local regulation, myogenic/metabolic theories, Laplace and mediators). Slide numbering/source mapping should be rechecked against the exact uploaded lecture PDF before merging. Reference physiology: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, chapters on local and humoral control of tissue blood flow; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology, vascular smooth-muscle and regional-flow control. Supplementary precision: Autoregulatory ranges are tissue-specific; mediator examples are not universal; Laplace equations refer to ideal geometries; Poiseuille's law is an explanatory approximation in biological vessels."},{"slug":"neural-humoral","title":"Neural and Hormonal Regulation","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-whole-body-need-cardiovascular-regulation","title":"1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?"},{"id":"2-the-medullary-control-network","title":"2. The medullary control network"},{"id":"3-sympathetic-effects-on-the-heart-arterioles-and-veins","title":"3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins"},{"id":"4-parasympathetic-effects-and-tonic-vagal-restraint","title":"4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint"},{"id":"5-hormones-and-circulating-vasomotor-agents","title":"5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents"},{"id":"6-what-changes-in-haemorrhage-and-in-exercise","title":"6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?"},{"id":"7-time-scales-and-reset-limits","title":"7. Time scales and reset limits"},{"id":"8-clinical-application","title":"8. Clinical application"},{"id":"further-physiological-insights","title":"Further physiological insights"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Neural and Hormonal Regulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-whole-body-need-cardiovascular-regulation\">1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?</h2>\n<p>Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes. <strong>MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × SVR</strong>, and <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>. These are interconnected: changing venous tone can alter preload and stroke volume, while arteriolar tone changes afterload. A higher heart rate is therefore not proof of a higher output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-medullary-control-network\">2. The medullary control network</h2>\n<p>The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sensory information reaches the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong>. The <strong>rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)</strong> provides a major tonic sympathoexcitatory output; the <strong>caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)</strong> contributes inhibitory relay activity. Cardiac vagal output involves nucleus ambiguus and dorsal motor nucleus of vagus. The <em>pressor</em> and <em>depressor</em> regions describe the net effects of stimulating these networks. Increased sympathetic vasomotor firing narrows many arterioles; reducing its tonic activity permits dilation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-sympathetic-effects-on-the-heart-arterioles-and-veins\">3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins</h2>\n<p>Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release <strong>noradrenaline (norepinephrine)</strong>. Cardiac <strong>β₁</strong> signalling increases SA node firing, AV conduction and ventricular contractility. Vascular <strong>α₁</strong> stimulation constricts many arterioles, increasing resistance, and many veins, reducing venous capacitance and mobilising blood toward the thorax. <strong>Venoconstriction does not create blood</strong>: its contribution is limited by circulating volume and right-heart function. During haemorrhage, systemic vascular resistance can preserve MAP while tissue flow remains inadequate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-parasympathetic-effects-and-tonic-vagal-restraint\">4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint</h2>\n<p>Vagal <strong>acetylcholine</strong> binds cardiac <strong>M₂ muscarinic receptors</strong>, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic innervation is relatively modest in most systemic resistance vessels; the vagus therefore regulates the heart much more directly than systemic vascular resistance. At rest, vagal restraint often keeps heart rate below the intrinsic SA nodal rate. Atropine blocks muscarinic signalling and usually increases resting heart rate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-hormones-and-circulating-vasomotor-agents\">5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents</h2>\n<p><strong>Adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution. <strong>Angiotensin II</strong> acts mainly at AT₁ receptors to constrict arterioles, stimulate aldosterone and thirst, and support ADH release. <strong>Aldosterone</strong> increases distal nephron sodium reabsorption over hours to days; water retention follows depending on conditions. <strong>Vasopressin (ADH)</strong> increases renal water reabsorption through V₂ receptors and can cause V₁-mediated vasoconstriction at higher levels. <strong>ANP/BNP</strong> counter volume overload by promoting natriuresis and related effects. Endothelial NO and endothelin modify this systemic control locally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-changes-in-haemorrhage-and-in-exercise\">6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?</h2>\n<p><strong>Haemorrhage:</strong> reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Baroreflex unloading increases sympathetic activity and reduces vagal tone, raising heart rate, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. RAAS and ADH contribute over a longer time scale. If haemorrhage is severe, increased resistance cannot restore lost oxygen delivery. <strong>Exercise:</strong> sympathetic cardiac stimulation raises output while local metabolic dilation in active muscles can overcome much of the local vasoconstrictor influence. Other vascular beds may constrict, allowing redistribution. The final response is the sum of central and regional signals.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-time-scales-and-reset-limits\">7. Time scales and reset limits</h2>\n<p>Fast <strong>neural</strong> adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. The arterial baroreflex can reset during sustained pressure changes and does not by itself determine long-term blood pressure. Kidney-mediated sodium and water balance are central to long-term volume and pressure regulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-application\">8. Clinical application</h2>\n<p>An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. An individual taking an α₁ blocker may develop postural symptoms because peripheral vasoconstriction is impaired. <strong>Do not diagnose shock from blood pressure alone</strong>; follow perfusion, mental state, urine output, capillary refill and the clinical context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"further-physiological-insights\">Further physiological insights</h2>\n<p>The lecture additionally names kinins (bradykinin), vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP) and ANP as vasodilator influences. It describes specialised sympathetic cholinergic vasodilator pathways, although widespread human sympathetic vascular regulation is predominantly noradrenergic and constrictor. Cortical and hypothalamic signals, pain, lung inflation afferents, hypoxia and hypercapnia also modify medullary cardiovascular output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Sympathetic activity rises but pulse barely changes. How can MAP still rise?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>α₁-mediated arteriolar constriction increases systemic vascular resistance; venoconstriction can support preload. MAP depends on both output and resistance, not heart rate alone.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Why does vagal withdrawal increase SA nodal firing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Removing ACh–M₂ signalling reduces inhibitory effects on pacemaker activity, allowing rate to approach the intrinsic SA nodal rate.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Which receptor most directly increases ventricular contractility during sympathetic activation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Myocardial β₁ adrenergic receptor stimulation increases calcium availability and excitation–contraction coupling, increasing inotropy.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. What is the difference between constricting an arteriole and constricting a vein?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Arteriolar constriction mainly increases resistance and alters tissue flow; venoconstriction reduces capacitance and can move stored venous blood centrally to support preload.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Can a strong sympathetic response restore the blood volume lost in severe haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. It redistributes remaining blood and increases vascular tone but cannot manufacture lost volume; fluid replacement and control of bleeding address the primary deficit.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. How does the NTS participate in rapid BP regulation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It receives baroreceptor afferent signals and engages medullary pathways that adjust sympathetic and vagal outflow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can adrenaline dilate some vessels yet constrict others?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Adrenergic receptor subtype distribution and concentration matter: β₂ can favour skeletal-muscle vasodilation, while α activation causes constriction in other beds.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. Why is aldosterone slower than direct sympathetic vasoconstriction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Aldosterone modifies renal tubular transporter activity and sodium retention, influencing circulating volume over hours or days rather than acting as an immediate nerve impulse.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. How can ADH influence both vascular tone and water retention?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>V₂ receptor stimulation increases collecting-duct water reabsorption; V₁ receptors mediate vasoconstriction, especially at higher vasopressin levels.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why might active muscle receive more blood during exercise despite sympathetic activity?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Local metabolic vasodilator signals in active muscle can attenuate vasoconstriction, while cardiac output rises and blood is redistributed.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. If heart rate doubles but stroke volume halves, what happens to cardiac output?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Since CO = HR × SV, the product remains unchanged. Tachycardia alone is not proof of improved blood delivery.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why is the baroreflex insufficient as the sole explanation for chronic BP control?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It resets and buffers short-term changes; renal pressure natriuresis, fluid balance and longer-term hormonal mechanisms matter critically for persistent pressure regulation.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?</strong> — Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes.</li>\n<li><strong>The medullary control network</strong> — The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins</strong> — Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine).</li>\n<li><strong>Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint</strong> — Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction.</li>\n<li><strong>Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents</strong> — Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?</strong> — Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch.</li>\n<li><strong>Time scales and reset limits</strong> — Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days.</li>\n<li><strong>Clinical application</strong> — An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Neural and Hormonal Regulation 1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation? Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes. MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × SVR, and CO = HR × SV. These are interconnected: changing venous tone can alter preload and stroke volume, while arteriolar tone changes afterload. A higher heart rate is therefore not proof of a higher output. 2. The medullary control network The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sensory information reaches the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS). The rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM) provides a major tonic sympathoexcitatory output; the caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM) contributes inhibitory relay activity. Cardiac vagal output involves nucleus ambiguus and dorsal motor nucleus of vagus. The pressor and depressor regions describe the net effects of stimulating these networks. Increased sympathetic vasomotor firing narrows many arterioles; reducing its tonic activity permits dilation. 3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine). Cardiac β₁ signalling increases SA node firing, AV conduction and ventricular contractility. Vascular α₁ stimulation constricts many arterioles, increasing resistance, and many veins, reducing venous capacitance and mobilising blood toward the thorax. Venoconstriction does not create blood: its contribution is limited by circulating volume and right-heart function. During haemorrhage, systemic vascular resistance can preserve MAP while tissue flow remains inadequate. 4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic innervation is relatively modest in most systemic resistance vessels; the vagus therefore regulates the heart much more directly than systemic vascular resistance. At rest, vagal restraint often keeps heart rate below the intrinsic SA nodal rate. Atropine blocks muscarinic signalling and usually increases resting heart rate. 5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution. Angiotensin II acts mainly at AT₁ receptors to constrict arterioles, stimulate aldosterone and thirst, and support ADH release. Aldosterone increases distal nephron sodium reabsorption over hours to days; water retention follows depending on conditions. Vasopressin (ADH) increases renal water reabsorption through V₂ receptors and can cause V₁-mediated vasoconstriction at higher levels. ANP/BNP counter volume overload by promoting natriuresis and related effects. Endothelial NO and endothelin modify this systemic control locally. 6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise? Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Baroreflex unloading increases sympathetic activity and reduces vagal tone, raising heart rate, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. RAAS and ADH contribute over a longer time scale. If haemorrhage is severe, increased resistance cannot restore lost oxygen delivery. Exercise: sympathetic cardiac stimulation raises output while local metabolic dilation in active muscles can overcome much of the local vasoconstrictor influence. Other vascular beds may constrict, allowing redistribution. The final response is the sum of central and regional signals. 7. Time scales and reset limits Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. The arterial baroreflex can reset during sustained pressure changes and does not by itself determine long-term blood pressure. Kidney-mediated sodium and water balance are central to long-term volume and pressure regulation. 8. Clinical application An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. An individual taking an α₁ blocker may develop postural symptoms because peripheral vasoconstriction is impaired. Do not diagnose shock from blood pressure alone; follow perfusion, mental state, urine output, capillary refill and the clinical context. Further physiological insights The lecture additionally names kinins (bradykinin), vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP) and ANP as vasodilator influences. It describes specialised sympathetic cholinergic vasodilator pathways, although widespread human sympathetic vascular regulation is predominantly noradrenergic and constrictor. Cortical and hypothalamic signals, pain, lung inflation afferents, hypoxia and hypercapnia also modify medullary cardiovascular output. Understanding Checks 01. Sympathetic activity rises but pulse barely changes. How can MAP still rise? Show explanation α₁-mediated arteriolar constriction increases systemic vascular resistance; venoconstriction can support preload. MAP depends on both output and resistance, not heart rate alone. 02. Why does vagal withdrawal increase SA nodal firing? Show explanation Removing ACh–M₂ signalling reduces inhibitory effects on pacemaker activity, allowing rate to approach the intrinsic SA nodal rate. 03. Which receptor most directly increases ventricular contractility during sympathetic activation? Show explanation Myocardial β₁ adrenergic receptor stimulation increases calcium availability and excitation–contraction coupling, increasing inotropy. 04. What is the difference between constricting an arteriole and constricting a vein? Show explanation Arteriolar constriction mainly increases resistance and alters tissue flow; venoconstriction reduces capacitance and can move stored venous blood centrally to support preload. 05. Can a strong sympathetic response restore the blood volume lost in severe haemorrhage? Show explanation No. It redistributes remaining blood and increases vascular tone but cannot manufacture lost volume; fluid replacement and control of bleeding address the primary deficit. 06. How does the NTS participate in rapid BP regulation? Show explanation It receives baroreceptor afferent signals and engages medullary pathways that adjust sympathetic and vagal outflow. 07. Why can adrenaline dilate some vessels yet constrict others? Show explanation Adrenergic receptor subtype distribution and concentration matter: β₂ can favour skeletal-muscle vasodilation, while α activation causes constriction in other beds. 08. Why is aldosterone slower than direct sympathetic vasoconstriction? Show explanation Aldosterone modifies renal tubular transporter activity and sodium retention, influencing circulating volume over hours or days rather than acting as an immediate nerve impulse. 09. How can ADH influence both vascular tone and water retention? Show explanation V₂ receptor stimulation increases collecting-duct water reabsorption; V₁ receptors mediate vasoconstriction, especially at higher vasopressin levels. 10. Why might active muscle receive more blood during exercise despite sympathetic activity? Show explanation Local metabolic vasodilator signals in active muscle can attenuate vasoconstriction, while cardiac output rises and blood is redistributed. 11. If heart rate doubles but stroke volume halves, what happens to cardiac output? Show explanation Since CO = HR × SV, the product remains unchanged. Tachycardia alone is not proof of improved blood delivery. 12. Why is the baroreflex insufficient as the sole explanation for chronic BP control? Show explanation It resets and buffers short-term changes; renal pressure natriuresis, fluid balance and longer-term hormonal mechanisms matter critically for persistent pressure regulation. Chapter Essentials Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation? — Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes. The medullary control network — The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins — Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine). Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint — Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents — Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise? — Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Time scales and reset limits — Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. Clinical application — An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology."},{"slug":"reflexes","title":"Cardiovascular Reflexes and Cushing Response","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"18-36","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-makes-a-cardiovascular-reflex","title":"1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex?"},{"id":"2-baroreceptors-and-their-afferents","title":"2. Baroreceptors and their afferents"},{"id":"3-arterial-pressure-rises-trace-negative-feedback","title":"3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback"},{"id":"4-arterial-pressure-falls-trace-the-reverse-response","title":"4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response"},{"id":"5-chemoreceptor-reflexes","title":"5. Chemoreceptor reflexes"},{"id":"6-low-pressure-receptors-and-the-bainbridge-response","title":"6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response"},{"id":"7-cushing-response-threatened-cerebral-perfusion","title":"7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion"},{"id":"8-compare-apparently-opposite-reflex-responses","title":"8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses"},{"id":"further-physiological-insights","title":"Further physiological insights"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-makes-a-cardiovascular-reflex\">1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex?</h2>\n<p>A reflex involves a <strong>stimulus</strong>, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Reflexes use negative feedback to stabilise arterial pressure or support filling. Understanding the direction of each link avoids memorising isolated arrows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-baroreceptors-and-their-afferents\">2. Baroreceptors and their afferents</h2>\n<p>Arterial <strong>stretch-sensitive endings</strong> in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Carotid sinus signals pass through <strong>Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) → medullary NTS</strong>. Aortic arch signals travel primarily through <strong>vagus (CN X) → NTS</strong>. They respond to mechanical stretch associated with pressure, not directly to blood oxygen concentration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-arterial-pressure-rises-trace-negative-feedback\">3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback</h2>\n<p>Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. The medulla decreases sympathetic cardiac/vascular drive and increases cardiac vagal action. <strong>Heart rate, contractility, arteriolar resistance and often venous tone fall</strong>, opposing the rise in MAP. The baroreflex buffers <em>rapid</em> changes; prolonged hypertension can reset its operating point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-arterial-pressure-falls-trace-the-reverse-response\">4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response</h2>\n<p>Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. HR and contractility tend to rise; arterioles constrict and veins mobilise blood centrally. These responses defend pressure but cannot replace major blood loss. Reflex tachycardia can be absent or blunted because of medications, conduction disease or other competing influences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-chemoreceptor-reflexes\">5. Chemoreceptor reflexes</h2>\n<p>Peripheral chemoreceptors in <strong>carotid and aortic bodies</strong> sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Carotid body afferents travel via CN IX; aortic body afferents primarily via CN X. Their chief role includes stimulating ventilation, while cardiovascular effects depend on the interaction with respiratory and other reflexes. <strong>Do not confuse carotid body chemoreceptors with carotid sinus baroreceptors.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-low-pressure-receptors-and-the-bainbridge-response\">6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response</h2>\n<p>Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. Physiology distinguishes <strong>type A atrial receptor discharge around atrial contraction</strong> and <strong>type B discharge around filling</strong>. Stretch at the atrial–venous junction can promote a <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong> increase in heart rate, helping manage increased venous return. The outcome depends on concurrent baroreflex input; atrial filling does not invariably produce tachycardia. Atrial stretch can also promote ANP release and alter volume-related hormonal responses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-cushing-response-threatened-cerebral-perfusion\">7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion</h2>\n<p><strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Markedly rising intracranial pressure reduces perfusion. Severe brainstem ischaemia can provoke <strong>sympathetic vasoconstriction and hypertension</strong> to sustain perfusion. The resulting raised MAP activates <strong>arterial baroreceptors</strong>, contributing to <strong>bradycardia</strong>. Abnormal respiration can develop from brainstem dysfunction. Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration comprise the classic <strong>Cushing triad</strong>—an ominous, often late sign, not a universal early presentation of elevated ICP.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compare-apparently-opposite-reflex-responses\">8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses</h2>\n<p><strong>Ordinary blood loss</strong> tends to cause baroreflex <strong>tachycardia</strong> and vasoconstriction. <strong>Increased ICP</strong> may create a Cushing sequence of sympathetic <strong>hypertension</strong> followed by <strong>baroreflex bradycardia</strong>. <strong>Increased atrial filling</strong> can stimulate a Bainbridge tendency toward tachycardia. Different primary stimuli can therefore yield different HR directions; always identify the initiating stimulus before predicting output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"further-physiological-insights\">Further physiological insights</h2>\n<p>Additional lecture inputs include cortical and hypothalamic influences, pulmonary inflation afferents, hypoxia, hypercapnia and pain. Brief painful stimuli may increase sympathetic pressure responses, while emotional or sustained-pain triggers can evoke vasovagal hypotension. Increased atrial filling can also suppress ADH and stimulate natriuretic peptide signalling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Where are the two major high-pressure arterial baroreceptor sites?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>At the carotid sinus and aortic arch, where stretch-sensitive endings report pressure-related changes in vessel-wall distension.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Which cranial nerve carries carotid sinus baroreceptor signals centrally?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The carotid sinus stretch receptors send impulses via the carotid sinus nerve (Hering's nerve), a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla. Aortic arch receptors instead travel mainly through the vagus nerve.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Which cranial nerve primarily carries aortic arch baroreceptor afferents?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Aortic arch baroreceptor afferents travel through the vagus nerve (CN X) to the medullary nucleus tractus solitarius. This sensory input allows the brainstem to alter sympathetic and parasympathetic output to the heart and blood vessels.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. A sudden rise in MAP activates the baroreflex. What happens to HR and resistance?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Increased baroreceptor firing promotes vagal activity and decreases sympathetic output, lowering HR and arteriolar resistance to oppose high MAP.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Why is haemorrhagic reflex tachycardia not evidence of restored oxygen delivery?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>HR may rise while reduced venous return and stroke volume lower cardiac output; tissue delivery can remain insufficient.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. How does a carotid body differ physiologically from a carotid sinus?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The carotid body is a peripheral chemoreceptor sensing blood gas and acid-base changes; the sinus is a stretch-sensitive arterial baroreceptor.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. What stimulus is most closely associated with the Bainbridge reflex?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Increased venous return and stretch at atrial or venous junctions may increase HR, depending on competing reflexes.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. What are the lecturer's atrial receptor type A and B timing distinctions?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Type A impulses occur around atrial contraction; type B impulses are associated with atrial filling. They are low-pressure volume-related receptors.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why does the Cushing response initially increase systemic BP?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Severe intracranial hypertension compromises brainstem perfusion, provoking intense sympathetic vasoconstriction that raises systemic arterial pressure.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why can the pulse slow while BP rises in the Cushing response?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>As intracranial pressure rises and cerebral perfusion falls, the brainstem can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction, raising systemic arterial pressure. This elevated pressure stimulates arterial baroreceptors and can produce reflex slowing of heart rate. Bradycardia is therefore not a direct effect of raised intracranial pressure on the SA node in every case.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. What does CPP approximate when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Cerebral perfusion pressure is approximated by mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, provided ICP is the main downstream pressure. If ICP rises while MAP remains unchanged, the pressure available to drive cerebral blood flow falls. Actual flow also depends on cerebrovascular resistance and autoregulatory capacity.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Is the Cushing triad a reliable early screening test for raised ICP?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration together form the classic Cushing triad, usually suggesting severe or late intracranial hypertension and brainstem compromise. Earlier raised ICP can occur without the complete triad, so its absence cannot exclude dangerous pathology.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>What makes a cardiovascular reflex?</strong> — A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response.</li>\n<li><strong>Baroreceptors and their afferents</strong> — Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback</strong> — Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response</strong> — Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemoreceptor reflexes</strong> — Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response</strong> — Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling.</li>\n<li><strong>Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion</strong> — Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare apparently opposite reflex responses</strong> — Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response 1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex? A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Reflexes use negative feedback to stabilise arterial pressure or support filling. Understanding the direction of each link avoids memorising isolated arrows. 2. Baroreceptors and their afferents Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Carotid sinus signals pass through Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) → medullary NTS. Aortic arch signals travel primarily through vagus (CN X) → NTS. They respond to mechanical stretch associated with pressure, not directly to blood oxygen concentration. 3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. The medulla decreases sympathetic cardiac/vascular drive and increases cardiac vagal action. Heart rate, contractility, arteriolar resistance and often venous tone fall, opposing the rise in MAP. The baroreflex buffers rapid changes; prolonged hypertension can reset its operating point. 4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. HR and contractility tend to rise; arterioles constrict and veins mobilise blood centrally. These responses defend pressure but cannot replace major blood loss. Reflex tachycardia can be absent or blunted because of medications, conduction disease or other competing influences. 5. Chemoreceptor reflexes Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Carotid body afferents travel via CN IX; aortic body afferents primarily via CN X. Their chief role includes stimulating ventilation, while cardiovascular effects depend on the interaction with respiratory and other reflexes. Do not confuse carotid body chemoreceptors with carotid sinus baroreceptors. 6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. Physiology distinguishes type A atrial receptor discharge around atrial contraction and type B discharge around filling. Stretch at the atrial–venous junction can promote a Bainbridge reflex increase in heart rate, helping manage increased venous return. The outcome depends on concurrent baroreflex input; atrial filling does not invariably produce tachycardia. Atrial stretch can also promote ANP release and alter volume-related hormonal responses. 7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Markedly rising intracranial pressure reduces perfusion. Severe brainstem ischaemia can provoke sympathetic vasoconstriction and hypertension to sustain perfusion. The resulting raised MAP activates arterial baroreceptors, contributing to bradycardia. Abnormal respiration can develop from brainstem dysfunction. Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration comprise the classic Cushing triad —an ominous, often late sign, not a universal early presentation of elevated ICP. 8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction. Increased ICP may create a Cushing sequence of sympathetic hypertension followed by baroreflex bradycardia. Increased atrial filling can stimulate a Bainbridge tendency toward tachycardia. Different primary stimuli can therefore yield different HR directions; always identify the initiating stimulus before predicting output. Further physiological insights Additional lecture inputs include cortical and hypothalamic influences, pulmonary inflation afferents, hypoxia, hypercapnia and pain. Brief painful stimuli may increase sympathetic pressure responses, while emotional or sustained-pain triggers can evoke vasovagal hypotension. Increased atrial filling can also suppress ADH and stimulate natriuretic peptide signalling. Understanding Checks 01. Where are the two major high-pressure arterial baroreceptor sites? Show explanation At the carotid sinus and aortic arch, where stretch-sensitive endings report pressure-related changes in vessel-wall distension. 02. Which cranial nerve carries carotid sinus baroreceptor signals centrally? Show explanation The carotid sinus stretch receptors send impulses via the carotid sinus nerve (Hering's nerve), a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla. Aortic arch receptors instead travel mainly through the vagus nerve. 03. Which cranial nerve primarily carries aortic arch baroreceptor afferents? Show explanation Aortic arch baroreceptor afferents travel through the vagus nerve (CN X) to the medullary nucleus tractus solitarius. This sensory input allows the brainstem to alter sympathetic and parasympathetic output to the heart and blood vessels. 04. A sudden rise in MAP activates the baroreflex. What happens to HR and resistance? Show explanation Increased baroreceptor firing promotes vagal activity and decreases sympathetic output, lowering HR and arteriolar resistance to oppose high MAP. 05. Why is haemorrhagic reflex tachycardia not evidence of restored oxygen delivery? Show explanation HR may rise while reduced venous return and stroke volume lower cardiac output; tissue delivery can remain insufficient. 06. How does a carotid body differ physiologically from a carotid sinus? Show explanation The carotid body is a peripheral chemoreceptor sensing blood gas and acid-base changes; the sinus is a stretch-sensitive arterial baroreceptor. 07. What stimulus is most closely associated with the Bainbridge reflex? Show explanation Increased venous return and stretch at atrial or venous junctions may increase HR, depending on competing reflexes. 08. What are the lecturer's atrial receptor type A and B timing distinctions? Show explanation Type A impulses occur around atrial contraction; type B impulses are associated with atrial filling. They are low-pressure volume-related receptors. 09. Why does the Cushing response initially increase systemic BP? Show explanation Severe intracranial hypertension compromises brainstem perfusion, provoking intense sympathetic vasoconstriction that raises systemic arterial pressure. 10. Why can the pulse slow while BP rises in the Cushing response? Show explanation As intracranial pressure rises and cerebral perfusion falls, the brainstem can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction, raising systemic arterial pressure. This elevated pressure stimulates arterial baroreceptors and can produce reflex slowing of heart rate. Bradycardia is therefore not a direct effect of raised intracranial pressure on the SA node in every case. 11. What does CPP approximate when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure? Show explanation Cerebral perfusion pressure is approximated by mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, provided ICP is the main downstream pressure. If ICP rises while MAP remains unchanged, the pressure available to drive cerebral blood flow falls. Actual flow also depends on cerebrovascular resistance and autoregulatory capacity. 12. Is the Cushing triad a reliable early screening test for raised ICP? Show explanation No. Hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration together form the classic Cushing triad, usually suggesting severe or late intracranial hypertension and brainstem compromise. Earlier raised ICP can occur without the complete triad, so its absence cannot exclude dangerous pathology. Chapter Essentials What makes a cardiovascular reflex? — A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Baroreceptors and their afferents — Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback — Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response — Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. Chemoreceptor reflexes — Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response — Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion — Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses — Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology."},{"slug":"gravity","title":"Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"2-11; duplicate Lecture 5 pp2-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-standing-changes-the-hydrostatic-column","title":"1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column"},{"id":"2-why-venous-pooling-lowers-cardiac-output","title":"2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output"},{"id":"3-the-seconds-long-baroreflex-rescue","title":"3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue"},{"id":"4-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-standing-at-attention","title":"4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention"},{"id":"5-orthostatic-hypotension-and-its-clinical-definition","title":"5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition"},{"id":"6-vasovagal-syncope-is-not-simply-normal-orthostatic-compensation","title":"6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation"},{"id":"7-gravity-also-explains-dependent-oedema","title":"7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema"},{"id":"8-acceleration-aviation-and-clinical-consequences","title":"8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences"},{"id":"additional-mechanisms-worth-understanding","title":"Additional mechanisms worth understanding"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-standing-changes-the-hydrostatic-column\">1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column</h2>\n<p>In the upright body, pressure at points <strong>below</strong> heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure <strong>above</strong> heart level is relatively lower. Hydrostatic pressure depends on density, gravitational acceleration and vertical height difference (<strong>ΔP = ρgh</strong>). This is distinct from the pressure generated by the heart itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-venous-pooling-lowers-cardiac-output\">2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output</h2>\n<p>Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. Central blood volume and right atrial filling decline, lowering ventricular <strong>end-diastolic volume → stroke volume</strong> by the Frank–Starling mechanism. CO can transiently fall, endangering blood pressure at head level and cerebral blood flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-seconds-long-baroreflex-rescue\">3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue</h2>\n<p>Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Vagal restraint decreases and sympathetic drive increases: <strong>HR rises, contractility rises, arterioles constrict and veins constrict</strong>. This response raises systemic resistance and supports venous return. A strong heartbeat alone cannot compensate if preload is critically low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-standing-at-attention\">4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention</h2>\n<p>Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Walking and subtle <strong>isometric calf contraction</strong> can reduce dependent pooling. Soldiers standing still may perform small muscular contractions to support venous return. The muscle pump complements—not replaces—the baroreflex.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-orthostatic-hypotension-and-its-clinical-definition\">5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition</h2>\n<p>Classical <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong> is a sustained drop of at least <strong>20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing</strong> from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Dehydration, medications, autonomic neuropathy, haemorrhage and prolonged bed rest can contribute. Symptoms include dizziness, visual darkening and syncope from impaired cerebral perfusion. Some forms occur later than three minutes or immediately; formal definitions vary by timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-vasovagal-syncope-is-not-simply-normal-orthostatic-compensation\">6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation</h2>\n<p>Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate <strong>vasodilation and/or bradycardia</strong>, leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Contrast this with the usual immediate compensation to standing, which produces <strong>sympathetic activation and tachycardia</strong>. A patient can also have mixed mechanisms; clinical history matters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-gravity-also-explains-dependent-oedema\">7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema</h2>\n<p>Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Lymphatic return and muscular movement limit swelling; long immobility can overcome these defences. This connects Chapter 19 directly to capillary exchange and oedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-acceleration-aviation-and-clinical-consequences\">8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences</h2>\n<p>Under positive <strong>+Gz</strong> (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Anti-G manoeuvres and suits reduce dependent pooling. Under <strong>−Gz</strong>, blood shifts toward the head, contributing to congestion and visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, intensity and duration. Supine positioning reduces the head-heart hydrostatic gradient during a simple fainting episode, but persistent or unexplained loss of consciousness needs medical assessment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"additional-mechanisms-worth-understanding\">Additional mechanisms worth understanding</h2>\n<p>Another important effect is partial cerebral flow protection because both head-level arterial and venous pressures fall on standing; a decrease in cerebral venous or intracranial pressure and local cerebral vasodilation can partially defend perfusion, but these mechanisms fail in severe hypotension. Autonomic neuropathy, sympatholytic drugs and perioperative autonomic impairment can cause orthostatic hypotension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Why is ankle venous pressure higher than heart-level venous pressure when standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>When a person stands, gravity creates a hydrostatic pressure difference proportional to the vertical distance from heart level: ΔP = ρgh. Pressure increases in dependent vessels, promoting venous pooling, while pressure at head level is lower. This is a distribution of pressures, not a creation of extra blood volume.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. What is the first cardiac consequence of sudden dependent venous pooling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced central venous return lowers ventricular filling and end-diastolic volume, reducing stroke volume through Frank–Starling physiology.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. What happens to baroreceptor firing after a sudden orthostatic fall in arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Firing falls due to reduced arterial stretch, increasing sympathetic output and withdrawing cardiac vagal restraint.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why does heart rate usually rise on standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The baroreflex increases sympathetic cardiac drive and reduces vagal inhibition to support cardiac output and MAP.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. How do brief calf contractions help a person standing still?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Muscle compression of deep veins, with one-way valves, propels venous blood toward the heart and reduces pooling.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. What is the standard three-minute definition of classical orthostatic hypotension?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Classical orthostatic hypotension is generally defined as a sustained fall in systolic pressure of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic pressure of at least 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing or head-up tilt. Symptoms may accompany the fall, but the definition is based on measured pressure.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can a dehydrated person develop more severe orthostatic symptoms?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced circulating volume limits venous return and preload, making gravitational pooling harder to compensate for.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. How does vasovagal syncope differ from ordinary compensation to standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Instead of protective vasoconstriction and tachycardia, vasovagal reflexes may cause inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, dropping cerebral perfusion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why can walking relieve dizziness felt during prolonged motionless standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Contraction of leg muscles compresses deep veins. One-way venous valves favour movement of blood toward the heart rather than back toward the feet, reducing pooling and helping restore ventricular filling and stroke volume during standing or walking.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why does ankle oedema worsen after standing motionless for hours?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure increases net filtration; prolonged excess filtration can exceed lymphatic drainage.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why can positive head-to-foot acceleration impair vision?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It drives blood toward dependent tissues and reduces pressure and flow to the retina and brain.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Does lying flat restore lost blood volume after haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. It can improve the gravitational relationship and venous return transiently but does not replace lost volume or treat the source of bleeding.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Standing changes the hydrostatic column</strong> — In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower.</li>\n<li><strong>Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output</strong> — Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins.</li>\n<li><strong>The seconds-long baroreflex rescue</strong> — Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing.</li>\n<li><strong>Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention</strong> — Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement.</li>\n<li><strong>Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition</strong> — Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation</strong> — Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity also explains dependent oedema</strong> — Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing.</li>\n<li><strong>Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences</strong> — Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n<li>Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis 1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower. Hydrostatic pressure depends on density, gravitational acceleration and vertical height difference ( ΔP = ρgh). This is distinct from the pressure generated by the heart itself. 2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. Central blood volume and right atrial filling decline, lowering ventricular end-diastolic volume → stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. CO can transiently fall, endangering blood pressure at head level and cerebral blood flow. 3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Vagal restraint decreases and sympathetic drive increases: HR rises, contractility rises, arterioles constrict and veins constrict. This response raises systemic resistance and supports venous return. A strong heartbeat alone cannot compensate if preload is critically low. 4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Walking and subtle isometric calf contraction can reduce dependent pooling. Soldiers standing still may perform small muscular contractions to support venous return. The muscle pump complements—not replaces—the baroreflex. 5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Dehydration, medications, autonomic neuropathy, haemorrhage and prolonged bed rest can contribute. Symptoms include dizziness, visual darkening and syncope from impaired cerebral perfusion. Some forms occur later than three minutes or immediately; formal definitions vary by timing. 6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Contrast this with the usual immediate compensation to standing, which produces sympathetic activation and tachycardia. A patient can also have mixed mechanisms; clinical history matters. 7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Lymphatic return and muscular movement limit swelling; long immobility can overcome these defences. This connects Chapter 19 directly to capillary exchange and oedema. 8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Anti-G manoeuvres and suits reduce dependent pooling. Under −Gz, blood shifts toward the head, contributing to congestion and visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, intensity and duration. Supine positioning reduces the head-heart hydrostatic gradient during a simple fainting episode, but persistent or unexplained loss of consciousness needs medical assessment. Additional mechanisms worth understanding Another important effect is partial cerebral flow protection because both head-level arterial and venous pressures fall on standing; a decrease in cerebral venous or intracranial pressure and local cerebral vasodilation can partially defend perfusion, but these mechanisms fail in severe hypotension. Autonomic neuropathy, sympatholytic drugs and perioperative autonomic impairment can cause orthostatic hypotension. Understanding Checks 01. Why is ankle venous pressure higher than heart-level venous pressure when standing? Show explanation When a person stands, gravity creates a hydrostatic pressure difference proportional to the vertical distance from heart level: ΔP = ρgh. Pressure increases in dependent vessels, promoting venous pooling, while pressure at head level is lower. This is a distribution of pressures, not a creation of extra blood volume. 02. What is the first cardiac consequence of sudden dependent venous pooling? Show explanation Reduced central venous return lowers ventricular filling and end-diastolic volume, reducing stroke volume through Frank–Starling physiology. 03. What happens to baroreceptor firing after a sudden orthostatic fall in arterial pressure? Show explanation Firing falls due to reduced arterial stretch, increasing sympathetic output and withdrawing cardiac vagal restraint. 04. Why does heart rate usually rise on standing? Show explanation The baroreflex increases sympathetic cardiac drive and reduces vagal inhibition to support cardiac output and MAP. 05. How do brief calf contractions help a person standing still? Show explanation Muscle compression of deep veins, with one-way valves, propels venous blood toward the heart and reduces pooling. 06. What is the standard three-minute definition of classical orthostatic hypotension? Show explanation Classical orthostatic hypotension is generally defined as a sustained fall in systolic pressure of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic pressure of at least 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing or head-up tilt. Symptoms may accompany the fall, but the definition is based on measured pressure. 07. Why can a dehydrated person develop more severe orthostatic symptoms? Show explanation Reduced circulating volume limits venous return and preload, making gravitational pooling harder to compensate for. 08. How does vasovagal syncope differ from ordinary compensation to standing? Show explanation Instead of protective vasoconstriction and tachycardia, vasovagal reflexes may cause inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, dropping cerebral perfusion. 09. Why can walking relieve dizziness felt during prolonged motionless standing? Show explanation Contraction of leg muscles compresses deep veins. One-way venous valves favour movement of blood toward the heart rather than back toward the feet, reducing pooling and helping restore ventricular filling and stroke volume during standing or walking. 10. Why does ankle oedema worsen after standing motionless for hours? Show explanation Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure increases net filtration; prolonged excess filtration can exceed lymphatic drainage. 11. Why can positive head-to-foot acceleration impair vision? Show explanation It drives blood toward dependent tissues and reduces pressure and flow to the retina and brain. 12. Does lying flat restore lost blood volume after haemorrhage? Show explanation No. It can improve the gravitational relationship and venous return transiently but does not replace lost volume or treat the source of bleeding. Chapter Essentials Standing changes the hydrostatic column — In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output — Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue — Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention — Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition — Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation — Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Gravity also explains dependent oedema — Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences — Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology. Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims."},{"slug":"shock","title":"Circulatory Shock","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"12-30; duplicate Lecture 5 pp12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-shock-is-failure-of-tissue-perfusion-not-a-bp-threshold","title":"1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold"},{"id":"2-oxygen-delivery-connects-physiology-to-cellular-injury","title":"2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury"},{"id":"3-four-mechanistic-classes","title":"3. Four mechanistic classes"},{"id":"4-follow-blood-loss-all-the-way-to-organ-hypoperfusion","title":"4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion"},{"id":"5-compensation-can-preserve-pressure-while-organs-suffer","title":"5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer"},{"id":"6-progressive-shock-and-positive-feedback","title":"6. Progressive shock and positive feedback"},{"id":"7-why-acute-haemorrhage-may-initially-show-normal-hb-and-haematocrit","title":"7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit"},{"id":"8-the-physiology-behind-bedside-signs","title":"8. The physiology behind bedside signs"},{"id":"9-hypovolaemic-cardiogenic-distributive-and-obstructive-reasoning","title":"9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning"},{"id":"10-clinical-synthesis-and-limits","title":"10. Clinical synthesis and limits"},{"id":"further-physiological-insights","title":"Further physiological insights"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Circulatory Shock</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-shock-is-failure-of-tissue-perfusion-not-a-bp-threshold\">1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold</h2>\n<p><strong>Circulatory shock</strong> is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Blood pressure can be near normal early, because systemic resistance rises while cardiac output and regional perfusion fall. <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> describes how a low CO can be masked by compensatory constriction. Evaluate circulation and tissue perfusion, not one cuff reading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-oxygen-delivery-connects-physiology-to-cellular-injury\">2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury</h2>\n<p>Systemic oxygen delivery <strong>DO₂ = CO × CaO₂</strong>, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Approximately <strong>CaO₂ = 1.34 × Hb × SaO₂ + 0.003 × PaO₂</strong> (mL O₂/dL, with Hb in g/dL and PaO₂ in mmHg). Thus haemorrhage can reduce delivery by reducing CO even before a measured Hb concentration falls. Failure of oxygen delivery/utilisation impairs ATP production; lactate can rise, although elevated lactate is not specific proof of tissue hypoxia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-four-mechanistic-classes\">3. Four mechanistic classes</h2>\n<p><strong>Hypovolaemic:</strong> reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns). <strong>Cardiogenic:</strong> inadequate pumping (large myocardial infarction, critical arrhythmia). <strong>Distributive:</strong> abnormal vascular tone and flow distribution (sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock). <strong>Obstructive:</strong> impeded filling or forward flow (tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism). Causes can overlap; early septic shock may have warm extremities, so cool skin is not universal.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-follow-blood-loss-all-the-way-to-organ-hypoperfusion\">4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion</h2>\n<p>Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. The fall in arterial stretch unloads baroreceptors, driving sympathetic <strong>tachycardia, increased contractility, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction</strong>. Skin may become pale and cool while renal perfusion and urine output decline. Reduced venous filling limits the benefit of simply making the heart beat faster.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-compensation-can-preserve-pressure-while-organs-suffer\">5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer</h2>\n<p>Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow. <strong>Excessive tachycardia</strong> shortens diastolic filling time, potentially making stroke volume smaller. Strong vasoconstriction diverts flow away from skin and some organs; it cannot restore absent circulating volume. Angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH and thirst help support volume over longer periods but are insufficient without control of major ongoing losses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-progressive-shock-and-positive-feedback\">6. Progressive shock and positive feedback</h2>\n<p>Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Capillary leak and microvascular dysfunction can reduce effective circulating volume and oxygen extraction further. <strong>Worsening flow → tissue injury → poorer pump/vascular function → still worse flow</strong> is a reinforcing loop. Severe stages may become refractory despite treatment; there is no universal clock at which shock becomes irreversible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-acute-haemorrhage-may-initially-show-normal-hb-and-haematocrit\">7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit</h2>\n<p>A person loses <strong>whole blood</strong>, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. Their <em>ratio</em>—haematocrit—and Hb <em>concentration</em> may remain near baseline immediately. Subsequent interstitial fluid recruitment and infused IV fluid dilute the residual red cells; Hb and haematocrit can then fall. Initial laboratory concentrations must not override evidence of acute bleeding and hypoperfusion. Erythropoietin-driven red cell replacement takes much longer than volume adjustments.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-the-physiology-behind-bedside-signs\">8. The physiology behind bedside signs</h2>\n<p><strong>Tachycardia</strong> reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes. <strong>Cool skin</strong> often reflects peripheral constriction; <strong>warm skin</strong> can occur early in distributive shock. <strong>Oliguria</strong> suggests reduced renal perfusion or renal dysfunction; altered mentation may signal cerebral hypoperfusion. Lactate, capillary refill and trends can contribute, but none alone establishes or excludes shock.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-hypovolaemic-cardiogenic-distributive-and-obstructive-reasoning\">9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning</h2>\n<p>In <strong>hypovolaemia</strong>, filling pressure is often low; in <strong>cardiogenic pump failure</strong>, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in <strong>distributive</strong> forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in <strong>obstructive</strong> forms flow is mechanically impeded. These are <strong>typical patterns</strong>, not hard rules. Identify the failing link rather than treating all hypotension as a lack of fluid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-clinical-synthesis-and-limits\">10. Clinical synthesis and limits</h2>\n<p>The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting. <strong>Bleeding requires haemorrhage control</strong>, pump failure requires cause-directed management, obstructive causes need relief, and distributive causes need targeted treatment. This chapter teaches physiology, not a substitute for emergency clinical protocols.</p>\n<h2 id=\"further-physiological-insights\">Further physiological insights</h2>\n<p>The lecture includes concealed internal bleeding after femoral fracture, splenic rupture or ectopic pregnancy; burn-related plasma losses may initially produce haemoconcentration rather than the typical immediate whole-blood Hb concentration pattern. It also covers myoglobin-associated renal injury in trauma, low urine output and tubular injury, myocardial ischaemia, capillary stasis and eventual vascular hyporesponsiveness. Vasovagal, postural, micturition and carotid-sinus syncope have distinct triggers; ordinary orthostatic syncope does not require bradycardia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Can a patient be shocked with a near-normal MAP after major bleeding?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Yes. Raised SVR may sustain MAP while reduced venous return and cardiac output impair tissue oxygen delivery. Clinical perfusion matters.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Write the equation connecting oxygen delivery to cardiac output.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Systemic oxygen delivery equals cardiac output multiplied by arterial oxygen content: DO₂ = CO × CaO₂. During haemorrhage, cardiac output may fall immediately from reduced venous return, even while haemoglobin concentration initially remains near its starting value. Normal early Hb does not imply normal oxygen delivery.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Why does severe tachycardia sometimes worsen haemorrhagic shock?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>A compensatory faster heartbeat initially supports output, but extreme tachycardia shortens diastole, the period for ventricular filling. If blood volume and venous return are already low, each beat may start with less blood, decreasing stroke volume enough to offset the increased rate.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why is vasoconstriction initially adaptive in haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It increases SVR and supports arterial pressure while diverting limited blood toward vital organs, but cannot replace lost blood.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Which shock type is most directly caused by cardiac tamponade?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Obstructive shock, because pericardial pressure mechanically restricts ventricular filling and forward output.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. Which shock type usually follows a large infarct with severe pump dysfunction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Cardiogenic shock: inadequate myocardial pumping reduces forward output, sometimes despite elevated filling pressures.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can early septic shock have warm skin?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Distributive vasodilation can increase cutaneous blood flow despite severe circulatory dysfunction.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. Why may Hb and haematocrit be nearly unchanged immediately after acute whole-blood loss?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Immediately after an acute whole-blood haemorrhage, both red cells and plasma are lost, so the concentration of haemoglobin and haematocrit may initially change little despite a substantial reduction in total circulating red-cell mass. Later interstitial fluid shifts or intravenous fluids dilute the remaining blood.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why does measured Hb often fall later after bleeding?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Over time, fluid moves from the interstitium into the vascular space, and administered crystalloid may add further plasma volume. Because the lost red cells have not yet been replaced, haemoglobin concentration and haematocrit commonly fall after this haemodilution.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why can lactate rise in shock, and why is it not fully specific?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Low oxygen delivery may increase anaerobic glycolysis and lactate, but adrenergic stimulation, clearance changes and other causes also affect lactate.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Explain one positive feedback loop in progressive shock.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Low perfusion produces acidosis and myocardial/endothelial injury, worsening cardiac function, microvascular flow and effective volume; this further lowers perfusion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why is the treatment target different in obstructive and hypovolaemic shock?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Obstructive shock requires relief of the mechanical block; hypovolaemic shock requires correcting volume loss and its cause. Treating every low BP identically misses the mechanism.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold</strong> — Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury</strong> — Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content.</li>\n<li><strong>Four mechanistic classes</strong> — Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns).</li>\n<li><strong>Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion</strong> — Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓.</li>\n<li><strong>Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer</strong> — Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive shock and positive feedback</strong> — Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury.</li>\n<li><strong>Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit</strong> — A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together.</li>\n<li><strong>The physiology behind bedside signs</strong> — Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning</strong> — In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded.</li>\n<li><strong>Clinical synthesis and limits</strong> — The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Circulatory Shock 1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Blood pressure can be near normal early, because systemic resistance rises while cardiac output and regional perfusion fall. MAP ≈ CO × SVR describes how a low CO can be masked by compensatory constriction. Evaluate circulation and tissue perfusion, not one cuff reading. 2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Approximately CaO₂ = 1.34 × Hb × SaO₂ + 0.003 × PaO₂ (mL O₂/dL, with Hb in g/dL and PaO₂ in mmHg). Thus haemorrhage can reduce delivery by reducing CO even before a measured Hb concentration falls. Failure of oxygen delivery/utilisation impairs ATP production; lactate can rise, although elevated lactate is not specific proof of tissue hypoxia. 3. Four mechanistic classes Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns). Cardiogenic: inadequate pumping (large myocardial infarction, critical arrhythmia). Distributive: abnormal vascular tone and flow distribution (sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock). Obstructive: impeded filling or forward flow (tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism). Causes can overlap; early septic shock may have warm extremities, so cool skin is not universal. 4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. The fall in arterial stretch unloads baroreceptors, driving sympathetic tachycardia, increased contractility, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction. Skin may become pale and cool while renal perfusion and urine output decline. Reduced venous filling limits the benefit of simply making the heart beat faster. 5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow. Excessive tachycardia shortens diastolic filling time, potentially making stroke volume smaller. Strong vasoconstriction diverts flow away from skin and some organs; it cannot restore absent circulating volume. Angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH and thirst help support volume over longer periods but are insufficient without control of major ongoing losses. 6. Progressive shock and positive feedback Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Capillary leak and microvascular dysfunction can reduce effective circulating volume and oxygen extraction further. Worsening flow → tissue injury → poorer pump/vascular function → still worse flow is a reinforcing loop. Severe stages may become refractory despite treatment; there is no universal clock at which shock becomes irreversible. 7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. Their ratio —haematocrit—and Hb concentration may remain near baseline immediately. Subsequent interstitial fluid recruitment and infused IV fluid dilute the residual red cells; Hb and haematocrit can then fall. Initial laboratory concentrations must not override evidence of acute bleeding and hypoperfusion. Erythropoietin-driven red cell replacement takes much longer than volume adjustments. 8. The physiology behind bedside signs Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes. Cool skin often reflects peripheral constriction; warm skin can occur early in distributive shock. Oliguria suggests reduced renal perfusion or renal dysfunction; altered mentation may signal cerebral hypoperfusion. Lactate, capillary refill and trends can contribute, but none alone establishes or excludes shock. 9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded. These are typical patterns, not hard rules. Identify the failing link rather than treating all hypotension as a lack of fluid. 10. Clinical synthesis and limits The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting. Bleeding requires haemorrhage control, pump failure requires cause-directed management, obstructive causes need relief, and distributive causes need targeted treatment. This chapter teaches physiology, not a substitute for emergency clinical protocols. Further physiological insights The lecture includes concealed internal bleeding after femoral fracture, splenic rupture or ectopic pregnancy; burn-related plasma losses may initially produce haemoconcentration rather than the typical immediate whole-blood Hb concentration pattern. It also covers myoglobin-associated renal injury in trauma, low urine output and tubular injury, myocardial ischaemia, capillary stasis and eventual vascular hyporesponsiveness. Vasovagal, postural, micturition and carotid-sinus syncope have distinct triggers; ordinary orthostatic syncope does not require bradycardia. Understanding Checks 01. Can a patient be shocked with a near-normal MAP after major bleeding? Show explanation Yes. Raised SVR may sustain MAP while reduced venous return and cardiac output impair tissue oxygen delivery. Clinical perfusion matters. 02. Write the equation connecting oxygen delivery to cardiac output. Show explanation Systemic oxygen delivery equals cardiac output multiplied by arterial oxygen content: DO₂ = CO × CaO₂. During haemorrhage, cardiac output may fall immediately from reduced venous return, even while haemoglobin concentration initially remains near its starting value. Normal early Hb does not imply normal oxygen delivery. 03. Why does severe tachycardia sometimes worsen haemorrhagic shock? Show explanation A compensatory faster heartbeat initially supports output, but extreme tachycardia shortens diastole, the period for ventricular filling. If blood volume and venous return are already low, each beat may start with less blood, decreasing stroke volume enough to offset the increased rate. 04. Why is vasoconstriction initially adaptive in haemorrhage? Show explanation It increases SVR and supports arterial pressure while diverting limited blood toward vital organs, but cannot replace lost blood. 05. Which shock type is most directly caused by cardiac tamponade? Show explanation Obstructive shock, because pericardial pressure mechanically restricts ventricular filling and forward output. 06. Which shock type usually follows a large infarct with severe pump dysfunction? Show explanation Cardiogenic shock: inadequate myocardial pumping reduces forward output, sometimes despite elevated filling pressures. 07. Why can early septic shock have warm skin? Show explanation Distributive vasodilation can increase cutaneous blood flow despite severe circulatory dysfunction. 08. Why may Hb and haematocrit be nearly unchanged immediately after acute whole-blood loss? Show explanation Immediately after an acute whole-blood haemorrhage, both red cells and plasma are lost, so the concentration of haemoglobin and haematocrit may initially change little despite a substantial reduction in total circulating red-cell mass. Later interstitial fluid shifts or intravenous fluids dilute the remaining blood. 09. Why does measured Hb often fall later after bleeding? Show explanation Over time, fluid moves from the interstitium into the vascular space, and administered crystalloid may add further plasma volume. Because the lost red cells have not yet been replaced, haemoglobin concentration and haematocrit commonly fall after this haemodilution. 10. Why can lactate rise in shock, and why is it not fully specific? Show explanation Low oxygen delivery may increase anaerobic glycolysis and lactate, but adrenergic stimulation, clearance changes and other causes also affect lactate. 11. Explain one positive feedback loop in progressive shock. Show explanation Low perfusion produces acidosis and myocardial/endothelial injury, worsening cardiac function, microvascular flow and effective volume; this further lowers perfusion. 12. Why is the treatment target different in obstructive and hypovolaemic shock? Show explanation Obstructive shock requires relief of the mechanical block; hypovolaemic shock requires correcting volume loss and its cause. Treating every low BP identically misses the mechanism. Chapter Essentials Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold — Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury — Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Four mechanistic classes — Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns). Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion — Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer — Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow. Progressive shock and positive feedback — Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit — A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. The physiology behind bedside signs — Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning — In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded. Clinical synthesis and limits — The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology."},{"slug":"heart-failure","title":"Heart Failure Physiology","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"31-33; duplicate Lecture 5 pp31-33","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"define-the-pump-problem","title":"Define the pump problem"},{"id":"forward-versus-backward-consequences","title":"Forward versus backward consequences"},{"id":"compensation-can-become-harmful","title":"Compensation can become harmful"},{"id":"systolic-and-diastolic-problems","title":"Systolic and diastolic problems"},{"id":"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion","title":"Forward flow and backward congestion"},{"id":"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm","title":"What compensates and why compensation can harm"},{"id":"extra-clinical-connection","title":"Extra clinical connection"},{"id":"comprehensive-understanding-checks","title":"Comprehensive Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Failure Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"define-the-pump-problem\">Define the pump problem</h2>\n<p>Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forward-versus-backward-consequences\">Forward versus backward consequences</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward consequences</strong> relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow). <strong>Backward consequences</strong> reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compensation-can-become-harmful\">Compensation can become harmful</h2>\n<p>Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output, <strong>RAAS</strong> and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"systolic-and-diastolic-problems\">Systolic and diastolic problems</h2>\n<p>In <strong>systolic dysfunction</strong>, ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In <strong>diastolic dysfunction</strong>, filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion.</p>\n<p><strong>Why oedema can coexist with renal sodium retention:</strong> reduced effective arterial perfusion activates RAAS and ADH despite total-body fluid excess. This raises venous pressures and worsens congestion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion\">Forward flow and backward congestion</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward failure</strong> emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion. <strong>Backward failure</strong> emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites.</p>\n<p>These are explanatory perspectives, <strong>not four mutually exclusive diseases</strong>. Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm\">What compensates and why compensation can harm</h2>\n<p>Reduced forward output activates <strong>sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH</strong>. Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or <strong>preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"extra-clinical-connection\">Extra clinical connection</h2>\n<p>Raised left atrial pressure elevates pulmonary venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging lung interstitial fluid accumulation. In right ventricular failure, systemic venous congestion contributes to increased JVP, peripheral oedema and hepatic congestion. Elevated filling pressures may occur even when measured ejection fraction is preserved.</p>\n<h2 id=\"comprehensive-understanding-checks\">Comprehensive Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Why can ejection fraction remain normal in heart failure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>A stiff ventricle resists filling, so pressure rises sharply even when its end-diastolic volume is normal or reduced. It may still eject a normal percentage of that volume (preserved EF) while total stroke volume or exercise capacity is inadequate. EF alone does not establish whether filling is normal.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Why is pulmonary oedema associated with left-sided failure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>When left ventricular filling pressure rises, pressure backs up into the left atrium and pulmonary veins. The resulting rise in pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes more fluid into lung tissue; once lymphatic clearance is overwhelmed, interstitial and then alveolar oedema may develop.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Why does right-heart failure elevate JVP?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Systemic venous blood meets elevated right atrial pressure and venous congestion develops.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why can forward failure cause oliguria?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced effective arterial blood delivery activates sympathetic and renin–angiotensin–aldosterone pathways and promotes antidiuretic hormone release. The kidneys then retain sodium and water, which may temporarily support circulation but worsen congestion. GFR may also decrease.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Why does RAAS activation initially help but ultimately worsen congestion?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It supports pressure and volume initially, but persistent fluid retention raises venous pressures and afterload.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. What distinguishes orthopnoea from exertional dyspnoea?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Recumbency redistributes blood centrally and can aggravate pulmonary congestion even without exertion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can tachycardia reduce filling in heart failure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Shortened diastole reduces filling time, especially when ventricular relaxation is already impaired.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. How does right-sided forward failure limit left ventricular filling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Right ventricular failure can reduce blood reaching pulmonary capillaries and subsequently the left atrium. This reduces left ventricular preload and may lower systemic cardiac output, even though systemic venous pressure is high.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why can total-body fluid excess coexist with effective arterial underfilling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced cardiac performance makes the arterial circulation underfilled relative to tissue needs despite venous congestion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. What produces ascites in right backward failure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>High right atrial pressure is transmitted back into systemic and hepatic veins. Higher capillary and sinusoidal hydrostatic pressures increase filtration and hepatic lymph formation; when removal cannot keep up, peripheral oedema or ascites develops.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why does increased afterload burden a failing ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It must generate more wall stress to eject blood, potentially decreasing stroke volume.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. How do forward and backward failure interact?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The same ventricular dysfunction may decrease delivered flow and increase upstream filling pressures.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Heart failure may present with high filling pressure despite normal resting output.</li>\n<li>Forward failure impairs effective delivery; backward failure raises upstream venous pressure.</li>\n<li>Left failure causes pulmonary congestion; right failure causes systemic congestion.</li>\n<li>RAAS and sympathetic responses may turn maladaptive.</li>\n<li>Preserved EF does not exclude high-pressure impaired filling.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Heart Failure Physiology Define the pump problem Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output. Forward versus backward consequences Forward consequences relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow). Backward consequences reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema. Compensation can become harmful Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output, RAAS and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency. Systolic and diastolic problems In systolic dysfunction, ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In diastolic dysfunction, filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion. Why oedema can coexist with renal sodium retention: reduced effective arterial perfusion activates RAAS and ADH despite total-body fluid excess. This raises venous pressures and worsens congestion. Forward flow and backward congestion Forward failure emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion. Backward failure emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites. These are explanatory perspectives, not four mutually exclusive diseases. Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem. What compensates and why compensation can harm Reduced forward output activates sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH. Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal. Practice: Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately. Extra clinical connection Raised left atrial pressure elevates pulmonary venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging lung interstitial fluid accumulation. In right ventricular failure, systemic venous congestion contributes to increased JVP, peripheral oedema and hepatic congestion. Elevated filling pressures may occur even when measured ejection fraction is preserved. Comprehensive Understanding Checks 01. Why can ejection fraction remain normal in heart failure? Show explanation A stiff ventricle resists filling, so pressure rises sharply even when its end-diastolic volume is normal or reduced. It may still eject a normal percentage of that volume (preserved EF) while total stroke volume or exercise capacity is inadequate. EF alone does not establish whether filling is normal. 02. Why is pulmonary oedema associated with left-sided failure? Show explanation When left ventricular filling pressure rises, pressure backs up into the left atrium and pulmonary veins. The resulting rise in pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes more fluid into lung tissue; once lymphatic clearance is overwhelmed, interstitial and then alveolar oedema may develop. 03. Why does right-heart failure elevate JVP? Show explanation Systemic venous blood meets elevated right atrial pressure and venous congestion develops. 04. Why can forward failure cause oliguria? Show explanation Reduced effective arterial blood delivery activates sympathetic and renin–angiotensin–aldosterone pathways and promotes antidiuretic hormone release. The kidneys then retain sodium and water, which may temporarily support circulation but worsen congestion. GFR may also decrease. 05. Why does RAAS activation initially help but ultimately worsen congestion? Show explanation It supports pressure and volume initially, but persistent fluid retention raises venous pressures and afterload. 06. What distinguishes orthopnoea from exertional dyspnoea? Show explanation Recumbency redistributes blood centrally and can aggravate pulmonary congestion even without exertion. 07. Why can tachycardia reduce filling in heart failure? Show explanation Shortened diastole reduces filling time, especially when ventricular relaxation is already impaired. 08. How does right-sided forward failure limit left ventricular filling? Show explanation Right ventricular failure can reduce blood reaching pulmonary capillaries and subsequently the left atrium. This reduces left ventricular preload and may lower systemic cardiac output, even though systemic venous pressure is high. 09. Why can total-body fluid excess coexist with effective arterial underfilling? Show explanation Reduced cardiac performance makes the arterial circulation underfilled relative to tissue needs despite venous congestion. 10. What produces ascites in right backward failure? Show explanation High right atrial pressure is transmitted back into systemic and hepatic veins. Higher capillary and sinusoidal hydrostatic pressures increase filtration and hepatic lymph formation; when removal cannot keep up, peripheral oedema or ascites develops. 11. Why does increased afterload burden a failing ventricle? Show explanation It must generate more wall stress to eject blood, potentially decreasing stroke volume. 12. How do forward and backward failure interact? Show explanation The same ventricular dysfunction may decrease delivered flow and increase upstream filling pressures. Chapter Essentials Heart failure may present with high filling pressure despite normal resting output. Forward failure impairs effective delivery; backward failure raises upstream venous pressure. Left failure causes pulmonary congestion; right failure causes systemic congestion. RAAS and sympathetic responses may turn maladaptive. Preserved EF does not exclude high-pressure impaired filling."},{"slug":"cerebral","title":"Cerebral Circulation and CSF","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"2-25; extra diagrams Circulation 3 39-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-is-circulation-to-the-brain-different","title":"1. Why is circulation to the brain different?"},{"id":"2-where-does-cerebral-arterial-blood-come-from","title":"2. Where does cerebral arterial blood come from?"},{"id":"3-how-does-the-brain-keep-its-blood-flow-stable","title":"3. How does the brain keep its blood flow stable?"},{"id":"4-why-can-normal-arm-bp-coexist-with-poor-brain-perfusion","title":"4. Why can normal arm BP coexist with poor brain perfusion?"},{"id":"5-meninges-ventricles-and-cerebrospinal-fluid","title":"5. Meninges, ventricles and cerebrospinal fluid"},{"id":"6-what-makes-the-blood-brain-barrier-selective","title":"6. What makes the blood–brain barrier selective?"},{"id":"7-integrate-the-mechanisms-clinically","title":"7. Integrate the mechanisms clinically"},{"id":"comprehensive-understanding-checks","title":"Comprehensive Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-is-circulation-to-the-brain-different\">1. Why is circulation to the brain different?</h2>\n<p>Neurons need a near-continuous supply of <strong>oxygen and glucose</strong> because their energy stores are limited. Cerebral blood flow must therefore be maintained despite changes in posture, arterial pressure and local neuronal activity. Yet the brain sits inside a rigid skull: swelling or bleeding can raise intracranial pressure and interfere with blood supply.</p>\n<p>Three distinct ideas must not be confused:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mean arterial pressure (MAP)</strong> is the arterial pressure available to drive blood into the cranial circulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)</strong> is the pressure difference driving blood through the brain.</li>\n<li><strong>Cerebral blood flow (CBF)</strong> is the volume of blood actually delivered per unit time; it also depends on cerebrovascular resistance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In simple terms, <strong>flow ≈ driving pressure ÷ resistance</strong>. A reasonable CPP does not guarantee adequate flow if resistance is abnormally high.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-where-does-cerebral-arterial-blood-come-from\">2. Where does cerebral arterial blood come from?</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Circle_of_Willis_en.svg\" alt=\"Detailed inferior view of cerebral arterial anatomy showing vertebral and basilar arteries, internal carotids, anterior communicating arteries, posterior communicating arteries and branches of the Circle of Willis.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. The real anatomical arrangement of the Circle of Willis.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Rhcastilhos, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Circle_of_Willis_en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Two <strong>internal carotid arteries</strong> supply much of the anterior circulation. Two <strong>vertebral arteries</strong> join to form the <strong>basilar artery</strong>, supplying the posterior circulation. These systems connect through the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>, an arterial ring around the base of the brain that may offer alternative pathways when an upstream vessel narrows.</p>\n<p>The ring normally includes the paired anterior cerebral arteries near the anterior communicating artery, proximal internal carotid portions, posterior communicating arteries and proximal posterior cerebral arteries. <strong>The middle cerebral artery (MCA) is a major internal carotid branch, but is not itself part of the ring.</strong> Anatomical variations are common; the Circle of Willis cannot always compensate for an occlusion.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Main artery</th>\n  <th>Typical territory</th>\n  <th>A useful clinical association</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Medial frontal/parietal cortex</td>\n  <td>Contralateral leg weakness may be prominent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Middle cerebral artery (MCA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Much of the lateral cerebral hemisphere</td>\n  <td>Contralateral face/arm deficits; aphasia when the dominant hemisphere is affected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Posterior cerebral artery (PCA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Occipital cortex and parts of inferomedial temporal lobe</td>\n  <td>Visual-field loss may occur</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are <strong>general patterns, not rigid borders</strong>. Collaterals and individual arterial anatomy vary.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Cerebral arterial supply — conceptual route map</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 248\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Internal carotid systems lead to anterior and middle cerebral arteries; paired vertebral arteries join into basilar artery and reach posterior cerebral arteries; communicating vessels link the two systems\"><g font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#22354e\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"16\" width=\"240\" height=\"46\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6efff\" stroke=\"#638bd0\"/><text x=\"134\" y=\"44\">Internal carotid systems</text><rect x=\"408\" y=\"16\" width=\"238\" height=\"46\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f5ea\" stroke=\"#76a586\"/><text x=\"527\" y=\"44\">Vertebral arteries → basilar</text><rect x=\"12\" y=\"128\" width=\"192\" height=\"48\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6efff\" stroke=\"#638bd0\"/><text x=\"108\" y=\"148\">ACA: medial</text><text x=\"108\" y=\"166\">hemisphere</text><rect x=\"218\" y=\"128\" width=\"192\" height=\"48\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6efff\" stroke=\"#638bd0\"/><text x=\"314\" y=\"148\">MCA: lateral</text><text x=\"314\" y=\"166\">hemisphere</text><rect x=\"424\" y=\"128\" width=\"222\" height=\"48\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f5ea\" stroke=\"#76a586\"/><text x=\"535\" y=\"148\">PCA: occipital</text><text x=\"535\" y=\"166\">and related regions</text><rect x=\"167\" y=\"195\" width=\"332\" height=\"36\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fff3d9\" stroke=\"#bb9e5e\"/><text x=\"333\" y=\"218\">Circle of Willis: potential collateral links</text></g><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#587ca6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><path d=\"M134 62v33H108v32M134 95h180v32M527 62v65M134 95v-8h393v40\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M108 176v19M314 176v19M535 176v19H498\"/></g></svg><p>This is a flow-and-territory learning schematic, not an anatomical depiction of the ring's exact vessel positions. The MCA arises from the internal carotid but is not part of the Circle of Willis.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Reason through an occlusion:</strong> A left MCA blockage might damage the lateral motor cortex, affecting the right face and arm. If the language-dominant hemisphere is left-sided, speech or language deficits may accompany weakness. The symptoms depend on the tissue actually deprived of blood.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-does-the-brain-keep-its-blood-flow-stable\">3. How does the brain keep its blood flow stable?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Pressure autoregulation — conceptual blood-flow curve</figcaption><svg role=\"img\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 235\" aria-label=\"Cerebral blood flow rises at low pressure, changes relatively little across an autoregulated range, then rises again at high pressure; exact limits vary\"><g stroke=\"#62758d\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M65 20v180h510\"/></g><path d=\"M75 187Q125 178 170 100Q230 83 335 85Q415 87 455 79Q500 65 565 24\" stroke=\"#2468c5\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#263d61\"><text x=\"18\" y=\"19\">CBF</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"222\">Cerebral perfusion pressure →</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"153\">Low pressure</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"67\">Buffered flow</text><text x=\"462\" y=\"116\">High pressure</text></g><g stroke=\"#a7aeb8\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 5\"><path d=\"M190 45v152M448 45v152\"/></g></svg><p>As driving pressure changes, cerebral arterioles adjust resistance to buffer cerebral blood flow. The apparent plateau is approximate, not perfectly flat; its boundaries vary and can shift with disease or chronic hypertension.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>Pressure autoregulation</h3>\n<p>When perfusion pressure rises, cerebral resistance arterioles can <strong>constrict</strong>; when pressure falls, they can <strong>dilate</strong>. This buffers changes in blood flow over a range of perfusion pressures. Outside that range—or after severe brain injury—flow becomes more pressure-dependent. The exact limits vary with individuals and conditions; a single textbook pressure interval must not be treated as universal.</p>\n<p>Autoregulation is <em>not</em> the same as saying blood pressure never changes. Rather, <strong>arteriolar resistance changes to protect flow</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Carbon dioxide and oxygen</h3>\n<p>A rise in arterial <strong>CO₂</strong> generally increases hydrogen ion concentration around brain vessels and causes <strong>cerebral arteriolar dilation</strong>, raising CBF. A fall in CO₂, as during excessive hyperventilation, causes vasoconstriction and can reduce CBF. Severe hypoxaemia can also dilate cerebral vessels. These responses are not interchangeable with systemic vascular responses.</p>\n<h3>Local activity</h3>\n<p>Active neural regions use more energy and can receive more blood through <strong>neurovascular coupling</strong>. Thus regional cerebral flow can change even when total systemic BP changes very little.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-can-normal-arm-bp-coexist-with-poor-brain-perfusion\">4. Why can normal arm BP coexist with poor brain perfusion?</h2>\n<p>The commonly used approximation is <strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong>, provided intracranial pressure is the relevant downstream pressure. More precisely, the effective downstream pressure can be influenced by venous outflow pressure, so CPP is related to MAP minus the higher relevant downstream pressure.</p>\n<p>If <strong>MAP = 90 mmHg</strong> and <strong>ICP = 10 mmHg</strong>, the approximate CPP is <strong>80 mmHg</strong>. If ICP increases to <strong>35 mmHg</strong> while MAP stays at 90, CPP falls to <strong>55 mmHg</strong>. The arm BP did not need to fall for cerebral perfusion to become threatened.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Increasing ICP reduces the driving pressure available to perfuse the brain</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 630 170\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"At constant MAP 90 mmHg, ICP rising from 10 to 35 mmHg lowers CPP from 80 to 55 mmHg\"><g font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" fill=\"#253b57\" font-size=\"15\"><text x=\"10\" y=\"25\" font-weight=\"bold\">Example at MAP = 90 mmHg</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"69\">ICP = 10</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"129\">ICP = 35</text><rect x=\"115\" y=\"47\" width=\"400\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e2ebf6\"/><rect x=\"115\" y=\"47\" width=\"356\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#73a4dc\"/><rect x=\"115\" y=\"107\" width=\"400\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e2ebf6\"/><rect x=\"115\" y=\"107\" width=\"244\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dc9873\"/><text x=\"480\" y=\"68\">CPP 80</text><text x=\"369\" y=\"128\">CPP 55</text></g></svg><p>Illustrative pressure calculation; these bars are not a patient-specific treatment threshold. Cerebral flow additionally depends on resistance.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>The Monro–Kellie principle and Cushing response</h3>\n<p>The adult cranial cavity contains mainly <strong>brain tissue, blood and CSF</strong>. Because the skull is relatively rigid, an expanding lesion can initially be compensated for by shifting venous blood and CSF. Once those reserves are exhausted, even a small additional volume can cause a large rise in ICP.</p>\n<p>An extreme increase in ICP threatens perfusion of the brainstem and can evoke a <strong>Cushing response</strong>: sympathetic hypertension to defend perfusion, with reflex bradycardia from arterial baroreceptors, sometimes accompanied by abnormal respiration. This is a <strong>late, serious sign</strong>, not the normal first response to every small ICP rise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-meninges-ventricles-and-cerebrospinal-fluid\">5. Meninges, ventricles and cerebrospinal fluid</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meninges-en.svg\" alt=\"Cross-sectional anatomical illustration identifying dura mater, arachnoid mater, subarachnoid space and pia mater\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Meninges and the subarachnoid compartment.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Mysid and Wikimedia contributors, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meninges-en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/CSF_circulation.png\" alt=\"Illustration of the brain ventricles, subarachnoid cerebrospinal fluid spaces and CSF circulation.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Ventricular and subarachnoid CSF compartments.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\"><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CSF_circulation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons source and contributors</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>From outside to inside, the coverings are <strong>dura mater → arachnoid mater → subarachnoid space → pia mater</strong>. The <strong>subarachnoid space normally contains CSF and vessels</strong>; the subdural space is only a <em>potential</em> space in normal anatomy.</p>\n<p>CSF is produced mainly by <strong>choroid plexuses</strong> in the ventricles. Adult total CSF volume is approximately <strong>150 mL</strong>, while production is several hundred millilitres per day, meaning the fluid is continually renewed.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. CSF route from ventricular production to subarachnoid circulation</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flow sequence from lateral ventricles via Monro, third ventricle, aqueduct and fourth ventricle to subarachnoid space\"><g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#1b3557\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"32\" width=\"112\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e7f0ff\" stroke=\"#5c8bd7\"/><text x=\"64\" y=\"55\">Lateral</text><text x=\"64\" y=\"75\">ventricles</text><rect x=\"152\" y=\"32\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f6ed\" stroke=\"#5d9d7e\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"55\">Third</text><text x=\"205\" y=\"75\">ventricle</text><rect x=\"290\" y=\"32\" width=\"105\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff1d7\" stroke=\"#bf953e\"/><text x=\"342\" y=\"55\">Cerebral</text><text x=\"342\" y=\"75\">aqueduct</text><rect x=\"427\" y=\"32\" width=\"105\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f6ed\" stroke=\"#5d9d7e\"/><text x=\"479\" y=\"55\">Fourth</text><text x=\"479\" y=\"75\">ventricle</text><rect x=\"564\" y=\"32\" width=\"108\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e7f0ff\" stroke=\"#5c8bd7\"/><text x=\"618\" y=\"55\">Subarachnoid</text><text x=\"618\" y=\"75\">space</text></g><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#436f9f\" stroke-width=\"3\"><path d=\"M121 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6M259 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6M396 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6M533 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6\"/></g><g font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#415772\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"136\" y=\"119\">Monro</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"118\">Magendie/</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"133\">Luschka</text></g></svg><p>Original conceptual diagram; the choroid plexus produces most CSF, and the arrows represent bulk flow rather than exact anatomical proportions.</p></figure>\n\n<p>The median aperture of <strong>Magendie</strong> and lateral apertures of <strong>Luschka</strong> allow CSF to leave the fourth ventricle. CSF circulates in the subarachnoid space and exits through multiple routes including <strong>arachnoid granulations and lymphatic-associated pathways</strong>. Its important functions include cushioning, buoyancy and solute exchange.</p>\n<h3>What happens if CSF cannot circulate normally?</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Noncommunicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus:</strong> A blockage within the ventricular system, for example at the cerebral aqueduct, prevents downstream flow and dilates upstream ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Communicating hydrocephalus:</strong> Ventricular pathways remain open, but CSF handling or outflow beyond those pathways is impaired.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both may raise ICP, but clinical patterns and causes differ. A lumbar puncture accesses <strong>subarachnoid CSF</strong>, usually in the lower lumbar region; suspected mass effect or dangerous pressure gradients require appropriate clinical assessment before performing one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-makes-the-blood-brain-barrier-selective\">6. What makes the blood–brain barrier selective?</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blood_brain_barrier.png\" alt=\"Comparison of brain capillary endothelial barrier with the more permeable capillaries of other tissues\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. What makes cerebral capillaries different?</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">David Nascari and Alan Sved, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blood_brain_barrier.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Most cerebral capillaries have <strong>continuous, nonfenestrated endothelial cells joined by tight junctions</strong>. Together with basement membrane, pericytes, astrocytic endfeet and regulated transporters, they form a neurovascular unit that tightly controls exchange.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Substance</th>\n  <th>Main route across the BBB</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Oxygen and carbon dioxide</td>\n  <td>Diffuse across membranes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Glucose</td>\n  <td>Carrier-mediated transport, especially <strong>GLUT1</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Many ions and water-soluble molecules</td>\n  <td>Restricted or regulated passage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Some lipid-soluble substances</td>\n  <td>Can cross relatively easily, depending on properties</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>blood–CSF barrier</strong> at the choroid plexus is different: its key junctional barrier is the choroid plexus <strong>epithelium</strong>, not simply the capillary endothelium. This distinction matters when interpreting CNS drug entry and fluid physiology.</p>\n<p>The brain relies heavily on glucose in normal fed conditions. During prolonged fasting, <strong>ketone bodies</strong> become important alternative fuels; it is inaccurate to assume ordinary long-chain fatty acids freely replace glucose as the brain's main normal fuel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-integrate-the-mechanisms-clinically\">7. Integrate the mechanisms clinically</h2>\n<p><strong>Stroke:</strong> Ischaemic stroke reflects interrupted perfusion, while haemorrhagic stroke involves bleeding. Haemorrhage can damage tissue directly and may also raise ICP. The Circle of Willis offers <em>potential</em>, not guaranteed, protection.</p>\n<p><strong>Head injury or expanding tumour:</strong> Additional intracranial volume raises ICP; as CPP decreases, cerebral delivery may become inadequate. Initial compensation can hide an evolving problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Excessive hyperventilation:</strong> Lower CO₂ can constrict cerebral vessels and lower CBF despite normal systemic BP. It must not be assumed universally beneficial.</p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> Good cerebral circulation requires arterial inflow, an adequate driving pressure, regulated vascular resistance and a stable intracranial environment—not simply a normal reading on an arm cuff.</p>\n<h2 id=\"comprehensive-understanding-checks\">Comprehensive Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. How can the internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems compensate for one another, and why might compensation fail?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>The Circle of Willis links anterior and posterior arterial systems through communicating arteries, creating potential collateral routes when a major inflow vessel narrows. These channels vary in size and may be incomplete, so a sudden occlusion may still deprive brain tissue of oxygen. The MCA is a major branch but is not itself part of the ring.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>02. A patient develops right face and arm weakness with aphasia. Which cerebral vascular territory is especially concerning, and why?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>A left middle cerebral artery territory lesion is a strong possibility, assuming left-hemisphere language dominance. The left motor cortex controls the right side of the body, while dominant-hemisphere lateral cortical regions often support language. This is a territorial clue, not a diagnosis without examination and imaging.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>03. MAP remains 90 mmHg while ICP rises from 10 to 35 mmHg. How does the approximate CPP change?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>Using CPP ≈ MAP − ICP, the initial CPP is 90 − 10 = 80 mmHg. With ICP at 35, CPP falls to 90 − 35 = 55 mmHg. Perfusion driving pressure therefore drops by 25 mmHg even though arm arterial pressure is unchanged. Actual blood flow also depends on cerebrovascular resistance.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why can hyperventilation reduce cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>Hyperventilation lowers arterial CO₂. This reduces local CO₂-related acidification and tends to constrict cerebral resistance arterioles, increasing resistance and reducing cerebral blood flow. The response illustrates why systemic pressure alone cannot predict tissue perfusion.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>05. A growing intracranial haematoma initially produces little ICP change, then pressure rises rapidly. Explain the change.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>The Monro–Kellie principle states that brain tissue, blood and CSF occupy a constrained cranial space. Early expansion can be buffered by displacement of CSF and venous blood. Once this compensation is exhausted, additional volume causes a steep rise in ICP, threatening CPP and cerebral perfusion.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>06. Explain the mechanism of hypertension with bradycardia in a severe Cushing response.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>Severely raised ICP threatens brainstem perfusion, provoking sympathetic vasoconstriction and systemic hypertension in an attempt to restore driving pressure. The resulting arterial pressure rise stimulates baroreceptors and can produce reflex bradycardia. It is a late warning sign, not an ordinary autoregulatory response.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Trace CSF from its production site until it enters the subarachnoid space.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>CSF produced mainly by ventricular choroid plexuses passes from the lateral ventricles through the foramina of Monro to the third ventricle, then through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle. It exits through the median Magendie and lateral Luschka apertures into the subarachnoid space.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>08. How would aqueduct obstruction differ from impaired CSF absorption?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>A blocked cerebral aqueduct interrupts flow within the ventricular system and causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus, often dilating ventricles upstream of the obstruction. Reduced CSF absorption despite open ventricular routes is a common mechanism of communicating hydrocephalus. Both can increase intracranial pressure.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>09. What distinguishes the normal subarachnoid space from the subdural space?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>The subarachnoid space between arachnoid and pia normally contains CSF and blood vessels. The subdural space between dura and arachnoid is normally a potential space, not an additional freely circulating CSF compartment. This matters when identifying meninges, bleeding sites and lumbar puncture anatomy.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why does the BBB allow glucose entry while restricting unrestricted passage of many polar substances?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>Brain capillary endothelial tight junctions restrict paracellular passage. Glucose crosses mainly through selective carriers such as GLUT1 instead of freely leaking between cells. Controlled transport helps keep the chemical environment around neurons stable while supplying essential nutrients.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>11. How is the blood–CSF barrier different from the blood–brain barrier?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>At most brain capillaries, specialised endothelial tight junctions form the main blood–brain barrier. At the choroid plexus, the principal tight-junction barrier between blood and CSF is provided by choroid plexus epithelial cells. The two barriers have different anatomical locations and exchange functions.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why can ketone bodies support brain metabolism during prolonged fasting without making fatty acids the brain's usual direct fuel?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary><p>During prolonged fasting, the liver makes water-soluble ketone bodies that can cross into the brain and be oxidised for energy, reducing but not eliminating its need for glucose. Long-chain fatty acids do not normally replace glucose as a freely available major brain fuel across the BBB.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems supply the brain; the Circle of Willis provides variable potential collateral pathways.</li>\n<li>The ACA, MCA and PCA supply characteristic but overlapping territories; the MCA is not part of the Circle of Willis itself.</li>\n<li>Cerebral flow depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance; autoregulation adjusts arteriolar tone to buffer changes in flow.</li>\n<li>CO₂ strongly modifies cerebral vascular resistance: hypercapnia tends to dilate and hypocapnia constricts.</li>\n<li>CPP is commonly approximated by MAP minus ICP, provided ICP represents the relevant downstream pressure.</li>\n<li>The Monro–Kellie principle explains why rising intracranial volume may eventually produce a steep ICP rise.</li>\n<li>A severe Cushing response involves sympathetic hypertension and secondary baroreflex bradycardia.</li>\n<li>CSF travels lateral ventricles → Monro → third ventricle → aqueduct → fourth ventricle → Magendie/Luschka → subarachnoid space.</li>\n<li>The subarachnoid space normally holds CSF; the subdural space is normally potential.</li>\n<li>The BBB depends chiefly on brain capillary endothelial tight junctions and selective transport; it differs from the choroid plexus blood–CSF barrier.</li>\n<li>Ischaemic and haemorrhagic strokes have different immediate mechanisms; neither is prevented reliably by the Circle of Willis.</li>\n<li>Normal systemic BP does not ensure normal cerebral tissue blood flow.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier 1. Why is circulation to the brain different? Neurons need a near-continuous supply of oxygen and glucose because their energy stores are limited. Cerebral blood flow must therefore be maintained despite changes in posture, arterial pressure and local neuronal activity. Yet the brain sits inside a rigid skull: swelling or bleeding can raise intracranial pressure and interfere with blood supply. Three distinct ideas must not be confused: Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is the arterial pressure available to drive blood into the cranial circulation. Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) is the pressure difference driving blood through the brain. Cerebral blood flow (CBF) is the volume of blood actually delivered per unit time; it also depends on cerebrovascular resistance. In simple terms, flow ≈ driving pressure ÷ resistance. A reasonable CPP does not guarantee adequate flow if resistance is abnormally high. 2. Where does cerebral arterial blood come from? Figure. The real anatomical arrangement of the Circle of Willis. Image credit Rhcastilhos, Wikimedia Commons · public domain · unmodified. Two internal carotid arteries supply much of the anterior circulation. Two vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery, supplying the posterior circulation. These systems connect through the Circle of Willis, an arterial ring around the base of the brain that may offer alternative pathways when an upstream vessel narrows. The ring normally includes the paired anterior cerebral arteries near the anterior communicating artery, proximal internal carotid portions, posterior communicating arteries and proximal posterior cerebral arteries. The middle cerebral artery (MCA) is a major internal carotid branch, but is not itself part of the ring. Anatomical variations are common; the Circle of Willis cannot always compensate for an occlusion. Main artery Typical territory A useful clinical association Anterior cerebral artery (ACA) Medial frontal/parietal cortex Contralateral leg weakness may be prominent Middle cerebral artery (MCA) Much of the lateral cerebral hemisphere Contralateral face/arm deficits; aphasia when the dominant hemisphere is affected Posterior cerebral artery (PCA) Occipital cortex and parts of inferomedial temporal lobe Visual-field loss may occur These are general patterns, not rigid borders. Collaterals and individual arterial anatomy vary. Figure. Cerebral arterial supply — conceptual route map Internal carotid systems Vertebral arteries → basilar ACA: medial hemisphere MCA: lateral hemisphere PCA: occipital and related regions Circle of Willis: potential collateral links This is a flow-and-territory learning schematic, not an anatomical depiction of the ring's exact vessel positions. The MCA arises from the internal carotid but is not part of the Circle of Willis. Reason through an occlusion: A left MCA blockage might damage the lateral motor cortex, affecting the right face and arm. If the language-dominant hemisphere is left-sided, speech or language deficits may accompany weakness. The symptoms depend on the tissue actually deprived of blood. 3. How does the brain keep its blood flow stable? Pressure autoregulation — conceptual blood-flow curve CBF Cerebral perfusion pressure → Low pressure Buffered flow High pressure As driving pressure changes, cerebral arterioles adjust resistance to buffer cerebral blood flow. The apparent plateau is approximate, not perfectly flat; its boundaries vary and can shift with disease or chronic hypertension. Pressure autoregulation When perfusion pressure rises, cerebral resistance arterioles can constrict; when pressure falls, they can dilate. This buffers changes in blood flow over a range of perfusion pressures. Outside that range—or after severe brain injury—flow becomes more pressure-dependent. The exact limits vary with individuals and conditions; a single textbook pressure interval must not be treated as universal. Autoregulation is not the same as saying blood pressure never changes. Rather, arteriolar resistance changes to protect flow. Carbon dioxide and oxygen A rise in arterial CO₂ generally increases hydrogen ion concentration around brain vessels and causes cerebral arteriolar dilation, raising CBF. A fall in CO₂, as during excessive hyperventilation, causes vasoconstriction and can reduce CBF. Severe hypoxaemia can also dilate cerebral vessels. These responses are not interchangeable with systemic vascular responses. Local activity Active neural regions use more energy and can receive more blood through neurovascular coupling. Thus regional cerebral flow can change even when total systemic BP changes very little. 4. Why can normal arm BP coexist with poor brain perfusion? The commonly used approximation is CPP = MAP − ICP, provided intracranial pressure is the relevant downstream pressure. More precisely, the effective downstream pressure can be influenced by venous outflow pressure, so CPP is related to MAP minus the higher relevant downstream pressure. If MAP = 90 mmHg and ICP = 10 mmHg, the approximate CPP is 80 mmHg. If ICP increases to 35 mmHg while MAP stays at 90, CPP falls to 55 mmHg. The arm BP did not need to fall for cerebral perfusion to become threatened. Figure. Increasing ICP reduces the driving pressure available to perfuse the brain Example at MAP = 90 mmHg ICP = 10 ICP = 35 CPP 80 CPP 55 Illustrative pressure calculation; these bars are not a patient-specific treatment threshold. Cerebral flow additionally depends on resistance. The Monro–Kellie principle and Cushing response The adult cranial cavity contains mainly brain tissue, blood and CSF. Because the skull is relatively rigid, an expanding lesion can initially be compensated for by shifting venous blood and CSF. Once those reserves are exhausted, even a small additional volume can cause a large rise in ICP. An extreme increase in ICP threatens perfusion of the brainstem and can evoke a Cushing response: sympathetic hypertension to defend perfusion, with reflex bradycardia from arterial baroreceptors, sometimes accompanied by abnormal respiration. This is a late, serious sign, not the normal first response to every small ICP rise. 5. Meninges, ventricles and cerebrospinal fluid Figure. Meninges and the subarachnoid compartment. Image credit Mysid and Wikimedia contributors, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. Figure. Ventricular and subarachnoid CSF compartments. Image credit Wikimedia Commons source and contributors · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. From outside to inside, the coverings are dura mater → arachnoid mater → subarachnoid space → pia mater. The subarachnoid space normally contains CSF and vessels; the subdural space is only a potential space in normal anatomy. CSF is produced mainly by choroid plexuses in the ventricles. Adult total CSF volume is approximately 150 mL, while production is several hundred millilitres per day, meaning the fluid is continually renewed. Figure. CSF route from ventricular production to subarachnoid circulation Lateral ventricles Third ventricle Cerebral aqueduct Fourth ventricle Subarachnoid space Monro Magendie/ Luschka Original conceptual diagram; the choroid plexus produces most CSF, and the arrows represent bulk flow rather than exact anatomical proportions. The median aperture of Magendie and lateral apertures of Luschka allow CSF to leave the fourth ventricle. CSF circulates in the subarachnoid space and exits through multiple routes including arachnoid granulations and lymphatic-associated pathways. Its important functions include cushioning, buoyancy and solute exchange. What happens if CSF cannot circulate normally? Noncommunicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus: A blockage within the ventricular system, for example at the cerebral aqueduct, prevents downstream flow and dilates upstream ventricles. Communicating hydrocephalus: Ventricular pathways remain open, but CSF handling or outflow beyond those pathways is impaired. Both may raise ICP, but clinical patterns and causes differ. A lumbar puncture accesses subarachnoid CSF, usually in the lower lumbar region; suspected mass effect or dangerous pressure gradients require appropriate clinical assessment before performing one. 6. What makes the blood–brain barrier selective? Figure. What makes cerebral capillaries different? Image credit David Nascari and Alan Sved, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Most cerebral capillaries have continuous, nonfenestrated endothelial cells joined by tight junctions. Together with basement membrane, pericytes, astrocytic endfeet and regulated transporters, they form a neurovascular unit that tightly controls exchange. Substance Main route across the BBB Oxygen and carbon dioxide Diffuse across membranes Glucose Carrier-mediated transport, especially GLUT1 Many ions and water-soluble molecules Restricted or regulated passage Some lipid-soluble substances Can cross relatively easily, depending on properties The blood–CSF barrier at the choroid plexus is different: its key junctional barrier is the choroid plexus epithelium, not simply the capillary endothelium. This distinction matters when interpreting CNS drug entry and fluid physiology. The brain relies heavily on glucose in normal fed conditions. During prolonged fasting, ketone bodies become important alternative fuels; it is inaccurate to assume ordinary long-chain fatty acids freely replace glucose as the brain's main normal fuel. 7. Integrate the mechanisms clinically Stroke: Ischaemic stroke reflects interrupted perfusion, while haemorrhagic stroke involves bleeding. Haemorrhage can damage tissue directly and may also raise ICP. The Circle of Willis offers potential, not guaranteed, protection. Head injury or expanding tumour: Additional intracranial volume raises ICP; as CPP decreases, cerebral delivery may become inadequate. Initial compensation can hide an evolving problem. Excessive hyperventilation: Lower CO₂ can constrict cerebral vessels and lower CBF despite normal systemic BP. It must not be assumed universally beneficial. Key distinction: Good cerebral circulation requires arterial inflow, an adequate driving pressure, regulated vascular resistance and a stable intracranial environment—not simply a normal reading on an arm cuff. Comprehensive Understanding Checks 01. How can the internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems compensate for one another, and why might compensation fail? Show explanation The Circle of Willis links anterior and posterior arterial systems through communicating arteries, creating potential collateral routes when a major inflow vessel narrows. These channels vary in size and may be incomplete, so a sudden occlusion may still deprive brain tissue of oxygen. The MCA is a major branch but is not itself part of the ring. 02. A patient develops right face and arm weakness with aphasia. Which cerebral vascular territory is especially concerning, and why? Show explanation A left middle cerebral artery territory lesion is a strong possibility, assuming left-hemisphere language dominance. The left motor cortex controls the right side of the body, while dominant-hemisphere lateral cortical regions often support language. This is a territorial clue, not a diagnosis without examination and imaging. 03. MAP remains 90 mmHg while ICP rises from 10 to 35 mmHg. How does the approximate CPP change? Show explanation Using CPP ≈ MAP − ICP, the initial CPP is 90 − 10 = 80 mmHg. With ICP at 35, CPP falls to 90 − 35 = 55 mmHg. Perfusion driving pressure therefore drops by 25 mmHg even though arm arterial pressure is unchanged. Actual blood flow also depends on cerebrovascular resistance. 04. Why can hyperventilation reduce cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic arterial pressure? Show explanation Hyperventilation lowers arterial CO₂. This reduces local CO₂-related acidification and tends to constrict cerebral resistance arterioles, increasing resistance and reducing cerebral blood flow. The response illustrates why systemic pressure alone cannot predict tissue perfusion. 05. A growing intracranial haematoma initially produces little ICP change, then pressure rises rapidly. Explain the change. Show explanation The Monro–Kellie principle states that brain tissue, blood and CSF occupy a constrained cranial space. Early expansion can be buffered by displacement of CSF and venous blood. Once this compensation is exhausted, additional volume causes a steep rise in ICP, threatening CPP and cerebral perfusion. 06. Explain the mechanism of hypertension with bradycardia in a severe Cushing response. Show explanation Severely raised ICP threatens brainstem perfusion, provoking sympathetic vasoconstriction and systemic hypertension in an attempt to restore driving pressure. The resulting arterial pressure rise stimulates baroreceptors and can produce reflex bradycardia. It is a late warning sign, not an ordinary autoregulatory response. 07. Trace CSF from its production site until it enters the subarachnoid space. Show explanation CSF produced mainly by ventricular choroid plexuses passes from the lateral ventricles through the foramina of Monro to the third ventricle, then through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle. It exits through the median Magendie and lateral Luschka apertures into the subarachnoid space. 08. How would aqueduct obstruction differ from impaired CSF absorption? Show explanation A blocked cerebral aqueduct interrupts flow within the ventricular system and causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus, often dilating ventricles upstream of the obstruction. Reduced CSF absorption despite open ventricular routes is a common mechanism of communicating hydrocephalus. Both can increase intracranial pressure. 09. What distinguishes the normal subarachnoid space from the subdural space? Show explanation The subarachnoid space between arachnoid and pia normally contains CSF and blood vessels. The subdural space between dura and arachnoid is normally a potential space, not an additional freely circulating CSF compartment. This matters when identifying meninges, bleeding sites and lumbar puncture anatomy. 10. Why does the BBB allow glucose entry while restricting unrestricted passage of many polar substances? Show explanation Brain capillary endothelial tight junctions restrict paracellular passage. Glucose crosses mainly through selective carriers such as GLUT1 instead of freely leaking between cells. Controlled transport helps keep the chemical environment around neurons stable while supplying essential nutrients. 11. How is the blood–CSF barrier different from the blood–brain barrier? Show explanation At most brain capillaries, specialised endothelial tight junctions form the main blood–brain barrier. At the choroid plexus, the principal tight-junction barrier between blood and CSF is provided by choroid plexus epithelial cells. The two barriers have different anatomical locations and exchange functions. 12. Why can ketone bodies support brain metabolism during prolonged fasting without making fatty acids the brain's usual direct fuel? Show explanation During prolonged fasting, the liver makes water-soluble ketone bodies that can cross into the brain and be oxidised for energy, reducing but not eliminating its need for glucose. Long-chain fatty acids do not normally replace glucose as a freely available major brain fuel across the BBB. Chapter Essentials Internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems supply the brain; the Circle of Willis provides variable potential collateral pathways. The ACA, MCA and PCA supply characteristic but overlapping territories; the MCA is not part of the Circle of Willis itself. Cerebral flow depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance; autoregulation adjusts arteriolar tone to buffer changes in flow. CO₂ strongly modifies cerebral vascular resistance: hypercapnia tends to dilate and hypocapnia constricts. CPP is commonly approximated by MAP minus ICP, provided ICP represents the relevant downstream pressure. The Monro–Kellie principle explains why rising intracranial volume may eventually produce a steep ICP rise. A severe Cushing response involves sympathetic hypertension and secondary baroreflex bradycardia. CSF travels lateral ventricles → Monro → third ventricle → aqueduct → fourth ventricle → Magendie/Luschka → subarachnoid space. The subarachnoid space normally holds CSF; the subdural space is normally potential. The BBB depends chiefly on brain capillary endothelial tight junctions and selective transport; it differs from the choroid plexus blood–CSF barrier. Ischaemic and haemorrhagic strokes have different immediate mechanisms; neither is prevented reliably by the Circle of Willis. Normal systemic BP does not ensure normal cerebral tissue blood flow."},{"slug":"coronary","title":"Coronary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"26-29; extra diagrams Circulation 3 42-43","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply","title":"1. Why does the heart need a separate blood supply?"},{"id":"2-trace-coronary-inflow-and-venous-drainage","title":"2. Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage"},{"id":"3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole","title":"3. Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole?"},{"id":"4-how-does-coronary-flow-match-changing-myocardial-work","title":"4. How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work?"},{"id":"5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia","title":"5. Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia?"},{"id":"6-coronary-flow-reserve-angina-and-myocardial-infarction","title":"6. Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction"},{"id":"comprehensive-understanding-checks","title":"Comprehensive Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Coronary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\">1. Why does the heart need a separate blood supply?</h2>\n<p>Blood inside the chambers does not adequately nourish the thick ventricular myocardium. <strong>Right and left coronary arteries</strong> arise from the <strong>aortic sinuses</strong>, just above the aortic valve, and supply oxygen and nutrients to cardiac muscle.</p>\n<p>The heart works continuously and consumes considerable oxygen. Its resting <strong>oxygen extraction is already high</strong>, so when cardiac work increases, it cannot simply extract much more oxygen from unchanged blood flow. Instead, coronary blood flow generally needs to rise.</p>\n<p><strong>Oxygen delivery = coronary blood flow × arterial oxygen content.</strong> A decrease in either can compromise supply.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-trace-coronary-inflow-and-venous-drainage\">2. Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png\" alt=\"Anatomical anterior view of the human heart showing the left and right ventricles and coronary arteries running over their surfaces\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Anterior heart and ventricular coronary vessels.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_arteries.svg\" alt=\"Medical illustration of the heart with labelled right coronary, left coronary, left anterior descending, circumflex and posterior descending coronary artery branches.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Coronary arteries in their anatomical locations.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Patrick J. Lynch; derivative and labels by Fred the Oyster and Mikael Häggström, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronary_arteries.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>The <strong>left main coronary artery</strong> usually divides into the <strong>left anterior descending (LAD, or anterior interventricular artery)</strong> and <strong>left circumflex artery (LCx)</strong>. The LAD supplies much of the anterior ventricular wall and interventricular septum; the LCx supplies much of the lateral left ventricular wall. The <strong>right coronary artery (RCA)</strong> commonly supplies the right heart and portions of the inferior heart, but patterns vary.</p>\n<p><strong>Coronary dominance</strong> is defined by the artery that gives rise to the <strong>posterior descending artery (PDA/posterior interventricular artery)</strong>. In right-dominant circulation, the PDA arises from the RCA; in left dominance, it typically arises from the circumflex. This naming does <strong>not</strong> mean one whole side of the heart is supplied by only one artery.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Major coronary routes and drainage — simplified flow map</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 250\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Aortic root branches into left main and right coronary arteries. Left main divides into LAD and circumflex; posterior descending artery origin defines dominance. Venous return passes chiefly via coronary sinus to right atrium\"><g font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" fill=\"#22354e\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><rect x=\"240\" y=\"9\" width=\"200\" height=\"38\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e8f0ff\" stroke=\"#638dce\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"15\">Aortic root</text><rect x=\"67\" y=\"84\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6f3ff\" stroke=\"#6d96c7\"/><text x=\"172\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Left main coronary</text><rect x=\"405\" y=\"84\" width=\"212\" height=\"43\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6f3ff\" stroke=\"#6d96c7\"/><text x=\"511\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Right coronary</text><rect x=\"16\" y=\"160\" width=\"152\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#e8f6ec\" stroke=\"#70a987\"/><text x=\"92\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">LAD</text><text x=\"92\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"12\">anterior + septal</text><rect x=\"183\" y=\"160\" width=\"150\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#e8f6ec\" stroke=\"#70a987\"/><text x=\"258\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">Circumflex</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"12\">lateral LV</text><rect x=\"404\" y=\"160\" width=\"250\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fff0da\" stroke=\"#c4a16b\"/><text x=\"529\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">PDA origin defines</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"13\">coronary dominance</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"239\" font-size=\"13\">Venous blood: cardiac veins → coronary sinus → right atrium</text></g><g stroke=\"#5278a5\" stroke-width=\"2.6\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M340 47v18H172v19M340 65h171v19M172 127v16H92v17M172 143h86v17M511 127v33M333 183h71\"/></g></svg><p>Conceptual branches, not an anatomical map of coronary vessel positions. Dominance and individual territories vary.</p></figure>\n\n<p>Most venous drainage passes through <strong>cardiac veins → coronary sinus → right atrium</strong>. Some anterior cardiac veins and smallest cardiac veins drain directly into heart chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole\">3. Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle — qualitative waveform</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 650 235\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Illustrative left coronary flow falls during early systolic contraction, remains relatively low in systole and rises sharply in early diastole before gradually declining\"><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#667b93\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M62 24v172h560\"/></g><rect x=\"88\" y=\"28\" width=\"240\" height=\"164\" fill=\"#fff0e6\"/><rect x=\"328\" y=\"28\" width=\"275\" height=\"164\" fill=\"#e9f5ff\"/><path d=\"M88 88C112 93 117 159 150 162S255 133 328 143C347 145 351 32 380 39C432 38 472 75 509 94S571 109 603 121\" stroke=\"#1d72c8\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"#293d58\" font-size=\"14\"><text x=\"140\" y=\"47\">Systole</text><text x=\"427\" y=\"47\">Diastole</text><text x=\"13\" y=\"19\">Flow</text><text x=\"257\" y=\"220\">Time →</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"211\">S1</text><text x=\"315\" y=\"211\">S2</text></g></svg><p>Conceptual shape, not measured patient data: left-ventricular compression restricts flow during systole; relaxation allows a marked early-diastolic increase. Right coronary flow may be less strongly diastolic.</p></figure>\n\n<p>A vessel's flow depends on <strong>driving pressure and resistance</strong>. During <strong>left ventricular systole</strong>, strong myocardial contraction compresses vessels inside the muscular wall, especially near the endocardium. This limits forward flow and can transiently reverse flow in some intramural vessels.</p>\n<p>During <strong>diastole</strong>, ventricular muscle relaxes, extravascular compression falls and the aortic root still provides pressure to drive blood through coronary arteries. Consequently, <strong>left ventricular coronary perfusion is predominantly diastolic</strong>, although some coronary flow occurs in systole and the right ventricle can have a different pattern.</p>\n<p>A useful simplified estimate of <strong>left ventricular diastolic coronary driving pressure</strong> is:</p>\n<p><strong>Aortic diastolic pressure − left ventricular end-diastolic pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>This is a driving-pressure approximation, not a complete calculation of coronary blood flow, because vascular resistance, vessel compression and timing also matter.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 2. Myocardial compression explains diastolic left-coronary flow</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"During systole intramyocardial vessels are compressed; during diastole their compression is relieved and left ventricular coronary flow increases\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"20\" width=\"270\" height=\"112\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fce9ec\" stroke=\"#bf6777\"/><rect x=\"338\" y=\"20\" width=\"270\" height=\"112\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f3ff\" stroke=\"#668dbf\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24364d\"><text x=\"147\" y=\"50\">Systole</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"50\">Diastole</text></g><g font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24364d\"><text x=\"147\" y=\"77\">Muscle squeezes vessels</text><text x=\"147\" y=\"102\">LV flow is restricted</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"77\">Muscle relaxes</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"102\">LV flow increases</text></g><path d=\"M289 78h40m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7\" stroke=\"#456d95\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></svg><p>Qualitative comparison; the amount of systolic flow depends on vessel depth, ventricular pressures and the territory being supplied.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>Why is the subendocardium especially vulnerable?</h3>\n<p>The <strong>subendocardium</strong> is the inner portion of ventricular muscle. It experiences strong intramural compression and is relatively vulnerable when perfusion pressure falls, ventricular filling pressure rises or diastole becomes very short.</p>\n<p>Consider <strong>aortic stenosis</strong>: the left ventricle may need to generate much higher pressure to eject blood. Higher wall stress increases oxygen demand, while elevated ventricular pressures and impaired diastolic perfusion can narrow the oxygen supply margin.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-does-coronary-flow-match-changing-myocardial-work\">4. How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work?</h2>\n<p>Three major contributors increase myocardial oxygen demand: <strong>heart rate</strong>, <strong>contractility</strong> and <strong>ventricular wall stress</strong>. Coronary resistance arterioles respond to local metabolic conditions. Signals involving <strong>adenosine</strong>, potassium, oxygen tension and endothelial <strong>nitric oxide (NO)</strong> contribute to vasodilatation; no single metabolite explains all normal metabolic regulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Active hyperaemia</strong> means coronary flow increases as myocardial work increases. During exercise, the heart beats faster and contracts more strongly; coronary vessels usually dilate enough to raise oxygen delivery.</p>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation has two interacting effects. Direct <strong>α-mediated coronary vasoconstriction</strong> can occur, but cardiac <strong>β₁ stimulation increases rate and force</strong>, raising myocardial metabolism and generally promoting <strong>secondary metabolic vasodilatation</strong>. Therefore, it is misleading to say that β₁-generated metabolic products ordinarily constrict coronary vessels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia\">5. Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia?</h2>\n<p>Follow both sides of the balance:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Faster heart rate and stronger work tend to <strong>raise myocardial oxygen demand</strong>.</li>\n<li>Faster heart rate shortens the cardiac cycle, with <strong>disproportionate shortening of diastole</strong> at high rates.</li>\n<li>The time during which the left ventricular muscle is relaxed and most readily perfused becomes shorter.</li>\n<li>If coronary blood flow cannot compensate, <strong>oxygen supply becomes inadequate relative to demand</strong>, causing ischaemia.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A fast heart rate is not automatically ischaemic: healthy coronary vessels can usually increase flow. The risk is higher with severe tachycardia, significant stenosis, low aortic diastolic pressure or elevated left ventricular filling pressure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-coronary-flow-reserve-angina-and-myocardial-infarction\">6. Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png\" alt=\"Medical comparison of normal coronary artery flow and an artery narrowed by atherosclerotic plaque.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Normal coronary artery versus a narrowed vessel.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronary_Artery_Disease.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Coronary flow reserve</strong> describes the capacity to increase coronary blood flow above its resting level when resistance vessels dilate. With a significant coronary narrowing, vessels downstream may already be dilated to maintain resting flow, leaving less capacity to meet demand during exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>Angina</strong> is a symptom of myocardial ischaemia; reversible ischaemia does not necessarily mean myocytes have died. <strong>Myocardial infarction</strong> involves acute myocardial injury with necrosis from prolonged severe ischaemia. Plaque rupture and thrombosis are important causes, but coronary spasm and other mechanisms can also impair supply.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning application:</strong> A patient with severe coronary stenosis can feel comfortable at rest but develop chest discomfort on climbing stairs. Their resting supply may suffice; when the heart works harder, restricted <strong>flow reserve</strong> prevents oxygen delivery from keeping pace.</p>\n<h2 id=\"comprehensive-understanding-checks\">Comprehensive Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Where do coronary arteries originate?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The right and left coronary arteries originate from the aortic sinuses just above the aortic valve. The aortic root therefore supplies blood to myocardium as well as the systemic outflow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. What determines coronary dominance?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The posterior descending artery's origin defines dominance: most often RCA in right dominance, or circumflex in left dominance. It does not describe the overall size of the arteries.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Why is left ventricular coronary flow greatest in diastole?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Left ventricular contraction squeezes intramyocardial vessels, especially near the subendocardium. In diastole compression decreases while aortic pressure remains available to drive flow. Flow is predominantly, not exclusively, diastolic.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why is the subendocardium vulnerable to ischaemia?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The subendocardium lies closest to the high-pressure ventricular cavity. During systole the surrounding contracting muscle compresses its smaller vessels strongly, so it relies especially on diastolic perfusion and is vulnerable when perfusion pressure falls, demand rises or diastole shortens.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. What is a useful approximation of left coronary driving pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Aortic diastolic pressure minus left ventricular diastolic pressure is a useful approximation: higher aortic driving pressure supports perfusion, while elevated ventricular filling pressure reduces the pressure gradient. Actual flow also depends on vascular resistance and contraction.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. Why can tachycardia produce an oxygen supply-demand mismatch?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Tachycardia increases oxygen demand and disproportionately reduces diastolic duration at high rates. If coronary vasodilatation and flow cannot compensate, the myocardium develops an oxygen supply–demand mismatch.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why must coronary flow increase during exercise?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The working myocardium extracts a large proportion of oxygen from coronary blood even at rest. It therefore has relatively limited room to increase extraction during exercise, so additional oxygen demand must chiefly be met by coronary arteriolar dilation and increased blood flow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. What is coronary active hyperaemia?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Active hyperaemia is the increase in flow when the heart works harder. Coronary resistance vessels dilate in response to metabolic and endothelial signals, supplying more oxygen when resting oxygen extraction is already high.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. How can adenosine affect coronary resistance?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Adenosine is one of several local vasodilator signals linked to increased metabolic activity. By helping relax coronary resistance vessels, it can increase blood supply; it is not the only mechanism controlling flow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why does a fixed stenosis cause exertional angina before resting symptoms?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Downstream resistance vessels dilate to offset the pressure drop across a narrowed coronary artery. This may maintain near-normal flow at rest but uses up vasodilator reserve; when exercise raises myocardial oxygen demand, flow cannot increase adequately and ischaemia can appear.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. What is the usual indirect effect of cardiac beta-1 stimulation on coronary flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Cardiac β₁ stimulation raises heart rate and contraction strength, which raises oxygen demand. Local metabolic vasodilatation normally increases coronary blood flow in response, even though direct sympathetic α-mediated constriction can also occur.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. How does infarction differ from reversible angina?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Angina indicates myocardial ischaemia and can be reversible if oxygen delivery is restored. Infarction means prolonged severe ischaemia has caused cardiac muscle injury and cell death; pain alone cannot reliably distinguish them.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses.</li>\n<li>Left coronary flow is mainly diastolic.</li>\n<li>Myocardial oxygen extraction is already high at rest.</li>\n<li>Increased work drives metabolic vasodilation.</li>\n<li>Fixed stenosis may exhaust flow reserve before reducing resting flow.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Coronary Circulation 1. Why does the heart need a separate blood supply? Blood inside the chambers does not adequately nourish the thick ventricular myocardium. Right and left coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses, just above the aortic valve, and supply oxygen and nutrients to cardiac muscle. The heart works continuously and consumes considerable oxygen. Its resting oxygen extraction is already high, so when cardiac work increases, it cannot simply extract much more oxygen from unchanged blood flow. Instead, coronary blood flow generally needs to rise. Oxygen delivery = coronary blood flow × arterial oxygen content. A decrease in either can compromise supply. 2. Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage Figure. Anterior heart and ventricular coronary vessels. Image credit Blausen Medical Communications, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Figure. Coronary arteries in their anatomical locations. Image credit Patrick J. Lynch; derivative and labels by Fred the Oyster and Mikael Häggström, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. The left main coronary artery usually divides into the left anterior descending (LAD, or anterior interventricular artery) and left circumflex artery (LCx). The LAD supplies much of the anterior ventricular wall and interventricular septum; the LCx supplies much of the lateral left ventricular wall. The right coronary artery (RCA) commonly supplies the right heart and portions of the inferior heart, but patterns vary. Coronary dominance is defined by the artery that gives rise to the posterior descending artery (PDA/posterior interventricular artery). In right-dominant circulation, the PDA arises from the RCA; in left dominance, it typically arises from the circumflex. This naming does not mean one whole side of the heart is supplied by only one artery. Figure 1. Major coronary routes and drainage — simplified flow map Aortic root Left main coronary Right coronary LAD anterior + septal Circumflex lateral LV PDA origin defines coronary dominance Venous blood: cardiac veins → coronary sinus → right atrium Conceptual branches, not an anatomical map of coronary vessel positions. Dominance and individual territories vary. Most venous drainage passes through cardiac veins → coronary sinus → right atrium. Some anterior cardiac veins and smallest cardiac veins drain directly into heart chambers. 3. Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole? Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle — qualitative waveform Systole Diastole Flow Time → S1 S2 Conceptual shape, not measured patient data: left-ventricular compression restricts flow during systole; relaxation allows a marked early-diastolic increase. Right coronary flow may be less strongly diastolic. A vessel's flow depends on driving pressure and resistance. During left ventricular systole, strong myocardial contraction compresses vessels inside the muscular wall, especially near the endocardium. This limits forward flow and can transiently reverse flow in some intramural vessels. During diastole, ventricular muscle relaxes, extravascular compression falls and the aortic root still provides pressure to drive blood through coronary arteries. Consequently, left ventricular coronary perfusion is predominantly diastolic, although some coronary flow occurs in systole and the right ventricle can have a different pattern. A useful simplified estimate of left ventricular diastolic coronary driving pressure is: Aortic diastolic pressure − left ventricular end-diastolic pressure. This is a driving-pressure approximation, not a complete calculation of coronary blood flow, because vascular resistance, vessel compression and timing also matter. Figure 2. Myocardial compression explains diastolic left-coronary flow Systole Diastole Muscle squeezes vessels LV flow is restricted Muscle relaxes LV flow increases Qualitative comparison; the amount of systolic flow depends on vessel depth, ventricular pressures and the territory being supplied. Why is the subendocardium especially vulnerable? The subendocardium is the inner portion of ventricular muscle. It experiences strong intramural compression and is relatively vulnerable when perfusion pressure falls, ventricular filling pressure rises or diastole becomes very short. Consider aortic stenosis: the left ventricle may need to generate much higher pressure to eject blood. Higher wall stress increases oxygen demand, while elevated ventricular pressures and impaired diastolic perfusion can narrow the oxygen supply margin. 4. How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work? Three major contributors increase myocardial oxygen demand: heart rate, contractility and ventricular wall stress. Coronary resistance arterioles respond to local metabolic conditions. Signals involving adenosine, potassium, oxygen tension and endothelial nitric oxide (NO) contribute to vasodilatation; no single metabolite explains all normal metabolic regulation. Active hyperaemia means coronary flow increases as myocardial work increases. During exercise, the heart beats faster and contracts more strongly; coronary vessels usually dilate enough to raise oxygen delivery. Sympathetic stimulation has two interacting effects. Direct α-mediated coronary vasoconstriction can occur, but cardiac β₁ stimulation increases rate and force, raising myocardial metabolism and generally promoting secondary metabolic vasodilatation. Therefore, it is misleading to say that β₁-generated metabolic products ordinarily constrict coronary vessels. 5. Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia? Follow both sides of the balance: Faster heart rate and stronger work tend to raise myocardial oxygen demand. Faster heart rate shortens the cardiac cycle, with disproportionate shortening of diastole at high rates. The time during which the left ventricular muscle is relaxed and most readily perfused becomes shorter. If coronary blood flow cannot compensate, oxygen supply becomes inadequate relative to demand, causing ischaemia. A fast heart rate is not automatically ischaemic: healthy coronary vessels can usually increase flow. The risk is higher with severe tachycardia, significant stenosis, low aortic diastolic pressure or elevated left ventricular filling pressure. 6. Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction Figure. Normal coronary artery versus a narrowed vessel. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Coronary flow reserve describes the capacity to increase coronary blood flow above its resting level when resistance vessels dilate. With a significant coronary narrowing, vessels downstream may already be dilated to maintain resting flow, leaving less capacity to meet demand during exercise. Angina is a symptom of myocardial ischaemia; reversible ischaemia does not necessarily mean myocytes have died. Myocardial infarction involves acute myocardial injury with necrosis from prolonged severe ischaemia. Plaque rupture and thrombosis are important causes, but coronary spasm and other mechanisms can also impair supply. Reasoning application: A patient with severe coronary stenosis can feel comfortable at rest but develop chest discomfort on climbing stairs. Their resting supply may suffice; when the heart works harder, restricted flow reserve prevents oxygen delivery from keeping pace. Comprehensive Understanding Checks 01. Where do coronary arteries originate? Show explanation The right and left coronary arteries originate from the aortic sinuses just above the aortic valve. The aortic root therefore supplies blood to myocardium as well as the systemic outflow. 02. What determines coronary dominance? Show explanation The posterior descending artery's origin defines dominance: most often RCA in right dominance, or circumflex in left dominance. It does not describe the overall size of the arteries. 03. Why is left ventricular coronary flow greatest in diastole? Show explanation Left ventricular contraction squeezes intramyocardial vessels, especially near the subendocardium. In diastole compression decreases while aortic pressure remains available to drive flow. Flow is predominantly, not exclusively, diastolic. 04. Why is the subendocardium vulnerable to ischaemia? Show explanation The subendocardium lies closest to the high-pressure ventricular cavity. During systole the surrounding contracting muscle compresses its smaller vessels strongly, so it relies especially on diastolic perfusion and is vulnerable when perfusion pressure falls, demand rises or diastole shortens. 05. What is a useful approximation of left coronary driving pressure? Show explanation Aortic diastolic pressure minus left ventricular diastolic pressure is a useful approximation: higher aortic driving pressure supports perfusion, while elevated ventricular filling pressure reduces the pressure gradient. Actual flow also depends on vascular resistance and contraction. 06. Why can tachycardia produce an oxygen supply-demand mismatch? Show explanation Tachycardia increases oxygen demand and disproportionately reduces diastolic duration at high rates. If coronary vasodilatation and flow cannot compensate, the myocardium develops an oxygen supply–demand mismatch. 07. Why must coronary flow increase during exercise? Show explanation The working myocardium extracts a large proportion of oxygen from coronary blood even at rest. It therefore has relatively limited room to increase extraction during exercise, so additional oxygen demand must chiefly be met by coronary arteriolar dilation and increased blood flow. 08. What is coronary active hyperaemia? Show explanation Active hyperaemia is the increase in flow when the heart works harder. Coronary resistance vessels dilate in response to metabolic and endothelial signals, supplying more oxygen when resting oxygen extraction is already high. 09. How can adenosine affect coronary resistance? Show explanation Adenosine is one of several local vasodilator signals linked to increased metabolic activity. By helping relax coronary resistance vessels, it can increase blood supply; it is not the only mechanism controlling flow. 10. Why does a fixed stenosis cause exertional angina before resting symptoms? Show explanation Downstream resistance vessels dilate to offset the pressure drop across a narrowed coronary artery. This may maintain near-normal flow at rest but uses up vasodilator reserve; when exercise raises myocardial oxygen demand, flow cannot increase adequately and ischaemia can appear. 11. What is the usual indirect effect of cardiac beta-1 stimulation on coronary flow? Show explanation Cardiac β₁ stimulation raises heart rate and contraction strength, which raises oxygen demand. Local metabolic vasodilatation normally increases coronary blood flow in response, even though direct sympathetic α-mediated constriction can also occur. 12. How does infarction differ from reversible angina? Show explanation Angina indicates myocardial ischaemia and can be reversible if oxygen delivery is restored. Infarction means prolonged severe ischaemia has caused cardiac muscle injury and cell death; pain alone cannot reliably distinguish them. Chapter Essentials Coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses. Left coronary flow is mainly diastolic. Myocardial oxygen extraction is already high at rest. Increased work drives metabolic vasodilation. Fixed stenosis may exhaust flow reserve before reducing resting flow."},{"slug":"pulmonary","title":"Pulmonary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"30-32","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"one-flow-different-pressures","title":"One flow, different pressures"},{"id":"gravity-and-distribution","title":"Gravity and distribution"},{"id":"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen","title":"The unusual response to low oxygen"},{"id":"oedema-prevention","title":"Oedema prevention"},{"id":"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits","title":"Pulmonary vs systemic circuits"},{"id":"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment","title":"Gravity and vascular recruitment"},{"id":"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations","title":"Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations"},{"id":"work-through-the-pulmonary-pressure-and-flow-mechanisms","title":"Work through the pulmonary pressure and flow mechanisms"},{"id":"comprehensive-understanding-checks","title":"Comprehensive Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Pulmonary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"one-flow-different-pressures\">One flow, different pressures</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg\" alt=\"Anatomical illustration of the pulmonary circuit showing right ventricular outflow to pulmonary arteries, gas exchange in lungs and pulmonary venous return to the left atrium\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Pulmonary circuit anatomy.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has <strong>far lower vascular resistance and pressure</strong>. Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The <strong>bronchial circulation</strong> from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-distribution\">Gravity and distribution</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>How upright lung perfusion changes from apex to base</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 630 240\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Upright lung outline with apex less perfused than base and conceptual zone 2 and zone 3 relationships, with zone 1 being a possible not normal resting zone\"><path d=\"M205 25C135 35 85 96 91 168C95 202 151 212 230 214V68Z\" fill=\"#e1f2fb\" stroke=\"#5e93ad\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M256 68V214C336 212 392 202 396 168C403 96 352 35 281 25Z\" fill=\"#e1f2fb\" stroke=\"#5e93ad\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><g stroke=\"#7d9cae\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\"><path d=\"M83 100h320M83 159h320\"/></g><g fill=\"#273d5c\" font-size=\"14\"><text x=\"423\" y=\"65\">Apex: less perfusion</text><text x=\"423\" y=\"119\">Zone 2 pattern</text><text x=\"423\" y=\"179\">Zone 3 pattern</text><text x=\"423\" y=\"216\">Base: greater perfusion</text></g><g fill=\"#28679e\"><path d=\"M120 50v116m-7 -12 7 12 7 -12\"/></g></svg><p>Illustrative upright distribution: blood flow generally increases toward dependent bases. Zone 1 (alveolar pressure exceeding arterial pressure) may develop at the apex in low-pressure or positive-pressure ventilation states, but is generally absent in healthy resting lungs. Zone positions shift with conditions.</p></figure>\n\n<p>In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with <strong>West lung zones</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Functional West zones of upright lung perfusion</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 176\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three functional pressure regimes: zone one alveolar pressure exceeds arterial; zone two arterial exceeds alveolar exceeds venous; zone three arterial and venous exceed alveolar\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23384f\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"22\" width=\"195\" height=\"125\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff0e2\" stroke=\"#d99c51\"/><rect x=\"222\" y=\"22\" width=\"195\" height=\"125\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#edf2ff\" stroke=\"#718dc9\"/><rect x=\"436\" y=\"22\" width=\"195\" height=\"125\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e5f4eb\" stroke=\"#65a48d\"/><g font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"105\" y=\"50\">Zone 1</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"50\">Zone 2</text><text x=\"533\" y=\"50\">Zone 3</text></g><g font-size=\"17\"><text x=\"105\" y=\"83\">PA &gt; Pa &gt; Pv</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"83\">Pa &gt; PA &gt; Pv</text><text x=\"533\" y=\"83\">Pa &gt; Pv &gt; PA</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"105\" y=\"113\">Flow absent / minimal</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"113\">Waterfall flow</text><text x=\"533\" y=\"113\">Continuous flow</text></g></g></svg><p>Original conceptual comparison. PA = alveolar pressure, Pa = pulmonary arterial pressure, Pv = pulmonary venous pressure. Actual zones vary with posture and physiological conditions; zone 1 is ordinarily absent or minimal in healthy resting lungs.</p></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen\">The unusual response to low oxygen</h2>\n<p>Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but <strong>small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂</strong>. This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oedema-prevention\">Oedema prevention</h2>\n<p>Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema.</p>\n<p><strong>Local versus widespread hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction:</strong> local constriction redistributes blood to better ventilated alveoli; diffuse constriction increases overall pulmonary vascular resistance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits\">Pulmonary vs systemic circuits</h2>\n<p>Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations: <strong>pulmonary vessels</strong> for gas exchange and <strong>bronchial arteries</strong> (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment\">Gravity and vascular recruitment</h2>\n<p>In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic <strong>West lung zones</strong> compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations\">Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations</h2>\n<p>When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict (<strong>hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong>) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical <strong>systemic local hypoxic dilation</strong> that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region.</p>\n<h2 id=\"work-through-the-pulmonary-pressure-and-flow-mechanisms\">Work through the pulmonary pressure and flow mechanisms</h2>\n<h3>Why can the lungs handle full cardiac output at low pressure?</h3>\n<p>The pulmonary and systemic circuits are arranged <strong>in series</strong>, so their flow rates are nearly equal over time. Yet the pulmonary circuit has a much lower resistance because its vessels are relatively short, compliant and arranged in an extensive parallel network. As cardiac output rises during exercise, previously underperfused capillaries <strong>recruit</strong> and already open vessels <strong>distend</strong>. Both effects limit the rise in pulmonary vascular resistance. A rise in flow therefore need not produce an equally large rise in pulmonary arterial pressure.</p>\n<h3>West zones: use the pressures, not just the names</h3>\n<p>Compare <strong>alveolar pressure (P_A)</strong> with pulmonary arterial pressure (P_a) and venous pressure (P_v):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zone 1 (P_A &gt; P_a &gt; P_v):</strong> alveolar pressure can compress capillaries so flow is absent or very low. It is usually absent in healthy resting lungs but can develop with low vascular pressure or high alveolar pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Zone 2 (P_a &gt; P_A &gt; P_v):</strong> flow depends more on the arterial-to-alveolar pressure difference, sometimes called the <em>waterfall</em> relationship.</li>\n<li><strong>Zone 3 (P_a &gt; P_v &gt; P_A):</strong> vessels remain distended and flow depends predominantly on arterial-to-venous pressure difference.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With gravity, vascular pressures usually increase toward the lung bases, which is one reason perfusion generally rises from apex to base in an upright person. <strong>West zones are pressure relationships</strong>, not fixed anatomical stripes.</p>\n<h3>Link perfusion to ventilation</h3>\n<p>Air does not necessarily reach every alveolus in the same proportion as blood. The <strong>ventilation–perfusion ratio (V/Q)</strong> compares air delivery to blood delivery. Both ventilation and perfusion generally increase toward the dependent lung, but perfusion usually increases more steeply, making V/Q relatively higher toward the apex. An alveolus receiving too little ventilation relative to blood has low alveolar oxygen. Nearby pulmonary arterioles then constrict, diverting some flow toward better-ventilated alveoli.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical reasoning:</strong> If only one small region is hypoxic, redistribution can improve matching. If nearly every alveolus is hypoxic, there is nowhere well-ventilated to redirect flow; widespread constriction instead raises pulmonary resistance, loading the right ventricle.</p>\n<h3>Pulmonary oedema: where the fluid goes</h3>\n<p>Left ventricular dysfunction can raise left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure. This increases pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging excess filtration into lung interstitium. Once barrier and lymphatic clearance are overwhelmed, fluid may enter alveoli and impair gas exchange. This is different from the <strong>normal</strong> modest filtration controlled by the pulmonary capillary barrier.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding checkpoint:</strong> Why can exercise raise pulmonary flow without a proportionate pressure increase? <em>Recruitment and distension create more effective pathways for flow and lower resistance.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"comprehensive-understanding-checks\">Comprehensive Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. How does right ventricular minute output compare with left ventricular output?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>In a closed circulation at steady state, whatever volume the right ventricle sends through the lungs must eventually return to the left heart and enter systemic circulation. Therefore average right and left ventricular outputs are nearly the same, despite the large difference in pressures and resistance.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Why can pulmonary flow increase without a large pressure rise?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>As flow rises, previously less-perfused capillaries open (recruitment) and already open vessels widen (distension). These changes create more effective parallel pathways and limit the rise in pulmonary pressure needed to carry greater cardiac output.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. What does the bronchial arterial circulation supply?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The bronchial arteries arise from systemic circulation and nourish airways and supporting tissues. Pulmonary arteries, in contrast, carry the major blood supply for alveolar gas exchange; a portion of bronchial venous blood reaches pulmonary veins.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why does gravity change regional pulmonary flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Gravity increases intravascular hydrostatic pressure toward the dependent lung while alveolar gas pressure is comparatively similar across its height. This changes which vessels are open and their distension, generally producing more perfusion at the bases in an upright healthy person.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. What characterizes West zone 1?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Zone 1 means alveolar pressure is higher than local arterial and venous pressures. Alveolar capillaries can then be compressed, so perfusion becomes very low or absent. It is generally absent in healthy resting lungs but can emerge with low blood pressure or positive-pressure ventilation.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. What characterizes West zone 2?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>In Zone 2, local pulmonary arterial pressure exceeds alveolar pressure, but alveolar pressure exceeds venous pressure. Flow is therefore influenced mainly by the arterial-to-alveolar pressure difference rather than the full arterial-to-venous gradient.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. What characterizes West zone 3?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>In Zone 3, both arterial and venous pressures exceed alveolar pressure, keeping capillaries relatively open. Flow is then governed predominantly by the arterial-to-venous pressure gradient and vessel resistance.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. Why does low alveolar oxygen constrict pulmonary arterioles?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Low oxygen in an alveolus triggers constriction of nearby small pulmonary arteries. Less blood then reaches the poorly ventilated region, helping match perfusion to the alveoli where oxygen transfer is more effective.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why can widespread hypoxia cause pulmonary hypertension?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>If most alveoli are hypoxic, many pulmonary resistance vessels constrict at once. Rather than simply redistributing flow, this raises total pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure, increasing the workload on the right ventricle.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. How is pulmonary oedema limited under ordinary conditions?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Normally, low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure limits excess filtration. Endothelial and epithelial barriers limit leakage, while lymphatics remove filtered interstitial fluid and alveolar epithelium helps clear alveolar fluid. Oedema develops when these safeguards are overwhelmed or disrupted.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why can left-heart failure cause pulmonary oedema?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Left-heart failure can raise left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, increasing pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure. Excess filtration first accumulates in interstitial tissue; if clearance is overwhelmed it can enter alveoli and impair gas exchange.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why might lying flat worsen orthopnoea?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Recumbency increases central and pulmonary blood volume, aggravating congestion in a failing left heart.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Right and left outputs are equal over time.</li>\n<li>Low pulmonary resistance reflects recruitment and distension.</li>\n<li>Gravity changes regional perfusion and West-zone relationships.</li>\n<li>Low alveolar oxygen causes local pulmonary vasoconstriction.</li>\n<li>Diffuse hypoxia and high venous pressure can impair lung function.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Pulmonary Circulation One flow, different pressures Figure. Pulmonary circuit anatomy. Image credit OpenStax College, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has far lower vascular resistance and pressure. Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The bronchial circulation from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation. Gravity and distribution How upright lung perfusion changes from apex to base Apex: less perfusion Zone 2 pattern Zone 3 pattern Base: greater perfusion Illustrative upright distribution: blood flow generally increases toward dependent bases. Zone 1 (alveolar pressure exceeding arterial pressure) may develop at the apex in low-pressure or positive-pressure ventilation states, but is generally absent in healthy resting lungs. Zone positions shift with conditions. In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with West lung zones. Figure. Functional West zones of upright lung perfusion Zone 1 Zone 2 Zone 3 PA > Pa > Pv Pa > PA > Pv Pa > Pv > PA Flow absent / minimal Waterfall flow Continuous flow Original conceptual comparison. PA = alveolar pressure, Pa = pulmonary arterial pressure, Pv = pulmonary venous pressure. Actual zones vary with posture and physiological conditions; zone 1 is ordinarily absent or minimal in healthy resting lungs. The unusual response to low oxygen Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂. This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension. Oedema prevention Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema. Local versus widespread hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: local constriction redistributes blood to better ventilated alveoli; diffuse constriction increases overall pulmonary vascular resistance. Pulmonary vs systemic circuits Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations: pulmonary vessels for gas exchange and bronchial arteries (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture. Gravity and vascular recruitment In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic West lung zones compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail. Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict ( hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical systemic local hypoxic dilation that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain. Practice: Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region. Work through the pulmonary pressure and flow mechanisms Why can the lungs handle full cardiac output at low pressure? The pulmonary and systemic circuits are arranged in series, so their flow rates are nearly equal over time. Yet the pulmonary circuit has a much lower resistance because its vessels are relatively short, compliant and arranged in an extensive parallel network. As cardiac output rises during exercise, previously underperfused capillaries recruit and already open vessels distend. Both effects limit the rise in pulmonary vascular resistance. A rise in flow therefore need not produce an equally large rise in pulmonary arterial pressure. West zones: use the pressures, not just the names Compare alveolar pressure (P_A) with pulmonary arterial pressure (P_a) and venous pressure (P_v): Zone 1 (P_A > P_a > P_v): alveolar pressure can compress capillaries so flow is absent or very low. It is usually absent in healthy resting lungs but can develop with low vascular pressure or high alveolar pressure. Zone 2 (P_a > P_A > P_v): flow depends more on the arterial-to-alveolar pressure difference, sometimes called the waterfall relationship. Zone 3 (P_a > P_v > P_A): vessels remain distended and flow depends predominantly on arterial-to-venous pressure difference. With gravity, vascular pressures usually increase toward the lung bases, which is one reason perfusion generally rises from apex to base in an upright person. West zones are pressure relationships, not fixed anatomical stripes. Link perfusion to ventilation Air does not necessarily reach every alveolus in the same proportion as blood. The ventilation–perfusion ratio (V/Q) compares air delivery to blood delivery. Both ventilation and perfusion generally increase toward the dependent lung, but perfusion usually increases more steeply, making V/Q relatively higher toward the apex. An alveolus receiving too little ventilation relative to blood has low alveolar oxygen. Nearby pulmonary arterioles then constrict, diverting some flow toward better-ventilated alveoli. Clinical reasoning: If only one small region is hypoxic, redistribution can improve matching. If nearly every alveolus is hypoxic, there is nowhere well-ventilated to redirect flow; widespread constriction instead raises pulmonary resistance, loading the right ventricle. Pulmonary oedema: where the fluid goes Left ventricular dysfunction can raise left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure. This increases pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging excess filtration into lung interstitium. Once barrier and lymphatic clearance are overwhelmed, fluid may enter alveoli and impair gas exchange. This is different from the normal modest filtration controlled by the pulmonary capillary barrier. Understanding checkpoint: Why can exercise raise pulmonary flow without a proportionate pressure increase? Recruitment and distension create more effective pathways for flow and lower resistance. Comprehensive Understanding Checks 01. How does right ventricular minute output compare with left ventricular output? Show explanation In a closed circulation at steady state, whatever volume the right ventricle sends through the lungs must eventually return to the left heart and enter systemic circulation. Therefore average right and left ventricular outputs are nearly the same, despite the large difference in pressures and resistance. 02. Why can pulmonary flow increase without a large pressure rise? Show explanation As flow rises, previously less-perfused capillaries open (recruitment) and already open vessels widen (distension). These changes create more effective parallel pathways and limit the rise in pulmonary pressure needed to carry greater cardiac output. 03. What does the bronchial arterial circulation supply? Show explanation The bronchial arteries arise from systemic circulation and nourish airways and supporting tissues. Pulmonary arteries, in contrast, carry the major blood supply for alveolar gas exchange; a portion of bronchial venous blood reaches pulmonary veins. 04. Why does gravity change regional pulmonary flow? Show explanation Gravity increases intravascular hydrostatic pressure toward the dependent lung while alveolar gas pressure is comparatively similar across its height. This changes which vessels are open and their distension, generally producing more perfusion at the bases in an upright healthy person. 05. What characterizes West zone 1? Show explanation Zone 1 means alveolar pressure is higher than local arterial and venous pressures. Alveolar capillaries can then be compressed, so perfusion becomes very low or absent. It is generally absent in healthy resting lungs but can emerge with low blood pressure or positive-pressure ventilation. 06. What characterizes West zone 2? Show explanation In Zone 2, local pulmonary arterial pressure exceeds alveolar pressure, but alveolar pressure exceeds venous pressure. Flow is therefore influenced mainly by the arterial-to-alveolar pressure difference rather than the full arterial-to-venous gradient. 07. What characterizes West zone 3? Show explanation In Zone 3, both arterial and venous pressures exceed alveolar pressure, keeping capillaries relatively open. Flow is then governed predominantly by the arterial-to-venous pressure gradient and vessel resistance. 08. Why does low alveolar oxygen constrict pulmonary arterioles? Show explanation Low oxygen in an alveolus triggers constriction of nearby small pulmonary arteries. Less blood then reaches the poorly ventilated region, helping match perfusion to the alveoli where oxygen transfer is more effective. 09. Why can widespread hypoxia cause pulmonary hypertension? Show explanation If most alveoli are hypoxic, many pulmonary resistance vessels constrict at once. Rather than simply redistributing flow, this raises total pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure, increasing the workload on the right ventricle. 10. How is pulmonary oedema limited under ordinary conditions? Show explanation Normally, low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure limits excess filtration. Endothelial and epithelial barriers limit leakage, while lymphatics remove filtered interstitial fluid and alveolar epithelium helps clear alveolar fluid. Oedema develops when these safeguards are overwhelmed or disrupted. 11. Why can left-heart failure cause pulmonary oedema? Show explanation Left-heart failure can raise left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, increasing pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure. Excess filtration first accumulates in interstitial tissue; if clearance is overwhelmed it can enter alveoli and impair gas exchange. 12. Why might lying flat worsen orthopnoea? Show explanation Recumbency increases central and pulmonary blood volume, aggravating congestion in a failing left heart. Chapter Essentials Right and left outputs are equal over time. Low pulmonary resistance reflects recruitment and distension. Gravity changes regional perfusion and West-zone relationships. Low alveolar oxygen causes local pulmonary vasoconstriction. Diffuse hypoxia and high venous pressure can impair lung function."},{"slug":"splanchnic","title":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"33-37","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks","title":"An unusual route through two capillary networks"},{"id":"hepatic-microcirculation","title":"Hepatic microcirculation"},{"id":"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock","title":"Blood flow after meals and in shock"},{"id":"ascites","title":"Ascites"},{"id":"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route","title":"Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route"},{"id":"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage","title":"Digestion, shock and venous storage"},{"id":"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story","title":"Explain ascites without one misleading protein story"},{"id":"follow-the-circulation-from-gut-to-liver","title":"Follow the circulation from gut to liver"},{"id":"comprehensive-understanding-checks","title":"Comprehensive Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks\">An unusual route through two capillary networks</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portal_venous_system-en.svg\" alt=\"Labelled anatomical illustration of portal venous return from abdominal digestive organs to the liver\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Portal venous system.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portal_venous_system-en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the <strong>coeliac trunk</strong>, <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong> and <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>. Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the <strong>portal vein</strong> to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hepatic-microcirculation\">Hepatic microcirculation</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_scheme1.jpg\" alt=\"Illustration of hepatic lobule organization, central vein, portal triads and acinar zones with oxygen delivery gradients\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Hepatic lobule and acinar oxygen zonation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">MarquardtM, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liver_scheme1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg\" alt=\"Detailed labelled anatomical illustration of liver vessels, portal tract and hepatic lobule structures\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Liver vascular and lobular anatomy.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Gwilz, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver <strong>sinusoids</strong>, and drains toward <strong>central veins</strong>. Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while <strong>zone 3</strong> hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock\">Blood flow after meals and in shock</h2>\n<p>Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ascites\">Ascites</h2>\n<p>Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours <strong>ascites</strong>, accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why shock can injure the gut:</strong> sympathetic redistribution supports systemic pressure but can reduce mucosal oxygen delivery below demand.</p>\n<h2 id=\"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route\">Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route</h2>\n<p>Arteries from the <strong>coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries</strong> supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through <strong>portal vein and hepatic artery</strong> branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to <strong>central veins → hepatic veins → IVC</strong>. The <strong>portal triad</strong> contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage.</p>\n<p>In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow; <strong>centrilobular (zone 3)</strong> hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This oxygen gradient helps explain why zone 3 is relatively vulnerable during impaired perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage\">Digestion, shock and venous storage</h2>\n<p>Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases <strong>splanchnic flow</strong> (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial <strong>blood reservoir</strong>. During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story\">Explain ascites without one misleading protein story</h2>\n<p><strong>Ascites</strong> is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is <strong>not universal</strong> in all ascites.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen.</p>\n<h2 id=\"follow-the-circulation-from-gut-to-liver\">Follow the circulation from gut to liver</h2>\n<h3>The portal route has a special purpose</h3>\n<p>Blood arrives at the gastrointestinal organs through systemic arterial branches. After passing through intestinal capillaries, much venous drainage enters the <strong>superior mesenteric and splenic veins</strong>, which typically join to form the <strong>portal vein</strong>; the inferior mesenteric vein commonly drains into the splenic vein, though anatomy varies. The portal vein carries absorbed nutrients and gut-derived substances to the liver <strong>before</strong> they enter the wider systemic circulation.</p>\n<p>The liver has <strong>two inflows</strong>: portal venous blood supplies most of its flow, while hepatic arterial blood makes an important oxygen contribution. Both reach sinusoids, then pass toward central veins, hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava. Meanwhile, bile moves toward bile ductules in the portal tracts, generally <strong>opposite</strong> the direction of sinusoidal blood flow.</p>\n<h3>Why is zone 3 vulnerable?</h3>\n<p>Think of a hepatic sinusoid as a pathway through which mixed portal and arterial blood passes. <strong>Zone 1</strong> hepatocytes lie closer to entry and receive relatively oxygen-rich blood first. By the time blood reaches <strong>zone 3</strong>, oxygen has been extracted along the way. A fall in overall delivery therefore threatens downstream zone 3 earlier. This does not mean zone 3 is always damaged, or that every liver injury follows the same pattern.</p>\n<h3>What changes after a meal and during haemorrhage?</h3>\n<p>Digestion increases local metabolic activity, producing <strong>postprandial hyperaemia</strong>: intestinal resistance falls and blood flow rises to support absorption. The splanchnic veins are also highly compliant, so they hold substantial blood volume. During haemorrhage sympathetic activation narrows splanchnic vessels and mobilizes venous blood toward the central circulation. This helps maintain venous return and pressure, but <strong>prolonged loss of gut perfusion</strong> can impair mucosal integrity and cause injury.</p>\n<h3>Why does fluid accumulate as ascites?</h3>\n<p>Follow the chain: increased resistance to portal flow (commonly in cirrhosis) raises <strong>portal/sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure</strong> and hepatic lymph formation. At the same time, altered effective arterial filling promotes kidney <strong>sodium and water retention</strong>. When filtration and lymph formation exceed removal, fluid accumulates in the peritoneal cavity. Lower albumin may contribute but is neither the only nor a necessary explanation. The protein content of ascites varies with its cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding checkpoint:</strong> Why might a patient with portal hypertension have ascites despite adequate drinking? <em>The problem is altered vascular pressures, lymph formation and renal fluid retention, not simply insufficient or excessive water intake.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"comprehensive-understanding-checks\">Comprehensive Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Name the three main unpaired abdominal visceral arteries.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric artery and inferior mesenteric artery arise from the abdominal aorta and supply different portions of the digestive organs. Their venous drainage predominantly joins the portal system before reaching the liver.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Where does most intestinal venous blood travel before reaching the IVC?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Most intestinal venous blood enters the hepatic portal vein, reaches liver sinusoids, then leaves through central veins and hepatic veins to the IVC. This arrangement lets the liver process nutrients and other absorbed substances first.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Why is hepatic zone 3 susceptible to hypoxic injury?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Zone 3 lies downstream toward the central venous outflow. Blood has already supplied oxygen to upstream hepatocytes, so zone 3 has less oxygen reserve and is vulnerable when overall hepatic perfusion falls.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. What structures lie near a portal triad?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>A portal triad region contains branches of a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Blood from the first two enters sinusoids toward central veins, while bile travels in the opposite general direction toward ductules.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Why does gut blood flow increase after eating?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Digestive and absorptive demand promotes local metabolic vasodilation and postprandial hyperaemia.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. How can abdominal veins contribute to blood pressure compensation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The splanchnic venous bed stores a substantial volume of blood. Sympathetic venoconstriction reduces its capacity and shifts blood into central veins, supporting venous return during haemorrhage even while gut blood flow may decrease.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can shock damage intestinal mucosa despite sympathetic compensation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>During major blood loss, the body narrows splanchnic vessels to defend central pressure and perfusion. If intestinal flow falls too far or too long, mucosal oxygen delivery cannot meet demand and tissue injury may follow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. What is the space of Disse?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and the fenestrated endothelium of liver sinusoids. It allows exchange between blood plasma and hepatocytes; it is distinct from the sinusoidal blood lumen itself.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why does portal hypertension promote ascites?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It increases filtration into hepatic interstitium and lymph production, which can overwhelm drainage.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Is all ascitic fluid rich in protein?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Ascitic protein concentration differs by cause. Portal hypertension, hepatic lymph formation, renal sodium retention and plasma protein changes contribute differently across disorders, so protein-rich fluid is not universal.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. How can right-heart failure aggravate ascites?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Right-heart failure can increase central and hepatic venous pressures. Back-pressure in hepatic sinusoids increases filtration and lymph formation; if removal is exceeded, abdominal fluid accumulation can worsen.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why do arterial anastomoses not guarantee gut survival after a major blockage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Anastomoses may offer alternate routes after an arterial blockage, but their diameter and pressure gradient may not deliver enough blood. The affected gut segment can therefore remain ischaemic despite some collateral pathways.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Portal flow passes through hepatic sinusoids before returning to IVC.</li>\n<li>Hepatic zone 3 is relatively vulnerable to low oxygen delivery.</li>\n<li>Digestion increases regional mucosal perfusion.</li>\n<li>Splanchnic veins are an important blood reservoir.</li>\n<li>Portal and hepatic venous pressures affect ascites production.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation An unusual route through two capillary networks Figure. Portal venous system. Image credit Jmarchn, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric artery and inferior mesenteric artery. Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the portal vein to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution. Hepatic microcirculation Figure. Hepatic lobule and acinar oxygen zonation. Image credit MarquardtM, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. Figure. Liver vascular and lobular anatomy. Image credit Gwilz, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver sinusoids, and drains toward central veins. Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while zone 3 hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources. Blood flow after meals and in shock Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia. Ascites Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours ascites, accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed. Why shock can injure the gut: sympathetic redistribution supports systemic pressure but can reduce mucosal oxygen delivery below demand. Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route Arteries from the coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through portal vein and hepatic artery branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to central veins → hepatic veins → IVC. The portal triad contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage. In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow; centrilobular (zone 3) hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This oxygen gradient helps explain why zone 3 is relatively vulnerable during impaired perfusion. Digestion, shock and venous storage Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases splanchnic flow (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial blood reservoir. During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel. Explain ascites without one misleading protein story Ascites is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is not universal in all ascites. Practice: Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen. Follow the circulation from gut to liver The portal route has a special purpose Blood arrives at the gastrointestinal organs through systemic arterial branches. After passing through intestinal capillaries, much venous drainage enters the superior mesenteric and splenic veins, which typically join to form the portal vein; the inferior mesenteric vein commonly drains into the splenic vein, though anatomy varies. The portal vein carries absorbed nutrients and gut-derived substances to the liver before they enter the wider systemic circulation. The liver has two inflows: portal venous blood supplies most of its flow, while hepatic arterial blood makes an important oxygen contribution. Both reach sinusoids, then pass toward central veins, hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava. Meanwhile, bile moves toward bile ductules in the portal tracts, generally opposite the direction of sinusoidal blood flow. Why is zone 3 vulnerable? Think of a hepatic sinusoid as a pathway through which mixed portal and arterial blood passes. Zone 1 hepatocytes lie closer to entry and receive relatively oxygen-rich blood first. By the time blood reaches zone 3, oxygen has been extracted along the way. A fall in overall delivery therefore threatens downstream zone 3 earlier. This does not mean zone 3 is always damaged, or that every liver injury follows the same pattern. What changes after a meal and during haemorrhage? Digestion increases local metabolic activity, producing postprandial hyperaemia: intestinal resistance falls and blood flow rises to support absorption. The splanchnic veins are also highly compliant, so they hold substantial blood volume. During haemorrhage sympathetic activation narrows splanchnic vessels and mobilizes venous blood toward the central circulation. This helps maintain venous return and pressure, but prolonged loss of gut perfusion can impair mucosal integrity and cause injury. Why does fluid accumulate as ascites? Follow the chain: increased resistance to portal flow (commonly in cirrhosis) raises portal/sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation. At the same time, altered effective arterial filling promotes kidney sodium and water retention. When filtration and lymph formation exceed removal, fluid accumulates in the peritoneal cavity. Lower albumin may contribute but is neither the only nor a necessary explanation. The protein content of ascites varies with its cause. Understanding checkpoint: Why might a patient with portal hypertension have ascites despite adequate drinking? The problem is altered vascular pressures, lymph formation and renal fluid retention, not simply insufficient or excessive water intake. Comprehensive Understanding Checks 01. Name the three main unpaired abdominal visceral arteries. Show explanation The coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric artery and inferior mesenteric artery arise from the abdominal aorta and supply different portions of the digestive organs. Their venous drainage predominantly joins the portal system before reaching the liver. 02. Where does most intestinal venous blood travel before reaching the IVC? Show explanation Most intestinal venous blood enters the hepatic portal vein, reaches liver sinusoids, then leaves through central veins and hepatic veins to the IVC. This arrangement lets the liver process nutrients and other absorbed substances first. 03. Why is hepatic zone 3 susceptible to hypoxic injury? Show explanation Zone 3 lies downstream toward the central venous outflow. Blood has already supplied oxygen to upstream hepatocytes, so zone 3 has less oxygen reserve and is vulnerable when overall hepatic perfusion falls. 04. What structures lie near a portal triad? Show explanation A portal triad region contains branches of a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Blood from the first two enters sinusoids toward central veins, while bile travels in the opposite general direction toward ductules. 05. Why does gut blood flow increase after eating? Show explanation Digestive and absorptive demand promotes local metabolic vasodilation and postprandial hyperaemia. 06. How can abdominal veins contribute to blood pressure compensation? Show explanation The splanchnic venous bed stores a substantial volume of blood. Sympathetic venoconstriction reduces its capacity and shifts blood into central veins, supporting venous return during haemorrhage even while gut blood flow may decrease. 07. Why can shock damage intestinal mucosa despite sympathetic compensation? Show explanation During major blood loss, the body narrows splanchnic vessels to defend central pressure and perfusion. If intestinal flow falls too far or too long, mucosal oxygen delivery cannot meet demand and tissue injury may follow. 08. What is the space of Disse? Show explanation The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and the fenestrated endothelium of liver sinusoids. It allows exchange between blood plasma and hepatocytes; it is distinct from the sinusoidal blood lumen itself. 09. Why does portal hypertension promote ascites? Show explanation It increases filtration into hepatic interstitium and lymph production, which can overwhelm drainage. 10. Is all ascitic fluid rich in protein? Show explanation No. Ascitic protein concentration differs by cause. Portal hypertension, hepatic lymph formation, renal sodium retention and plasma protein changes contribute differently across disorders, so protein-rich fluid is not universal. 11. How can right-heart failure aggravate ascites? Show explanation Right-heart failure can increase central and hepatic venous pressures. Back-pressure in hepatic sinusoids increases filtration and lymph formation; if removal is exceeded, abdominal fluid accumulation can worsen. 12. Why do arterial anastomoses not guarantee gut survival after a major blockage? Show explanation Anastomoses may offer alternate routes after an arterial blockage, but their diameter and pressure gradient may not deliver enough blood. The affected gut segment can therefore remain ischaemic despite some collateral pathways. Chapter Essentials Portal flow passes through hepatic sinusoids before returning to IVC. Hepatic zone 3 is relatively vulnerable to low oxygen delivery. Digestion increases regional mucosal perfusion. Splanchnic veins are an important blood reservoir. Portal and hepatic venous pressures affect ascites production."},{"slug":"skin","title":"Cutaneous Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"38-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat","title":"Skin blood flow regulates heat"},{"id":"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin","title":"Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin"},{"id":"shock-and-temperature","title":"Shock and temperature"},{"id":"distinguish-blood-flow-functions","title":"Distinguish blood-flow functions"},{"id":"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin","title":"Lewis triple response to stroking the skin"},{"id":"understand-the-skin-s-unusual-circulation","title":"Understand the skin's unusual circulation"},{"id":"comprehensive-understanding-checks","title":"Comprehensive Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat\">Skin blood flow regulates heat</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png\" alt=\"Detailed medical cutaway illustration of dermal circulation showing vessels in the skin and their relationship to superficial layers\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Blood vessels within the skin.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Skin has a rich vascular network, including <strong>arteriovenous anastomoses</strong> in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin\">Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin</h2>\n<p>A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the <strong>white reaction</strong>). Firmer stroking may produce the <strong>triple response of Lewis</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Red line</strong> near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation.</li>\n<li><strong>Flare</strong>, a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators.</li>\n<li><strong>Wheal</strong>, a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"shock-and-temperature\">Shock and temperature</h2>\n<p>In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for <strong>thermoregulation</strong>, not simply nutrient demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Wheal versus flare:</strong> a wheal is a raised area of oedema from microvascular permeability; a flare is spreading erythema from local vasodilation involving sensory axon reflexes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-blood-flow-functions\">Distinguish blood-flow functions</h2>\n<p>Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must <strong>lose heat</strong>. Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin\">Lewis triple response to stroking the skin</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Lewis triple response — what each visible sign means</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 210\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three skin response illustrations: red line at stroke, broad surrounding red flare, and raised wheal swelling\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#293d5b\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><rect x=\"221\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><rect x=\"432\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><path d=\"M36 90h145\" stroke=\"#d83e54\" stroke-width=\"7\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><ellipse cx=\"319\" cy=\"90\" rx=\"74\" ry=\"42\" fill=\"#ee8b8b\" opacity=\".6\"/><path d=\"M267 90h104\" stroke=\"#d83e54\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"530\" cy=\"94\" rx=\"67\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#e69e8d\" stroke=\"#cd7662\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M470 95Q530 51 590 95\" stroke=\"#fff6ed\" stroke-width=\"11\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"108\" y=\"180\">Red line</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"180\">Flare</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"180\">Wheal</text><text x=\"108\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Local dilation</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Axon reflex</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Fluid swelling</text></g></svg><p>A schematic of three related features after firm stroking: narrow local redness, wider surrounding redness and raised oedema. The brief white response to light stroking is separate from Lewis's triple response.</p></figure>\n\n<p>A firm, blunt stroke produces a <strong>red line</strong> at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding <strong>flare</strong> (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve <strong>axon reflex</strong>) and a raised <strong>wheal</strong> (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. A brief <strong>white line response</strong> with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The <strong>wheal</strong>; the flare is spreading redness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understand-the-skin-s-unusual-circulation\">Understand the skin's unusual circulation</h2>\n<h3>Why isn't skin blood flow controlled only by oxygen demand?</h3>\n<p>Skin has two jobs: nourish its tissues and control body heat. When cold, narrowing cutaneous vessels conserves heat and can make the hands pale. During heat exposure, greater skin flow moves warm blood toward the surface, allowing heat loss. Special <strong>arteriovenous anastomoses</strong> in regions such as fingers and palms can carry blood between arterial and venous pathways while bypassing some nutritional capillaries.</p>\n<p>Some cutaneous vessels are under sympathetic <strong>noradrenergic vasoconstrictor</strong> control; active vasodilator mechanisms also contribute to heat stress responses in humans. Therefore, simply saying <em>sympathetic stimulation always constricts every skin vessel</em> misses the physiological context.</p>\n<h3>Understand the three components of Lewis's response</h3>\n<p>Imagine gently drawing a blunt object along the forearm, then applying a firmer stroke.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A brief <strong>white line</strong> may appear after light stroking and is distinct from the triple response.</li>\n<li>A firmer stimulus produces a <strong>red line</strong> at the contact site due to local vasodilatation.</li>\n<li>A wider surrounding <strong>flare</strong> develops because sensory nerve endings activate nearby branches through an <strong>axon reflex</strong>, encouraging arteriolar dilatation beyond the stroke.</li>\n<li>A raised <strong>wheal</strong> forms where increased vascular permeability allows plasma fluid to move into surrounding tissue, producing local swelling.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Histamine can contribute importantly to redness, flare and swelling. <strong>Red line = local redness; flare = spreading redness; wheal = raised oedema</strong>. These signs are related but not interchangeable.</p>\n<h3>Why is cool skin important in shock?</h3>\n<p>During haemorrhage, the body prioritizes blood flow to organs such as the brain and heart. Sympathetic vasoconstriction reduces skin flow, which may produce cool, pale extremities <strong>even before a large fall in measured arterial pressure</strong>. Skin findings alone cannot diagnose the type or severity of shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding checkpoint:</strong> A patient has a raised mark after a skin stroke but no large surrounding red area. Which component is raised, and what caused it? <em>The wheal, from locally increased microvascular permeability and fluid entry into tissue.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"comprehensive-understanding-checks\">Comprehensive Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. What makes skin perfusion different from most tissue perfusion?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Cutaneous vessels do supply nutrients, but their flow can change far more than local metabolic demand requires. Increased surface flow supports heat loss, while reduced flow conserves heat during cold exposure.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Where are specialized cutaneous arteriovenous shunts especially important?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Special arteriovenous anastomoses are especially important in non-hairy skin of fingers, palms and similar areas. They can route blood past superficial nutritional capillaries to adjust heat transfer quickly.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Why does cold generally reduce skin blood flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>In the cold, sympathetic vasoconstrictor activity narrows skin vessels and reduces warm blood reaching the surface, limiting heat loss. This does not mean skin circulation is regulated only by oxygen demand.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why does skin often become pale and cool in haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>When blood volume falls, sympathetic activation narrows skin vessels to help preserve central pressure. Less warm blood reaches the surface, so extremities may feel cool and look pale even while blood pressure is initially maintained.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. What is the white reaction after gentle skin stroking?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>A light stroke can produce a brief pale streak, traditionally linked to local reduction in superficial vessel filling. It is separate from the stronger red line, flare and wheal of Lewis's triple response.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. Name the Lewis triple response components.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The red line is narrow redness at the site; the flare is wider surrounding redness mediated partly by a local sensory axon reflex; the wheal is raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. What produces the red line?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The red line occurs at the stroke itself when local vessels dilate and more blood fills the superficial microcirculation. It differs from the surrounding flare, which spreads beyond the stimulated line.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. Why does flare extend beyond the initial stroke?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Sensory nerve endings activated at the stimulus spread signals along nearby branches of the same peripheral neuron. Released mediators cause wider arteriolar dilation, explaining why the flare extends beyond the contact line.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Does an axon reflex require a brainstem integration centre?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. A local axon reflex travels within branches of a peripheral sensory neuron and can cause nearby mediator release without requiring a spinal cord or brainstem integration centre.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. How does a wheal differ from flare?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>A wheal is raised because plasma fluid passes into the tissue when local microvascular permeability rises. A flare is broader redness from increased vessel diameter and blood flow, not the swelling itself.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why can early septic shock present with warm skin?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Early distributive or septic shock can involve widespread loss of vascular tone, leaving skin warm despite inadequate tissue perfusion. Warm skin therefore does not rule out shock, and later stages may look different.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why is skin temperature alone insufficient to diagnose shock?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Skin temperature depends on environmental temperature, local vasomotor tone, sweating and heat transfer, not only the amount of blood reaching vital organs. Cool skin can support a concern about reduced perfusion in context, but it cannot by itself diagnose shock.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Cutaneous perfusion has a strong thermoregulatory role.</li>\n<li>Sympathetic constriction reduces skin flow in cold and bleeding.</li>\n<li>Lewis triple response comprises red line, flare and wheal.</li>\n<li>Flare involves a peripheral sensory axon reflex.</li>\n<li>Wheal reflects increased capillary permeability and oedema.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response Skin blood flow regulates heat Figure. Blood vessels within the skin. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Skin has a rich vascular network, including arteriovenous anastomoses in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage. Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the white reaction). Firmer stroking may produce the triple response of Lewis: Red line near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation. Flare, a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators. Wheal, a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage. Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary. Shock and temperature In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for thermoregulation, not simply nutrient demand. Wheal versus flare: a wheal is a raised area of oedema from microvascular permeability; a flare is spreading erythema from local vasodilation involving sensory axon reflexes. Distinguish blood-flow functions Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must lose heat. Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness. Lewis triple response to stroking the skin Lewis triple response — what each visible sign means Red line Flare Wheal Local dilation Axon reflex Fluid swelling A schematic of three related features after firm stroking: narrow local redness, wider surrounding redness and raised oedema. The brief white response to light stroking is separate from Lewis's triple response. A firm, blunt stroke produces a red line at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding flare (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve axon reflex) and a raised wheal (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. A brief white line response with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response. Practice: Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The wheal; the flare is spreading redness. Understand the skin's unusual circulation Why isn't skin blood flow controlled only by oxygen demand? Skin has two jobs: nourish its tissues and control body heat. When cold, narrowing cutaneous vessels conserves heat and can make the hands pale. During heat exposure, greater skin flow moves warm blood toward the surface, allowing heat loss. Special arteriovenous anastomoses in regions such as fingers and palms can carry blood between arterial and venous pathways while bypassing some nutritional capillaries. Some cutaneous vessels are under sympathetic noradrenergic vasoconstrictor control; active vasodilator mechanisms also contribute to heat stress responses in humans. Therefore, simply saying sympathetic stimulation always constricts every skin vessel misses the physiological context. Understand the three components of Lewis's response Imagine gently drawing a blunt object along the forearm, then applying a firmer stroke. A brief white line may appear after light stroking and is distinct from the triple response. A firmer stimulus produces a red line at the contact site due to local vasodilatation. A wider surrounding flare develops because sensory nerve endings activate nearby branches through an axon reflex, encouraging arteriolar dilatation beyond the stroke. A raised wheal forms where increased vascular permeability allows plasma fluid to move into surrounding tissue, producing local swelling. Histamine can contribute importantly to redness, flare and swelling. Red line = local redness; flare = spreading redness; wheal = raised oedema. These signs are related but not interchangeable. Why is cool skin important in shock? During haemorrhage, the body prioritizes blood flow to organs such as the brain and heart. Sympathetic vasoconstriction reduces skin flow, which may produce cool, pale extremities even before a large fall in measured arterial pressure. Skin findings alone cannot diagnose the type or severity of shock. Understanding checkpoint: A patient has a raised mark after a skin stroke but no large surrounding red area. Which component is raised, and what caused it? The wheal, from locally increased microvascular permeability and fluid entry into tissue. Comprehensive Understanding Checks 01. What makes skin perfusion different from most tissue perfusion? Show explanation Cutaneous vessels do supply nutrients, but their flow can change far more than local metabolic demand requires. Increased surface flow supports heat loss, while reduced flow conserves heat during cold exposure. 02. Where are specialized cutaneous arteriovenous shunts especially important? Show explanation Special arteriovenous anastomoses are especially important in non-hairy skin of fingers, palms and similar areas. They can route blood past superficial nutritional capillaries to adjust heat transfer quickly. 03. Why does cold generally reduce skin blood flow? Show explanation In the cold, sympathetic vasoconstrictor activity narrows skin vessels and reduces warm blood reaching the surface, limiting heat loss. This does not mean skin circulation is regulated only by oxygen demand. 04. Why does skin often become pale and cool in haemorrhage? Show explanation When blood volume falls, sympathetic activation narrows skin vessels to help preserve central pressure. Less warm blood reaches the surface, so extremities may feel cool and look pale even while blood pressure is initially maintained. 05. What is the white reaction after gentle skin stroking? Show explanation A light stroke can produce a brief pale streak, traditionally linked to local reduction in superficial vessel filling. It is separate from the stronger red line, flare and wheal of Lewis's triple response. 06. Name the Lewis triple response components. Show explanation The red line is narrow redness at the site; the flare is wider surrounding redness mediated partly by a local sensory axon reflex; the wheal is raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability. 07. What produces the red line? Show explanation The red line occurs at the stroke itself when local vessels dilate and more blood fills the superficial microcirculation. It differs from the surrounding flare, which spreads beyond the stimulated line. 08. Why does flare extend beyond the initial stroke? Show explanation Sensory nerve endings activated at the stimulus spread signals along nearby branches of the same peripheral neuron. Released mediators cause wider arteriolar dilation, explaining why the flare extends beyond the contact line. 09. Does an axon reflex require a brainstem integration centre? Show explanation No. A local axon reflex travels within branches of a peripheral sensory neuron and can cause nearby mediator release without requiring a spinal cord or brainstem integration centre. 10. How does a wheal differ from flare? Show explanation A wheal is raised because plasma fluid passes into the tissue when local microvascular permeability rises. A flare is broader redness from increased vessel diameter and blood flow, not the swelling itself. 11. Why can early septic shock present with warm skin? Show explanation Early distributive or septic shock can involve widespread loss of vascular tone, leaving skin warm despite inadequate tissue perfusion. Warm skin therefore does not rule out shock, and later stages may look different. 12. Why is skin temperature alone insufficient to diagnose shock? Show explanation Skin temperature depends on environmental temperature, local vasomotor tone, sweating and heat transfer, not only the amount of blood reaching vital organs. Cool skin can support a concern about reduced perfusion in context, but it cannot by itself diagnose shock. Chapter Essentials Cutaneous perfusion has a strong thermoregulatory role. Sympathetic constriction reduces skin flow in cold and bleeding. Lewis triple response comprises red line, flare and wheal. Flare involves a peripheral sensory axon reflex. Wheal reflects increased capillary permeability and oedema."},{"slug":"fetal","title":"Fetal Circulation and Birth Transitions","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"42-45","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts","title":"Why fetal flow takes shortcuts"},{"id":"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths","title":"What changes after the first breaths?"},{"id":"fetal-haemoglobin","title":"Fetal haemoglobin"},{"id":"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream","title":"Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream"},{"id":"what-closes-after-delivery","title":"What closes after delivery?"},{"id":"trace-the-entire-fetal-circuit-rather-than-memorising-three-shunts","title":"Trace the entire fetal circuit rather than memorising three shunts"},{"id":"comprehensive-understanding-checks","title":"Comprehensive Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth</h1>\n<h2 id=\"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts\">Why fetal flow takes shortcuts</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg\" alt=\"Anatomical illustration tracing placenta, umbilical veins and arteries, fetal heart, ductus venosus, foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Complete fetal circulation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Before birth, gas exchange happens in the <strong>placenta</strong>, not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the <strong>umbilical vein</strong>. Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the inferior vena cava.</p>\n<p>Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foramen ovale:</strong> right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow).</li>\n<li><strong>Ductus arteriosus:</strong> pulmonary trunk → descending aorta.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two <strong>umbilical arteries</strong> carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Three fetal vascular shortcuts</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 171\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Ductus venosus bypasses liver sinusoids, foramen ovale connects right atrium with left atrium, and ductus arteriosus connects pulmonary trunk with descending aorta\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24354d\"><rect x=\"7\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ecf3ff\" stroke=\"#6f97c8\"/><rect x=\"229\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf5ee\" stroke=\"#76a988\"/><rect x=\"451\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff1dc\" stroke=\"#bb914f\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"107\" y=\"53\">Ductus venosus</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"53\">Foramen ovale</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"53\">Ductus arteriosus</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"107\" y=\"84\">Umbilical vein → IVC</text><text x=\"107\" y=\"107\">Partial liver bypass</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"84\">Right atrium → left</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"107\">atrium</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"84\">Pulmonary trunk →</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"107\">descending aorta</text></g></g></svg><p>Original route schematic; arrows show preferential fetal flow, not every stream or complete anatomical connection. Increased pulmonary flow after birth changes the pressure gradients that maintain these pathways.</p></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths\">What changes after the first breaths?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>At birth — two changes that redirect circulation</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"First breaths decrease pulmonary vascular resistance and increase left atrial return, while cord clamping removes low resistance placenta and raises systemic resistance, leading to functional shunt closure\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#253e5b\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"12\" width=\"315\" height=\"78\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e4f2ff\" stroke=\"#76a1c8\"/><rect x=\"337\" y=\"12\" width=\"315\" height=\"78\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf5e9\" stroke=\"#75a884\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"40\">First breaths</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"40\">Cord clamping</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"65\">Pulmonary resistance falls</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"65\">Placental circuit removed</text></g><rect x=\"8\" y=\"130\" width=\"315\" height=\"73\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f0f6ff\" stroke=\"#76a1c8\"/><rect x=\"337\" y=\"130\" width=\"315\" height=\"73\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f1f8ed\" stroke=\"#75a884\"/><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"156\">Lung flow and left atrial</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"174\">return increase</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"156\">Systemic resistance rises</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"174\">Umbilical venous flow stops</text></g><g stroke=\"#53778c\" stroke-width=\"3\"><path d=\"M165 90v39M158 120l7 9 7 -9M495 90v39M488 120l7 9 7 -9\"/></g></g></svg><p>These simultaneous changes shift atrial pressures, favor functional foramen ovale closure and contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure occurs later and varies by structure.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png\" alt=\"Medical illustration of neonatal heart circulation showing postnatal pulmonary blood flow and altered fetal shunt pathways\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Circulation after birth.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising <strong>left atrial pressure relative to right</strong> functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fetal-haemoglobin\">Fetal haemoglobin</h2>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds <strong>2,3-BPG</strong> less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Why first breaths increase left atrial return:</strong> aeration and higher oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary flow and pulmonary venous filling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream\">Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream</h2>\n<p>The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The <strong>umbilical vein</strong> brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs. <strong>Umbilical arteries</strong> return lower-oxygen blood to placenta.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-closes-after-delivery\">What closes after delivery?</h2>\n<p>First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing <strong>pulmonary vascular resistance</strong> and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases <strong>systemic vascular resistance</strong>. Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote <strong>ductus arteriosus constriction</strong>. Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce <strong>pulmonary</strong> resistance; placental separation independently raises <strong>systemic</strong> resistance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trace-the-entire-fetal-circuit-rather-than-memorising-three-shunts\">Trace the entire fetal circuit rather than memorising three shunts</h2>\n<h3>Why are the fetal lungs bypassed?</h3>\n<p>The placenta performs gas exchange before birth. Because the lungs are fluid-filled and pulmonary resistance is high, only a smaller proportion of right ventricular output passes through pulmonary capillaries. Oxygen-rich placental blood returns through the <strong>single umbilical vein</strong>. Some perfuses liver sinusoids; much travels through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> toward the inferior vena cava. Streamed IVC blood preferentially crosses the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> to the left atrium, then the left ventricle and ascending aorta, supporting the heart and brain.</p>\n<p>Blood from the superior vena cava predominantly enters the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk. Much then passes through the <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> into the descending aorta, because pulmonary vascular resistance remains high. <strong>Two umbilical arteries</strong> return blood from the fetus toward the placenta. These routes involve mixing: <em>oxygen-rich</em> never means that fetal blood has adult systemic arterial oxygen saturation.</p>\n<h3>Follow the first breaths and clamping as two linked changes</h3>\n<p><strong>First breaths:</strong> alveoli expand and oxygen tension increases. Pulmonary arterioles dilate, pulmonary vascular resistance falls, lung blood flow rises, and more blood returns through pulmonary veins to the <strong>left atrium</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cord clamping:</strong> placental circulation, a low-resistance parallel pathway, is removed. Systemic vascular resistance generally rises, while umbilical venous return stops. Changes in atrial pressure help press the foramen ovale flap closed as <strong>left atrial pressure becomes greater than right atrial pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Higher arterial oxygen and declining placental prostaglandin influence promote <strong>functional constriction of the ductus arteriosus</strong>. Ductus venosus flow ceases after umbilical venous return stops and the vessel also closes. <strong>Functional closure occurs before anatomical remodelling</strong>; the exact timing varies by structure and infant.</p>\n<h3>Trace the fate of the fetal pathways</h3>\n<p>The foramen ovale becomes the <strong>fossa ovalis</strong> when anatomical fusion occurs; the ductus arteriosus becomes the <strong>ligamentum arteriosum</strong>; and the ductus venosus becomes the <strong>ligamentum venosum</strong>. The umbilical vein becomes the <strong>ligamentum teres hepatis</strong>, while distal umbilical artery remnants become the <strong>medial umbilical ligaments</strong> (proximal portions remain patent as branches supplying the bladder).</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding checkpoint:</strong> Why does blood flow through the lungs increase at birth while systemic resistance increases too? <em>These are separate changes: lung aeration lowers pulmonary resistance, and removal of the placental pathway raises systemic resistance.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"comprehensive-understanding-checks\">Comprehensive Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Which organ provides gas exchange before birth?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The placenta exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide with maternal blood before birth. Fetal lungs are fluid-filled and have high vascular resistance, so only a smaller fraction of right ventricular output perfuses them.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. How many umbilical arteries and veins are usually present?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The two umbilical arteries carry comparatively deoxygenated fetal blood from the internal iliac arterial circulation toward the placenta. The single umbilical vein carries oxygen-enriched placental blood back to the fetus. These names describe flow relative to the fetal heart, not oxygen content.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Which fetal vessel bypasses part of hepatic sinusoidal blood flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The ductus venosus carries part of umbilical venous flow toward the inferior vena cava, bypassing much of the hepatic sinusoidal circulation. Some umbilical blood still passes through the liver.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Which fetal opening connects the atria?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The foramen ovale is an opening between the fetal atria. Preferentially oxygenated blood entering the right atrium from the IVC can cross it into the left atrium, then reach the left ventricle and ascending aorta.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. What does the ductus arteriosus connect?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta. Because fetal pulmonary resistance is high, much blood entering the pulmonary trunk flows through this channel rather than through the lungs.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. Why does fetal blood bypass much of the pulmonary circulation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Before birth, low alveolar oxygen and fluid-filled lungs maintain relatively high pulmonary vascular resistance. The foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus provide alternative routes, while the placenta handles gas exchange.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. What makes pulmonary vascular resistance fall after birth?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The first breaths open and aerate alveoli and increase oxygen tension. This relaxes pulmonary arterioles, sharply reducing pulmonary vascular resistance and raising flow through the lung capillaries.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. Why does the foramen ovale close functionally?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>With the lungs open, pulmonary venous return to the left atrium increases. Left atrial pressure rises relative to right atrial pressure, pressing the foramen ovale flap against the septum for functional closure; anatomical fusion may occur much later.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. What promotes ductus arteriosus constriction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>After birth, higher oxygen tension and reduced placental prostaglandin influence favor constriction of the ductus arteriosus. Functional constriction precedes anatomical remodelling into the ligamentum arteriosum.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. What is the postnatal remnant of ductus venosus?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The ductus venosus closes as umbilical venous flow stops after birth, eventually becoming the ligamentum venosum. This is separate from the remnant of the umbilical vein.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. What is the postnatal remnant of ductus arteriosus?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The ductus arteriosus functionally constricts as oxygen rises and prostaglandin influence falls, and later becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. Persistent patency can create abnormal aorta-to-pulmonary flow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why does fetal haemoglobin have high oxygen affinity?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Fetal haemoglobin binds 2,3-BPG less strongly than adult HbA, giving it higher oxygen affinity at a given oxygen pressure. This helps fetal blood take up oxygen across the placenta; replacement by HbA occurs gradually after birth.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Placenta is the fetal gas-exchange organ.</li>\n<li>Ductus venosus, foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus redirect fetal flow.</li>\n<li>First breaths lower pulmonary resistance.</li>\n<li>Cord clamping increases systemic resistance.</li>\n<li>HbF has higher oxygen affinity because of reduced 2,3-BPG binding.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth Why fetal flow takes shortcuts Figure. Complete fetal circulation. Image credit OpenStax College, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Before birth, gas exchange happens in the placenta, not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the umbilical vein. Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the ductus venosus to the inferior vena cava. Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways: Foramen ovale: right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow). Ductus arteriosus: pulmonary trunk → descending aorta. Two umbilical arteries carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers. Figure. Three fetal vascular shortcuts Ductus venosus Foramen ovale Ductus arteriosus Umbilical vein → IVC Partial liver bypass Right atrium → left atrium Pulmonary trunk → descending aorta Original route schematic; arrows show preferential fetal flow, not every stream or complete anatomical connection. Increased pulmonary flow after birth changes the pressure gradients that maintain these pathways. What changes after the first breaths? At birth — two changes that redirect circulation First breaths Cord clamping Pulmonary resistance falls Placental circuit removed Lung flow and left atrial return increase Systemic resistance rises Umbilical venous flow stops These simultaneous changes shift atrial pressures, favor functional foramen ovale closure and contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure occurs later and varies by structure. Figure. Circulation after birth. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising left atrial pressure relative to right functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus. Fetal haemoglobin HbF has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds 2,3-BPG less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months. Why first breaths increase left atrial return: aeration and higher oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary flow and pulmonary venous filling. Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The umbilical vein brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the ductus venosus to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the foramen ovale supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through ductus arteriosus into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs. Umbilical arteries return lower-oxygen blood to placenta. What closes after delivery? First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing pulmonary vascular resistance and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases systemic vascular resistance. Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant. HbF holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months. Practice: Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce pulmonary resistance; placental separation independently raises systemic resistance. Trace the entire fetal circuit rather than memorising three shunts Why are the fetal lungs bypassed? The placenta performs gas exchange before birth. Because the lungs are fluid-filled and pulmonary resistance is high, only a smaller proportion of right ventricular output passes through pulmonary capillaries. Oxygen-rich placental blood returns through the single umbilical vein. Some perfuses liver sinusoids; much travels through the ductus venosus toward the inferior vena cava. Streamed IVC blood preferentially crosses the foramen ovale to the left atrium, then the left ventricle and ascending aorta, supporting the heart and brain. Blood from the superior vena cava predominantly enters the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk. Much then passes through the ductus arteriosus into the descending aorta, because pulmonary vascular resistance remains high. Two umbilical arteries return blood from the fetus toward the placenta. These routes involve mixing: oxygen-rich never means that fetal blood has adult systemic arterial oxygen saturation. Follow the first breaths and clamping as two linked changes First breaths: alveoli expand and oxygen tension increases. Pulmonary arterioles dilate, pulmonary vascular resistance falls, lung blood flow rises, and more blood returns through pulmonary veins to the left atrium. Cord clamping: placental circulation, a low-resistance parallel pathway, is removed. Systemic vascular resistance generally rises, while umbilical venous return stops. Changes in atrial pressure help press the foramen ovale flap closed as left atrial pressure becomes greater than right atrial pressure. Higher arterial oxygen and declining placental prostaglandin influence promote functional constriction of the ductus arteriosus. Ductus venosus flow ceases after umbilical venous return stops and the vessel also closes. Functional closure occurs before anatomical remodelling; the exact timing varies by structure and infant. Trace the fate of the fetal pathways The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis when anatomical fusion occurs; the ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum; and the ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum. The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres hepatis, while distal umbilical artery remnants become the medial umbilical ligaments (proximal portions remain patent as branches supplying the bladder). Understanding checkpoint: Why does blood flow through the lungs increase at birth while systemic resistance increases too? These are separate changes: lung aeration lowers pulmonary resistance, and removal of the placental pathway raises systemic resistance. Comprehensive Understanding Checks 01. Which organ provides gas exchange before birth? Show explanation The placenta exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide with maternal blood before birth. Fetal lungs are fluid-filled and have high vascular resistance, so only a smaller fraction of right ventricular output perfuses them. 02. How many umbilical arteries and veins are usually present? Show explanation The two umbilical arteries carry comparatively deoxygenated fetal blood from the internal iliac arterial circulation toward the placenta. The single umbilical vein carries oxygen-enriched placental blood back to the fetus. These names describe flow relative to the fetal heart, not oxygen content. 03. Which fetal vessel bypasses part of hepatic sinusoidal blood flow? Show explanation The ductus venosus carries part of umbilical venous flow toward the inferior vena cava, bypassing much of the hepatic sinusoidal circulation. Some umbilical blood still passes through the liver. 04. Which fetal opening connects the atria? Show explanation The foramen ovale is an opening between the fetal atria. Preferentially oxygenated blood entering the right atrium from the IVC can cross it into the left atrium, then reach the left ventricle and ascending aorta. 05. What does the ductus arteriosus connect? Show explanation The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta. Because fetal pulmonary resistance is high, much blood entering the pulmonary trunk flows through this channel rather than through the lungs. 06. Why does fetal blood bypass much of the pulmonary circulation? Show explanation Before birth, low alveolar oxygen and fluid-filled lungs maintain relatively high pulmonary vascular resistance. The foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus provide alternative routes, while the placenta handles gas exchange. 07. What makes pulmonary vascular resistance fall after birth? Show explanation The first breaths open and aerate alveoli and increase oxygen tension. This relaxes pulmonary arterioles, sharply reducing pulmonary vascular resistance and raising flow through the lung capillaries. 08. Why does the foramen ovale close functionally? Show explanation With the lungs open, pulmonary venous return to the left atrium increases. Left atrial pressure rises relative to right atrial pressure, pressing the foramen ovale flap against the septum for functional closure; anatomical fusion may occur much later. 09. What promotes ductus arteriosus constriction? Show explanation After birth, higher oxygen tension and reduced placental prostaglandin influence favor constriction of the ductus arteriosus. Functional constriction precedes anatomical remodelling into the ligamentum arteriosum. 10. What is the postnatal remnant of ductus venosus? Show explanation The ductus venosus closes as umbilical venous flow stops after birth, eventually becoming the ligamentum venosum. This is separate from the remnant of the umbilical vein. 11. What is the postnatal remnant of ductus arteriosus? Show explanation The ductus arteriosus functionally constricts as oxygen rises and prostaglandin influence falls, and later becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. Persistent patency can create abnormal aorta-to-pulmonary flow. 12. Why does fetal haemoglobin have high oxygen affinity? Show explanation Fetal haemoglobin binds 2,3-BPG less strongly than adult HbA, giving it higher oxygen affinity at a given oxygen pressure. This helps fetal blood take up oxygen across the placenta; replacement by HbA occurs gradually after birth. Chapter Essentials Placenta is the fetal gas-exchange organ. Ductus venosus, foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus redirect fetal flow. First breaths lower pulmonary resistance. Cord clamping increases systemic resistance. HbF has higher oxygen affinity because of reduced 2,3-BPG binding."}];
